{"items":[{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"A class should have one reason to change: it answers to a single actor, not to everything it happens to do.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Martin's later wording is about **actors**, the people or teams whose requirements drive change. If finance, HR and operations can each force an edit to the same class, those edits collide. **Cohesion** is measured by what changes together, not by how few methods a class has.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Reason to change:** a stakeholder's rule, not a code feature.","**Smell:** a class imported by unrelated teams, or constant merge conflicts in one file.","**Fix:** separate by axis of change, then compose the pieces."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"\"Does one thing\" is a misreading. A class with ten methods that all change for the same reason is cohesive and fine.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"DB <- 3000\nA1 x2 B7 x1 = 30\nA1 x2 B7 x1 = 30\n1 stored","file":"Srp.java","regions":{"before":[9,22],"format":[29,35],"model":[24,27],"service":[40,53],"store":[37,38]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nrecord Line(String sku, int qty, long cents) {\n    long amount() { return qty * cents; }\n    String text() { return sku + \" x\" + qty; }\n}\n\nfinal class InvoiceBefore {                    // three reasons to change\n    private final List<Line> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    void add(Line line) { lines.add(line); }\n    long total() {                             // pricing rules\n        return lines.stream().mapToLong(Line::amount).sum();\n    }\n    String render() {                          // presentation\n        var sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (Line l : lines) sb.append(l.text()).append(' ');\n        return sb.append(\"= \").append(total() / 100).toString();\n    }\n    void save() { System.out.println(\"DB <- \" + total()); }   // persistence\n}\n\nrecord Invoice(List<Line> lines) {\n    Invoice { lines = List.copyOf(lines); }\n    long total() { return lines.stream().mapToLong(Line::amount).sum(); }\n}\n\nfinal class InvoiceRenderer {\n    String render(Invoice inv) {\n        var sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (Line l : inv.lines()) sb.append(l.text()).append(' ');\n        return sb.append(\"= \").append(inv.total() / 100).toString();\n    }\n}\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface InvoiceStore { void save(Invoice inv); }\n\nfinal class BillingService {\n    private final InvoiceRenderer renderer;\n    private final InvoiceStore store;\n\n    BillingService(InvoiceRenderer r, InvoiceStore s) {\n        renderer = r;\n        store = s;\n    }\n\n    String issue(Invoice inv) {\n        store.save(inv);\n        return renderer.render(inv);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var lines = List.of(new Line(\"A1\", 2, 1250), new Line(\"B7\", 1, 500));\n        var before = new InvoiceBefore();\n        lines.forEach(before::add);\n        before.save();\n        System.out.println(before.render());\n\n        var rows = new ArrayList<Invoice>();\n        var service = new BillingService(new InvoiceRenderer(), rows::add);\n        System.out.println(service.issue(new Invoice(lines)));\n        System.out.println(rows.size() + \" stored\");\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`InvoiceBefore` changes for three audiences: pricing rules, presentation and persistence. Edits for one can break the others.","region":"before"},{"note":"The domain object keeps only what changes with business rules: lines and totals. It is immutable, so it is safe to share.","region":"model"},{"note":"Presentation moves out. A new layout or locale is now an edit to the renderer alone.","region":"format"},{"note":"Persistence becomes a port. A one-method interface can be a method reference, here `rows::add`, in tests.","region":"store"},{"note":"The service only coordinates. It has two collaborators and no rules of its own, so it rarely needs to change.","region":"service"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Payroll code shared by finance, HR and the DBAs.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An `Employee` class held `calculatePay()` for finance, `reportHours()` for HR and `save()` for the database team. HR asked for overtime to be counted differently in reports; both methods shared a helper, so the change silently altered paychecks. Splitting it into `PayCalculator`, `HoursReporter` and `EmployeeRepository` gave each actor a class it can change without touching the others.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["class Employee {","    Money calculatePay() { ... }   // finance","    String reportHours() { ... }   // HR","    void save() { ... }            // DBAs","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: an HR change can no longer alter a paycheck, and each class has a test suite owned by one team.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["Two teams or rules regularly edit the same class.","A class needs unrelated dependencies: mailer, tax table and database.","Tests need heavy setup for logic that has nothing to do with it."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The class is small and every method changes together: splitting only adds indirection.","The second reason to change is speculative; split when it appears."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Splitting by technical layer instead of actor yields anemic classes and shotgun surgery.","Pieces with no orchestrator hide the use case across many files.","Separating data from behavior that always change together."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`XManager`, `XHelper` and `XUtil` classes that collect leftovers.","Every small feature touches five files."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and lambdas make small collaborators cheap, so splitting costs less than it did.","Sealed hierarchies keep variants together when they change together."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"SRP isolates one axis of change in one place; OCP then makes that axis extensible without edits.","ref":"ocp","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"ISP is SRP seen from the client: narrow the interface to the one role a caller needs.","ref":"isp","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A facade deliberately aggregates a subsystem for callers. It is fine while it only delegates and holds no rules.","ref":"facade","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Cross-cutting concerns such as logging or caching can leave a class by wrapping it.","ref":"decorator","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Once responsibilities are split, injection wires the pieces without the pieces knowing each other.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"A `Handler` decides where records go, a `Formatter` how they look, a `Filter` which pass.","name":"java.util.logging Handler, Formatter, Filter","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A step separates reading, transforming and writing; each changes independently.","name":"ItemReader, ItemProcessor, ItemWriter","where":"Spring Batch"},{"detail":"Sending, describing the request and decoding the body are separate types.","name":"java.net.http HttpClient, HttpRequest, HttpResponse.BodyHandler","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Stereotypes encode web, business and persistence concerns as separate beans.","name":"@Controller, @Service, @Repository","where":"Spring"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Employee {","    String name; int hours; long rate;","","    long calculatePay() { return hours * rate; }              // finance","    String hoursReport() { return name + \": \" + hours; }      // HR","    void save(List<String> db) { db.add(name + \",\" + rate); } // DBAs","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"srp","why":"Finance, HR and the DBAs each have a reason to change this one class."},{"ref":"isp","why":"There is no interface that forces clients to depend on methods they do not use."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"There is no subtype relationship, so nothing can fail to substitute."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Nothing here depends on a concrete collaborator; the issue is mixed reasons to change."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this class most clearly violate?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"srp","why":"The four edits come from four different reasons. Separate calculation, formatting, storage and delivery so each change lands in one class."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Extension points would help future variants, but they do not separate these four unrelated reasons to change."},{"ref":"isp","why":"The class implements no fat interface; splitting interfaces does not reduce the edits."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Inverting dependencies helps testing, but the class would still hold all four reasons to change."}],"prompt":"What is the most useful refactoring?","scenario":"A `ReportService` was edited four times last quarter: a tax-rate change, a new CSV column layout, an S3 bucket move and a new email subject. Different people made each edit, and two collided in review."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Mailer { void send(String s); }","interface InvoiceStore { void save(Invoice i); }","record Invoice(String id, long net) {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The orchestrating service embeds a tax rule, so a VAT change now edits the service. Move the rule into the invoice or a tax calculator and keep the service coordinating only.","flawLines":[11],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class InvoiceService {","    private final InvoiceStore store;","    private final Mailer mailer;","","    InvoiceService(InvoiceStore store, Mailer mailer) {","        this.store = store;","        this.mailer = mailer;","    }","","    void issue(Invoice inv) {","        long gross = inv.net() * 119 / 100;","        store.save(inv);","        mailer.send(inv.id() + \": \" + gross);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that adds a second reason to change."},{"blocks":[["var lines = List.of(new Line(\"A1\", 2, 1250));"],["var invoice = new Invoice(lines);"],["var rows = new ArrayList<Invoice>();"],["var service = new BillingService(new InvoiceRenderer(), rows::add);"],["String text = service.issue(invoice);"],["System.out.println(text);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Line(String sku, int qty, long cents) {}","record Invoice(List<Line> lines) {}","final class InvoiceRenderer {}","interface InvoiceStore { void save(Invoice i); }","final class BillingService {","    BillingService(InvoiceRenderer r, InvoiceStore s) {}","    String issue(Invoice i) { return \"ok\"; }","}","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Data first, then the store the service needs, then the service; only a built service can issue, and only an issued invoice has text to print.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the wiring so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Responsibility = reason to change = one actor","Not 'does one thing' or one method","Smell: unrelated teams editing the same class","Split by axis of change, then compose","Do not split speculatively"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A responsibility is a reason to change, tied to one actor whose requirements drive that code. A class should answer to one. It does not mean one method or one verb: a cohesive class with many methods is fine. The test is whether unrelated stakeholders can force edits to the same file.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Single Responsibility Principle in your own words. What counts as a 'responsibility', and what does it not mean?"}],"id":"srp"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Software entities should be open for extension and closed for modification: add behavior by adding code, not by editing tested code.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Find the axis that varies, put an **abstraction** in front of it, and make the stable code depend only on that abstraction. New variants then arrive as new classes. The module is **closed** to edits and **open** through its extension point. Meyer proposed it for inheritance; Martin recast it around polymorphism.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Closed:** the orchestrating code, already tested and released.","**Open:** a new `PriceRule` implementation, lambda or plugin.","**Cost:** you must guess the axis; abstracting every axis is waste."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"OCP is not \"never edit\". Fixing a bug is a modification. It targets the recurring edit: the next `case` added to the same switch.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"before VIP: 1800\nafter VIP: 1800\nblack friday: 1350\nblack friday floor: 1000","file":"Ocp.java","regions":{"abstraction":[13,16],"before":[3,11],"closed":[18,28],"extensions":[30,36],"plug":[46,50]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\nfinal class PricerBefore {\n    long price(String customerType, long cents) {\n        return switch (customerType) {        // every new tier edits this\n            case \"VIP\" -> cents * 90 / 100;\n            case \"STAFF\" -> cents * 70 / 100;\n            default -> cents;\n        };\n    }\n}\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface PriceRule {\n    long apply(long cents);\n}\n\nfinal class Pricer {                          // closed for modification\n    private final List<PriceRule> rules;\n\n    Pricer(List<PriceRule> rules) { this.rules = List.copyOf(rules); }\n\n    long price(long cents) {\n        long result = cents;\n        for (PriceRule rule : rules) result = rule.apply(result);\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n\nrecord PercentOff(int percent) implements PriceRule {\n    public long apply(long cents) { return cents * (100 - percent) / 100; }\n}\n\nrecord MinimumPrice(long floor) implements PriceRule {\n    public long apply(long cents) { return Math.max(cents, floor); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var old = new PricerBefore();\n        System.out.println(\"before VIP: \" + old.price(\"VIP\", 2000));\n\n        var vip = new Pricer(List.of(new PercentOff(10)));\n        System.out.println(\"after VIP: \" + vip.price(2000));\n\n        var blackFriday = new Pricer(List.of(\n            new PercentOff(10),\n            new PercentOff(25),\n            new MinimumPrice(1000),\n            cents -> cents / 10 * 10));       // a lambda too\n        System.out.println(\"black friday: \" + blackFriday.price(2000));\n        System.out.println(\"black friday floor: \" + blackFriday.price(1200));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Every new customer tier edits this `switch`, so already-tested pricing is reopened on each release.","region":"before"},{"note":"The extension point is a one-method interface. It names the axis that varies and nothing else.","region":"abstraction"},{"note":"`Pricer` depends only on the abstraction. It is closed: new rules never require editing it.","region":"closed"},{"note":"New behavior arrives as new types. Each rule is small, tested alone and unaware of the others.","region":"extensions"},{"note":"Composition happens at the edge. The Black Friday pricer is a new list of rules, including a lambda, with no edit to `Pricer`.","region":"plug"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Alert routing in a monitoring platform.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each new alert destination (Slack, PagerDuty, SMS) meant a new `case` in `AlertRouter`, a core release and a regression run on every existing channel. The router now depends on an `AlertChannel` interface and discovers implementations with `ServiceLoader`. A channel ships as a separate jar; the router is untouched and its tests never change.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface AlertChannel { String name(); void send(Alert a); }","","for (AlertChannel c : ServiceLoader.load(AlertChannel.class)) {","    registry.put(c.name(), c);","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: teams add channels without a core release, and no existing channel can regress.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["The same `switch` or `if` chain grows with every feature.","Variants come from other teams or plugins.","The core is costly to retest or release."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Only one variant exists and no second is in sight.","The axis of change is unknown: wait for the second case."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Guessing the wrong axis: the abstraction fits yesterday's variants and blocks tomorrow's.","Rules that depend on order or on each other make the extension point leaky.","Plugin discovery moves failures from compile time to runtime."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Interfaces with one implementation \"for future extension\".","A strategy hierarchy for a rule unchanged in five years."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Lambdas and `ServiceLoader` are lightweight extension points.","A sealed interface closes the set on purpose; the compiler flags every `switch` to update."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"OCP names the goal; Strategy is the most common structure that achieves it for one varying algorithm.","ref":"strategy","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Decorator extends behavior by wrapping an object, so the wrapped class is never edited.","ref":"decorator","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Template Method extends through inheritance hooks, fixed at compile time. Interface-based OCP extends by composition.","ref":"template-method","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Visitor opens the set of operations but closes the set of types; adding a type edits every visitor.","ref":"visitor","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Extensions are only safe if they honor the abstraction's contract, which is what LSP states.","ref":"lsp","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Loads providers of a service interface from the classpath or module path without changing the consumer.","name":"java.util.ServiceLoader","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Registered `Driver` implementations extend JDBC to a new database with no change to client code.","name":"java.sql.DriverManager","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Filters are added through `@WebFilter` or deployment configuration; the container is unchanged.","name":"jakarta.servlet.Filter","where":"Jakarta Servlet"},{"detail":"Starters contribute auto-configuration classes listed in `META-INF/spring/`, extending the app without edits.","name":"AutoConfiguration.imports","where":"Spring Boot"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["record Order(String carrier, double kg) {}","class Snippet {","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["double fee(Order o) {","    switch (o.carrier()) {","        case \"DHL\": return o.kg() * 4.0;","        case \"UPS\": return o.kg() * 3.5 + 2;","        case \"FEDEX\": return o.kg() * 5.0;","        default: throw new IllegalArgumentException(o.carrier());","    }","}","// each quarter another carrier arrives, and another case"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"ocp","why":"Every new carrier reopens and edits tested code; the variation should sit behind an abstraction."},{"ref":"srp","why":"The method has one job, computing a fee; the problem is the recurring edit, not mixed concerns."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"No inheritance or subtyping is involved."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Nothing depends on an infrastructure detail; the pain is the growing switch."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this code most clearly strain?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"ocp","why":"Keep the engine stable behind a `FraudCheck` interface and let checks be added as plugins, so extension needs no edits."},{"ref":"srp","why":"Splitting the engine into classes by concern would not let outside parties add checks."},{"ref":"isp","why":"The interface is not too wide; there is no interface yet, only a long conditional."},{"ref":"tell-dont-ask","why":"Encapsulating state does not address how new checks get added."}],"prompt":"Which principle should drive the redesign?","scenario":"A fraud engine ends in a growing if-else of 40 country-specific checks edited by one team. Partners now want to supply their own checks without repository access, and one check must never force retesting the others."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.DayOfWeek;","import java.util.List;","interface PriceRule { long apply(long cents); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"A hardcoded Friday discount lives inside the class that is supposed to be closed. Express it as a `PriceRule`, so promotions are added without editing `Pricer`.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Pricer {","    private final List<PriceRule> rules;","","    Pricer(List<PriceRule> rules) { this.rules = rules; }","","    long price(long cents, DayOfWeek day) {","        long result = cents;","        for (PriceRule rule : rules) result = rule.apply(result);","        if (day == DayOfWeek.FRIDAY) result = result * 90 / 100;","        return result;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that reopens a closed class."},{"blocks":[["var blackFriday = new PercentOff(25);"],["var rules = List.of(new PercentOff(10), blackFriday);"],["var pricer = new Pricer(rules);"],["long price = pricer.price(2000);"],["System.out.println(price);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","interface PriceRule { long apply(long cents); }","record PercentOff(int percent) implements PriceRule { public long apply(long c) { return c; } }","final class Pricer { Pricer(List<? extends PriceRule> r) {} long price(long c) { return c; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Rules come first, then the list of them, then the pricer built from the list. Only a built pricer can price, and printing needs the result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the setup so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Closed to edits, open to extension","Achieved through an abstraction on the varying axis","Targets recurring edits, not bug fixes","Requires picking the axis; premature abstraction is waste","Safe extension relies on LSP"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Stable, tested code is closed: new behavior should not require editing it. It stays open because it depends on an abstraction that new implementations can fill. OCP does not forbid bug fixes. It targets the recurring change, like another switch case, and only pays off if you guessed the right axis.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Open/Closed Principle in your own words. What is closed, what is open, and what does it not forbid?"}],"id":"ocp"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Code written against a supertype must keep working, unchanged, when handed any subtype.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Liskov's rule concerns **behavioral** compatibility, not signatures. A subtype may weaken preconditions and strengthen postconditions and invariants, never the reverse, and must not allow state changes the supertype forbids. Substitutability is judged by what clients **rely on**, which is why real-world 'is-a' is a poor guide.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Preconditions:** a subtype may accept more, never less.","**Postconditions and invariants:** may promise more, never less.","**History:** no new state transitions the supertype disallows."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The compiler checks signatures only. LSP violations compile cleanly and surface later as `instanceof` checks, thrown `UnsupportedOperationException` or wrong results.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Rect: 10\nSquare: 4\ntotal area: 19\n5","file":"Lsp.java","regions":{"after":[15,27],"before":[1,13],"client":[30,34]},"role":"implementation","source":"class Rect {\n    protected int w, h;\n    Rect(int w, int h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }\n    void setW(int w) { this.w = w; }\n    void setH(int h) { this.h = h; }\n    int area() { return w * h; }\n}\n\nfinal class SquareBefore extends Rect {      // mathematically \"is a\" Rect\n    SquareBefore(int side) { super(side, side); }\n    @Override void setW(int w) { this.w = w; this.h = w; }\n    @Override void setH(int h) { this.w = h; this.h = h; }\n}\n\nsealed interface Shape permits Rectangle, Square {\n    int area();\n}\n\nrecord Rectangle(int w, int h) implements Shape {\n    public int area() { return w * h; }\n    Rectangle withW(int w) { return new Rectangle(w, h); }\n}\n\nrecord Square(int side) implements Shape {\n    public int area() { return side * side; }\n    Square withSide(int side) { return new Square(side); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static int stretch(Rect r) {\n        r.setW(5);\n        r.setH(2);\n        return r.area();                     // contract: 5 * 2\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Rect: \" + stretch(new Rect(1, 1)));\n        System.out.println(\"Square: \" + stretch(new SquareBefore(1)));\n\n        Shape[] shapes = { new Rectangle(5, 2), new Square(3) };\n        int total = 0;\n        for (Shape s : shapes) total += s.area();\n        System.out.println(\"total area: \" + total);\n        System.out.println(new Rectangle(1, 1).withW(5).area());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A mutable `Square` extends `Rect` and keeps its sides equal. It compiles, and geometry says it is a rectangle.","region":"before"},{"note":"`stretch` relies on a contract the supertype implies: setting width and height independently gives `5 * 2`.","region":"client"},{"note":"Substitutability is restored by removing mutation. Both shapes are immutable records behind a sealed `Shape` that promises only `area()`.","region":"after"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A payment gateway where one method cannot refund.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A `PaymentMethod` interface had `charge` and `refund`. A `GiftCard` implementation threw on `refund`, and the nightly reconciliation job, which reverses failed orders through the same interface, crashed. The fix was not a `try/catch` in the job. Refund moved to a `Refundable` sub-interface, so the job's signature states exactly what it can rely on.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface PaymentMethod { Receipt charge(Money amount); }","interface Refundable extends PaymentMethod {","    void refund(Receipt r);","}","","void reverse(Order o, Refundable method) { method.refund(o.receipt()); }"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the unsupported case becomes a compile error instead of a 3 a.m. exception.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["Designing an abstract type that third parties will implement.","Reviewing a subclass that overrides a method to throw, no-op or narrow its inputs.","Writing contract tests every implementation must pass."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Using inheritance as taxonomy: a square \"is a\" rectangle only in geometry.","Patching a violation with `instanceof` in the client."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Overrides that throw `UnsupportedOperationException` for an inherited method.","A subclass rejecting inputs the parent accepted, such as `null`.","Mutable subtypes breaking an invariant clients assumed."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and sealed interfaces remove most of the mutation that causes LSP breaks.","`equals`, `hashCode` and `compareTo` have contracts that every override must honor."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Fat interfaces force implementers to stub methods and thereby break substitutability; narrow roles prevent it.","ref":"isp","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Composition avoids the subtype relationship altogether, so there is no supertype contract to violate.","ref":"composition-over-inheritance","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"OCP extends through new subtypes; LSP is what keeps those extensions safe for existing clients.","ref":"ocp","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Immutability removes the history-rule violations that make mutable subtypes unsafe.","ref":"immutable-object","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`add` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`: the JDK documents these as optional operations, a known compromise.","name":"List.of / Collections.unmodifiableList","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Their documented contracts (symmetry, transitivity, consistency) are LSP rules every subclass must keep.","name":"Object.equals and Comparable.compareTo","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Extends `Hashtable<Object,Object>`, so callers can insert non-`String` entries that `getProperty` ignores.","name":"java.util.Properties","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Extends `Vector`, so a stack can be indexed and inserted into at any position, violating stack discipline.","name":"java.util.Stack","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["class FileStore { String read() { return \"\"; } void write(String d) {} }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["class ReadOnlyStore extends FileStore {","    @Override","    void write(String data) {","        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(\"read-only\");","    }","}","// callers elsewhere: store.write(report); String s = store.read();"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"lsp","why":"Callers of `FileStore` expect `write` to work; the subtype refuses a promise of its supertype."},{"ref":"isp","why":"Splitting read and write would be the remedy, but the violation is the subtype breaking the contract."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Nothing was edited in the base class; the extension itself is the problem."},{"ref":"dip","why":"No concrete dependency is hard-wired anywhere in this code."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this subclass break?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"lsp","why":"The adapter satisfies the signatures but not the documented FIFO contract that clients rely on."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Extending without edits is what OCP wants; the issue is that the extension is not substitutable."},{"ref":"dip","why":"The scheduler depends on an abstraction already; the abstraction was not honored."},{"ref":"isp","why":"The interface is not too wide; one implementation breaks its promises."}],"prompt":"Which principle was violated?","scenario":"A `Queue` interface documents FIFO order. A teammate adds `PriorityQueueAdapter` implementing it so the scheduler can use it. All unit tests pass, but an ordering-sensitive job produces wrong results in production."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`CappedCounter` silently ignores `increment()`, so code expecting `value()` to grow by one breaks. Do not extend `Counter` for a bounded variant; model it as a separate type or put the cap in the base contract.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["class Counter {","    protected int value;","    void increment() { value++; }   // value() grows by one","    int value() { return value; }","}","","final class CappedCounter extends Counter {","    @Override void increment() {","        if (value < 10) super.increment();","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that makes the subtype unsubstitutable."},{"blocks":[["Rect r = factory.get();"],["r.setW(5);","r.setH(2);"],["int area = r.area();"],["System.out.println(area == 10 ? \"ok\" : \"broken\");"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.Supplier;","class Rect { int w, h; void setW(int w) { this.w = w; } void setH(int h) { this.h = h; } int area() { return w * h; } }","class Snippet { void run(Supplier<Rect> factory) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Obtain the object through the supertype, mutate it, read the derived value, then check the contract. Each step consumes the previous result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the contract check so that each step only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Substitutability is behavioral, not just type-level","Preconditions cannot tighten; postconditions cannot weaken","Mutable Square breaks the independent width/height expectation","Symptoms: instanceof checks, UnsupportedOperationException","Fix by redesigning: immutability, composition or narrower interfaces"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Any code written for a supertype must work with any subtype without knowing which. It is about behavior, not signatures: preconditions cannot tighten, postconditions cannot weaken. A mutable Square couples width and height, so code that sets them independently and expects the product gets the wrong area.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"State the Liskov Substitution Principle in your own words and explain why a mutable `Square extends Rectangle` breaks it."}],"id":"lsp"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use; define narrow interfaces per client role.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A fat interface couples every client to every method: a change to `fax` recompiles and retests print-only code, and implementers must stub operations they cannot support. Split by **role** as the client sees it, then let one class implement several **role interfaces** when it really does several things.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Cut line:** what a client calls, not what an implementer offers.","**Symptoms:** stubs that throw, mocks with fifteen stubbed methods, unrelated imports.","**Result:** small interfaces, often one method, that lambdas can implement."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"ISP is LSP's preventive medicine: a narrow interface leaves nothing for an implementer to fake.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"before: scan() unsupported\nbasic: [Q3 report]\nprint Q3 report\nlambda Q3 report\nfax memo to 555-0100","file":"Isp.java","regions":{"before":[4,16],"clients":[35,40],"impls":[22,33],"roles":[18,20]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface Machine {\n    void print(String doc);\n    String scan();\n    void fax(String doc, String number);\n}\n\nfinal class BasicPrinterBefore implements Machine {\n    public void print(String doc) { System.out.println(\"print \" + doc); }\n    public String scan() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }\n    public void fax(String d, String n) {\n        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();\n    }\n}\n\ninterface Printer { void print(String doc); }\ninterface DocScanner { String scan(); }\ninterface Fax { void fax(String doc, String number); }\n\nfinal class BasicPrinter implements Printer {\n    final List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void print(String doc) { out.add(doc); }\n}\n\nfinal class OfficeMachine implements Printer, DocScanner, Fax {\n    public void print(String doc) { System.out.println(\"print \" + doc); }\n    public String scan() { return \"scan#1\"; }\n    public void fax(String doc, String number) {\n        System.out.println(\"fax \" + doc + \" to \" + number);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class ReportJob {\n    private final Printer printer;            // needs one capability only\n\n    ReportJob(Printer printer) { this.printer = printer; }\n    void run() { printer.print(\"Q3 report\"); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            new BasicPrinterBefore().scan();\n        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"before: scan() unsupported\");\n        }\n        var basic = new BasicPrinter();\n        new ReportJob(basic).run();\n        System.out.println(\"basic: \" + basic.out);\n        new ReportJob(new OfficeMachine()).run();\n        new ReportJob(doc -> System.out.println(\"lambda \" + doc)).run();\n        new OfficeMachine().fax(\"memo\", \"555-0100\");\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"One `Machine` interface bundles three capabilities. A printer-only device must implement `scan` and `fax` by throwing.","region":"before"},{"note":"Role interfaces, cut by what a client calls. Each has one method, so each is also a valid lambda target.","region":"roles"},{"note":"Implementers choose the roles they really support. `BasicPrinter` is only a `Printer`; `OfficeMachine` is all three.","region":"impls"},{"note":"`ReportJob` asks only for `Printer`. It runs against a basic printer, an office machine or a lambda, and can never call `fax`.","region":"clients"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"One user-store interface serving a dashboard and a batch importer.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A `UserStore` interface had fourteen methods. The read-only dashboard depended on all of them, so tests mocked writes it never made, and a read replica could not implement the interface at all. It was split into `UserReader` and `UserWriter`. The primary store implements both, the replica only the reader, and the dashboard is wired to `UserReader`.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface UserReader {","    Optional<User> byId(UserId id);","    List<User> search(Query q);","}","interface UserWriter { void save(User u); }","","final class Dashboard { Dashboard(UserReader users) { ... } }","final class PgStore implements UserReader, UserWriter { ... }"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the dashboard cannot write, replicas fit the type system, and mocks shrink to two methods.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["An implementer must throw or no-op for some inherited methods.","Clients use disjoint subsets of one interface.","Mocks or fakes are large."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Roles always change and are used together: splitting adds names, not decoupling.","A published interface has many implementers: splitting later breaks them."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Interface-per-method with no role meaning, such as `Doer` or `Getter`.","A client needing five role interfaces often signals an SRP problem.","Intersection bounds like `<T extends A & B>` spreading through APIs."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Functional interfaces are ISP at its limit: one method, any lambda.","`default` methods evolve an interface without breaking implementers, but do not shrink what clients see."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"SRP shapes classes by reasons to change; ISP shapes interfaces by what each client calls.","ref":"srp","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Narrow interfaces leave no method an implementer must fake, which keeps subtypes substitutable.","ref":"lsp","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"When a fat third-party interface cannot be split, adapt it to the narrow role your client needs.","ref":"adapter","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A facade simplifies a subsystem for callers by hiding it; ISP narrows an existing interface to a client's needs.","ref":"facade","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`Supplier`, `Function`, `Predicate` and friends are one-method role interfaces.","name":"java.util.function","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`java.nio.channels` separates read and write roles; `ByteChannel` combines them.","name":"ReadableByteChannel, WritableByteChannel, ByteChannel","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Independent capabilities that a class can add separately, rather than one stream mega-interface.","name":"java.io.Closeable and Flushable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Repository capabilities are separate interfaces that you extend selectively.","name":"CrudRepository, ListCrudRepository, PagingAndSortingRepository","where":"Spring Data"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface Storage {","    byte[] read(String key);","    void write(String key, byte[] value);","    void backup(java.nio.file.Path target);","}","final class Thumbnailer {","    private final Storage storage;     // only ever calls read()","    Thumbnailer(Storage storage) { this.storage = storage; }","    byte[] thumb(String key) { return storage.read(key); }","}","// MemoryCache implements Storage; its backup() throws"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"isp","why":"Thumbnailer is forced to depend on `write` and `backup`, and implementers must fake them."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"The throwing `backup` looks like LSP, but it is a symptom; the cause is the fat interface."},{"ref":"srp","why":"SRP is about a class's reasons to change; here a client is coupled to methods it never calls."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Depending on an interface is the right direction; the interface itself is too broad."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this design most clearly violate?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"isp","why":"Split into `EmailSender`, `SmsSender` and `PushSender`. Each adapter and client then depends only on its role."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"Making adapters return silently would only hide the broken promise; the interface still forces it."},{"ref":"srp","why":"The adapter has a single job already; the shared interface is what is too wide."},{"ref":"dip","why":"The jobs already depend on an interface; its shape is the problem."}],"prompt":"What is the right fix?","scenario":"A `NotificationSender` interface declares `sendEmail`, `sendSms` and `sendPush`. The SMS vendor adapter throws for the other two, and the invoice job, which only sends email, needs three stubbed methods in every test."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The constructor asks for a `ReadWriter` although `Report` only reads, so callers must supply write access and tests must fake it. Accept `Reader`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["interface Reader { String read(String key); }","interface Writer { void write(String key, String v); }","interface ReadWriter extends Reader, Writer { }","","final class Report {","    private final Reader store;","","    Report(ReadWriter store) { this.store = store; }","    String title() { return store.read(\"title\").toUpperCase(); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that asks for more than the class needs."},{"blocks":[["var basic = new BasicPrinter();"],["var job = new ReportJob(basic);"],["job.run();"],["System.out.println(basic.out);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Printer { void print(String d); }","final class BasicPrinter implements Printer { final List<String> out = new ArrayList<>(); public void print(String d) { out.add(d); } }","final class ReportJob { ReportJob(Printer p) {} void run() {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the device, hand it to the job as a `Printer`, run the job, then inspect what the device recorded.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the wiring so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Cut interfaces by the client's role","Fat interfaces force stubs and wide mocks","Throwing stubs signal an ISP/LSP problem","A class may implement many role interfaces","Single-method interfaces are lambda-friendly"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Clients should depend only on the methods they call, so interfaces are cut by client role rather than by implementer capability. A fat interface forces implementers to stub unsupported methods, usually by throwing UnsupportedOperationException, which is an LSP violation in disguise. Several small role interfaces fix both.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Interface Segregation Principle in your own words, and how it relates to throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`."}],"id":"isp"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"High-level policy should not depend on low-level details. Both should depend on abstractions that the policy owns.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The rule inverts twice. Source dependencies point at an **abstraction**, and that abstraction is **owned by the consumer**: `OrderService` defines `Notifier` in terms of what orders need, and SMTP adapts to it. That is what lets the business core build and test without infrastructure.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Not the same as DI:** DIP is the design rule; injection is one way to satisfy it.","**Ownership matters:** an interface that mirrors the vendor API is not inversion.","**Boundary:** invert at module and layer seams, not around every `new`."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"`new ArrayList<>()` in a method does not violate DIP. Depending on a slow, external or unstable detail does.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"SMTP ada\nSMTP ada: Order confirmed\ntest double saw [grace/Order confirmed]","file":"Dip.java","regions":{"abstraction":[16,18],"before":[4,14],"details":[30,41],"highlevel":[20,28],"wiring":[46,49]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nfinal class SmtpClient {\n    void send(String to, String body) { System.out.println(\"SMTP \" + to); }\n}\n\nfinal class OrderServiceBefore {\n    private final SmtpClient smtp = new SmtpClient();   // hard-wired detail\n\n    void place(String customer) {\n        smtp.send(customer, \"Order confirmed\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface Notifier {                         // owned by the high-level policy\n    void notify(String customer, String message);\n}\n\nfinal class OrderService {\n    private final Notifier notifier;\n\n    OrderService(Notifier notifier) { this.notifier = notifier; }\n\n    void place(String customer) {\n        notifier.notify(customer, \"Order confirmed\");\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class SmtpNotifier implements Notifier {\n    public void notify(String customer, String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"SMTP \" + customer + \": \" + message);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class RecordingNotifier implements Notifier {\n    final List<String> sent = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void notify(String customer, String message) {\n        sent.add(customer + \"/\" + message);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new OrderServiceBefore().place(\"ada\");\n        new OrderService(new SmtpNotifier()).place(\"ada\");\n\n        var fake = new RecordingNotifier();\n        new OrderService(fake).place(\"grace\");\n        System.out.println(\"test double saw \" + fake.sent);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The high-level `OrderServiceBefore` constructs `SmtpClient` itself. Policy now depends on a detail and cannot run without it.","region":"before"},{"note":"The abstraction is shaped by what orders need to say, not by SMTP. It lives with the high-level code.","region":"abstraction"},{"note":"`OrderService` depends only on `Notifier`. Its source no longer mentions SMTP, so it compiles and tests without infrastructure.","region":"highlevel"},{"note":"The details now point inward: both the SMTP adapter and a recording test double implement the abstraction.","region":"details"},{"note":"Composition happens at the edge. Production passes the SMTP notifier; a test passes the recorder and asserts on what it saw.","region":"wiring"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A trading engine that must run live and in backtests.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A strategy engine called a vendor's WebSocket client directly, so strategies could only be tested against the live market. The core now declares `MarketData` with the two operations it needs. A `VendorFeed` adapter implements it in production and a `CsvReplay` in backtests. The same engine produces both results, and switching vendors touches one adapter.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface MarketData { Quote latest(Symbol s); }","","Engine live = new Engine(new VendorFeed(client));","Engine backtest = new Engine(new CsvReplay(path));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: backtests are deterministic, and a vendor change never reaches the trading logic.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"orders","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"notifier"},{"from":"smtp","rel":"realizes","to":"notifier"},{"from":"fake","rel":"realizes","to":"notifier"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"orders","kind":"class","members":["- notifier: Notifier","+ place(customer)"],"name":"OrderService","role":"HighLevel","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"notifier","kind":"interface","members":["+ notify(customer, msg)"],"name":"Notifier","role":"Abstraction","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"smtp","kind":"class","members":["+ notify(customer, msg)"],"name":"SmtpNotifier","role":"LowLevel","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"fake","kind":"class","members":["+ notify(customer, msg)"],"name":"RecordingNotifier","role":"LowLevel","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"notifier","text":"owned by the high-level side"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Core logic must be tested without network, disk or clock.","An infrastructure choice such as vendor or database may change.","Several environments need different implementations."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The dependency is a stable platform type such as `String`, `List` or `Instant`.","The script is tiny and wiring costs more than the coupling."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["An interface shaped like the vendor API leaks the detail it should hide.","Placing the abstraction in the infrastructure module: the arrow does not invert.","A Service Locator hides dependencies instead of inverting them."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`FooService` plus `FooServiceImpl` for every class.","Mocking every collaborator so tests restate the implementation."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Constructor injection with `final` fields satisfies DIP with no container.","JPMS `provides ... with` lets a module supply an implementation of another module's service."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"DIP says who owns the abstraction; injection is the mechanism that hands the implementation in.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A locator also decouples from the concrete class but hides dependencies inside method bodies and makes them harder to see and test.","ref":"service-locator","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Ports and adapters is DIP applied to a whole architecture: the domain owns the ports, infrastructure adapts.","ref":"hexagonal","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"The low-level detail is usually wrapped in an adapter that implements the high-level abstraction.","ref":"adapter","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Programming to an interface is the local habit; DIP adds that the consumer owns the interface and the arrow flips.","ref":"program-to-interface","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Application code depends on the JDBC abstraction; each vendor driver implements it.","name":"java.sql.Connection and javax.sql.DataSource","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Libraries log through the facade; Logback or Log4j 2 bind behind it.","name":"org.slf4j.Logger","where":"SLF4J"},{"detail":"Business code uses the API; Hibernate or EclipseLink implement it as the provider.","name":"jakarta.persistence.EntityManager","where":"Jakarta Persistence"},{"detail":"Beans declare abstractions they need; the container supplies implementations.","name":"Constructor injection in the ApplicationContext","where":"Spring Framework"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["class PostgresClient { PostgresClient(String h) {} String query(String q) { return \"\"; } }","class SmtpClient { SmtpClient(String h) {} void send(String to, String b) {} }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class ReportJob {","    private final PostgresClient db = new PostgresClient(\"prod-db:5432\");","    private final SmtpClient mail = new SmtpClient(\"smtp.corp\");","","    void run() {","        mail.send(\"ops@corp\", db.query(\"select count(*) from orders\"));","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dip","why":"The high-level job constructs and depends on concrete infrastructure instead of abstractions it owns."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Adding behavior is not the issue; the issue is hard-wired details."},{"ref":"srp","why":"The job has one purpose; the problem is who it constructs, not what it does."},{"ref":"isp","why":"There are no interfaces here at all, so none can be too wide."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this class most clearly violate?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dip","why":"Define a `BlobStore` abstraction in the domain module and make the S3 code implement it, so the domain no longer knows the vendor."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Extension points would not remove the vendor import from the domain."},{"ref":"srp","why":"Splitting classes would not change what they depend on."},{"ref":"composition-over-inheritance","why":"The coupling is to a concrete service, not to a parent class."}],"prompt":"Which principle should guide the redesign?","scenario":"Your domain module imports `com.vendor.s3.S3Client` and calls it directly in 60 classes. Switching to another object store next year would touch all of them, and unit tests need a MinIO container."},{"compile":{"template":["class SmtpMailer implements Mailer { SmtpMailer(String host) {} public void send(String t, String b) {} }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The service takes a `Mailer` but ignores it and builds a concrete `SmtpMailer`, so it stays coupled to SMTP and cannot be tested in isolation. Use the injected `mailer`.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["interface Mailer { void send(String to, String body); }","","final class SignupService {","    private final Mailer mailer;","","    SignupService(Mailer mailer) { this.mailer = mailer; }","","    void register(String email) {","        new SmtpMailer(\"smtp.corp\").send(email, \"Welcome\");","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that bypasses the injected abstraction."},{"blocks":[["var fake = new RecordingNotifier();"],["var service = new OrderService(fake);"],["service.place(\"grace\");"],["System.out.println(fake.sent);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Notifier { void notify(String c, String m); }","final class RecordingNotifier implements Notifier { final List<String> sent = new ArrayList<>(); public void notify(String c, String m) { sent.add(c); } }","final class OrderService { OrderService(Notifier n) {} void place(String c) {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the test double, inject it, trigger the behavior, then inspect what the double recorded.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the test so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Both sides depend on an abstraction","The consumer owns the abstraction","Source dependency arrow is inverted","DI is a mechanism, not the principle","Invert at seams, not around every new"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"High-level policy and low-level details both depend on an abstraction, and the policy owns that abstraction, so source dependencies point toward the business core instead of away from it. Dependency injection is only a mechanism for supplying the implementation; DIP is the rule about direction and ownership.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Dependency Inversion Principle in your own words. What is inverted, and how is it different from dependency injection?"}],"id":"dip"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Reuse behavior by holding collaborators and delegating to them, rather than extending a class and inheriting its internals.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Inheritance couples a subclass to the parent's **implementation**, including the calls the parent makes to its own overridable methods. **Composition** couples you only to a public contract, can change at runtime and lets one object use several behaviors. Inherit only for a true is-a that the parent was designed to support.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Fragile base class:** a parent change breaks children.","**Forwarding:** the wrapper uses only the declared contract.","**One parent only:** a class extends once but can hold many collaborators."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Effective Java's advice: design and document for inheritance, or prohibit it. Most classes should be `final` or sealed.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"inheritance: 6\ncomposition: 3","file":"Composition.java","regions":{"before":[7,16],"client":[46,52],"count":[19,30],"forward":[31,41]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Collection;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Iterator;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\n@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")\nclass CountingSetBefore<E> extends HashSet<E> {\n    private int added;\n    @Override public boolean add(E e) { added++; return super.add(e); }\n    @Override public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c) {\n        added += c.size();                   // HashSet.addAll calls add() too\n        return super.addAll(c);\n    }\n    int added() { return added; }\n}\n\nfinal class CountingSet<E> implements Set<E> {\n    private final Set<E> inner;               // has-a, not is-a\n    private int added;\n\n    CountingSet(Set<E> inner) { this.inner = inner; }\n\n    public boolean add(E e) { added++; return inner.add(e); }\n    public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c) {\n        boolean changed = false;\n        for (E e : c) changed |= add(e);      // our own, known path\n        return changed;\n    }\n    int added() { return added; }\n    public int size() { return inner.size(); }\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return inner.isEmpty(); }\n    public boolean contains(Object o) { return inner.contains(o); }\n    public Iterator<E> iterator() { return inner.iterator(); }\n    public Object[] toArray() { return inner.toArray(); }\n    public <T> T[] toArray(T[] a) { return inner.toArray(a); }\n    public boolean remove(Object o) { return inner.remove(o); }\n    public boolean containsAll(Collection<?> c) { return inner.containsAll(c); }\n    public boolean retainAll(Collection<?> c) { return inner.retainAll(c); }\n    public boolean removeAll(Collection<?> c) { return inner.removeAll(c); }\n    public void clear() { inner.clear(); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var before = new CountingSetBefore<String>();\n        before.addAll(List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));\n        System.out.println(\"inheritance: \" + before.added());\n\n        var after = new CountingSet<>(new HashSet<String>());\n        after.addAll(List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));\n        System.out.println(\"composition: \" + after.added());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`CountingSetBefore` extends `HashSet` and counts in `add` and `addAll`. But `HashSet.addAll` already calls `add`, so each element is counted twice.","region":"before"},{"note":"The wrapper holds the set instead of extending it. `addAll` loops over its own `add`, so counting depends only on code we control.","region":"count"},{"note":"Everything else forwards to `inner`. The class promises the `Set` contract and nothing about how `HashSet` is implemented.","region":"forward"},{"note":"Same calls, different answers: inheritance reports 6 for three elements, composition 3. The wrapper also accepts any `Set`, such as a `TreeSet`.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Enemy types in a game.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An `Enemy` hierarchy grew `FlyingEnemy`, `FireEnemy` and `FlyingFireEnemy`, and designers then asked for ice-breathing flyers that can be frozen. Behaviors were extracted into `Movement` and `Attack` components held by a single `Enemy`. A new enemy is configuration, not a class, and a frozen enemy swaps its movement at runtime.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["record Enemy(String name, Movement move, Attack attack) {}","","var dragon = new Enemy(\"dragon\", new Fly(8), new Breath(FIRE));","var frozen = new Enemy(dragon.name(), new Stuck(), dragon.attack());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: N movements and M attacks cost N + M classes, not N times M.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["Behavior varies along several independent axes.","Behavior must change at runtime.","The parent is not designed for extension or lives in another library."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["There is a genuine, documented is-a in a hierarchy you own, such as an abstract skeleton.","The interface is wide and hand-written forwarding adds little."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The self-use problem: a wrapped object that passes `this` bypasses the wrapper.","Forwarders go stale when the interface gains new `default` methods.","Sharing a mutable collaborator between several wrappers."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A wrapper that forwards everything and adds nothing.","Long delegate chains that hide who does the work."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records, sealed interfaces and lambdas make small collaborators cheap.","Java has no built-in delegation; IDEs generate the forwarding methods."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Decorator is composition with the same interface: it wraps an object to add behavior while remaining substitutable.","ref":"decorator","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A strategy is a swappable collaborator held by composition, the standard replacement for subclass-per-variant.","ref":"strategy","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Template Method reuses an algorithm skeleton through inheritance, fixed at compile time; composition varies it at runtime.","ref":"template-method","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Bridge uses composition to split abstraction from implementation so both hierarchies vary independently.","ref":"bridge","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Avoiding implementation inheritance avoids most substitutability traps that LSP describes.","ref":"lsp","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Pass a `Runnable` to `Thread` instead of subclassing it; the task is composed in.","name":"Thread with Runnable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Wraps any `InputStream` and adds buffering; it holds the stream rather than extending a specific one.","name":"java.io BufferedInputStream","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Returns a wrapper that forwards to the given list under a lock, adding behavior by composition.","name":"Collections.synchronizedList","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Extends `Vector`, exposing indexed access that breaks stack discipline: a widely cited inheritance mistake.","name":"java.util.Stack","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["class AuditedList<E> extends ArrayList<E> {","    @Override","    public boolean add(E e) {","        log(\"add \" + e);","        return super.add(e);","    }","    private void log(String s) { System.out.println(s); }","}","// addAll, set, addFirst... all remain unaudited"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"composition-over-inheritance","why":"It extends a concrete collection to bolt on behavior, inheriting a large surface it cannot fully control."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"`AuditedList` still behaves as a list; the issue is brittle reuse, not substitutability."},{"ref":"isp","why":"`ArrayList` is not a fat interface that this class is forced to implement."},{"ref":"srp","why":"The class has one purpose; its flaw is how it reuses `ArrayList`."}],"prompt":"Which guideline does this class most clearly ignore?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"composition-over-inheritance","why":"Wrap one `HttpClient` in small decorators (retry, metrics, cache) and combine them per service; no combinatorial subclasses."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"Subclasses can stay substitutable, but the number of combinations still explodes."},{"ref":"srp","why":"Separating concerns helps, but SRP alone does not say how to combine them."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Depending on an interface is good, but it does not solve the combination problem."}],"prompt":"Which approach fits best?","scenario":"You need an HTTP client with retries and metrics today, and caching and tracing next quarter, in varying combinations per service. A colleague proposes `RetryingMetricsHttpClient extends BaseHttpClient`."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record User(String id, boolean active) {}","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"A cache has a map; it is not one. Extending exposes `put`, `putAll` and `clear`, so callers bypass the capacity rule. Hold a `Map` in a private field and expose only `find`, `remember` and `evict`.","flawLines":[1],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class UserCache extends HashMap<String, User> {","    private final int capacity;","","    UserCache(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }","","    User find(String id) { return get(id); }","","    void remember(User u) {","        if (size() < capacity) put(u.id(), u);","    }","","    void evictInactive() { values().removeIf(u -> !u.active()); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that couples the class to an implementation."},{"blocks":[["var inner = new HashSet<String>();"],["var set = new CountingSet<>(inner);"],["set.addAll(List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));"],["System.out.println(set.added());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","class CountingSet<E> { CountingSet(Set<E> s) {} void addAll(Collection<E> c) {} int added() { return 0; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Build the delegate, wrap it, perform the operation, then read the counter the wrapper maintained.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use of the counting set so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Inheritance couples to implementation, composition to contract","Self-use of overridable methods causes double counting","Wrapper forwards and controls its own paths","Composition allows runtime swapping and multiple behaviors","Inherit only for designed-for-extension is-a"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Inheritance ties a subclass to the parent's implementation, including self-calls between its methods. HashSet.addAll calls add, so a subclass counting in both counts each element twice. Holding a Set and forwarding depends only on its contract, avoids that coupling, and lets behavior be combined or swapped at runtime.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain 'composition over inheritance' in your own words. Why did the `HashSet.addAll` double count happen?"}],"id":"composition-over-inheritance"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Declare variables, parameters and return types as the narrowest supertype that offers what you need, not as a concrete class.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Name the **capability** you require, such as `Collection`, `Map` or `Iterable`, and let the caller choose the class. The concrete type appears once, at construction. Implementations swap in one line, tests substitute fakes, and an API stops leaking decisions like `ArrayList` versus `LinkedList`. \"Interface\" here means any supertype.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Parameters:** accept the most general type that works.","**Returns:** promise as little as is useful, but no less.","**Locals and fields:** declare the interface, construct the class."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Do not abstract stable value types such as `String` or `Instant`. The goal is flexibility where change is likely.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"before: 7\n{go=2, java=4, kata=4}\nmap values: 10\nimmutable: 6","file":"ProgramToInterface.java","regions":{"after":[15,19],"before":[9,13],"client":[31,35],"swap":[21,25]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collection;\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\nclass Main {\n    static int totalBefore(ArrayList<Integer> xs) {   // demands one class\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int x : xs) sum += x;\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    static int total(Collection<Integer> xs) {        // demands a capability\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int x : xs) sum += x;\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    static Map<String, Integer> lengths(List<String> ws, boolean sorted) {\n        Map<String, Integer> m = sorted ? new TreeMap<>() : new HashMap<>();\n        for (String w : ws) m.put(w, w.length());\n        return m;                             // callers see only Map\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 4));\n        System.out.println(\"before: \" + totalBefore(list));\n\n        var words = List.of(\"kata\", \"go\", \"java\");\n        Map<String, Integer> sizes = lengths(words, true);\n        System.out.println(sizes);\n        System.out.println(\"map values: \" + total(sizes.values()));\n        System.out.println(\"immutable: \" + total(List.of(1, 2, 3)));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The parameter demands `ArrayList`. A caller holding a `List.of(...)`, a set or `map.values()` must copy into one first.","region":"before"},{"note":"The parameter names the capability actually used, iteration over a `Collection`. Every collection, view or immutable, now works.","region":"after"},{"note":"The concrete class appears once, at construction. Switching `HashMap` to `TreeMap` is one expression; callers only see `Map`.","region":"swap"},{"note":"One method accepts a map's `values()` view and an immutable list without copies, because it asked for a capability, not a class.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A CSV export that must stream a million rows.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An export function took `List<Order>` and read it by index. When the source moved to a database cursor, a list meant loading every row into memory. The method only iterated, so its parameter became `Iterable<Order>`. A cursor-backed iterable streams, while tests still pass `List.of(...)`. Callers that passed lists needed no change.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["void export(Iterable<Order> orders, Writer out) throws IOException {","    for (Order o : orders) out.write(toCsv(o));","}","export(List.of(o1, o2), out);       // test","export(cursor.asIterable(), out);   // production"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the same method serves a three-row test and a streaming million-row export.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["A method only iterates, looks up or queries: ask for that capability.","The implementation may change per environment, such as ordering or concurrency.","You want to substitute fakes in tests."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["You need a concrete guarantee, such as constant-time indexing: require `RandomAccess` or an array.","The type is a stable value like `String`."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["A parameter wider than needed, such as `List` when `Iterable` suffices.","Casting or `instanceof ArrayList` to reach implementation details.","Assuming guarantees the interface does not give: `Map` ordering, mutability of `List.of`."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One-implementation interfaces for plain value objects.","`IFoo` and `FooImpl` pairs created reflexively."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`SequencedCollection`, `SequencedSet` and `SequencedMap` (Java 21) let an API ask for defined encounter order.","`var` infers the concrete type; write the interface type when the abstraction is the point."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Programming to an interface is the local habit. DIP adds that the high-level consumer owns the interface and details implement it.","ref":"dip","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Choose the narrowest interface that offers what you need; narrow interfaces make that choice available.","ref":"isp","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Strategy is exactly this: the context is programmed against an interface and the algorithm is chosen elsewhere.","ref":"strategy","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A factory hides which concrete class is constructed, so callers never name it.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Held collaborators are typed by interface, which keeps composition swappable.","ref":"composition-over-inheritance","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Callers use the interface; `ArrayList`, `LinkedList`, `HashMap`, `TreeMap` are interchangeable.","name":"java.util Collection, List, Set, Map","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`Executors` factories return the interface; the pool implementation is hidden.","name":"java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"An abstract class injected instead of calling `Instant.now()`, so time can be fixed in tests.","name":"java.time.Clock","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Code uses the JDBC interface; drivers supply the implementation.","name":"java.sql.Connection","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Customer(boolean active) {}","class Snippet {","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["ArrayList<Customer> loadActive(HashMap<String, Customer> byId) {","    ArrayList<Customer> out = new ArrayList<>();","    for (Customer c : byId.values()) {","        if (c.active()) out.add(c);","    }","    return out;","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"program-to-interface","why":"It demands and returns concrete classes, so callers cannot pass a `TreeMap` or receive an immutable list."},{"ref":"tell-dont-ask","why":"Querying `active()` to filter is a legitimate query, not a rule pulled out of an object."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"No subtype relationship is being defined here."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"Nothing here is growing by edits; the types are the problem."}],"prompt":"Which guideline does this method ignore?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"program-to-interface","why":"Change the parameter to `Collection<Double>` (or `Iterable<Double>`) and remove the copies."},{"ref":"dip","why":"Inverting ownership of an abstraction is overkill for a pure utility method."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"No subtype contract is broken; the signature is simply too specific."},{"ref":"tell-dont-ask","why":"Moving the average into a caller's object would not remove the copies."}],"prompt":"What is the best change?","scenario":"A utility `static double avg(ArrayList<Double> xs)` has three callers. One holds a `LinkedList`, another a `TreeSet`, and the third `map.values()`. Each currently copies into an `ArrayList` first."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The return type exposes `HashMap`, locking callers to that class and preventing a switch to a sorted or immutable map. Return `Map<String, Integer>`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Inventory {","    private final Map<String, Integer> stock = new HashMap<>();","","    void add(String sku, int qty) { stock.merge(sku, qty, Integer::sum); }","","    int units(String sku) { return stock.getOrDefault(sku, 0); }","","    HashMap<String, Integer> snapshot() {","        return new HashMap<>(stock);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that leaks the concrete class."},{"blocks":[["Map<String, Integer> m = new TreeMap<>();"],["m.put(\"go\", 2);"],["Collection<Integer> values = m.values();"],["int sum = total(values);"],["System.out.println(sum);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","class Snippet {","static int total(Collection<Integer> xs) { int s = 0; for (int x : xs) s += x; return s; }","void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the map, fill it, take the values view, pass it to the method that accepts any `Collection`, then print the result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Depend on the capability, not the class","Construct the concrete class in one place","Choose the narrowest sufficient supertype","Skip for stable types like String","Beware guarantees the interface does not make"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Depend on the capability you need, not the class that provides it. Parameters and fields use the most general useful type, and the concrete class is named once at construction. That lets implementations change and tests substitute fakes. It is not worth it for stable value types, or where you need a concrete performance guarantee.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain 'program to an interface' in your own words. What does it buy you, and when is it not worth it?"}],"id":"program-to-interface"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Send objects commands and let them decide; do not pull out their state to make the decision elsewhere.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Asking a getter and then branching in the caller moves an object's rules out of the object, so each caller reimplements them. Put behavior beside the data it guards and expose **intent** (`withdraw`) instead of **state** (`getBalance`, `setBalance`). The invariant then lives in one place. The related **Law of Demeter** forbids reaching through chains like `a.b().c().d()`.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Smell:** `if (x.getA() ...) x.setB(...)` in another class.","**Fix:** move the decision into `x` and name the intent.","**Limits:** queries for display and reporting are fine."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"It is guidance for domain behavior, not a ban on getters. Records, DTOs and view models are data by design.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"before: false\nbalance -50\nrejected: insufficient funds","file":"TellDontAsk.java","regions":{"after":[17,32],"before":[1,15],"client":[40,47]},"role":"implementation","source":"final class AccountBefore {\n    private long balance;\n    long getBalance() { return balance; }\n    void setBalance(long b) { balance = b; }\n}\n\nfinal class TellerBefore {\n    boolean withdraw(AccountBefore a, long amount) {\n        if (a.getBalance() >= amount) {       // asks, decides, mutates\n            a.setBalance(a.getBalance() - amount);\n            return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Account {\n    private final long overdraft;\n    private long balance;\n\n    Account(long balance, long overdraft) {\n        this.balance = balance;\n        this.overdraft = overdraft;\n    }\n\n    void withdraw(long amount) {             // the rule lives with the data\n        if (amount > balance + overdraft)\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"insufficient funds\");\n        balance -= amount;\n    }\n    String statement() { return \"balance \" + balance; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var old = new AccountBefore();\n        old.setBalance(100);\n        System.out.println(\"before: \" + new TellerBefore().withdraw(old, 150));\n\n        var account = new Account(100, 50);\n        account.withdraw(150);                // tell, don't ask\n        System.out.println(account.statement());\n        try {\n            account.withdraw(1);\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`TellerBefore` reads the balance, decides and writes it back. The overdraft rule would be duplicated in every caller that withdraws.","region":"before"},{"note":"`Account` owns the rule and exposes the intent, `withdraw`. The balance has no setter, so the invariant cannot be bypassed.","region":"after"},{"note":"The caller states what it wants and handles the outcome. It cannot get the rule wrong because it never sees the state.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Shipping eligibility in e-commerce.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Checkout code asked each `Order` for its items, then each item for its type, and decided eligibility itself in four services. When hazmat rules changed, three of the four were missed. Now the order answers `shippingOptions(carrier)`, and the hazmat rule lives in `Item.shippableBy(carrier)`. Callers never see item types.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["// asking","if (order.getItems().stream()","        .anyMatch(i -> i.getType() == HAZMAT)) { ... }","","// telling","List<Option> options = order.shippingOptions(carrier);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a rule change is one edit in one class, and no caller can skip it.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"lists":[{"items":["The same conditional on an object's state repeats across callers.","An invariant must always hold, such as a balance or a state transition.","You see getter chains reaching deep into a structure."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Presentation and serialization need raw data: records and DTOs are fine.","It would push UI or persistence concerns into the domain just to avoid a getter."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["God objects that absorb every rule and dependency.","A single `process()` hiding steps the caller should control.","Hiding queries that tests or reports legitimately need."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Every getter replaced by a method that only delegates.","Wrapper methods that dodge the dots but leak the same structure."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Keep records as data and put decisions in the aggregate that owns them.","Pattern matching on a sealed type is deliberate asking, fine for closed, data-oriented models."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Visitor deliberately exposes structure so new operations can be added from outside, trading encapsulation for extensibility.","ref":"visitor","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A command object is a literal way to tell: you send an intent and the receiver decides how.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"State pushes transition decisions into the object, which is Tell, Don't Ask carried to its end.","ref":"state","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A specification moves a rule into its own object that callers evaluate: still asking, but with the rule in one place.","ref":"specification","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Tell the map how to combine or create a value instead of `get`, test and `put`.","name":"Map.merge / compute / computeIfAbsent","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Hand the follow-up action to the `Optional` rather than checking `isPresent()` and calling `get()`.","name":"Optional.ifPresent / map / orElseGet","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Supplies the update function so the decision is atomic, unlike separate `get` and `set`.","name":"AtomicInteger.updateAndGet","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Pass the rule to the collection instead of iterating over it and removing from outside.","name":"Collection.removeIf","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["class Address { String getCountry() { return \"\"; } }","class Customer { Address getAddress() { return null; } }","class Cart { java.util.List<Object> getItems() { return null; } Customer getCustomer() { return null; } long getTotal() { return 0; } void setTotal(long t) {} }","class Snippet { void run(Cart cart) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["if (cart.getItems().size() > 0","        && cart.getCustomer().getAddress().getCountry().equals(\"DE\")) {","    cart.setTotal(cart.getTotal() * 119 / 100);","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"tell-dont-ask","why":"The caller digs through the cart's state and applies a rule that belongs inside it."},{"ref":"srp","why":"This is a call site, not a class with several reasons to change."},{"ref":"lsp","why":"No subtype is substituted anywhere here."},{"ref":"isp","why":"There is no interface that is too wide; the issue is pulling state out."}],"prompt":"Which principle does this code most clearly violate?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"tell-dont-ask","why":"Move the rule into `User` (for example `user.invite(email)`), so the decision lives with the data and callers cannot skip it."},{"ref":"srp","why":"The services are not doing too many things; they are repeating one rule."},{"ref":"ocp","why":"No switch is growing; the same condition is duplicated."},{"ref":"isp","why":"The problem is not an interface that is too broad."}],"prompt":"What is the right fix?","scenario":"Four services each check `user.getPlan().equals(\"PRO\") && user.getSeats() > limit` before allowing an invite. One service omitted the seats check and over-provisioned a customer."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Carrier { void pickUp(Order o); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`Fulfillment` interrogates the order and decides on its behalf, so another caller can ship an unpaid order. Move the guard into `Order.ship` and have it reject invalid states.","flawLines":[10],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Order {","    private boolean paid;","    private int itemCount;","    boolean isPaid() { return paid; }","    boolean isEmpty() { return itemCount == 0; }","    void ship(Carrier c) { c.pickUp(this); }","}","final class Fulfillment {","    void run(Order order, Carrier c) {","        if (order.isPaid() && !order.isEmpty()) {","            order.ship(c);","        }","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that decides outside the object."},{"blocks":[["var account = new Account(100, 50);"],["account.withdraw(150);"],["String s = account.statement();"],["System.out.println(s);"]],"compile":{"template":["final class Account { Account(long b, long o) {} void withdraw(long a) {} String statement() { return \"\"; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the account, tell it to withdraw, ask for its statement as a report, then print it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the calls so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Expose intent, not state","Rules live next to the data they guard","Asking duplicates logic and allows bypass","Law of Demeter: avoid getter chains","Queries and DTOs are legitimate exceptions"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Tell objects what you want and let them decide, instead of reading their state and deciding outside. When callers ask, the rule is duplicated, can be skipped, and invariants can be bypassed through setters. Asking is acceptable for queries that display or report data, and for data carriers like records.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain 'Tell, Don't Ask' in your own words. What goes wrong when callers ask and decide, and where is asking acceptable?"}],"id":"tell-dont-ask"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Move object creation into one overridable or injectable method, so the code that uses the product never names its concrete class.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **creator** declares a **factory method** that returns a product interface and calls it inside its own algorithm. Subclasses (or an injected `Supplier`) decide which concrete product comes back. The creator's logic stays identical for every product.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** which concrete class is instantiated.","**Stays stable:** the algorithm that consumes the product.","**Chosen by:** a subclass, a supplier, or a parameter."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Not the same as a static factory such as `List.of`. The GoF pattern is about a polymorphic creation step inside a larger operation.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"eu,5\nus,7\n{\"eu\":5,\"us\":7}\n{\"eu\":5,\"us\":7}","file":"FactoryMethod.java","regions":{"client":[53,55],"creator":[26,40],"injected":[56,57],"product":[7,24],"subclasses":[42,48]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\ninterface Sink {\n    void write(String name, int value);\n    String close();\n}\n\nfinal class CsvSink implements Sink {\n    private final List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void write(String name, int value) { rows.add(name + \",\" + value); }\n    public String close() { return String.join(\"\\n\", rows); }\n}\n\nfinal class JsonSink implements Sink {\n    private final List<String> fields = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void write(String name, int value) {\n        fields.add(\"\\\"\" + name + \"\\\":\" + value);\n    }\n    public String close() { return \"{\" + String.join(\",\", fields) + \"}\"; }\n}\n\nabstract class Report {\n    protected abstract Sink createSink();\n\n    final String render(Map<String, Integer> totals) {\n        Sink sink = createSink();\n        totals.forEach(sink::write);\n        return sink.close();\n    }\n\n    static Report using(Supplier<Sink> factory) {\n        return new Report() {\n            protected Sink createSink() { return factory.get(); }\n        };\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class CsvReport extends Report {\n    protected Sink createSink() { return new CsvSink(); }\n}\n\nfinal class JsonReport extends Report {\n    protected Sink createSink() { return new JsonSink(); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var totals = new TreeMap<>(Map.of(\"eu\", 5, \"us\", 7));\n        for (Report report : List.of(new CsvReport(), new JsonReport())) {\n            System.out.println(report.render(totals));\n        }\n        Report inline = Report.using(JsonSink::new);\n        System.out.println(inline.render(totals));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The product interface and two concrete products. The creator will only ever see `Sink`, never `CsvSink` or `JsonSink`.","region":"product"},{"note":"`createSink()` is the factory method. `render` is the stable algorithm that calls it, so it is `final`. `using` shows the same seam filled by a `Supplier`.","region":"creator"},{"note":"Each concrete creator overrides one method and returns its product. Adding a format means one new pair of classes.","region":"subclasses"},{"note":"The client holds `Report` references. The same `render` call yields CSV or JSON depending only on the creator it was handed.","region":"client"},{"note":"When subclassing is heavy, a constructor reference is a complete factory method. This is the form you meet most often in modern code.","region":"injected"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A file-ingestion service that supports many vendor formats.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Every vendor sends data in its own format, but the job is always the same: open the file, read records, validate, load. An abstract `ImportJob` owns that sequence and declares `createParser(InputStream)`. `AcmeImport` and `GlobexImport` return their own parsers. Tests subclass the job and return a parser that yields canned records, so no real file is needed.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["abstract class ImportJob {","    protected abstract RecordParser createParser(InputStream in);","    final void run(Path file) throws IOException {","        try (var in = Files.newInputStream(file)) {","            createParser(in).records().forEach(this::load);","        }","    }","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: onboarding a vendor never touches the retry, validation or loading code, and the template is tested once.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"csvr","rel":"extends","to":"report"},{"from":"jsonr","rel":"extends","to":"report"},{"from":"report","label":"uses","rel":"dependency","to":"sink"},{"from":"csv","rel":"realizes","to":"sink"},{"from":"json","rel":"realizes","to":"sink"},{"from":"csvr","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"csv"},{"from":"jsonr","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"json"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"report","kind":"abstract","members":["# createSink(): Sink","+ render(totals): String"],"name":"Report","role":"Creator","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"csvr","kind":"class","members":["# createSink(): Sink"],"name":"CsvReport","role":"ConcreteCreator","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"jsonr","kind":"class","members":["# createSink(): Sink"],"name":"JsonReport","role":"ConcreteCreator","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"sink","kind":"interface","members":["+ write(name, value)","+ close(): String"],"name":"Sink","role":"Product","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"csv","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"CsvSink","role":"ConcreteProduct","row":3},{"col":1,"id":"json","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"JsonSink","role":"ConcreteProduct","row":3}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"report","text":"render() calls createSink()"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"render(totals)","style":"call","to":"rep"},{"from":"rep","label":"createSink()","style":"create","to":"sink"},{"from":"rep","label":"write(name, value)","style":"call","to":"sink"},{"from":"rep","label":"close()","style":"call","to":"sink"},{"from":"sink","label":"text","style":"return","to":"rep"},{"from":"rep","label":"text","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"rep","name":"CsvReport","role":"ConcreteCreator"},{"id":"sink","name":"CsvSink","role":"Product"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A framework algorithm must create objects whose type only the user code knows.","Tests need to substitute a fake product without touching the algorithm.","Creation needs a name or hook point, such as pooling or logging."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A plain constructor call is enough and the type will not vary.","Subclassing just to change one `new` forces a hierarchy on you."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Calling an overridable factory method from a constructor sees a half-initialized subclass.","A parameterized factory with a `switch` on a string slowly re-creates the coupling.","Returning `null` instead of failing for unknown input."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A creator hierarchy that mirrors the product hierarchy one to one with no shared algorithm.","A `FooFactory` with one method and one implementation."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A `Supplier<T>` or constructor reference replaces most subclass-based creators.","Sealed interfaces plus a `switch` expression are enough for a closed set of products."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Abstract Factory is a group of factory methods that must produce a consistent family. Factory Method creates one product and is usually a single hook in a bigger algorithm.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"A factory method is typically one step inside a Template Method. The template fixes the sequence; the factory method supplies the object.","ref":"template-method","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Builder assembles one complex object in steps and validates it at the end. Factory Method only decides which class to instantiate.","ref":"builder","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Prototype creates by copying a configured instance and needs no creator subclasses. Factory Method creates by calling a constructor chosen polymorphically.","ref":"prototype","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Strategy picks behavior to run. Factory Method picks an object to build, and the object is then used however the creator likes.","ref":"strategy","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Each collection decides which `Iterator` implementation to return; callers see only the interface.","name":"java.util.Collection#iterator","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`ThreadPoolExecutor` calls `newThread(Runnable)` to create workers, so you control naming, daemon status and handlers.","name":"java.util.concurrent.ThreadFactory","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Returns a locale-appropriate concrete `NumberFormat` without exposing its class.","name":"java.text.NumberFormat.getInstance","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A bean whose `getObject()` produces the actual bean the container exposes under its name.","name":"FactoryBean<T>","where":"Spring"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.*;","import java.nio.file.*;","import java.util.List;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["abstract class Exporter {","    protected abstract Writer open(Path target) throws IOException;","","    final void export(List<String> lines, Path target) throws IOException {","        try (Writer w = open(target)) {","            for (String line : lines) w.write(line + \"\\n\");","        }","    }","}","// PlainExporter returns a FileWriter; GzipExporter wraps it in GZIPOutputStream"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"factory-method","why":"A fixed algorithm calls an abstract creation method that subclasses implement to supply the concrete `Writer`."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"It uses the same skeleton, but the varying step here is object creation, which is exactly what Factory Method names."},{"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"There is one product and one creation method; no family of related products is produced."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Gzip wraps a stream, but the snippet's structure is about subclasses choosing what to create, not wrapping."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"factory-method","why":"The loop is fixed and one creation step is deferred to the application, which can be overridden or injected in tests."},{"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"Only one kind of object is needed. A factory of families adds interfaces with nothing to keep consistent."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"The decoders are not copies of configured instances; they are constructed fresh by application code."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"Nothing says there must be exactly one decoder, and a global instance would hurt the tests that need a fake."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A message-processing framework runs a fixed loop: poll, decode, handle, acknowledge. Each application needs its own decoder class, which only the application knows how to build, and tests want a fake decoder."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","interface Parser { List<String> parse(String s); }","class CsvParser implements Parser { public List<String> parse(String s) { return List.of(s); } }","class JsonParser implements Parser { public List<String> parse(String s) { return List.of(s); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`run` instantiates `CsvParser` directly and ignores the factory method, so `JsonImporter` still parses CSV. Call `createParser()` instead.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["abstract class Importer {","    protected abstract Parser createParser();","","    final int run(String data) {","        Parser parser = new CsvParser();","        return parser.parse(data).size();","    }","}","final class JsonImporter extends Importer {","    protected Parser createParser() { return new JsonParser(); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that defeats the Factory Method pattern."},{"blocks":[["Sink sink = createSink();"],["totals.forEach(sink::write);"],["String out = sink.close();"],["return out;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","interface Sink { void write(String n, Integer v); String close(); }","abstract class Creator {","  abstract Sink createSink();","  String run(Map<String, Integer> totals) {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"Create the product through the factory method, feed it, finish it, return the result. Each step depends on the value produced by the one before.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of a creator's template operation so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One creation step is deferred inside a larger algorithm","The creator depends only on the product interface","Subclass override or an injected `Supplier` picks the class","Abstract Factory produces consistent families, not one product","Never call the overridable method from a constructor"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A class runs a fixed algorithm but defers one creation step to an overridable or injected method. Subclasses or a supplier decide the concrete product, and the algorithm only sees the interface. Abstract Factory differs: it groups several creation methods to keep a whole family of products consistent.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Factory Method in your own words: what is deferred, who decides, and how it differs from Abstract Factory."}],"id":"factory-method"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Provide one interface for creating a family of related objects, so a client can never mix products from different families.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An **abstract factory** declares one creation method per product kind. Each **concrete factory** returns a matching set: everything from the same vendor, platform or environment. The client receives the factory once and asks it for every product it needs.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** the whole family, swapped by changing one factory.","**Guarantees:** products created together are compatible.","**Cost:** adding a new product kind changes every factory."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The point is consistency across products. If you create only one kind of object, you want a Factory Method, not this.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"mem-bus/uploaded: file:/data/q3.csv\nsns/uploaded: s3://acme-prod/q3.csv","file":"AbstractFactory.java","regions":{"client":[32,41],"cloud":[23,30],"factory":[11,14],"local":[16,21],"products":[3,9],"select":[45,51]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\ninterface BlobStore {\n    String put(String key, String body);\n}\n\ninterface EventBus {\n    String publish(String topic, String message);\n}\n\ninterface Infra {\n    BlobStore blobs();\n    EventBus events();\n}\n\nrecord LocalInfra() implements Infra {\n    public BlobStore blobs() { return (key, body) -> \"file:/data/\" + key; }\n    public EventBus events() {\n        return (topic, msg) -> \"mem-bus/\" + topic + \": \" + msg;\n    }\n}\n\nrecord CloudInfra(String bucket) implements Infra {\n    public BlobStore blobs() {\n        return (key, body) -> \"s3://\" + bucket + \"/\" + key;\n    }\n    public EventBus events() {\n        return (topic, msg) -> \"sns/\" + topic + \": \" + msg;\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Uploader {\n    private final Infra infra;\n\n    Uploader(Infra infra) { this.infra = infra; }\n\n    String upload(String key, String body) {\n        String where = infra.blobs().put(key, body);\n        return infra.events().publish(\"uploaded\", where);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String env : List.of(\"dev\", \"prod\")) {\n            Infra infra = switch (env) {\n                case \"dev\" -> new LocalInfra();\n                default -> new CloudInfra(\"acme-prod\");\n            };\n            System.out.println(new Uploader(infra).upload(\"q3.csv\", \"...\"));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Two abstract products. Each is a functional interface here, so implementations can be lambdas while the type stays distinct.","region":"products"},{"note":"The abstract factory: one method per product kind. This interface is the only thing the client ever depends on.","region":"factory"},{"note":"A concrete factory for development. Both products it returns belong to the local family and reference each other's conventions.","region":"local"},{"note":"A second family with state of its own, the bucket name. Swapping `Infra` swaps blob storage and messaging together.","region":"cloud"},{"note":"`Uploader` asks one factory for both products. It cannot pair S3 storage with an in-memory bus, because it never names a concrete product.","region":"client"},{"note":"The environment picks a factory once, at the edge. The rest of the program is identical in both runs.","region":"select"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A checkout that supports several payment providers.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each provider needs a matching trio: a `PaymentGateway`, a `WebhookVerifier` and a `ReceiptFormatter`. A Stripe webhook signature is meaningless to an Adyen verifier, so mixing them is a production incident. A `PaymentKit` factory hands out all three from the same provider, and the region configuration selects one kit per tenant.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface PaymentKit {","    PaymentGateway gateway();","    WebhookVerifier verifier();","    ReceiptFormatter receipts();","}","PaymentKit kit = kits.get(tenant.provider());","var checkout = new Checkout(kit);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: onboarding a provider is one new kit, and incompatible combinations cannot be written.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"infra"},{"from":"local","rel":"realizes","to":"infra"},{"from":"cloud","rel":"realizes","to":"infra"},{"from":"infra","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"blob"},{"from":"infra","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"bus"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["- infra: Infra"],"name":"Uploader","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"infra","kind":"interface","members":["+ blobs(): BlobStore","+ events(): EventBus"],"name":"Infra","role":"AbstractFactory","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"local","kind":"record","members":[],"name":"LocalInfra","role":"ConcreteFactory","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"cloud","kind":"record","members":["bucket: String"],"name":"CloudInfra","role":"ConcreteFactory","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"blob","kind":"interface","members":["+ put(key, body)"],"name":"BlobStore","role":"AbstractProductA","row":3},{"col":1,"id":"bus","kind":"interface","members":["+ publish(topic, msg)"],"name":"EventBus","role":"AbstractProductB","row":3}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"infra","text":"one method per product kind"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Products must be used together and come from one vendor, platform or environment.","You swap an entire stack, such as local versus cloud, with one decision.","Clients should be unable to mix incompatible products."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Only one product kind varies; a Factory Method or a `Supplier` is lighter.","New product kinds appear often, since each one edits every factory."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Product kinds that differ in capability force lowest-common-denominator interfaces.","Leaking a concrete product type to the client re-introduces the coupling.","Forgetting that every new family must implement all methods."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A factory interface whose products have no relationship to each other.","Factory-of-factories chains with one implementation each."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Dependency injection containers pick the family through configuration, replacing hand-written selection.","A record of suppliers can stand in for a factory interface of two or three products."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Each abstract-factory method is usually implemented as a factory method. The difference is scope: Factory Method defers one creation, Abstract Factory guarantees a coherent family.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Builder constructs one complex product step by step and hands it back at the end. Abstract Factory returns several simple products immediately, each on request.","ref":"builder","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A concrete factory can hold prototypes and clone them instead of calling constructors, so a family is configured rather than subclassed.","ref":"prototype","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A concrete factory is usually stateless or shared, so it is often held as one instance per application or container.","ref":"singleton","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A container can wire a whole family from configuration. Abstract Factory is the explicit, code-level version of the same decision.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Creates the matching `Statement`, `PreparedStatement` and `CallableStatement` for one database driver.","name":"java.sql.Connection","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`newInstance()` locates a provider; the factory then configures and produces parsers from that same implementation.","name":"javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Creates readers of several kinds (`createXMLStreamReader`, `createXMLEventReader`) from one provider.","name":"javax.xml.stream.XMLInputFactory","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Creates producers, consumers, messages and queues that all belong to the same JMS provider.","name":"jakarta.jms.Session","where":"Jakarta Messaging"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Button {} interface Checkbox {} interface Dialog {}","class DarkButton implements Button {} class DarkCheckbox implements Checkbox {} class DarkDialog implements Dialog {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface Theme {","    Button button();","    Checkbox checkbox();","    Dialog dialog();","}","final class DarkTheme implements Theme {","    public Button button() { return new DarkButton(); }","    public Checkbox checkbox() { return new DarkCheckbox(); }","    public Dialog dialog() { return new DarkDialog(); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"One interface creates several related products, and each implementation returns a consistent matching set."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"That would be one overridable creation step inside an algorithm; here three product kinds are produced as a family."},{"ref":"builder","why":"Nothing is assembled step by step into one object; each method returns a separate finished product."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The methods create objects rather than encapsulate an algorithm the caller runs."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"Several related products must come from the same family, and the choice of stack is made once."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"It creates one product at a time and cannot enforce that the dialect, pool and runner match."},{"ref":"builder","why":"The problem is not assembling one complex object but keeping several separate objects compatible."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Adapters translate an existing interface; they do not guarantee that created objects belong together."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A reporting tool must run against either a PostgreSQL or an Oracle stack. Each stack needs its own query dialect, connection pool and migration runner, and mixing a Postgres dialect with an Oracle pool is a known source of bugs."},{"compile":{"template":["interface BlobStore { String put(String k, String b); }","interface EventBus { String publish(String t, String m); }","interface Infra { BlobStore blobs(); EventBus events(); }","class SnsBus implements EventBus { public String publish(String t, String m) { return m; } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The client builds `SnsBus` itself, so with `LocalInfra` it pairs local storage with a cloud bus. Ask the factory: `infra.events()`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Uploader {","    private final Infra infra;","","    Uploader(Infra infra) { this.infra = infra; }","","    String upload(String key, String body) {","        BlobStore store = infra.blobs();","        EventBus bus = new SnsBus();","        String where = store.put(key, body);","        return bus.publish(\"uploaded\", where);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Abstract Factory pattern."},{"blocks":[["String env = System.getProperty(\"env\", \"dev\");"],["Infra infra = factories.get(env);"],["Uploader uploader = new Uploader(infra);"],["String result = uploader.upload(\"q3.csv\", body);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","interface Infra {}","final class Uploader { Uploader(Infra i) {} String upload(String k, String b) { return k; } }","class Snippet { void run(Map<String, Infra> factories, String body) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Read the environment, choose the factory from it, give that factory to the client, then use the client. The client never sees the environment name.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the startup code so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One factory interface, one method per product kind","Concrete factories return a consistent family","The client depends only on abstractions and cannot mix families","Adding a product kind edits every factory","Single product variation calls for Factory Method instead"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"An abstract factory has one creation method per product kind, and each concrete factory returns a matching family. The client holds only the factory, so it cannot mix products from different families. The price is rigidity: a new product kind means changing every factory.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Abstract Factory in your own words: what does it guarantee, and what does it make expensive?"}],"id":"abstract-factory"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Collect an object's parts through a readable sequence of calls, then create it in one validated step.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **builder** holds the mutable, in-progress state. Its `build()` method checks the invariants that span several fields and returns an **immutable** product. Callers name each value instead of passing a long positional argument list, and no half-built object ever escapes.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Solves:** telescoping constructors and boolean-flag argument lists.","**Guarantees:** the product is valid and immutable once built.","**Needs:** a place for cross-field validation, which is `build()`."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"For a record with two or three fields, a canonical constructor is clearer. Reach for a builder when optional values multiply.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ServerConfig[host=api.local, port=8443, timeout=PT5S, tls=true]\nrejected: tls on port 80","file":"Builder.java","regions":{"build":[24,33],"builder":[12,22],"client":[38,41],"product":[4,10],"reject":[42,46]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.time.Duration;\nimport java.util.Objects;\n\nrecord ServerConfig(String host, int port, Duration timeout, boolean tls) {\n    ServerConfig {\n        Objects.requireNonNull(host, \"host\");\n        Objects.requireNonNull(timeout, \"timeout\");\n    }\n\n    static Builder builder(String host) { return new Builder(host); }\n\n    static final class Builder {\n        private final String host;\n        private int port = 80;\n        private Duration timeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);\n        private boolean tls;\n\n        private Builder(String host) { this.host = host; }\n\n        Builder port(int port) { this.port = port; return this; }\n        Builder timeout(Duration t) { this.timeout = t; return this; }\n        Builder tls(boolean tls) { this.tls = tls; return this; }\n\n        ServerConfig build() {\n            if (port < 1 || port > 65535) {\n                throw new IllegalStateException(\"port out of range\");\n            }\n            if (tls && port == 80) {\n                throw new IllegalStateException(\"tls on port 80\");\n            }\n            return new ServerConfig(host, port, timeout, tls);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var ok = ServerConfig.builder(\"api.local\")\n            .port(8443).tls(true).timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))\n            .build();\n        System.out.println(ok);\n        try {\n            ServerConfig.builder(\"api.local\").tls(true).build();\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The product is an immutable record. Its compact constructor guards null, and `builder(host)` is the single entry point.","region":"product"},{"note":"The builder keeps defaults for optional fields and requires the mandatory `host` up front. Each setter returns `this` to allow chaining.","region":"builder"},{"note":"`build()` validates rules that involve several fields, like TLS on port 80. Only a consistent configuration can leave the builder.","region":"build"},{"note":"The call site reads like a named-argument list. Only the values that differ from defaults appear.","region":"client"},{"note":"An invalid combination fails at `build()` time with a precise message, not later inside the code that uses the config.","region":"reject"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Constructing an outbound HTTP request in an API client.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A request has a method, URI, headers, a timeout, optional body and authentication. Most combinations are rare and some are illegal, such as a body on `GET` in your API. A builder exposes only the legal chain, adds headers incrementally, and rejects the illegal combinations in `build()` before anything touches the network.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Request req = Request.post(\"/orders\")","    .header(\"Idempotency-Key\", key)","    .json(order)","    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))","    .build();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: call sites document themselves, and an invalid request fails fast at one spot.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"configures","rel":"dependency","to":"builder"},{"from":"builder","label":"builds","rel":"dependency","to":"product"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Client","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"builder","kind":"class","members":["- host, port, timeout","+ port(int): Builder","+ tls(boolean): Builder","+ build(): ServerConfig"],"name":"Builder","role":"Builder","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"product","kind":"record","members":["host, port","timeout, tls"],"name":"ServerConfig","role":"Product","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"builder","text":"build() validates"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"port(8443)","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"client","label":"tls(true)","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"client","label":"build()","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"validate fields","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"new ServerConfig(...)","style":"create","to":"p"},{"from":"b","label":"config","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"b","name":"Builder","role":"Builder"},{"id":"p","name":"ServerConfig","role":"Product"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A type has many optional parameters or a long constructor.","Validity depends on several fields together and must be checked once.","You want an immutable product assembled from mutable steps."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Two or three required fields: a constructor or a record is shorter.","Order or completeness of steps matters, so a staged API is needed."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Validating in setters instead of `build()` misses cross-field rules.","Reusing one builder after `build()` can leak shared mutable state.","A mandatory field made optional lets `null` reach the product."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A builder for a two-field value object.","A director class with one caller and one recipe."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records remove the boilerplate of the product; generators such as Lombok `@Builder` or Immutables remove the builder's.","Add a `toBuilder()` or a `with...` method for copy-with-change instead of mutating."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Factory Method chooses which class to instantiate. Builder gathers many values for one class and checks them together.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Abstract Factory returns several products right away. Builder returns one product, after a sequence of configuration calls.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Prototype starts from a configured copy and tweaks it. Builder starts from defaults and assembles; `toBuilder()` joins the two.","ref":"prototype","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Builders are a natural way to assemble Composite trees, with nested builders or `add` calls for children.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Builder is the standard way to produce an immutable object with many fields: mutate the builder, freeze the product.","ref":"immutable-object","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`HttpRequest.newBuilder()` chains `uri`, `header`, `timeout` and `POST(...)`, then `build()`.","name":"java.net.http.HttpRequest.Builder","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Configure command, directory, environment and redirects, then `start()` the process.","name":"java.lang.ProcessBuilder","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Sets language, region and script, and `build()` rejects ill-formed tags.","name":"java.util.Locale.Builder","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Accumulates elements, then `build()` returns an immutable list.","name":"ImmutableList.Builder","where":"Guava"},{"detail":"Every Java message has `newBuilder()` and `toBuilder()`; messages themselves are immutable.","name":"generated Builder classes","where":"Protocol Buffers"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","enum Prio { High }","class Pdf {}","class Mail {","  static Mail to(String a) { return new Mail(); }","  Mail subject(String s) { return this; }","  Mail cc(String s) { return this; }","  Mail attach(Pdf p) { return this; }","  Mail priority(Prio p) { return this; }","  Mail build() { return this; }","}","class Snippet { void run(Pdf pdf) { Prio High = Prio.High;","{{code}}","} }"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["var mail = Mail.to(\"ann@example.com\")","    .subject(\"Invoice\")","    .cc(\"bo@example.com\")","    .attach(pdf)","    .priority(High)","    .build();","// build() throws if the body and attachments are both empty"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"builder","why":"Values accumulate through chained calls, and `build()` validates and returns the finished object."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"No subclass or supplier decides which class to create; the focus is assembling one object's state."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Chained calls look like wrapping, but each call mutates a builder rather than wrapping an object with the same interface."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Nothing is copied from an existing instance; the object starts from `to(...)`."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"builder","why":"Many optional values, cross-field rules and an immutable result are exactly what a builder with a validating `build()` handles."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"The difficulty is assembling and validating many values, not choosing between classes."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Copying a template helps when instances are near-identical, but here the first configuration still has to be assembled."},{"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"There is one product, not a family of related ones."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A job-scheduler configuration has two required fields and eight optional ones. Some optional fields only make sense together, such as a retry count with a backoff, and the configuration should be immutable once created."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`build()` never validates, so a `Config` with a null host or an invalid port can exist. Check `host != null` and the port range before constructing.","flawLines":[11],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Config {","    final String host;","    final int port;","    Config(String host, int port) { this.host = host; this.port = port; }","","    static final class Builder {","        private String host;","        private int port = 80;","        Builder host(String h) { this.host = h; return this; }","        Builder port(int p) { this.port = p; return this; }","        Config build() { return new Config(host, port); }","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Builder pattern."},{"blocks":[["var builder = ServerConfig.builder(\"api.local\");"],["builder.port(8443).tls(true);"],["ServerConfig cfg = builder.build();"],["server.start(cfg);"]],"compile":{"template":["class ServerConfig {","  static B builder(String h) { return new B(); }","  static class B { B port(int p) { return this; } B tls(boolean t) { return this; } ServerConfig build() { return new ServerConfig(); } }","}","class Server { void start(ServerConfig c) {} }","class Snippet { void run(Server server) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the builder with its required value, set optional values, call `build()` to validate and freeze, then pass the immutable result on.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lifecycle of building and using a configuration."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Mutable builder, immutable product","Validation of cross-field rules lives in `build()`","Replaces telescoping constructors and flag arguments","Not worth it for two or three fields","`toBuilder()` gives copy-with-change"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A builder accumulates values through named, chainable calls and creates the product only in `build()`, which validates rules that span several fields and returns an immutable object. Skip it for small types where a constructor or record already reads clearly.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Builder in your own words: where should validation happen, and when would you skip it?"}],"id":"builder"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Create new objects by copying a configured instance instead of building each one from scratch.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **prototype** knows how to produce an independent **copy** of itself. Clients keep a registry of ready-made instances and clone the one they need, so they depend on the copy contract, not on concrete classes. The decisive question is always how deep the copy goes.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** the configured template, chosen by key at runtime.","**Stays stable:** the code that asks for a new instance.","**Hard part:** deep versus shallow copies of mutable state."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"In Java prefer a copy constructor or explicit `copy()` over `Cloneable`. The `clone()` contract is fragile and bypasses constructors.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"orc hp=30 [slash]\norc hp=30 [slash, rage]\norc hp=30 [slash]\ntrue","file":"Prototype.java","regions":{"check":[39,42],"contract":[5,7],"registry":[35,36],"spawn":[37,38],"unit":[9,31]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\ninterface Prototype<T> {\n    T copy();\n}\n\nfinal class Unit implements Prototype<Unit> {\n    private final String name;\n    private final int hp;\n    private final List<String> abilities;\n\n    Unit(String name, int hp, List<String> abilities) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.hp = hp;\n        this.abilities = new ArrayList<>(abilities);\n    }\n\n    private Unit(Unit other) {\n        this(other.name, other.hp, other.abilities);\n    }\n\n    public Unit copy() { return new Unit(this); }\n\n    Unit learn(String ability) { abilities.add(ability); return this; }\n\n    @Override public String toString() {\n        return name + \" hp=\" + hp + \" \" + abilities;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<String, Unit> prototypes = Map.of(\n            \"orc\", new Unit(\"orc\", 30, List.of(\"slash\")));\n        Unit grunt = prototypes.get(\"orc\").copy();\n        Unit elite = prototypes.get(\"orc\").copy().learn(\"rage\");\n        System.out.println(grunt);\n        System.out.println(elite);\n        System.out.println(prototypes.get(\"orc\"));\n        System.out.println(grunt != elite);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A one-method copy contract. The generic parameter makes `copy()` return the concrete type without casts.","region":"contract"},{"note":"`copy()` goes through a private copy constructor that clones the mutable list. This is a deep copy: the new unit owns its own `abilities`.","region":"unit"},{"note":"The registry holds configured templates under a key. Nobody mutates these instances; they exist only to be copied.","region":"registry"},{"note":"Each spawn copies the template, then customizes the copy. The second unit gets an extra ability.","region":"spawn"},{"note":"The template and the first copy are unchanged by the second copy's `learn`, and the copies are distinct objects. Aliasing the list would have failed this.","region":"check"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Cloning configured scenarios in a trading-system test suite.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Building a realistic `Portfolio` takes dozens of positions, limits and market data fixtures. A test base holds a few canonical portfolios and each test copies one, then nudges a position or limit. The copy is deep, so parallel tests cannot corrupt the shared template, and test setup drops from twenty lines to two.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Portfolio base = fixtures.get(\"balanced-eu\");","Portfolio p = base.copy();","p.position(\"SAP\").setQuantity(0);","assertThat(risk.check(p)).isEmpty();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: expensive setup is paid once, and every caller starts from an isolated, known state.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"copies","rel":"association","to":"proto"},{"from":"unit","rel":"realizes","to":"proto"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["- registry: Map<String,Unit>"],"name":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"proto","kind":"interface","members":["+ copy(): T"],"name":"Prototype<T>","role":"Prototype","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"unit","kind":"class","members":["- name, hp","- abilities: List","+ copy(): Unit"],"name":"Unit","role":"ConcretePrototype","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"unit","text":"copy() clones mutable parts"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Construction is costly or needs many steps, and variants differ slightly.","The set of templates is chosen or loaded at runtime.","You want isolated copies of a configured object, such as test fixtures."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Objects are cheap and immutable; just share or construct them.","The object graph has cycles or external resources that cannot be copied."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["A shallow copy aliasing a mutable list or map leaks changes between copies.","`Cloneable` skips constructors, so final fields and invariants can break.","Subclasses forgetting to override `copy()` return the parent type."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Cloning an object only to immediately overwrite every field.","Deep-copying large graphs on every request instead of sharing immutables."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records plus a `withX` method give copy-with-change for immutable values, with no cloning at all.","Serialization-based deep copy is slow and brittle; write explicit copy constructors."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Factory Method needs a creator hierarchy to decide which class to build. Prototype needs none: the configured instance is the template.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A concrete abstract factory can store prototypes and copy them to produce its family, avoiding a subclass per variant.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Builder starts from defaults and assembles values. Prototype starts from an already assembled instance and adjusts it; `toBuilder()` merges both.","ref":"builder","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Prototype makes independent copies. Flyweight shares one immutable instance among many users instead of copying it.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Both snapshot state. A memento exists to restore its originator later; a prototype exists to become a new, separate object.","ref":"memento","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The built-in copy mechanism: a field-by-field shallow copy, widely regarded as awkward to use correctly.","name":"Object.clone / Cloneable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Returns a shallow copy; the elements themselves are not copied.","name":"java.util.ArrayList#clone","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The container creates a fresh bean instance for each injection or lookup. The name echoes the pattern, though it constructs rather than copies.","name":"@Scope(\"prototype\")","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Starts a new message from an existing one, so you change a few fields and rebuild.","name":"Message#toBuilder","where":"Protocol Buffers"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Template {","    private final String title;","    private final List<String> sections;","","    Template(Template other) {","        this.title = other.title;","        this.sections = new ArrayList<>(other.sections);","    }","","    Template copy() { return new Template(this); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"prototype","why":"The class creates independent instances of itself by copying its own state, including the mutable list."},{"ref":"builder","why":"No step-by-step assembly or validated `build()` appears; the new object starts from an existing one."},{"ref":"memento","why":"A memento stores state to restore the original later; this copy is a new, usable object."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Flyweight shares one instance. Here every call deliberately produces a separate one."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"prototype","why":"Configured templates are loaded at runtime and each spawn needs an independent deep copy."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Sharing works for immutable state only; each enemy has an inventory it must change on its own."},{"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"A class per enemy type would hard-code what data already describes, and no family consistency is required."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"Exactly one instance per type is the opposite of what is needed: hundreds of separate ones."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A game spawns hundreds of enemies per level. Each enemy type is loaded from data and needs a pre-configured behavior tree and an inventory that every instance must modify independently."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The copy shares the same `abilities` list, so changing one unit changes the template and every other copy. Pass `new ArrayList<>(abilities)`.","flawLines":[10],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Unit {","    private final String name;","    private final List<String> abilities;","","    Unit(String name, List<String> abilities) {","        this.name = name;","        this.abilities = abilities;","    }","","    Unit copy() { return new Unit(name, abilities); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Prototype pattern."},{"blocks":[["Unit template = prototypes.get(\"orc\");"],["Unit unit = template.copy();"],["unit.learn(\"rage\");"],["level.add(unit);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","class Unit { Unit copy() { return new Unit(); } Unit learn(String s) { return this; } }","class Snippet { void run(Map<String, Unit> prototypes, List<Unit> level) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Look up the template, copy it, customize only the copy so the template stays pristine, then use the customized object.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the steps to spawn a customized unit from a registry."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Clients copy a configured template instead of constructing","Useful for costly setup and runtime-defined variants","Shallow copies alias mutable state and leak changes","Prefer copy constructors over `Cloneable`","Immutable data needs `withX`, not cloning"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Prototype creates new objects by copying a configured instance, which avoids repeated expensive setup and removes the need for a class per variant. The main risk is the depth of the copy: a shallow copy shares mutable state, so changes leak between copies.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Prototype in your own words: why copy, and what is the main risk?"}],"id":"prototype"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Guarantee that a scope holds exactly one instance of a class, and give it a well-known access point.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The classic form uses a private constructor and a static accessor, making the instance **global**. Modern Java gets the guarantee from an **enum** or from a **DI container's singleton scope**, which keeps the one-instance rule without hiding a global dependency.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Guarantees:** one instance per class loader or container.","**Costs:** global state, hidden dependencies, harder tests.","**Better form:** one instance, passed in, not looked up."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Singleton is the most overused pattern. Often you need one instance by choice, which is a wiring decision, not a class-level rule.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"1\n2\ncreated before: 0\nsame: true\ncreated after: 1","file":"Singleton.java","regions":{"enumSingleton":[3,9],"holder":[11,23],"useEnum":[27,28],"useHolder":[29,33]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\n\nenum Ids {\n    INSTANCE;\n\n    private final AtomicLong next = new AtomicLong();\n\n    long next() { return next.incrementAndGet(); }\n}\n\nfinal class AppConfig {\n    static int created;\n\n    private AppConfig() { created++; }\n\n    private static final class Holder {\n        static final AppConfig INSTANCE = new AppConfig();\n    }\n\n    static AppConfig get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n\n    String region() { return \"eu-west-1\"; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(Ids.INSTANCE.next());\n        System.out.println(Ids.INSTANCE.next());\n        System.out.println(\"created before: \" + AppConfig.created);\n        var a = AppConfig.get();\n        var b = AppConfig.get();\n        System.out.println(\"same: \" + (a == b));\n        System.out.println(\"created after: \" + AppConfig.created);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"An enum constant is the simplest correct singleton. The JVM guarantees one instance, thread-safe creation, and protection from reflection and serialization attacks.","region":"enumSingleton"},{"note":"The holder idiom is lazy and lock-free. `Holder` initializes only when `get()` first touches it, and class initialization is guaranteed thread-safe.","region":"holder"},{"note":"Each call reaches the same `AtomicLong`, so the numbers increase across call sites without synchronization.","region":"useEnum"},{"note":"Nothing is created before first use, the two references are identical, and the constructor ran exactly once.","region":"useHolder"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A shared metrics registry in a server application.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Counters and timers from every component must land in one registry that a scrape endpoint reads. Instead of a static accessor, the application creates a single `MetricRegistry` at startup and the container injects it into each component. There is still one instance, but tests can pass a fresh one and no class reaches out to a global.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["@Bean","MetricRegistry metrics() { return new MetricRegistry(); }","","@Service","class OrderService {","    OrderService(MetricRegistry metrics) { ... }","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: one shared instance without a global, so tests and parallel runs stay isolated.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"get()","rel":"dependency","to":"single"},{"from":"single","label":"lazy","rel":"composition","to":"holder"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Client","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"single","kind":"class","members":["- AppConfig()","- static Holder.INSTANCE","+ static get(): AppConfig"],"name":"AppConfig","role":"Singleton","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"holder","kind":"class","members":["static final INSTANCE"],"name":"Holder","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"single","text":"private constructor"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Exactly one instance is required by the domain, such as a hardware handle or a JVM-wide cache.","A container manages the single instance and you inject it."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["You only want to share an object; pass it as a dependency instead.","Tests need to replace it, which a static accessor makes hard."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Hidden dependencies: any code can call `get()` and become coupled to it.","Mutable singletons are shared state that needs explicit thread safety.","Hand-written double-checked locking without `volatile` is broken."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Every service reaches `X.getInstance()` instead of receiving X.","A singleton holding per-request or per-user data."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Use an enum when you need a true language-level singleton.","Otherwise prefer the container's singleton scope; one instance per container, injected."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Injection gives you one shared instance without making it global. The one-instance rule lives in wiring, so tests can substitute it.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Both are global access points. A locator looks up many services by key; a singleton exposes exactly one, and both hide dependencies.","ref":"service-locator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A singleton is one instance of a whole class. Flyweight shares many small immutable instances, one per distinct value.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Concrete factories are usually stateless, so one instance per application is enough and is often held as a singleton.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Lazy singletons were the original reason for this idiom; the holder class and enums make it unnecessary in Java.","ref":"double-checked-locking","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Returns the single `Runtime` of the running JVM.","name":"java.lang.Runtime#getRuntime","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Returns the `Desktop` instance of the current browser context for the application.","name":"java.awt.Desktop#getDesktop","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Beans are singletons per container, which is one instance injected everywhere, not a class-level rule.","name":"default bean scope","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Marks an injectable class so the injector creates a single instance and reuses it.","name":"jakarta.inject.Singleton","where":"Jakarta Inject"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Settings {","    private static final class Holder {","        static final Settings INSTANCE = new Settings();","    }","","    private Settings() {}","","    static Settings get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"singleton","why":"A private constructor and a static accessor over a lazily initialized holder ensure a single shared instance."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Flyweight keeps a pool of many shared instances keyed by value, not one instance of the whole class."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"The accessor never varies the type it returns; it returns the same object every time."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Nothing is copied; the accessor hands out the one instance itself."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"singleton","why":"The domain demands exactly one instance, and with no container a guarded static access point is the pragmatic way to provide it."},{"ref":"dependency-injection","why":"It would be preferable, but here there is no container to wire the instance, so a class-level guarantee is what enforces the rule."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Copying would create a second connection to the device, which is the one thing that must not happen."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Flyweight shares many small immutable values; this is a single stateful connection."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A desktop tool talks to one physical hardware dongle. Only one connection may exist per process, many unrelated components need it, and the tool runs without a DI container."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The unsynchronized check-then-act lets two threads both see null and create two instances. Use an enum, the holder idiom, or a synchronized accessor.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Registry {","    private static Registry instance;","","    private Registry() {}","","    static Registry get() {","        if (instance == null) {","            instance = new Registry();","        }","        return instance;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Singleton pattern."},{"blocks":[["Cfg local = instance;"],["if (local != null) return local;"],["synchronized (Cfg.class) {"],["    if (instance == null) instance = new Cfg();","    return instance;","}"]],"compile":{"template":["final class Cfg {","  private static volatile Cfg instance;","  private Cfg() {}","  static Cfg get() {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"Read the volatile field once, return on the fast path, take the lock only if needed, then re-check inside it before creating.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of a double-checked lazy accessor."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One instance per class loader, enforced by the class","Global access hides dependencies and hurts tests","Enum or holder idiom are the safe forms","Container singleton scope separates 'one' from 'global'","Lazy init without synchronization is a race"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Singleton guarantees one instance in a scope and exposes it globally. The global access hides dependencies and makes testing and parallel runs hard. Prefer one instance held by a DI container and injected, or an enum when a true language-level singleton is needed.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Singleton in your own words: what does it guarantee, and why is it often a poor choice?"}],"id":"singleton"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Wrap an existing class so it satisfies the interface your code expects, without changing either side.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **adapter** implements the **target** interface your clients use and holds an **adaptee**, usually a legacy class, a vendor SDK or a generated client. It translates calls, data shapes and errors in both directions. The rest of the code never learns the foreign API exists.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Translates:** method names, types, units, error models.","**Contains:** the vendor or legacy coupling in one class.","**Does not:** add behavior; that is Decorator's job."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Prefer an object adapter (composition). A class adapter needs multiple inheritance, which Java does not have for classes.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Optional[Point[lat=52.52, lon=13.4]]\nOptional.empty","file":"Adapter.java","regions":{"adaptee":[9,13],"adapter":[15,27],"client":[31,33],"target":[3,7]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Optional;\n\nrecord Point(double lat, double lon) {}\n\ninterface Locator {\n    Optional<Point> locate(String address);\n}\n\nfinal class LegacyGeoService {\n    String lookup(String query) {\n        return query.startsWith(\"Berlin\") ? \"52.52|13.40\" : \"\";\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class GeoAdapter implements Locator {\n    private final LegacyGeoService legacy;\n\n    GeoAdapter(LegacyGeoService legacy) { this.legacy = legacy; }\n\n    public Optional<Point> locate(String address) {\n        String raw = legacy.lookup(address);\n        if (raw.isEmpty()) return Optional.empty();\n        String[] parts = raw.split(\"\\\\|\");\n        return Optional.of(new Point(\n            Double.parseDouble(parts[0]), Double.parseDouble(parts[1])));\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Locator locator = new GeoAdapter(new LegacyGeoService());\n        System.out.println(locator.locate(\"Berlin, Mitte\"));\n        System.out.println(locator.locate(\"Atlantis\"));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The target is the domain's own interface: a typed `Point` and an `Optional` for not found. This is the shape the rest of the code wants.","region":"target"},{"note":"The adaptee has a different contract: it returns a delimited string and an empty string for failure. You cannot or will not change it.","region":"adaptee"},{"note":"The adapter implements the target, delegates to the adaptee, and converts both the data (string to `Point`) and the failure signal (empty string to `Optional.empty`).","region":"adapter"},{"note":"The client uses `Locator` only. Swapping the legacy service for a new provider means writing another adapter, not editing callers.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Integrating a vendor's payment SDK behind your own port.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The SDK is callback-based, throws its own checked exceptions, and takes amounts in minor units as `long`. Your domain code wants a `PaymentPort` with `Money` and a sealed result type. One `AcmePaymentAdapter` converts amounts, turns callbacks into a return value and maps vendor errors to domain failures. When the vendor changes or a second provider arrives, only that class changes.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface PaymentPort { PaymentResult charge(Money amount, Card card); }","","final class AcmePaymentAdapter implements PaymentPort {","    private final AcmeClient sdk;","    ...","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: vendor types stop at the adapter boundary, so the domain and its tests never import the SDK.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"target"},{"from":"adapter","rel":"realizes","to":"target"},{"from":"adapter","label":"wraps","rel":"aggregation","to":"adaptee"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"target","kind":"interface","members":["+ locate(addr): Optional"],"name":"Locator","role":"Target","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"adapter","kind":"class","members":["- legacy: LegacyGeoService","+ locate(addr)"],"name":"GeoAdapter","role":"Adapter","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"adaptee","kind":"class","members":["+ lookup(query): String"],"name":"LegacyGeoService","role":"Adaptee","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"locate(address)","style":"call","to":"ad"},{"from":"ad","label":"lookup(address)","style":"call","to":"old"},{"from":"old","label":"\"52.52|13.40\"","style":"return","to":"ad"},{"from":"ad","label":"Optional<Point>","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"ad","name":"GeoAdapter","role":"Adapter"},{"id":"old","name":"LegacyGeoService","role":"Adaptee"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["You must use a class whose interface does not match what your code needs.","You want vendor or legacy types confined to one boundary class.","Several providers must be swappable behind one port."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["You own the adaptee; change its interface directly.","The translation is trivial and used once: call it inline."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Leaking adaptee types or exceptions through the target interface.","Mismatched semantics, such as sync versus async or retries, hidden behind one method.","Adapters accreting business logic."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An adapter between two classes you both own.","A chain of adapters converting back and forth."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A lambda or method reference is an adapter to any functional interface.","Records make the target's data shape cheap to define."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Decorator keeps the same interface and adds behavior. Adapter changes the interface and adds none.","ref":"decorator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Facade defines a new, simpler interface over many classes. Adapter conforms one class to an interface that already exists.","ref":"facade","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Proxy exposes the same interface as its subject and controls access. Adapter exposes a different one.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Adapter reconciles two existing, incompatible interfaces after the fact. Bridge is designed up front so abstraction and implementation vary independently.","ref":"bridge","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Strategy chooses among algorithms built for one interface. Adapter makes a foreign class fit an interface it was not built for.","ref":"strategy","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Adapts a byte `InputStream` to the character `Reader` interface, decoding with a charset.","name":"java.io.InputStreamReader","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Presents an array through the `List` interface, backed by the array.","name":"java.util.Arrays#asList","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Adapts a `Runnable` and a result value to a `Callable`.","name":"java.util.concurrent.Executors#callable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`DispatcherServlet` uses adapters to invoke different handler types, such as annotated controllers, through one interface.","name":"HandlerAdapter","where":"Spring MVC"},{"detail":"Converts between a bound type and a value type used in XML.","name":"XmlAdapter","where":"Jakarta XML Binding"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","interface Reporter { void report(String name, Duration d); }","interface StatsdClient { void timing(String n, long ms); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class MetricsSink implements Reporter {","    private final StatsdClient client;","","    MetricsSink(StatsdClient client) { this.client = client; }","","    public void report(String name, Duration d) {","        client.timing(name.replace('.', '_'), d.toMillis());","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"adapter","why":"It implements the interface the app expects and converts names and units to fit a third-party client's API."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"It does not add behavior around the same interface; the two interfaces differ."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"It neither controls access to the client nor shares its interface."},{"ref":"facade","why":"It wraps a single client to match an existing interface, not a subsystem to simplify."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"adapter","why":"A class that implements `Notifier` and delegates to `PartnerMailer` conforms the foreign API to the expected interface."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade would invent a new simplified API over a subsystem; here an existing interface must be satisfied."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Decorator needs both sides to share an interface, and adds behavior; neither is true here."},{"ref":"bridge","why":"Bridge is a design-time split into two hierarchies, not a retrofit for an unmodifiable class."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Your domain code expects a `Notifier` interface. A partner library offers `PartnerMailer` with `deliver(Envelope, boolean)`. You cannot modify the library and want the domain to stay independent of it."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Optional;","record Point(double lat, double lon) { static Point parse(String s) { return new Point(0, 0); } }","class LegacyGeoService { String lookup(String q) { return \"\"; } }","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\") class LegacyFailure extends RuntimeException { LegacyFailure(String m) { super(m); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The adaptee's failure model leaks through the target: callers now handle `LegacyFailure`, though the interface promises `Optional`. Return `Optional.empty()` instead.","flawLines":[10],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["interface Locator { Optional<Point> locate(String address); }","","final class GeoAdapter implements Locator {","    private final LegacyGeoService legacy;","","    GeoAdapter(LegacyGeoService legacy) { this.legacy = legacy; }","","    public Optional<Point> locate(String address) {","        String raw = legacy.lookup(address);","        if (raw.isEmpty()) throw new LegacyFailure(raw);","        return Optional.of(Point.parse(raw));","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Adapter pattern."},{"blocks":[["String raw = legacy.lookup(address);"],["if (raw.isEmpty()) return Optional.empty();"],["String[] parts = raw.split(\"\\\\|\");"],["return Optional.of(new Point(Double.parseDouble(parts[0]), Double.parseDouble(parts[1])));"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Optional;","record Point(double lat, double lon) {}","class LegacyGeoService { String lookup(String q) { return \"\"; } }","class Snippet { LegacyGeoService legacy;","  Optional<Point> locate(String address) {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"Call the adaptee, map its failure signal to the target's, parse the data, then return it in the target's type. Parsing an empty string would fail.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of an adapter's method so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Implements the target interface, wraps the adaptee","Translates methods, data shapes and error models","Changes the interface, not the behavior","Decorator keeps the interface; Facade defines a new one","Confines vendor or legacy types to one class"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"An adapter implements the interface your code expects and delegates to an existing class that has a different one, translating calls, data and errors. It changes the interface and adds no behavior. Decorator keeps the interface and adds behavior; Facade invents a new simpler interface over a subsystem.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Adapter in your own words: what does it change, and how does it differ from Decorator and Facade?"}],"id":"adapter"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Separate an abstraction from its implementation so the two hierarchies can vary independently, joined by composition.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **abstraction** holds a reference to an **implementor** interface and delegates the low-level work to it. You can add new abstractions (alerts, digests) and new implementors (email, SMS) without a class for each combination. Two dimensions of variation cost M plus N classes, not M times N.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies independently:** what is done, and how or where it is done.","**Joined by:** a field of the implementor type, set at construction.","**Prevents:** a subclass explosion from inheriting along two axes."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Bridge is a design-time decision. If you are fitting two existing interfaces together after the fact, you want Adapter.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"email:ann <- ALERT: disk full\nemail:bo <- 2 items: [a, b]\nsms:ann <- ALERT: disk full\nsms:bo <- 2 items: [a, b]","file":"Bridge.java","regions":{"abstraction":[19,25],"client":[55,58],"implementor":[3,17],"refined":[27,51]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\ninterface Channel {\n    String send(String to, String text);\n}\n\nrecord Email() implements Channel {\n    public String send(String to, String text) {\n        return \"email:\" + to + \" <- \" + text;\n    }\n}\n\nrecord Sms() implements Channel {\n    public String send(String to, String text) {\n        return \"sms:\" + to + \" <- \" + text;\n    }\n}\n\nabstract class Message {\n    protected final Channel channel;\n\n    protected Message(Channel channel) { this.channel = channel; }\n\n    abstract String deliver(String to);\n}\n\nfinal class Alert extends Message {\n    private final String problem;\n\n    Alert(Channel channel, String problem) {\n        super(channel);\n        this.problem = problem;\n    }\n\n    String deliver(String to) {\n        return channel.send(to, \"ALERT: \" + problem);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Digest extends Message {\n    private final List<String> items;\n\n    Digest(Channel channel, List<String> items) {\n        super(channel);\n        this.items = items;\n    }\n\n    String deliver(String to) {\n        return channel.send(to, items.size() + \" items: \" + items);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (Channel ch : List.of(new Email(), new Sms())) {\n            System.out.println(new Alert(ch, \"disk full\").deliver(\"ann\"));\n            System.out.println(new Digest(ch, List.of(\"a\", \"b\")).deliver(\"bo\"));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The implementor hierarchy: one narrow `send` primitive and two transports. It knows nothing about what a message means.","region":"implementor"},{"note":"The abstraction owns a `Channel` and declares the high-level operation. This field is the bridge between the two hierarchies.","region":"abstraction"},{"note":"Refined abstractions decide what to say and delegate how to send. Neither mentions `Email` or `Sms`.","region":"refined"},{"note":"Any message works with any channel. Two abstractions and two implementors give four combinations from four classes, not eight.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Rendering reports on several output targets.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A reporting library has report types (`SalesReport`, `AuditReport`) and render targets (PDF, HTML, plain text). Subclassing along both axes gives a class per pair and grows with each new target. With a `Renderer` bridge, reports describe their structure through primitives such as `heading`, `table` and `text`, and each renderer draws them. A new target needs one class and works with every existing report.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface Renderer { void heading(String t); void table(Rows r); }","abstract class Report {","    protected final Renderer out;","    abstract void render();","}","new SalesReport(new PdfRenderer(file)).render();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: new report types and new output formats are added independently, with no class per combination.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"abs","label":"bridge","rel":"aggregation","to":"impl"},{"from":"alert","rel":"extends","to":"abs"},{"from":"digest","rel":"extends","to":"abs"},{"from":"email","rel":"realizes","to":"impl"},{"from":"sms","rel":"realizes","to":"impl"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"abs","kind":"abstract","members":["# channel: Channel","+ deliver(to): String"],"name":"Message","role":"Abstraction","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"alert","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Alert","role":"RefinedAbstraction","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"digest","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Digest","role":"RefinedAbstraction","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"impl","kind":"interface","members":["+ send(to, text): String"],"name":"Channel","role":"Implementor","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"email","kind":"record","members":[],"name":"Email","role":"ConcreteImplementor","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"sms","kind":"record","members":[],"name":"Sms","role":"ConcreteImplementor","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Two independent dimensions of variation would otherwise multiply classes.","The implementation must be chosen or swapped at runtime.","You publish an API and want implementations to evolve behind it."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Only one dimension varies; plain inheritance or Strategy suffices.","The split is speculative and neither side has two variants."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Choosing the wrong split: the implementor interface becomes either too thin or too wide.","Abstractions casting the implementor to a concrete type.","Extra indirection that hides simple code."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An implementor interface with one implementation.","A bridge introduced before the second axis of variation exists."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed hierarchies and `switch` are simpler for closed sets; Bridge pays off when both sides are open.","Service providers loaded with `ServiceLoader` are a standard way to supply implementors."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Adapter retrofits two existing, incompatible interfaces to work together. Bridge is planned up front to let abstraction and implementation evolve separately.","ref":"adapter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Same composition, wider scope. Strategy swaps one algorithm in one class. Bridge splits a whole hierarchy from its implementation hierarchy.","ref":"strategy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An abstract factory can create and configure the implementor a bridge needs, hiding which concrete family is used.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"State swaps its delegate over time as the object transitions. Bridge normally fixes the implementor at construction.","ref":"state","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Composite trees of abstractions can share one implementor, so the whole tree renders on the same target.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Application code uses the JDBC API (abstraction); each vendor driver (implementor) is registered and chosen by URL.","name":"java.sql.Driver and Connection","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`Path` and `Files` delegate to a provider, which is why the same code works on the default file system and on zip file systems.","name":"java.nio.file.spi.FileSystemProvider","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The `Logger` API is separate from backends such as Logback, found at runtime via `ServiceLoader`.","name":"LoggerFactory and providers","where":"SLF4J"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Canvas { void ellipse(int x, int y, int w, int h); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["abstract class Shape {","    protected final Canvas canvas;","    protected Shape(Canvas canvas) { this.canvas = canvas; }","    abstract void draw();","}","final class Circle extends Shape {","    Circle(Canvas c) { super(c); }","    void draw() { canvas.ellipse(0, 0, 10, 10); }","}","// Canvas: SvgCanvas, PngCanvas, TerminalCanvas"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"bridge","why":"Shapes and canvases are two independent hierarchies, linked by a `Canvas` field that shapes delegate drawing to."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Nothing is retrofitted; both hierarchies were designed together and no foreign interface is translated."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A single algorithm is not being swapped; a whole second hierarchy of renderers is."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"The canvas is not wrapping the shape or adding behavior with the same interface."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"bridge","why":"Two axes grow independently; splitting them turns nine classes and counting into three plus three."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Nothing foreign needs translating. The problem is the design of two growing axes, not an interface mismatch."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"It could cover the delivery axis, but the notification kinds form their own hierarchy that Bridge keeps separate and extensible."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Stacking wrappers would not model choosing a channel and would not remove the subclass-per-pair structure."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A product has three kinds of notification (alert, digest, reminder) and must deliver each by email, SMS or push. New kinds and new channels are both expected within the year, and the current code has a subclass per combination."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Channel { String send(String to, String text); }","final class Sms implements Channel { public String send(String to, String t) { return t; } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The abstraction inspects the concrete implementor, so it is coupled to `Sms` and every new channel may force an edit. Put the channel-specific rule inside the channel.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["abstract class Message {","    protected final Channel channel;","    protected Message(Channel channel) { this.channel = channel; }","    abstract String deliver(String to);","}","final class Alert extends Message {","    Alert(Channel channel) { super(channel); }","    String deliver(String to) {","        if (channel instanceof Sms) return channel.send(to, \"ALERT\");","        return channel.send(to, \"ALERT: disk full\");","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Bridge pattern."},{"blocks":[["Channel channel = new Sms();"],["Message alert = new Alert(channel, \"disk full\");"],["String out = alert.deliver(\"ann\");"],["System.out.println(out);"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Channel { String send(String to, String text); }","class Sms implements Channel { public String send(String to, String t) { return t; } }","abstract class Message { abstract String deliver(String to); }","class Alert extends Message { Alert(Channel c, String p) {} String deliver(String to) { return to; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the implementor, hand it to the abstraction at construction, call the high-level operation, then use its result. Each line consumes the previous variable.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the code that wires an abstraction to an implementor and uses it."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Two independent hierarchies joined by a field","Abstraction delegates primitives to the implementor","Avoids M times N subclasses; needs M plus N","Designed up front, unlike Adapter","Pointless unless both sides truly vary"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Bridge splits what an object does from how it is done, into two hierarchies connected by composition. The abstraction delegates to an implementor interface. Inheritance along both axes would need a class per combination; Bridge needs only one per variant on each side.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Bridge in your own words: what two things vary, and why not just use inheritance?"}],"id":"bridge"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Compose objects into a tree and give leaves and groups one interface, so clients treat both the same way.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **composite** holds child components and implements the component interface by delegating to them and combining the results. A **leaf** implements it directly. Callers hold a `Node` and never ask whether it is one thing or many; recursion does the traversal.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** the shape and depth of the tree.","**Stays stable:** the client code that calls the operation.","**Typical operations:** size, render, price, evaluate, authorize: anything that folds over children."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A sealed interface with records for leaves gives you a closed tree that `switch` can match exhaustively.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"total = 1200\nproject/ 1200\n  README.md 200\n  src/ 1000\n    Main.java 700\n    Util.java 300\n  docs/ 0","file":"Composite.java","regions":{"client":[33,40],"component":[3,6],"composite":[10,18],"leaf":[8,8],"traverse":[21,30]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\nsealed interface Node permits File, Dir {\n    String name();\n    long size();\n}\n\nrecord File(String name, long size) implements Node {}\n\nrecord Dir(String name, List<Node> children) implements Node {\n    Dir(String name, Node... children) {\n        this(name, List.of(children));\n    }\n\n    public long size() {\n        return children.stream().mapToLong(Node::size).sum();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static void print(Node node, int depth) {\n        String pad = \"  \".repeat(depth);\n        switch (node) {\n            case File f -> System.out.println(pad + f.name() + \" \" + f.size());\n            case Dir d -> {\n                System.out.println(pad + d.name() + \"/ \" + d.size());\n                d.children().forEach(c -> print(c, depth + 1));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node root = new Dir(\"project\",\n            new File(\"README.md\", 200),\n            new Dir(\"src\",\n                new File(\"Main.java\", 700),\n                new File(\"Util.java\", 300)),\n            new Dir(\"docs\"));\n        System.out.println(\"total = \" + root.size());\n        print(root, 0);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The component interface is all a client sees. `sealed` closes the hierarchy to exactly a leaf and a composite.","region":"component"},{"note":"A leaf does the real work itself: it knows its own size and has no children.","region":"leaf"},{"note":"The composite implements the same `size()` by folding over its children. Each child may itself be a `Dir`, so recursion handles any depth.","region":"composite"},{"note":"Operations outside the tree use a pattern `switch`. Being sealed, it needs no `default`, and a new node type breaks the build here.","region":"traverse"},{"note":"The client builds a tree and calls `size()` on the root. It never checks whether a node is a file or a directory.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Access rules in an authorization engine.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A policy such as 'admin, or an employee on the office network with MFA' is a tree of conditions. Leaves test one fact (`HasRole`, `FromNetwork`, `MfaPassed`); `AllOf`, `AnyOf` and `Not` are composites that combine child rules. Product teams assemble rules from configuration, nest them freely, and the engine just calls `allows(context)` on the root.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface Rule { boolean allows(Context c); }","","Rule policy = anyOf(","    hasRole(\"admin\"),","    allOf(fromNetwork(\"10.0.0.0/8\"), mfaPassed()));","boolean ok = policy.allows(context);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: new rule shapes need no engine change, and a whole sub-policy can be reused or tested as a single rule.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"file","rel":"realizes","to":"node"},{"from":"dir","rel":"realizes","to":"node"},{"from":"dir","label":"children","rel":"aggregation","to":"node"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"node","kind":"interface","members":["+ name(): String","+ size(): long"],"name":"Node","role":"Component","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"file","kind":"record","members":["+ size(): long"],"name":"File","role":"Leaf","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"dir","kind":"record","members":["- children: List<Node>","+ size(): long"],"name":"Dir","role":"Composite","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"dir","text":"size() sums the children"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"size()","style":"call","to":"root"},{"from":"root","label":"size()","style":"call","to":"src"},{"from":"src","label":"size()","style":"call","to":"file"},{"from":"file","label":"700","style":"return","to":"src"},{"from":"src","label":"1000","style":"return","to":"root"},{"from":"root","label":"1200","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"root","name":"root: Dir","role":"Composite"},{"id":"src","name":"src: Dir","role":"Composite"},{"id":"file","name":"Main.java","role":"Leaf"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The domain is naturally a tree: files, UI widgets, org charts, expressions, rules.","Clients should not care whether they hold one item or a group.","An operation is a fold over the whole structure."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The structure is flat; a plain `List` is enough.","Leaves and groups support genuinely different operations."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Cycles: a node added under itself recurses forever. Check on insertion or keep the tree immutable.","Leaf-only methods on the shared interface force `UnsupportedOperationException` stubs.","Deep trees can overflow the stack on naive recursion.","Parent pointers add mutable state that must stay consistent."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A composite with a single child everywhere.","Child management (`add`, `remove`) on the interface that leaves must ignore."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed interfaces plus records give an immutable tree that `switch` matches exhaustively.","`Stream` folds replace hand-written loops over children."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Both rely on recursive composition behind one interface. A decorator wraps exactly one component to add behavior; a composite holds many and combines their results.","ref":"decorator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Visitor adds new operations over a composite tree without editing its node classes, at the cost of making new node types expensive.","ref":"visitor","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Iterator walks a composite in a chosen order without exposing its structure, which helps when clients need flat traversal.","ref":"iterator","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Leaf nodes that are immutable and repeated, such as glyphs in a document tree, can be shared flyweights.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A chain passes one request along a line until a handler claims it. A composite fans an operation out to all children and combines the answers.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"A `Component` that holds other `Component`s via `add`, so layouts and painting treat panels and buttons uniformly.","name":"java.awt.Container","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Elements, text and attributes share one `Node` interface; `getChildNodes()` exposes the tree.","name":"org.w3c.dom.Node","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A `View` that contains other `View`s; measure, layout and draw recurse through the hierarchy.","name":"android.view.ViewGroup","where":"Android"},{"detail":"Components expose `getChildren()`, so a whole page is one tree rendered recursively.","name":"jakarta.faces.component.UIComponent","where":"Jakarta Faces"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","interface Priced { long total(); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["record Item(String sku, long cents) implements Priced {","    public long total() { return cents; }","}","","record Bundle(String name, List<Priced> parts) implements Priced {","    public long total() {","        return parts.stream().mapToLong(Priced::total).sum();","    }","}","// new Bundle(\"kit\", List.of(item, new Bundle(\"cables\", more)))"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"composite","why":"A group implements the same interface as its members and combines their results, so bundles nest to any depth."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"A decorator wraps a single component to add behavior. `Bundle` holds many parts and aggregates them."},{"ref":"iterator","why":"Nothing here walks a collection on behalf of a client; the recursion is inside `total()`."},{"ref":"visitor","why":"The operation lives inside the node classes. A visitor would move it into a separate object."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"composite","why":"Panels and widgets share one interface, so refresh and export recurse over arbitrarily nested layouts."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"It adds behavior to one element, but does not model containment of many children."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade simplifies a subsystem; it does not give uniform treatment of nested parts and wholes."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"It centralizes communication between peers, not a part-whole hierarchy."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A reporting tool lets users build dashboards from charts, tables and text boxes. Panels can contain other panels, and 'export to PDF' and 'refresh' must work on any element or on a whole dashboard with the same call."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","interface Node { long size(); }","record File(String name, long size) implements Node {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The children are typed as the leaf, so a directory can never contain another directory and the tree is one level deep. Type them as the component: `List<Node> children`.","flawLines":[1],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["record Dir(String name, List<File> children) implements Node {","    public long size() {","        return children.stream().mapToLong(Node::size).sum();","    }","","    int count() {","        return children.size();","    }","","    String label() {","        return name + \"/ (\" + count() + \")\";","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Composite pattern."},{"blocks":[["Node a = new File(\"a.txt\", 10);","Node b = new File(\"b.txt\", 20);"],["Node sub = new Dir(\"sub\", a, b);"],["Node root = new Dir(\"root\", sub, new File(\"c.txt\", 5));"],["long total = root.size();"],["System.out.println(total);"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Node { long size(); }","record File(String name, long size) implements Node {}","record Dir(String name, Node... kids) implements Node {","  public long size() { long s = 0; for (Node k : kids) s += k.size(); return s; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A composite needs its children to exist first, so the tree is built bottom-up. The operation runs on the root only after the whole tree is assembled.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Leaf and composite share one component interface","Composite delegates to children and combines results","Recursion handles any depth; the client never checks types","Decorator wraps one component to add behavior; composite holds many","Watch for cycles and leaf-only methods on the interface"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Composite lets a client treat one object and a group of objects the same way. Leaves and groups implement one component interface; a group implements it by delegating to its children and combining the results, so trees of any depth work through plain recursion. Decorator also nests, but wraps exactly one component to add behavior, while a composite aggregates many.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Composite in your own words: what is the shared interface for, and how does it differ from Decorator?"}],"id":"composite"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Add behavior to an object by wrapping it in another object that implements the same interface and delegates to it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **decorator** holds a reference to a component of the same type, forwards each call, and adds work before or after. Because the wrapper is itself a component, wrappers **stack** in any order and combination, chosen at runtime, without a subclass for every combination.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** which responsibilities are added, and in what order.","**Stays stable:** the component interface and the wrapped class.","**Chosen by:** the code that assembles the stack, often at startup."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A decorator and the object it wraps are interchangeable to the client. If the wrapper changes the interface, that is an Adapter.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"send: [ops] disk 91%\nsend: [ops] disk 95%\naudited: [disk 91%, disk 95%]","file":"Decorator.java","regions":{"base":[14,18],"client":[44,46],"component":[4,6],"concrete":[8,12],"stack":[41,43],"wrappers":[20,37]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface Notifier {\n    void send(String message);\n}\n\nfinal class ConsoleNotifier implements Notifier {\n    public void send(String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"send: \" + message);\n    }\n}\n\nabstract class NotifierDecorator implements Notifier {\n    protected final Notifier next;\n\n    NotifierDecorator(Notifier next) { this.next = next; }\n}\n\nfinal class Prefixed extends NotifierDecorator {\n    private final String tag;\n\n    Prefixed(Notifier next, String tag) { super(next); this.tag = tag; }\n\n    public void send(String message) { next.send(\"[\" + tag + \"] \" + message); }\n}\n\nfinal class Audited extends NotifierDecorator {\n    private final List<String> log;\n\n    Audited(Notifier next, List<String> log) { super(next); this.log = log; }\n\n    public void send(String message) {\n        log.add(message);\n        next.send(message);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var log = new ArrayList<String>();\n        Notifier notifier = new Audited(\n            new Prefixed(new ConsoleNotifier(), \"ops\"), log);\n        notifier.send(\"disk 91%\");\n        notifier.send(\"disk 95%\");\n        System.out.println(\"audited: \" + log);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The component interface that both the real object and every wrapper implement.","region":"component"},{"note":"The concrete component does the base work and knows nothing about decorators.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"An abstract decorator holds the wrapped `Notifier` and is itself a `Notifier`. It is the only place that knows about delegation.","region":"base"},{"note":"Each wrapper adds exactly one concern and then calls `next`. `Prefixed` changes the argument; `Audited` records it.","region":"wrappers"},{"note":"The stack is assembled by nesting constructors. The outermost wrapper runs first; swap the order and the behavior changes.","region":"stack"},{"note":"The client holds a plain `Notifier`. It cannot tell, and does not need to know, how many wrappers sit behind it.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Resilience layers around an outbound HTTP client.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A payments client needs auth headers, metrics, retries and a circuit breaker, but a batch job wants only metrics and retries, and tests want none. Each concern is a decorator around `HttpGateway`. Production composes all of them; each environment picks its own stack in one place and the callers never change.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["HttpGateway http = new Metered(","    new Retrying(3,","        new Authenticated(token,","            new JdkHttpGateway(client))));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: concerns are written and tested once, in isolation, and combined by assembly rather than by subclassing.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"console","rel":"realizes","to":"notifier"},{"from":"base","rel":"realizes","to":"notifier"},{"from":"base","label":"next","rel":"aggregation","to":"notifier"},{"from":"prefixed","rel":"extends","to":"base"},{"from":"audited","rel":"extends","to":"base"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"notifier","kind":"interface","members":["+ send(message)"],"name":"Notifier","role":"Component","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"console","kind":"class","members":["+ send(message)"],"name":"ConsoleNotifier","role":"ConcreteComponent","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"base","kind":"abstract","members":["# next: Notifier","+ send(message)"],"name":"NotifierDecorator","role":"Decorator","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"prefixed","kind":"class","members":["+ send(message)"],"name":"Prefixed","role":"ConcreteDecorator","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"audited","kind":"class","members":["+ send(message)"],"name":"Audited","role":"ConcreteDecorator","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"send(m)","style":"call","to":"audited"},{"from":"audited","label":"log.add(m)","style":"call","to":"audited"},{"from":"audited","label":"send(m)","style":"call","to":"prefixed"},{"from":"prefixed","label":"send(\"[ops] \" + m)","style":"call","to":"console"},{"from":"console","label":"","style":"return","to":"prefixed"},{"from":"prefixed","label":"","style":"return","to":"audited"},{"from":"audited","label":"","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"audited","name":"Audited","role":"ConcreteDecorator"},{"id":"prefixed","name":"Prefixed","role":"ConcreteDecorator"},{"id":"console","name":"Console","role":"ConcreteComponent"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Optional behaviors must combine freely at runtime.","Subclassing would explode into one class per combination.","You cannot modify the wrapped class, or should not."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The behavior is always on: put it in the class.","The interface is wide; every wrapper must forward every method."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Order matters: a retry outside versus inside a timeout behaves differently.","Identity checks and `instanceof` see the wrapper, not the real object.","Stack traces and debugging get deep, and wide interfaces drift when a method is forgotten."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Five wrappers that always appear together: merge them into one class.","A decorator that quietly changes the contract, such as swallowing exceptions."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["For a single-method interface, a higher-order function can wrap behavior without a class per layer.","`java.lang.reflect.Proxy` can generate forwarding wrappers for wide interfaces."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Same wrapping shape, different intent. A proxy controls access to its subject and usually manages its lifecycle; a decorator adds responsibilities that the client opts into and can stack.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An adapter changes the interface to fit a caller. A decorator keeps the interface identical and changes the behavior.","ref":"adapter","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Composite holds many children and aggregates their results; a decorator wraps exactly one component and augments it.","ref":"composite","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Strategy swaps the internals of an object through a hook it exposes. Decorator wraps from the outside and needs no hook.","ref":"strategy","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A decorator always delegates and adds. A chain handler may stop the request and answer it itself.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`BufferedInputStream` and `GZIPInputStream` wrap another `InputStream`, so `new BufferedInputStream(new GZIPInputStream(in))` stacks behaviors.","name":"java.io.FilterInputStream","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Wraps a `List` with the same interface and rejects mutation; `synchronizedList` adds locking the same way.","name":"Collections.unmodifiableList","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Wraps a request and overrides selected methods, the standard way filters change what later code sees.","name":"HttpServletRequestWrapper","where":"Jakarta Servlet"},{"detail":"A forwarding base class for decorating a `List`; override only the methods you change.","name":"ForwardingList","where":"Guava"},{"detail":"Wraps a reactive `ServerHttpRequest` so filters can alter headers or body.","name":"ServerHttpRequestDecorator","where":"Spring"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Instant;","interface Sink { void write(String line); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Timestamped implements Sink {","    private final Sink inner;","    Timestamped(Sink inner) { this.inner = inner; }","","    public void write(String line) {","        inner.write(Instant.EPOCH + \" \" + line);","    }","}","// Sink s = new Timestamped(new Masked(new FileSink(path)));"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"decorator","why":"The wrapper has the same interface as what it wraps, adds one behavior, delegates, and stacks with other wrappers."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"A proxy controls access to one subject, for example by laziness or permissions. These wrappers add behavior and stack freely."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"The interface is the same on both sides, so nothing is being adapted."},{"ref":"composite","why":"Each wrapper holds exactly one `Sink`, not a group of children whose results are combined."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"decorator","why":"Wrappers implementing the same interface can be stacked per service in any combination, without touching the vendor class."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"It swaps one algorithm, but here several independent concerns must layer on top of the same call."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"Needs subclasses of a class you cannot change, and yields a class per combination."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"The interface already fits callers; only behavior needs to be added."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"An HTTP client class is used by dozens of services. Some need logging, some need retries, some need auth headers, and a few need all three, in a specific order. The class is in a vendor library and cannot be changed."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Sink { void write(String line); }","class ConsoleSink implements Sink { public void write(String l) { System.out.println(l); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The wrapper ignores the component it was given and writes to a hard-coded sink, so anything stacked beneath it is bypassed. Delegate with `inner.write(out)`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Upper implements Sink {","    private final Sink inner;","","    Upper(Sink inner) { this.inner = inner; }","","    public void write(String line) {","        String out = line.toUpperCase();","        new ConsoleSink().write(out);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Decorator pattern."},{"blocks":[["Path path = Path.of(\"app.log\");"],["Sink base = new FileSink(path);"],["Sink masked = new Masked(base);"],["Sink stamped = new Timestamped(masked);"],["stamped.write(\"login ok\");"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.nio.file.Path;","interface Sink { void write(String line); }","record FileSink(Path p) implements Sink { public void write(String l) {} }","record Masked(Sink in) implements Sink { public void write(String l) { in.write(l); } }","record Timestamped(Sink in) implements Sink { public void write(String l) { in.write(l); } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A decorator needs the component it wraps, so the stack is built from the innermost object outward, and calls go to the outermost wrapper.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Same interface as the wrapped component, so it is transparent","Delegates, adding behavior before or after","Stacks at runtime in any order; avoids subclass explosion","Proxy controls access and lifecycle; decorator adds responsibilities","Order of wrappers changes behavior"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A decorator wraps a component that has the same interface and adds behavior before or after delegating. Because the wrapper is itself a component, wrappers stack in any order, avoiding a subclass per combination. A proxy looks the same but controls access to its subject, such as lazily creating it or checking permissions, rather than adding stackable features.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Decorator in your own words: why does it keep the same interface, and how does it differ from Proxy?"}],"id":"decorator"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Offer one simple, task-oriented entry point to a complex subsystem so clients stop depending on its internals.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **facade** exposes the handful of operations clients actually need and orchestrates the subsystem classes behind them. The subsystem knows nothing about the facade, and it stays usable directly for the rare caller who needs full control. The facade owns the *sequence*, including compensation when a step fails.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Hides:** which subsystem classes exist and the order of calls.","**Exposes:** use-case-sized methods with its own types.","**Does not:** add new business rules; it coordinates them."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A facade that grows logic of its own becomes a god object. Keep it thin and push rules back into the subsystem.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Placed[tracking=TRK-A1-1]\nRejected[reason=stock]\nRejected[reason=payment]","file":"Facade.java","regions":{"client":[43,46],"facade":[26,39],"result":[21,24],"subsystem":[1,19]},"role":"implementation","source":"final class Inventory {\n    private int stock = 3;\n\n    boolean reserve(String sku, int qty) {\n        if (qty > stock) return false;\n        stock -= qty;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    void release(String sku, int qty) { stock += qty; }\n}\n\nfinal class Payments {\n    boolean charge(String card, long cents) { return !card.startsWith(\"0000\"); }\n}\n\nfinal class Shipping {\n    String book(String sku, String address) { return \"TRK-\" + sku + \"-1\"; }\n}\n\nsealed interface Outcome {\n    record Placed(String tracking) implements Outcome {}\n    record Rejected(String reason) implements Outcome {}\n}\n\nfinal class OrderService {\n    private final Inventory inventory = new Inventory();\n    private final Payments payments = new Payments();\n    private final Shipping shipping = new Shipping();\n\n    Outcome place(String sku, int qty, String card, String address) {\n        if (!inventory.reserve(sku, qty)) return new Outcome.Rejected(\"stock\");\n        if (!payments.charge(card, 1999L * qty)) {\n            inventory.release(sku, qty);\n            return new Outcome.Rejected(\"payment\");\n        }\n        return new Outcome.Placed(shipping.book(sku, address));\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var orders = new OrderService();\n        System.out.println(orders.place(\"A1\", 2, \"4111\", \"Main St 1\"));\n        System.out.println(orders.place(\"A1\", 2, \"4111\", \"Main St 1\"));\n        System.out.println(orders.place(\"A1\", 1, \"0000\", \"Main St 1\"));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Three subsystem classes with their own small APIs. None references the facade, so each can be used and tested alone.","region":"subsystem"},{"note":"The facade returns its own sealed result type, so clients never see subsystem types or exceptions.","region":"result"},{"note":"`place` encodes the correct sequence: reserve, charge, ship. If payment fails it releases the reservation, so callers cannot forget the compensation.","region":"facade"},{"note":"One call per use case. The client names no subsystem class; stock runs out on the second call and the third fails at payment.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Media transcoding behind a single call.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Converting an upload means demuxing tracks, choosing codecs by device profile, encoding video and audio, muxing, and generating thumbnails. The web app only wants an MP4 and a poster image. A `MediaConverter` facade takes a source and a target profile and runs the pipeline, while the media team still calls the individual stages when they tune encoding.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var converter = new MediaConverter(toolchain);","Rendition out = converter.convert(upload, Profile.MOBILE_720P);","// out.video(), out.poster(), out.durationMs()"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the app depends on one method and one result type, so swapping the encoder or reordering stages touches the facade only.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","rel":"dependency","to":"facade"},{"from":"facade","rel":"dependency","to":"inv"},{"from":"facade","rel":"dependency","to":"pay"},{"from":"facade","rel":"dependency","to":"ship"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"facade","kind":"class","members":["+ place(sku, qty, card, addr)"],"name":"OrderService","role":"Facade","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"inv","kind":"class","members":["+ reserve(sku, qty)","+ release(sku, qty)"],"name":"Inventory","role":"Subsystem","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"pay","kind":"class","members":["+ charge(card, cents)"],"name":"Payments","role":"Subsystem","row":2},{"col":0.5,"id":"ship","kind":"class","members":["+ book(sku, address)"],"name":"Shipping","role":"Subsystem","row":3}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"place(...)","style":"call","to":"facade"},{"from":"facade","label":"reserve(sku, qty)","style":"call","to":"inv"},{"from":"inv","label":"true","style":"return","to":"facade"},{"from":"facade","label":"charge(card, cents)","style":"call","to":"pay"},{"from":"pay","label":"true","style":"return","to":"facade"},{"from":"facade","label":"book(sku, address)","style":"call","to":"ship"},{"from":"ship","label":"tracking","style":"return","to":"facade"},{"from":"facade","label":"Placed","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"facade","name":"OrderService","role":"Facade"},{"id":"inv","name":"Inventory","role":"Subsystem"},{"id":"pay","name":"Payments","role":"Subsystem"},{"id":"ship","name":"Shipping","role":"Subsystem"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A subsystem has many classes and a correct call order that clients keep getting wrong.","You want a stable boundary between layers or between teams.","Legacy code needs a clean entry point before refactoring."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The subsystem is already one small class.","Clients truly need fine-grained control of every step."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Logic creeping in until the facade is a god class.","Leaking subsystem types in signatures, which defeats the isolation.","Becoming the only door, so every new need requires a facade change."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One facade per class, forwarding each method unchanged.","A facade that every layer calls, including the subsystem itself."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A sealed result type or `Optional` keeps subsystem exceptions out of the facade's contract.","Modules (JPMS) can enforce that only the facade package is exported."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"An adapter makes one existing interface match another that a client expects. A facade defines a new, simpler interface over several classes.","ref":"adapter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Both sit among many objects. A mediator coordinates peers that talk through it in both directions; a facade is one-way and the subsystem never knows it exists.","ref":"mediator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A proxy has the same interface as one subject and controls access to it. A facade has its own interface and fronts many objects.","ref":"proxy","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A facade is often exposed as a single shared instance, though injecting it as a normal dependency is easier to test.","ref":"singleton","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An abstract factory can hide how subsystem objects are created, while a facade hides how they are used together.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The Simple Logging Facade for Java: one API in front of Logback, Log4j 2 or `java.util.logging`.","name":"org.slf4j.LoggerFactory","where":"SLF4J"},{"detail":"Hides connection, statement, result set and cleanup handling behind methods such as `query` and `update`.","name":"JdbcTemplate","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Static one-call operations like `readString` and `copy` over `FileSystemProvider`, channels and streams.","name":"java.nio.file.Files","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"One call that creates the environment, application context, auto-configuration and embedded server.","name":"SpringApplication.run","where":"Spring Boot"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.nio.file.Path;","import java.util.List;","class Demuxer { List<String> split(Path p) { return List.of(); } }","class Encoder { List<String> h264(List<String> t) { return t; } }","class Muxer { Path pack(List<String> t, Path out) { return out; } }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class MediaConverter {","    private final Demuxer demuxer = new Demuxer();","    private final Encoder encoder = new Encoder();","    private final Muxer muxer = new Muxer();","","    Path toMp4(Path source) {","        var tracks = demuxer.split(source);","        var encoded = encoder.h264(tracks);","        return muxer.pack(encoded, source.resolveSibling(\"out.mp4\"));","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"facade","why":"One method hides three collaborating subsystem classes and the order in which they must be called."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"The three collaborators never talk to each other through this class; it only sequences calls on them."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"No existing interface is being translated to an expected one; a new simple interface is created."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"The class does not stand in for any single object that shares its interface."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"facade","why":"A new simple interface over several services, which stay independently usable by other teams."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"It converts one interface to another; here a new, simpler interface over several services is needed."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"The services do not need to coordinate with each other; they need a single entry point."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Wrapping one service to add behavior does not unify three different ones."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A new mobile app must notify users by email, SMS and push. Each channel has its own internal service with a separate SDK, auth and retry rules. The app team wants a single call, and other teams must keep using the services directly."},{"compile":{"template":["class Inventory { boolean reserve(String s, int q) { return true; } }","class Payments { record Receipt(String id) {} Receipt charge(String c, long cents) { return new Receipt(\"r\"); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The signature returns a subsystem type, so every client now depends on `Payments`, defeating the isolation. Return the facade's own result type, for example `Outcome`.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class OrderService {","    private final Inventory inventory = new Inventory();","    private final Payments payments = new Payments();","","    Payments.Receipt place(String sku, int qty, String card) {","        inventory.reserve(sku, qty);","        return payments.charge(card, 1999L * qty);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Facade pattern."},{"blocks":[["if (!inventory.reserve(sku, qty)) return \"stock\";"],["if (!payments.charge(card, cents)) {","    inventory.release(sku, qty);","    return \"payment\";","}"],["String tracking = shipping.book(sku, address);"],["return \"placed \" + tracking;"]],"compile":{"template":["class Inventory { boolean reserve(String s, int q) { return true; } void release(String s, int q) {} }","class Payments { boolean charge(String c, long cents) { return true; } }","class Shipping { String book(String s, String a) { return \"T\"; } }","class Snippet {","  Inventory inventory = new Inventory(); Payments payments = new Payments(); Shipping shipping = new Shipping();","  String place(String sku, int qty, String card, long cents, String address) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Reserve first so nothing is charged without stock. Charging comes next, with the release compensating a failure. Shipping happens only after payment, and the result is returned last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of the facade method so that each step is only done when it is safe."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One simple, use-case-sized interface over many classes","Owns the sequencing and compensation","Subsystem stays unaware and still directly usable","Returns its own types, not subsystem types","Adapter converts an interface; facade simplifies a subsystem"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A facade gives clients one simple entry point to a subsystem of many classes. It owns the call sequence, including compensation, and returns its own types so clients never touch internals. The subsystem does not know about it. An adapter instead translates one existing interface into the one a client expects; it does not simplify a group.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Facade in your own words: what does it hide, and how does it differ from Adapter?"}],"id":"facade"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Share the immutable part of many similar objects so it is stored once, and pass the varying part in from outside.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A flyweight splits state in two. **Intrinsic** state is identical across objects, immutable, and lives in a shared flyweight. **Extrinsic** state differs per use, so the caller keeps it and passes it in. A **factory** hands out one shared instance per distinct intrinsic value.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Saves:** memory, when objects number in the millions and mostly repeat.","**Costs:** a lookup per creation and clumsier APIs.","**Requires:** shared parts to be immutable."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Flyweight is a measured optimization, not a default. Profile first: on the JVM, a lighter data layout often saves more.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"6 trees, 2 shared types\ntrue\npine@30,15","file":"Flyweight.java","regions":{"check":[33,36],"client":[26,32],"context":[20,22],"factory":[9,18],"flyweight":[5,7]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\n\nrecord TreeType(String species, String color) {\n    String draw(int x, int y) { return species + \"@\" + x + \",\" + y; }\n}\n\nfinal class TreeTypes {\n    private static final Map<TreeType, TreeType> POOL =\n        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    static TreeType of(String species, String color) {\n        return POOL.computeIfAbsent(new TreeType(species, color), t -> t);\n    }\n\n    static int size() { return POOL.size(); }\n}\n\nrecord Tree(int x, int y, TreeType type) {\n    String draw() { return type.draw(x, y); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var forest = new ArrayList<Tree>();\n        String[] species = {\"oak\", \"pine\"};\n        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\n            String s = species[i % 2];\n            String color = s.equals(\"oak\") ? \"green\" : \"dark\";\n            forest.add(new Tree(i * 10, i * 5, TreeTypes.of(s, color)));\n        }\n        System.out.println(forest.size() + \" trees, \"\n            + TreeTypes.size() + \" shared types\");\n        System.out.println(forest.get(0).type() == forest.get(2).type());\n        System.out.println(forest.get(3).draw());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The flyweight holds only intrinsic state, here species and color. As a record it is immutable and has value equality, which the pool relies on.","region":"flyweight"},{"note":"The factory guarantees one canonical instance per value. `computeIfAbsent` makes creation atomic, so concurrent callers still share.","region":"factory"},{"note":"A `Tree` stores only extrinsic state (position) plus a reference to the shared type. Its `draw` passes the extrinsic values in.","region":"context"},{"note":"Six trees are created from two species. Each asks the factory instead of calling `new TreeType`.","region":"client"},{"note":"Only two flyweights exist, and trees 0 and 2 hold the very same object (`==` is true), while each tree still draws at its own position.","region":"check"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A time-series database ingesting millions of points per second.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Every point carries a metric name and a set of tags such as `host`, `region` and `service`, but there are only a few thousand distinct combinations. Storing the strings with each point would cost gigabytes. Each distinct combination becomes a shared immutable `SeriesKey`, interned on ingest, and a data point holds just the key reference, a timestamp and a value.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["record SeriesKey(String metric, Map<String, String> tags) {}","record Point(SeriesKey series, long ts, double value) {}","","SeriesKey key = interner.intern(parse(line)); // shared"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: memory scales with the number of distinct series, not with the number of points, and key comparison becomes an identity check.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"factory","label":"pool","rel":"aggregation","to":"type"},{"from":"tree","label":"type","rel":"aggregation","to":"type"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"factory","kind":"class","members":["- POOL: Map","+ of(species, color)"],"name":"TreeTypes","role":"FlyweightFactory","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"type","kind":"record","members":["species, color","+ draw(x, y)"],"name":"TreeType","role":"Flyweight","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"tree","kind":"record","members":["x, y (extrinsic)","+ draw()"],"name":"Tree","role":"Context","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"type","text":"immutable, shared"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"of(\"oak\", \"green\")","style":"call","to":"factory"},{"from":"factory","label":"new TreeType(...)","style":"create","to":"type"},{"from":"factory","label":"shared instance","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"of(\"oak\", \"green\")","style":"call","to":"factory"},{"from":"factory","label":"same instance","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"draw(x, y)","style":"call","to":"type"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"factory","name":"TreeTypes","role":"FlyweightFactory"},{"id":"type","name":"TreeType","role":"Flyweight"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Millions of objects share a large, immutable part and a profile shows memory pressure.","Distinct intrinsic values are few compared with the object count."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Most objects are unique, so there is little to share.","The shared part is small; the lookup costs more than it saves."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Mutable intrinsic state: one caller's change appears in every user.","A strong pool never releases entries; use weak references if the key space is open.","Extrinsic state threaded through every call makes APIs awkward."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Pooling tiny objects the JIT could scalar-replace.","A global static pool hidden in domain code."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records give immutable value types that suit flyweights.","`String` deduplication in G1 shares character arrays without code changes."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A singleton is exactly one instance globally. Flyweight keeps one instance per distinct value, and many of them.","ref":"singleton","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Immutable leaf nodes of a composite, such as glyphs in a document tree, are natural flyweights.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The flyweight factory is a keyed creation method that returns existing instances before building new ones.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Prototype copies an object to avoid expensive construction. Flyweight avoids having many copies at all by sharing one.","ref":"prototype","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A proxy stands in for one specific object, often to defer or guard access. A flyweight is shared among many users.","ref":"proxy","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Caches boxed values from -128 to 127, so `valueOf(100) == valueOf(100)`; `Character.valueOf` caches 0 to 127.","name":"Integer.valueOf","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Returns a canonical instance from the JVM string pool for each distinct character sequence.","name":"String.intern","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A pool of canonical instances held by weak references, so unused entries can be collected.","name":"Interners.newWeakInterner","where":"Guava"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;","record Currency(String code) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Currencies {","    private static final Map<String, Currency> CACHE =","        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();","","    static Currency of(String code) {","        return CACHE.computeIfAbsent(code, Currency::new);","    }","}","","record Price(long cents, Currency currency) {} // millions of these"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"flyweight","why":"One immutable `Currency` per distinct code is shared by millions of `Price` objects, which hold only their own amount."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"A singleton has one instance in total. Here there is one instance per distinct code."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"There is a creation method, but its point is returning shared instances to save memory, not choosing a subclass."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Nothing is cloned; existing instances are looked up and reused."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"flyweight","why":"Forty immutable shared styles replace five million copies; value and position stay per cell."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Cloning a style per cell is exactly what multiplies memory here."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"There are forty styles, not one, so a single shared instance cannot work."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"Loading cells lazily defers cost, but each loaded cell would still carry its own style copy."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A spreadsheet viewer loads sheets with five million cells. Each cell has a style (font, fill, borders) drawn from about forty distinct combinations, plus its own value and position. Memory is the bottleneck."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Per-cell state lives in a shared object, so selecting one cell highlights every cell that shares the style. Keep `selected` outside and pass it in: `draw(text, selected)`.","flawLines":[3],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Style { // shared across cells","    private final String font;","    private boolean selected;","","    Style(String font) { this.font = font; }","","    void select(boolean on) { selected = on; }","","    String draw(String text) {","        return (selected ? \"*\" : \"\") + font + \":\" + text;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Flyweight pattern."},{"blocks":[["Map<String, Style> pool = new HashMap<>();","String key = cell.font();"],["Style style = pool.computeIfAbsent(key, Style::new);"],["String out = style.draw(cell.text(), cell.col());"],["screen.add(out);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Style(String font) { String draw(String t, int c) { return font + t + c; } }","record Cell(String font, String text, int col) {}","class Snippet { void run(Cell cell, List<String> screen) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The lookup key and pool exist first, then the shared instance is fetched or created. Only then can the extrinsic values from the cell be passed to it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Intrinsic state is shared, immutable, and stored once","Extrinsic state is held by the caller and passed in","A factory pools instances per distinct value","It is a memory optimization; measure first","Not a singleton: many flyweights, one per value"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Flyweight saves memory by sharing. Intrinsic state is identical across many objects and immutable, so it lives in one shared flyweight. Extrinsic state differs per use and is kept by the caller and passed in. A factory returns the existing flyweight for each distinct intrinsic value, so clients never call the constructor directly.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Flyweight in your own words: what is intrinsic versus extrinsic state, and who creates the flyweights?"}],"id":"flyweight"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Stand in for another object behind the same interface, to control access to it, defer its creation, or add checks.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **proxy** implements the subject's interface and holds, or can create, the **real subject**. Clients cannot tell the difference. The proxy decides *whether* and *when* a call reaches the real object. Common kinds: **virtual** (lazy creation), **protection** (permissions), **remote** (network stub), **smart** (caching, counting).","type":"p"},{"items":["**Controls:** access, timing and location of the real subject.","**Keeps:** the subject's exact interface.","**Often generated:** dynamic proxies and bytecode libraries create them."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The proxy usually owns the real subject's lifecycle. A decorator receives its component from outside and exists to add features.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"proxy ready\nconnect\nbody of a1\nbody of a1\ndenied: x9\n-> read\nbody of a1","file":"Proxy.java","regions":{"client":[34,43],"dynamic":[44,51],"proxy":[15,30],"real":[9,13],"subject":[5,7]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\ninterface Documents {\n    String read(String id);\n}\n\nfinal class RemoteDocuments implements Documents {\n    RemoteDocuments() { System.out.println(\"connect\"); }\n\n    public String read(String id) { return \"body of \" + id; }\n}\n\nfinal class GuardedDocuments implements Documents {\n    private final Supplier<Documents> factory;\n    private final Set<String> allowed;\n    private Documents real; // created on first use\n\n    GuardedDocuments(Supplier<Documents> factory, Set<String> allowed) {\n        this.factory = factory;\n        this.allowed = allowed;\n    }\n\n    public String read(String id) {\n        if (!allowed.contains(id)) throw new SecurityException(\"denied: \" + id);\n        if (real == null) real = factory.get();\n        return real.read(id);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Documents docs =\n            new GuardedDocuments(RemoteDocuments::new, Set.of(\"a1\"));\n        System.out.println(\"proxy ready\");\n        System.out.println(docs.read(\"a1\"));\n        System.out.println(docs.read(\"a1\"));\n        try {\n            docs.read(\"x9\");\n        } catch (SecurityException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n        }\n        Documents logged = (Documents) Proxy.newProxyInstance(\n            Documents.class.getClassLoader(),\n            new Class<?>[] {Documents.class},\n            (proxy, method, a) -> {\n                System.out.println(\"-> \" + method.getName());\n                return method.invoke(docs, a);\n            });\n        System.out.println(logged.read(\"a1\"));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The interface shared by the real object and its stand-in. Clients depend only on this.","region":"subject"},{"note":"The expensive real subject. Its constructor prints `connect` to show when it is actually built.","region":"real"},{"note":"Protection and virtual proxy in one. The permission check comes first, so a denied call never creates the real object, which is then built lazily on first use.","region":"proxy"},{"note":"The proxy is created without connecting. `connect` appears once, at the first allowed read; `x9` is rejected before any connection.","region":"client"},{"note":"`java.lang.reflect.Proxy` generates a stand-in at runtime for any interface. One handler intercepts every method; this is how frameworks add logging or transactions.","region":"dynamic"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A lazy-loading entity in an ORM.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Loading an `Order` should not fetch its 5,000 line items or its customer's full profile unless somebody looks. The ORM returns a generated proxy subclass for each association. The first access to `order.lines()` triggers the query inside the proxy, caches the result, and later calls hit the loaded data. Callers write ordinary code against the entity type.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Order order = em.find(Order.class, id);","// order.customer() is a proxy: no SQL yet","String name = order.customer().name(); // loads now"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: unused associations cost nothing, and data-access concerns stay out of the domain model.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"real","rel":"realizes","to":"subject"},{"from":"proxy","rel":"realizes","to":"subject"},{"from":"proxy","label":"creates lazily","rel":"association","to":"real"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"subject","kind":"interface","members":["+ read(id): String"],"name":"Documents","role":"Subject","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"real","kind":"class","members":["+ read(id): String"],"name":"RemoteDocuments","role":"RealSubject","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"proxy","kind":"class","members":["- real: Documents","- allowed: Set","+ read(id): String"],"name":"GuardedDocuments","role":"Proxy","row":1}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"read(\"a1\")","style":"call","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","label":"check allowed","style":"call","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","label":"new RemoteDocuments()","style":"create","to":"real"},{"from":"proxy","label":"read(\"a1\")","style":"call","to":"real"},{"from":"real","label":"body","style":"return","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","label":"body","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"proxy","name":"GuardedDocuments","role":"Proxy"},{"id":"real","name":"RemoteDocuments","role":"RealSubject"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Creating or reaching the real object is expensive and often unnecessary.","Callers must be checked, metered or audited without changing them.","Cross-cutting concerns (transactions, caching) apply to many interfaces."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The real object is cheap, local and unrestricted.","You only want stackable features: a decorator states that intent better."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Self-invocation bypasses framework proxies: `this.save()` skips the interceptor.","Final classes and methods cannot be subclassed by bytecode proxies.","Lazy initialization is not thread-safe unless you make it so.","A proxy hides that a call may now be slow or fail."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A proxy that only forwards.","Hand-written proxies for wide interfaces; generate them."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`java.lang.reflect.Proxy` handles interfaces only; classes need ByteBuddy or CGLIB.","Virtual proxies are often just a `Supplier` with memoization."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Both wrap with the same interface. A proxy controls access to its subject and often creates it; a decorator is handed a component and adds stackable responsibilities.","ref":"decorator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An adapter changes the interface. A proxy keeps it identical to the subject's.","ref":"adapter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A facade has its own, simpler interface over many objects. A proxy mirrors one subject's interface.","ref":"facade","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A circuit breaker is typically implemented as a proxy around a remote call that fails fast when the target is unhealthy.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An ambassador is a remote proxy lifted into a separate process beside the client, handling retries, TLS and routing.","ref":"ambassador","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Generates a class at runtime that implements given interfaces and routes every call to an `InvocationHandler`.","name":"java.lang.reflect.Proxy","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Spring AOP wraps beans in JDK or CGLIB proxies to apply `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable` and `@Async`.","name":"ProxyFactory","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Returns an uninitialized proxy that loads the row on first access to a field.","name":"EntityManager.getReference","where":"Hibernate"},{"detail":"`exportObject` returns a stub that forwards calls over the network to the remote object.","name":"java.rmi.server.UnicastRemoteObject","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.Supplier;","interface Report { String render(); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class LazyReport implements Report {","    private final Supplier<Report> source;","    private Report loaded;","","    LazyReport(Supplier<Report> source) { this.source = source; }","","    public String render() {","        if (loaded == null) loaded = source.get();","        return loaded.render();","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"proxy","why":"It shares the subject's interface and controls when the real object is created, on first use."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"A decorator adds behavior to a component it is given. This one adds none; it only defers creation."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"The interface on both sides is `Report`; nothing is translated."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Nothing is shared between many users; one object is simply created late."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"proxy","why":"A remote proxy implements the local interface and hides where and how the real object is reached."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"The interface already matches what callers want; the problem is location and access, not shape."},{"ref":"facade","why":"It simplifies many objects behind a new interface; here one service keeps its own interface."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Optional stackable features are not the issue; the stand-in is the only way to reach the remote object."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A mobile app calls a REST inventory service. Business code should keep using an `Inventory` interface as if it were local, while the stand-in serializes calls, attaches auth tokens and fails fast when offline."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Set;","interface Documents { String read(String id); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Handing out the real subject lets any caller bypass the permission check. A protection proxy must never expose the object it guards; remove `unwrap`.","flawLines":[12],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Guarded implements Documents {","    private final Documents real;","    private final Set<String> allowed;","","    Guarded(Documents r, Set<String> a) { real = r; allowed = a; }","","    public String read(String id) {","        if (!allowed.contains(id)) throw new SecurityException(id);","        return real.read(id);","    }","","    Documents unwrap() { return real; }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Proxy pattern."},{"blocks":[["if (!allowed.contains(id)) throw new SecurityException(id);"],["if (real == null) real = factory.get();"],["String body = real.read(id);"],["log.add(id + \" ok\");"],["return body;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.function.Supplier;","interface Documents { String read(String id); }","class Snippet {","  Supplier<Documents> factory; Documents real; Set<String> allowed; List<String> log;","  String read(String id) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The check comes first so a denied call never builds the subject. The real object must exist before it is called, and success is logged only after the call returns.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of the proxy's method so that denied calls never reach or create the real subject."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Same interface as the real subject; callers cannot tell","Controls access, timing or location of the subject","Kinds: virtual, protection, remote, smart","Usually creates and owns the real subject","Decorator adds features; proxy controls access"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A proxy implements the same interface as the real subject and decides whether and when calls reach it. That enables lazy creation, access checks, remote access, caching or metering without changing callers. It usually owns the subject's lifecycle. A decorator looks the same but adds stackable features to a component it is given.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Proxy in your own words: what can it control, and how does it differ from Decorator?"}],"id":"proxy"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Pass a request along a chain of handlers, where each one either handles it or forwards it to the next.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The sender knows only the first link. Each **handler** inspects the request and chooses: answer it and **stop**, change it and **forward**, or do both around the rest of the chain. The chain's order is the policy, and handlers can be added, removed or reordered without touching the sender or each other.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** which handlers exist and their order.","**Stays stable:** the sender and the request type.","**Short-circuit:** any handler may end the chain early."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Middleware pipelines (servlet filters, Netty, OkHttp interceptors) are the dominant modern form: every link runs, and each decides whether to continue.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Response[status=401, body=login]\nResponse[status=200, body=hello ann]\nResponse[status=200, body=hello ann]\nResponse[status=429, body=slow down]","file":"Chain.java","regions":{"assemble":[37,38],"client":[39,42],"filters":[32,36],"handler":[9,16],"link":[18,28]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nrecord Request(String user, String path) {}\n\nrecord Response(int status, String body) {}\n\ninterface Next {\n    Response proceed(Request request);\n}\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface Filter {\n    Response apply(Request request, Next next);\n}\n\nfinal class Chains {\n    static Next build(List<Filter> filters, Next terminal) {\n        Next next = terminal;\n        for (int i = filters.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            Filter filter = filters.get(i);\n            Next rest = next;\n            next = request -> filter.apply(request, rest);\n        }\n        return next;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Filter auth = (r, next) -> r.user().isEmpty()\n            ? new Response(401, \"login\") : next.proceed(r);\n        Map<String, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n        Filter limit = (r, next) -> seen.merge(r.user(), 1, Integer::sum) > 2\n            ? new Response(429, \"slow down\") : next.proceed(r);\n        Next app = r -> new Response(200, \"hello \" + r.user());\n        Next chain = Chains.build(List.of(auth, limit), app);\n        System.out.println(chain.proceed(new Request(\"\", \"/home\")));\n        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n            System.out.println(chain.proceed(new Request(\"ann\", \"/home\")));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`Filter` receives the request and a `Next` representing the rest of the chain. Calling `next.proceed` is the only way forward, so every filter controls continuation.","region":"handler"},{"note":"The chain is built from the end: each filter is bound to the already-built remainder. The result is a single `Next` the caller can invoke.","region":"link"},{"note":"`auth` stops the chain with 401 when there is no user. `limit` counts requests per user and stops with 429 after two. Otherwise both forward.","region":"filters"},{"note":"The terminal handler does the real work. Order is explicit: authentication runs before rate limiting, so anonymous callers are never counted.","region":"assemble"},{"note":"The anonymous request stops at `auth`. The third request from `ann` is stopped by `limit`; the application handler never runs for either.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Fraud screening before a card payment is authorized.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A payment passes through a blocklist check, a velocity check, a geo-mismatch check and an amount threshold, in that order. Each rule can approve, reject, or defer to the next one. Cheap checks come first so most transactions leave early, and risk teams reorder or add rules from configuration, with no change to the payment service.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Screen chain = Screens.of(","    new Blocklist(), new Velocity(10, Duration.ofMinutes(1)),","    new GeoMismatch(), new AmountOver(Money.eur(5_000)));","Verdict v = chain.check(payment); // first decisive rule wins"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: rules are independent and individually testable, and their order and membership are configuration, not code.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","rel":"dependency","to":"next"},{"from":"auth","rel":"realizes","to":"filter"},{"from":"limit","rel":"realizes","to":"filter"},{"from":"filter","label":"calls","rel":"dependency","to":"next"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"filter","kind":"interface","members":["+ apply(request, next)"],"name":"Filter","role":"Handler","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"next","kind":"interface","members":["+ proceed(request)"],"name":"Next","role":"Successor","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"auth","kind":"class","members":["401 or next"],"name":"auth","role":"ConcreteHandler","row":3},{"col":1,"id":"limit","kind":"class","members":["429 or next"],"name":"limit","role":"ConcreteHandler","row":3}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"proceed(req)","style":"call","to":"auth"},{"from":"auth","label":"next.proceed(req)","style":"call","to":"limit"},{"from":"limit","label":"next.proceed(req)","style":"call","to":"app"},{"from":"app","label":"200","style":"return","to":"limit"},{"from":"limit","label":"200","style":"return","to":"auth"},{"from":"auth","label":"200","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"auth","name":"auth","role":"ConcreteHandler"},{"id":"limit","name":"limit","role":"ConcreteHandler"},{"id":"app","name":"app","role":"Terminal"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Several objects may handle a request and the right one is not known up front.","Processing steps should be added, removed or reordered by configuration.","Cross-cutting checks (auth, limits, logging) wrap a core handler."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Exactly one handler is known from the request: pick it directly.","Every step must run regardless; a plain list loop is simpler."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["A request that falls off the end unhandled needs an explicit terminal.","Order bugs: a limiter before auth counts anonymous traffic.","Hard-to-debug flow; log which link decided.","A handler that forgets to forward silently ends the chain."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A chain with two links that never change.","Handlers sharing mutable request state in hidden ways."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A functional interface plus a fold over a list builds the chain without handler base classes.","A sealed result type makes handled, rejected and passed outcomes explicit."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Both wrap a successor behind one interface. A decorator always delegates and adds behavior; a chain handler may stop and answer the request itself.","ref":"decorator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Observer notifies every subscriber and none can veto the others. A chain stops at the first handler that claims the request.","ref":"observer","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Strategy picks one algorithm up front. A chain lets each handler decide at runtime whether the request is its.","ref":"strategy","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Requests in a chain are often command objects, so a handler can queue, log or reject them.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A composite fans an operation out to all children and combines results. A chain passes one request along a line until it is handled.","ref":"composite","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Each `Filter` calls `chain.doFilter(request, response)` to continue or returns early to short-circuit.","name":"Filter and FilterChain","where":"Jakarta Servlet"},{"detail":"An ordered list of `ChannelHandler`s; each uses `ctx.fireChannelRead` to pass an event to the next.","name":"ChannelPipeline","where":"Netty"},{"detail":"An ordered list of security filters, with `FilterChainProxy` selecting the chain that matches the request.","name":"SecurityFilterChain","where":"Spring Security"},{"detail":"Interceptors call `chain.proceed(request)` to continue and can return a response without doing so.","name":"Interceptor.Chain","where":"OkHttp"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Rule { String decide(Expense e); }","record Expense(long cents) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class LimitRule implements Rule {","    private final String name;","    private final long limit;","    private final Rule next; // may be null","","    LimitRule(String name, long limit, Rule next) {","        this.name = name; this.limit = limit; this.next = next;","    }","","    public String decide(Expense e) {","        if (e.cents() <= limit) return \"approved by \" + name;","        return next == null ? \"escalate\" : next.decide(e);","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"Each rule handles the request or passes it to its successor; the chain is wired by constructors."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"A decorator always delegates and adds behavior. This rule may answer and stop."},{"ref":"composite","why":"A composite aggregates many children. Here each rule holds one successor, and one answer ends the walk."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"Strategy is chosen up front and does the whole job. Here each rule decides whether to defer."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"An ordered line of handlers where the first to claim the request ends processing, and order is configuration."},{"ref":"command","why":"It packages a request to queue or undo; it does not decide which of several rules should handle it."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The applicable rule is not known up front; each rule must examine the ticket."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Every listener would see every ticket, and none could stop the others."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Incoming support tickets are tried against a spam filter, a VIP rule, a language router and a default queue. The first rule that claims a ticket stops processing, and teams add or reorder rules from configuration."},{"compile":{"template":["record Request(String user) {}","record Response(int status, String body) {}","interface Next { Response proceed(Request r); }","interface Filter { Response apply(Request r, Next next); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"A handler that does not claim the request must pass it on. Answering 200 here makes every later filter and the real handler unreachable. Return `next.proceed(r)` instead.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class AuthFilter implements Filter {","    public Response apply(Request r, Next next) {","        if (r.user().isEmpty()) {","            return new Response(401, \"login\");","        }","        System.out.println(\"auth ok: \" + r.user());","        return new Response(200, \"ok\");","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Chain of Responsibility pattern."},{"blocks":[["Next app = r -> new Response(200, \"ok\");"],["Next limited = r -> limit.apply(r, app);"],["Next secured = r -> auth.apply(r, limited);"],["Response res = secured.proceed(request);"]],"compile":{"template":["record Request(String user) {}","record Response(int status, String body) {}","interface Next { Response proceed(Request r); }","interface Filter { Response apply(Request r, Next next); }","class Snippet { void run(Filter auth, Filter limit, Request request) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A chain is built from the end: each link needs the already-built remainder. The request enters at the first link, so it is sent last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Sender knows only the first link","Each handler decides to handle, forward, or both","Order is the policy and is configurable","A handler can short-circuit; a decorator always delegates","Needs a terminal for unhandled requests"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A request enters the first handler of an ordered chain. Each handler decides: handle it and stop, or forward it to the next, possibly after changing it. The sender knows only the head, so handlers can be added or reordered freely. A decorator also wraps a successor but always delegates; a chain handler can end the request.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Chain of Responsibility in your own words: who decides where a request stops, and how does it differ from Decorator?"}],"id":"chain-of-responsibility"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Wrap a request in an object so it can be queued, logged, retried, or undone.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **command** turns an operation into data: it carries its receiver and arguments, and exposes `execute()` (and sometimes `undo()`). The **invoker** runs commands without knowing what they do, so it can schedule, record or reverse them. The **receiver** does the real work.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Decouples:** who asks for an action from who performs it, and when.","**Enables:** undo/redo, queues, retries, audit logs, macros.","**Requires:** commands capture what they need to undo themselves."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"If you only need to run something later, a lambda or `Runnable` is already a command. The pattern pays off once you add undo, persistence or logging.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"world\nHello world\nHello","file":"Command.java","regions":{"client":[46,55],"command":[8,11],"concrete":[13,34],"invoker":[36,42],"receiver":[4,6]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\nfinal class Doc {\n    final StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder();\n}\n\ninterface Command {\n    void execute();\n    void undo();\n}\n\nrecord Insert(Doc doc, int at, String s) implements Command {\n    public void execute() { doc.text.insert(at, s); }\n\n    public void undo() { doc.text.delete(at, at + s.length()); }\n}\n\nfinal class Delete implements Command {\n    private final Doc doc;\n    private final int from, to;\n    private String removed = \"\"; // captured so undo can restore it\n\n    Delete(Doc doc, int from, int to) {\n        this.doc = doc; this.from = from; this.to = to;\n    }\n\n    public void execute() {\n        removed = doc.text.substring(from, to);\n        doc.text.delete(from, to);\n    }\n\n    public void undo() { doc.text.insert(from, removed); }\n}\n\nfinal class History {\n    private final Deque<Command> done = new ArrayDeque<>();\n\n    void run(Command c) { c.execute(); done.push(c); }\n\n    void undo() { if (!done.isEmpty()) done.pop().undo(); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var doc = new Doc();\n        var history = new History();\n        history.run(new Insert(doc, 0, \"Hello\"));\n        history.run(new Insert(doc, 5, \" world\"));\n        history.run(new Delete(doc, 0, 6));\n        System.out.println(doc.text);\n        history.undo();\n        System.out.println(doc.text);\n        history.undo();\n        System.out.println(doc.text);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The receiver is plain state with no knowledge of commands. Commands are the only ones who modify it here.","region":"receiver"},{"note":"The command contract: do it, and reverse it. Invokers depend on nothing else.","region":"command"},{"note":"`Insert` is reversible from its own fields. `Delete` captures the removed text during `execute`, because undo cannot recompute what is gone.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"The invoker runs a command and keeps it on a stack. It never inspects the command type, so history and undo work for any command.","region":"invoker"},{"note":"Three edits, then two undos walk back through history in reverse order: delete, then the second insert.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A persistent background job queue.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An order service enqueues `SendInvoice(orderId)` and `ResizeImage(assetId, width)` as small serializable records in a database table. Workers on other machines load a row, run it, and mark it done. Failures retry with backoff, an admin screen lists and cancels pending jobs, and the same table is the audit log. The web request returns before any work happens.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["sealed interface Job permits SendInvoice, ResizeImage {}","record SendInvoice(long orderId) implements Job {}","","queue.enqueue(new SendInvoice(order.id()));","// worker: handlers.get(job.getClass()).run(job)"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: work becomes data. It can wait, move between machines, be retried and be inspected, with no change to the code that requested it.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"history","label":"done","rel":"aggregation","to":"cmd"},{"from":"insert","rel":"realizes","to":"cmd"},{"from":"delete","rel":"realizes","to":"cmd"},{"from":"insert","rel":"dependency","to":"doc"},{"from":"delete","rel":"dependency","to":"doc"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"history","kind":"class","members":["- done: Deque<Command>","+ run(c) / undo()"],"name":"History","role":"Invoker","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"cmd","kind":"interface","members":["+ execute()","+ undo()"],"name":"Command","role":"Command","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"insert","kind":"record","members":["doc, at, s"],"name":"Insert","role":"ConcreteCommand","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"delete","kind":"class","members":["doc, from, to","- removed: String"],"name":"Delete","role":"ConcreteCommand","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"doc","kind":"class","members":["+ text: StringBuilder"],"name":"Doc","role":"Receiver","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"new Delete(doc, 0, 6)","style":"create","to":"delete"},{"from":"client","label":"run(cmd)","style":"call","to":"history"},{"from":"history","label":"execute()","style":"call","to":"delete"},{"from":"delete","label":"delete(0, 6)","style":"call","to":"doc"},{"from":"client","label":"undo()","style":"call","to":"history"},{"from":"history","label":"undo()","style":"call","to":"delete"},{"from":"delete","label":"insert(0, removed)","style":"call","to":"doc"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"history","name":"History","role":"Invoker"},{"id":"delete","name":"Delete","role":"ConcreteCommand"},{"id":"doc","name":"Doc","role":"Receiver"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Operations need undo/redo, history or an audit trail.","Work must be queued, scheduled, retried or sent to another process.","Operations should be composable into macros or batches."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A direct call or a one-shot callback is enough.","Undo cannot be defined, for instance after an irreversible side effect."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Undo that does not capture prior state, such as the deleted text.","Commands holding live references that change before they run.","Retries need idempotent commands; otherwise they run twice.","Serialized commands need versioning when their shape changes."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A command class per button that only forwards one call.","Unbounded history that keeps every object alive."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Lambdas and `Runnable` are commands without undo.","Sealed interfaces of records make serializable, exhaustively handled commands."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A strategy answers how to do one job right now. A command packages a request to run, queue or undo later.","ref":"strategy","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Undo can restore a snapshot (memento) instead of computing an inverse. Heavy edits favor snapshots; small ones favor inverse operations.","ref":"memento","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A macro command is a composite: it holds a list of commands and executes or undoes them as one.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Commands travel down a handler chain so that one handler can validate, log or reject them.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A saga is a long-running sequence of commands where each step has a compensating command.","ref":"saga","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`Executor.execute(Runnable)` separates submitting an action from deciding when and on which thread it runs.","name":"Runnable and Executor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A reusable object that bundles behavior with its text, icon and enabled state for menus and buttons.","name":"javax.swing.Action","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Operations with `execute`, `undo` and `redo` managed by an `IOperationHistory`.","name":"IUndoableOperation","where":"Eclipse"},{"detail":"Scheduled work expressed as a `Job` with a `JobDetail`, stored and fired by the scheduler.","name":"org.quartz.Job","where":"Quartz"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Action { void run(); void revert(); }","class Account { void withdraw(long c) {} void deposit(long c) {} }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["record Transfer(Account from, Account to, long cents) implements Action {","    public void run() {","        from.withdraw(cents);","        to.deposit(cents);","    }","","    public void revert() {","        to.withdraw(cents);","        from.deposit(cents);","    }","}","// journal.add(new Transfer(a, b, 500)); ... journal.getLast().revert();"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"command","why":"The request is an object carrying its receivers and arguments, and can be recorded and reversed later."},{"ref":"memento","why":"A memento restores a captured snapshot. This reverses by applying inverse operations."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"Nothing here is a swappable algorithm; the object represents one specific request."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"There is no base class with a fixed skeleton and overridable steps."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"command","why":"Each operation becomes a serializable object that can be stored, executed later, retried and listed."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"It chooses how to do something now; it does not turn requests into storable data."},{"ref":"observer","why":"It broadcasts changes to listeners; it does not queue or retry requests."},{"ref":"memento","why":"It captures state for restoring, not an operation to execute later."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A scheduler must persist user-submitted operations such as resizing an image or sending an email, run them later on other workers, retry them after failures, and show them in an admin screen."},{"compile":{"template":["class Doc { final StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder(); }","interface Command { void execute(); void undo(); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`execute` never records the text it removes, so `undo` inserts an empty string. Capture state first: `removed = doc.text.substring(from, to);` before deleting.","flawLines":[10],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Delete implements Command {","    private final Doc doc;","    private final int from, to;","    private String removed = \"\";","","    Delete(Doc doc, int from, int to) {","        this.doc = doc; this.from = from; this.to = to;","    }","","    public void execute() { doc.text.delete(from, to); }","    public void undo() { doc.text.insert(from, removed); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Command pattern's undo."},{"blocks":[["var doc = new Doc();","var history = new History();"],["Command cmd = new Insert(doc, 0, \"Hi\");"],["history.run(cmd);"],["history.undo();"],["System.out.println(doc.text);"]],"compile":{"template":["class Doc { final StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder(); }","interface Command { void execute(); void undo(); }","record Insert(Doc d, int at, String s) implements Command { public void execute() { d.text.insert(at, s); } public void undo() { d.text.delete(at, at + s.length()); } }","class History { void run(Command c) { c.execute(); } void undo() {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The command needs its receiver, and the invoker must run it before there is anything to undo. The final state is printed only after the undo.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Request becomes an object: receiver plus arguments","Invoker is decoupled from what the command does","Enables queue, log, retry, undo and macros","Undo needs state captured during execute","Strategy chooses how now; command packages what to run later"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Command turns an operation into an object with its receiver and arguments. An invoker can then run it later, queue it, log it, retry it or send it elsewhere without knowing what it does. For undo, a command must capture whatever it needs to reverse itself, such as the deleted text, while executing. Strategy differs: it picks how to do a job now.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Command in your own words: what does packaging a request as an object buy you, and what must a command capture to be undoable?"}],"id":"command"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Model a small language as a class per grammar rule, so a sentence becomes a tree that evaluates itself.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each rule of the grammar becomes a node type. **Terminal** nodes (numbers, variables) answer directly; **nonterminal** nodes (sums, comparisons) combine their children. Parsing builds the tree once, then `eval(context)` runs it as often as needed against different inputs.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Fits:** small, stable grammars such as rules, filters, formulas and queries.","**Cost:** one class per rule; a large grammar becomes unmanageable.","**Separate concerns:** parsing builds the tree, the nodes only evaluate."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"GoF Interpreter does not include the parser. For anything beyond a toy grammar, reach for a parser generator or an existing expression library.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Add[left=Num[value=2], right=Mul[left=Var[name=x], right=Num[value=3]]]\n17","file":"Interpreter.java","regions":{"client":[62,64],"grammar":[6,8],"nonterminals":[22,32],"parser":[34,58],"terminals":[10,20]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nsealed interface Expr permits Num, Var, Add, Mul {\n    int eval(Map<String, Integer> env);\n}\n\nrecord Num(int value) implements Expr {\n    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) { return value; }\n}\n\nrecord Var(String name) implements Expr {\n    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) {\n        Integer v = env.get(name);\n        if (v == null) throw new IllegalStateException(\"unbound \" + name);\n        return v;\n    }\n}\n\nrecord Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {\n    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) {\n        return left.eval(env) + right.eval(env);\n    }\n}\n\nrecord Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {\n    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) {\n        return left.eval(env) * right.eval(env);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Parser {\n    private final Deque<String> tokens;\n\n    Parser(String src) {\n        String s = src.replace(\"(\", \" ( \").replace(\")\", \" ) \").trim();\n        tokens = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(s.split(\"\\\\s+\")));\n    }\n\n    Expr parse() {\n        String t = tokens.removeFirst();\n        if (!t.equals(\"(\")) {\n            return Character.isDigit(t.charAt(0))\n                ? new Num(Integer.parseInt(t)) : new Var(t);\n        }\n        String op = tokens.removeFirst();\n        Expr left = parse();\n        Expr right = parse();\n        tokens.removeFirst(); // the closing \")\"\n        return switch (op) {\n            case \"+\" -> new Add(left, right);\n            case \"*\" -> new Mul(left, right);\n            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"op \" + op);\n        };\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Expr e = new Parser(\"(+ 2 (* x 3))\").parse();\n        System.out.println(e);\n        System.out.println(e.eval(Map.of(\"x\", 5)));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The abstract expression. `sealed` closes the grammar: the compiler knows every node type, so adding a rule is a visible, deliberate change.","region":"grammar"},{"note":"Terminal expressions end the recursion. `Var` reads from the context (the environment map), which is what makes one tree reusable across inputs.","region":"terminals"},{"note":"Nonterminal expressions hold sub-expressions and delegate to them. Evaluation is a recursive walk with no central switch.","region":"nonterminals"},{"note":"A tiny prefix parser builds the tree. It is separate from the nodes: the pattern defines the tree, not how you obtain it.","region":"parser"},{"note":"Parse once, evaluate with any environment. The printed record shows the abstract syntax tree the sentence became.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Operator-defined eligibility rules for a promotion.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Marketing wants to write conditions such as `plan = 'pro' and seats > 10` without a deploy. The rule text is parsed once at save time into an AST of `And`, `Equals` and `GreaterThan` nodes, validated, and cached. Each request evaluates the cached tree against a `Customer` context. The grammar has six rules and rarely grows, which is where Interpreter pays off.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Rule rule = parser.parse(\"plan = 'pro' and seats > 10\");","cache.put(promo.id(), rule);","","boolean eligible = cache.get(promo.id()).eval(customer);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: non-engineers change rules at runtime, and the same validated tree is evaluated on every request without reparsing.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","rel":"association","to":"expr"},{"from":"num","rel":"realizes","to":"expr"},{"from":"add","rel":"realizes","to":"expr"},{"from":"add","label":"children","rel":"composition","to":"expr"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ eval(ast, env)"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"expr","kind":"interface","members":["+ eval(env): int"],"name":"Expr","role":"AbstractExpression","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"num","kind":"record","members":["+ value: int"],"name":"Num","role":"TerminalExpression","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"add","kind":"record","members":["+ left: Expr","+ right: Expr"],"name":"Add","role":"NonterminalExpression","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"client","text":"builds the tree, supplies env"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"eval(env)","style":"call","to":"add"},{"from":"add","label":"eval(env)","style":"call","to":"num"},{"from":"num","label":"2","style":"return","to":"add"},{"from":"add","label":"eval(env)","style":"call","to":"mul"},{"from":"mul","label":"15","style":"return","to":"add"},{"from":"add","label":"17","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"add","name":"Add","role":"Nonterminal"},{"id":"num","name":"Num","role":"Terminal"},{"id":"mul","name":"Mul","role":"Nonterminal"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The grammar is small and stable, and sentences are evaluated repeatedly.","Users or config supply the rules, so they cannot be compiled in.","You want the tree itself to be inspectable, printable and testable."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The grammar is large or evolving: class count and maintenance explode.","Performance is critical; tree walking is slower than compiled code.","A lambda, `Predicate` or existing expression engine already does the job."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Treating hand-written string splitting as a parser and getting precedence wrong.","Evaluating untrusted input with unbounded recursion depth or cost.","Mixing parsing into the nodes, which couples syntax to semantics."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed interfaces plus records give a closed, immutable AST.","A `switch` over the sealed type can host several operations outside the nodes, like Visitor."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"An interpreter tree is a Composite whose leaves and branches carry grammar meaning. Composite is about treating parts and wholes uniformly; Interpreter adds evaluation of a language.","ref":"composite","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Visitor moves operations such as eval, print and optimize out of the nodes, so you can add them without editing every node class. Natural once the grammar is stable.","ref":"visitor","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Terminal symbols that repeat (constants, keywords) can be shared as flyweights to save memory in big trees.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"If you only need to pick one of a few algorithms, a strategy or lambda is enough. Interpreter is for sentences with structure that users compose.","ref":"strategy","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"An iterator can walk the tree for tooling such as pretty-printing or validation, without adding methods to each node.","ref":"iterator","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Compiles a regular expression into an internal node graph that `Matcher` then evaluates against input.","name":"java.util.regex.Pattern","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Parses a format pattern once into segments, then formats arguments against it.","name":"java.text.MessageFormat","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Spring Expression Language parses text into an `Expression` AST that is evaluated against an `EvaluationContext`.","name":"SpelExpressionParser","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Parses repository method names such as `findByLastnameAndAge` into a query tree.","name":"PartTree","where":"Spring Data"},{"detail":"Parses query strings into a tree of `Query` objects that are then executed.","name":"QueryParser","where":"Apache Lucene"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["sealed interface Rule permits Eq, And, Not {","    boolean test(Map<String, String> row);","}","record Eq(String col, String val) implements Rule {","    public boolean test(Map<String, String> row) {","        return val.equals(row.get(col));","    }","}","record And(Rule a, Rule b) implements Rule {","    public boolean test(Map<String, String> row) {","        return a.test(row) && b.test(row);","    }","}","record Not(Rule r) implements Rule {","    public boolean test(Map<String, String> row) { return !r.test(row); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"interpreter","why":"Each grammar rule is a node type, and the tree evaluates itself recursively against a context row."},{"ref":"composite","why":"The shape is similar, but the nodes encode a language with meaning (equals, and, not), not a uniform part-whole tree."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"Every node is evaluated; none decides to handle or pass a request along."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The rules are not alternatives chosen from outside; they compose each other into one sentence."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"interpreter","why":"A small stable grammar, parsed once into a tree and evaluated repeatedly against changing context."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A strategy picks one algorithm; here users compose arbitrary nested conditions, which needs a tree."},{"ref":"visitor","why":"Visitor adds operations to an existing hierarchy. You first need the hierarchy that represents the language."},{"ref":"command","why":"Nothing is being queued, undone or logged; the need is evaluating a user-written condition."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Ops engineers define alert conditions in a config file, such as `cpu > 90 and region = 'eu'`. There are about eight operators, they rarely change, and a condition is evaluated thousands of times per minute against fresh metrics."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The nonterminal inspects the concrete type of its child. Nodes should only call `eval` on sub-expressions, so any new node type behaves correctly. Replace the line with `r.eval(env)`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["interface Expr { int eval(Map<String, Integer> env); }","record Num(int v) implements Expr {","    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) { return v; }","}","record Add(Expr l, Expr r) implements Expr {","    public int eval(Map<String, Integer> env) {","        int left = l.eval(env);","        int right = r instanceof Num n ? n.v() : r.eval(env);","        return left + right;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Interpreter pattern."},{"blocks":[["Rule rule = parser.parse(text);"],["Map<String, Integer> env = Map.of(\"x\", 5);"],["int result = rule.eval(env);"],["System.out.println(result);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","interface Rule { int eval(Map<String, Integer> env); }","interface RuleParser { Rule parse(String text); }","class Snippet { void run(RuleParser parser, String text) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Parsing produces the tree first, the environment supplies the context, and evaluation needs both before its result can be used.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the steps for evaluating a rule from text."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One class per grammar rule: terminals and nonterminals","The sentence is an AST that evaluates itself against a context","Parsing builds the tree and is not part of the pattern","Does not scale to large or changing grammars","Structurally a Composite with language semantics"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Interpreter turns each rule of a small grammar into a class. A parsed sentence is a tree of those nodes, and evaluating the root recursively evaluates the whole sentence against a context. Parsing is separate from the pattern. It suits small, stable grammars; for large ones use a parser generator or an existing engine.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Interpreter in your own words: what the classes represent, where parsing fits, and when you would not use it."}],"id":"interpreter"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give clients a uniform way to walk a collection's elements without exposing how it stores them.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **aggregate** hands out an **iterator**, a small cursor object that knows the traversal state: where it is and what comes next. Clients depend only on `hasNext` and `next`, so a list, a tree or a lazy generator all look the same, and several traversals can run at once.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Hides:** the internal structure (array, tree, linked nodes, database cursor).","**Holds:** the traversal position, separate from the collection.","**Enables:** several independent, lazy traversals over one aggregate."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"In Java, implementing `Iterable<T>` is what makes a type usable in the enhanced `for` loop.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"apple fig kiwi pear\napple fig","file":"Iterator.java","regions":{"aggregate":[6,22],"client":[50,52],"iterator":[24,44],"lazy":[53,54]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.Iterator;\nimport java.util.NoSuchElementException;\n\nfinal class SortedTree<T extends Comparable<T>> implements Iterable<T> {\n    private static final class Node<T> {\n        final T value;\n        Node<T> left, right;\n        Node(T value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node<T> root;\n\n    void add(T value) { root = insert(root, value); }\n\n    private Node<T> insert(Node<T> n, T v) {\n        if (n == null) return new Node<>(v);\n        if (v.compareTo(n.value) < 0) n.left = insert(n.left, v);\n        else n.right = insert(n.right, v);\n        return n;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public Iterator<T> iterator() {\n        return new Iterator<>() {\n            private final Deque<Node<T>> path = new ArrayDeque<>();\n            { descend(root); }\n\n            private void descend(Node<T> n) {\n                for (; n != null; n = n.left) path.push(n);\n            }\n\n            public boolean hasNext() { return !path.isEmpty(); }\n\n            public T next() {\n                if (path.isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();\n                Node<T> n = path.pop();\n                descend(n.right);\n                return n.value;\n            }\n        };\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var tree = new SortedTree<String>();\n        for (String s : \"pear fig apple kiwi\".split(\" \")) tree.add(s);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (String s : tree) sb.append(s).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n        Iterator<String> it = tree.iterator();\n        System.out.println(it.next() + \" \" + it.next());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The aggregate is a binary search tree. Its node type and links are private: callers can add elements but never see the structure.","region":"aggregate"},{"note":"`iterator()` returns a fresh cursor each call. The cursor keeps an explicit stack of pending nodes, so traversal state lives in the iterator, not in the tree.","region":"iterator"},{"note":"Because the tree implements `Iterable`, the enhanced `for` works. The client sees elements in sorted order and has no idea a tree exists.","region":"client"},{"note":"Iteration is lazy: only the nodes needed so far are visited. A second iterator would have its own stack and be independent.","region":"lazy"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Streaming a large database table to a CSV export.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A reporting job must export millions of orders without loading them into memory. A repository returns an iterator that fetches a page at a time through a server-side cursor, hiding the paging and the connection handling. The exporter loops over `orders` and writes rows. Closing the iterator releases the cursor, so it implements `AutoCloseable` as well.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["try (CloseableIterator<Order> it = repo.scanAll()) {","    while (it.hasNext()) {","        csv.write(it.next());","    }","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: constant memory for any table size, and the exporter works unchanged against an in-memory fake in tests.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","rel":"dependency","to":"agg"},{"from":"client","rel":"dependency","to":"it"},{"from":"tree","rel":"realizes","to":"agg"},{"from":"cur","rel":"realizes","to":"it"},{"from":"tree","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"cur"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"agg","kind":"interface","members":["+ iterator(): Iterator"],"name":"Iterable<T>","role":"Aggregate","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"it","kind":"interface","members":["+ hasNext(): boolean","+ next(): T"],"name":"Iterator<T>","role":"Iterator","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"tree","kind":"class","members":["+ iterator()"],"name":"SortedTree","role":"ConcreteAggregate","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"cur","kind":"class","members":["- path: Deque<Node>"],"name":"TreeCursor","role":"ConcreteIterator","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"iterator()","style":"call","to":"agg"},{"from":"agg","label":"new Cursor","style":"create","to":"it"},{"from":"client","label":"hasNext()","style":"call","to":"it"},{"from":"it","label":"true","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"next()","style":"call","to":"it"},{"from":"it","label":"element","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"agg","name":"SortedTree","role":"Aggregate"},{"id":"it","name":"Cursor","role":"Iterator"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Callers should traverse without depending on the storage structure.","You need several simultaneous or lazy traversals of one collection.","Different traversal orders (in-order, level-order) share one interface."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A plain list or array is enough and random access is needed.","Operations such as map and filter are the real goal: use streams."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Modifying the collection during traversal: JDK iterators are fail-fast and throw `ConcurrentModificationException`.","Iterators that hold resources (cursors, files) and are never closed.","Assuming `Iterator` instances are thread-safe; they are not."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`Iterable` powers the `for-each` loop; `Stream` and `spliterator()` cover bulk and parallel pipelines.","Most code writes `for` or streams; you hand-write an `Iterator` only for your own data structures."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Iterators are the usual way to traverse a composite tree without exposing its node structure to clients.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An iterator hands out elements and the client decides what to do. A visitor pushes an operation into the elements and picks behavior per element type.","ref":"visitor","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"An iterator can use a memento to save and restore its position, for example to rewind or resume a scan.","ref":"memento","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"`iterator()` is a factory method: each aggregate subclass decides which concrete iterator to create.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An iterator is pulled by the client, one element at a time. An observer is pushed to by the subject whenever something happens.","ref":"observer","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Every `Collection` returns an `Iterator`; implementing `Iterable` enables the enhanced `for` loop.","name":"java.util.Iterator and Iterable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A private inner iterator that tracks `modCount` to fail fast with `ConcurrentModificationException`.","name":"ArrayList.Itr","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A forward cursor over query rows, advanced with `next()`, hiding paging and the wire protocol.","name":"java.sql.ResultSet","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The splittable iterator behind `Stream`, used for both sequential and parallel traversal.","name":"java.util.Spliterator","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Base class that lets you implement `computeNext()` and get a correct lazy `Iterator`.","name":"AbstractIterator","where":"Guava"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Iterator;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Playlist implements Iterable<String> {","    private final String[] tracks;","    Playlist(String... tracks) { this.tracks = tracks; }","","    public Iterator<String> iterator() {","        return new Iterator<>() {","            private int i = 0;","            public boolean hasNext() { return i < tracks.length; }","            public String next() { return tracks[i++]; }","        };","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"iterator","why":"A separate cursor holds the position and exposes sequential access while the array stays hidden."},{"ref":"composite","why":"There is no tree of parts and wholes, only a flat sequence being walked."},{"ref":"memento","why":"Nothing captures state for later restoration; the index only advances."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Elements are pulled by the client; nothing is pushed to registered listeners."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"iterator","why":"Each reader gets its own cursor that hides the ring layout and tracks its own position."},{"ref":"visitor","why":"Samples are one type; there is no need to dispatch per element type, only sequential access."},{"ref":"memento","why":"Nobody needs to restore earlier buffer states; the need is ordered traversal."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"No group of objects needs coordinating; one structure needs a traversal API."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A metrics library stores samples in a ring buffer to avoid allocations. Callers must read samples oldest to newest without knowing about wrap-around, and a dashboard thread may read while another one also reads."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The position is stored in the aggregate, so every iterator shares it: a second loop starts where the first ended. Move `cursor` into the anonymous iterator so each traversal has its own state.","flawLines":[3],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Bag implements Iterable<String> {","    private final List<String> items = new ArrayList<>();","    private int cursor = 0;","","    void add(String s) { items.add(s); }","","    public Iterator<String> iterator() {","        return new Iterator<>() {","            public boolean hasNext() { return cursor < items.size(); }","            public String next() { return items.get(cursor++); }","        };","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Iterator pattern."},{"blocks":[["Iterator<String> it = names.iterator();"],["while (it.hasNext()) {"],["    String n = it.next();"],["    out.append(n);"],["}"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","class Snippet { void run(List<String> names, StringBuilder out) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The cursor must be created before it is tested, and each next() must follow a successful hasNext(); the body uses the element just returned.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the steps of a manual traversal."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Hides the internal structure of the aggregate","Traversal state lives in the iterator, not the collection","Multiple independent, lazy traversals are possible","Pull-based, unlike Observer which pushes","Concurrent modification needs a policy such as fail-fast"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Iterator gives clients sequential access to an aggregate without revealing its structure. The traversal state lives in a separate cursor object, not the collection, so several traversals can run independently and lazily. In Java, Iterable plus the for-each loop is the idiom.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Iterator in your own words: what it hides, where the traversal state lives, and why that matters."}],"id":"iterator"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Replace many-to-many references between objects with one object that owns their interaction.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Colleagues no longer know each other. They talk to a **mediator**, which decides who hears what and in which order. Coupling moves from a web of N-to-N links into a single class, which makes the colleagues reusable and the interaction rules easy to find and change.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Colleagues:** know only the mediator interface.","**Mediator:** holds routing and coordination rules.","**Trade:** simpler colleagues, a more complex mediator."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A mediator accumulates logic by design. Watch it: unchecked, it turns into a god class.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ana=[cy: hello]\nbo=[ana: hi, cy: hello]\ncy=[ana: hi]","file":"Mediator.java","regions":{"client":[44,48],"colleague":[11,24],"concrete":[26,40],"mediator":[6,9],"traffic":[49,52]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\ninterface Room {\n    void join(Member m);\n    void send(Member from, String text);\n}\n\nfinal class Member {\n    private final String name;\n    private final List<String> inbox = new ArrayList<>();\n    private Room room;\n\n    Member(String name) { this.name = name; }\n\n    String name() { return name; }\n    List<String> inbox() { return inbox; }\n\n    void enter(Room room) { this.room = room; room.join(this); }\n    void say(String text) { room.send(this, text); }\n    void receive(String from, String text) { inbox.add(from + \": \" + text); }\n}\n\nfinal class ModeratedRoom implements Room {\n    private final List<Member> members = new ArrayList<>();\n    private final Set<String> muted = new HashSet<>();\n\n    void mute(String name) { muted.add(name); }\n\n    public void join(Member m) { members.add(m); }\n\n    public void send(Member from, String text) {\n        if (muted.contains(from.name())) return;\n        for (Member m : members) {\n            if (m != from) m.receive(from.name(), text);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var room = new ModeratedRoom();\n        var ana = new Member(\"ana\");\n        var bo = new Member(\"bo\");\n        var cy = new Member(\"cy\");\n        for (Member m : List.of(ana, bo, cy)) m.enter(room);\n        ana.say(\"hi\");\n        room.mute(\"bo\");\n        bo.say(\"spam\");\n        cy.say(\"hello\");\n        System.out.println(\"ana=\" + ana.inbox());\n        System.out.println(\"bo=\" + bo.inbox());\n        System.out.println(\"cy=\" + cy.inbox());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The mediator interface is all colleagues ever see. It offers only the coordination operations they need.","region":"mediator"},{"note":"A colleague keeps a reference to the room, never to other members. It sends through the mediator and just records what it receives.","region":"colleague"},{"note":"The concrete mediator owns the rules: broadcast to everyone but the sender, and drop messages from muted members. Policy lives here alone.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"Wiring: each member enters the room, which registers it. Members are created independently of each other.","region":"client"},{"note":"Muting `bo` changes behavior without touching any `Member`. The room drops `spam`, so no one receives it.","region":"traffic"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Fields of a checkout form that depend on each other.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Choosing a country enables a state dropdown, changes the postal-code validator, hides VAT fields and recalculates shipping. Wiring each widget to the others yields dozens of cross-references. A `CheckoutFormMediator` receives change events from widgets and updates the rest. Widgets stay dumb and reusable; every rule about field dependencies reads top to bottom in one class.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["void changed(Widget source) {","    if (source == country) {","        state.setEnabled(country.value().hasStates());","        postal.setPattern(country.value().postalPattern());","        vat.setVisible(country.value().isEu());","    }","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: adding a field rule edits one class, and widgets are testable and reusable on other forms.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"cmed","rel":"realizes","to":"med"},{"from":"cmed","label":"members","rel":"aggregation","to":"m"},{"from":"m","label":"room","rel":"association","to":"med"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"med","kind":"interface","members":["+ join(Member)","+ send(from, text)"],"name":"Room","role":"Mediator","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"cmed","kind":"class","members":["- members: List<Member>","- muted: Set<String>"],"name":"ModeratedRoom","role":"ConcreteMediator","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"m","kind":"class","members":["- room: Room","+ say(text)","+ receive(from, text)"],"name":"Member","role":"Colleague","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"ana","label":"send(ana, hi)","style":"call","to":"room"},{"from":"room","label":"receive(ana, hi)","style":"call","to":"bo"},{"from":"room","label":"receive(ana, hi)","style":"call","to":"cy"}],"participants":[{"id":"ana","name":"ana","role":"Colleague"},{"id":"room","name":"Room","role":"Mediator"},{"id":"bo","name":"bo","role":"Colleague"},{"id":"cy","name":"cy","role":"Colleague"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Objects reference each other in a tangle that is hard to follow.","Interaction rules change more often than the objects themselves.","You want colleagues reusable in other contexts."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Two objects interact in one simple way: a direct call is clearer.","The interaction is pure notification; Observer is lighter."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The mediator becomes a god class holding all business logic.","Colleagues sneak references to each other, bypassing the mediator.","The mediator is a single point of change and a testing hotspot."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An in-process event bus or Spring's `ApplicationEventPublisher` acts as a mediator for decoupled components.","For UI state, a single state holder with reducers plays the mediator's role."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Observer is one-to-many notification with the subject unaware of listeners' logic. A mediator is many-to-many coordination that contains the rules. A mediator is often built on observers.","ref":"observer","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A facade simplifies access to a subsystem in one direction and the subsystem does not know it. A mediator is two-way: the colleagues know it and depend on it.","ref":"facade","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Colleagues often send commands or events to the mediator, which dispatches them to the right collaborators.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A chain passes one request through handlers that do not know each other. A mediator knows every participant and routes freely among them.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A controller in MVC mediates between the view and the model, so they stay unaware of each other.","ref":"mvc","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Submitters hand work to the executor, which mediates between tasks and worker threads.","name":"java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Components publish events through the context instead of referencing listeners directly.","name":"ApplicationEventPublisher","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"A central front controller routes requests between handlers, view resolution and exception handling.","name":"DispatcherServlet","where":"Spring MVC"},{"detail":"Coordinates forwarding and including between resources without them knowing each other.","name":"RequestDispatcher","where":"Jakarta Servlet"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Plane { boolean onRunway(); void holdPattern(); void land(); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Tower {","    private final List<Plane> planes = new ArrayList<>();","","    void register(Plane p) { planes.add(p); }","","    void requestLanding(Plane p) {","        boolean busy = planes.stream().anyMatch(Plane::onRunway);","        if (busy) p.holdPattern(); else p.land();","    }","}","// Planes call tower.requestLanding(this); they never talk to each other"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mediator","why":"Peers communicate only through a central object that holds the coordination rule."},{"ref":"observer","why":"The tower decides what each plane does; it is not just broadcasting a change to passive listeners."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade is used one way by clients and unknown to the subsystem; here planes depend on the tower and call it."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"The request is not passed from handler to handler; one object makes the decision."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mediator","why":"Centralizes the many-to-many dependencies into one class so widgets stop referencing each other."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Each widget would still subscribe to several others, keeping the tangle and scattering the rules."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade offers a simpler outward API; it does not coordinate two-way interactions among peers."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The problem is coordination among objects, not swapping one algorithm."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A dialog has ten widgets whose enabled state, visibility and validation depend on each other. Each widget currently holds references to several others, and changing a rule means editing five classes."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Dialog { void changed(Object source); }","interface Label { void setText(String s); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The colleague updates another colleague directly, bypassing the mediator. Only `dialog.changed(this)` should remain; the dialog decides that the label must change.","flawLines":[10],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Slider {","    private final Dialog dialog;","    private final Label label;","    int value;","","    Slider(Dialog dialog, Label label) { this.dialog = dialog; this.label = label; }","","    void move(int v) {","        value = v;","        label.setText(\"\" + v);","        dialog.changed(this);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Mediator pattern."},{"blocks":[["var room = new ModeratedRoom();"],["var ana = new Member(\"ana\");"],["ana.enter(room);"],["ana.say(\"hi\");"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Room { void join(Member m); void send(Member from, String text); }","final class Member { private Room room; Member(String n) {}","  void enter(Room r) { room = r; r.join(this); } void say(String t) { room.send(this, t); } }","final class ModeratedRoom implements Room {","  public void join(Member m) {} public void send(Member f, String t) {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The mediator must exist before a colleague enters it, and a colleague must be registered before it can send anything through it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the setup so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Peers talk through one object, not each other","Interaction rules live in the mediator","Colleagues become simple and reusable","Risk: the mediator becomes a god class","Observer notifies; Mediator coordinates"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Mediator replaces a tangle of direct references between peers with a central object that owns their interaction rules. Peers only know the mediator, so they become reusable. The cost is a mediator that can grow into a god class. Observer broadcasts changes one-to-many without coordination logic.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Mediator in your own words: what problem it solves, what it costs, and how it differs from Observer."}],"id":"mediator"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Capture an object's internal state in an opaque snapshot so it can be restored later.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **originator** creates a **memento** of its own state and later restores itself from one. A **caretaker** stores mementos, for example in an undo stack, but cannot read or change them. Encapsulation holds: only the originator understands the snapshot's contents.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Originator:** creates and consumes mementos.","**Memento:** immutable, opaque to everyone else.","**Caretaker:** decides when to save and restore, never inspects."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Typical uses are undo, checkpoints and transactional rollback of in-memory state.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Hello, world@12\nHello@5\n@0","file":"Memento.java","regions":{"caretaker":[29,34],"checkpoint":[40,43],"originator":[4,27],"undo":[45,48]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\nfinal class Editor {\n    /** Opaque to everyone except Editor. */\n    interface Memento {}\n\n    private record Snapshot(String text, int cursor) implements Memento {}\n\n    private StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder();\n    private int cursor;\n\n    void type(String s) {\n        text.insert(cursor, s);\n        cursor += s.length();\n    }\n\n    Memento save() { return new Snapshot(text.toString(), cursor); }\n\n    void restore(Memento m) {\n        var s = (Snapshot) m;\n        text = new StringBuilder(s.text());\n        cursor = s.cursor();\n    }\n\n    @Override public String toString() { return text + \"@\" + cursor; }\n}\n\nfinal class History {\n    private final Deque<Editor.Memento> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n\n    void push(Editor.Memento m) { stack.push(m); }\n    Editor.Memento pop() { return stack.pop(); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var editor = new Editor();\n        var history = new History();\n        history.push(editor.save());\n        editor.type(\"Hello\");\n        history.push(editor.save());\n        editor.type(\", world\");\n        System.out.println(editor);\n        editor.restore(history.pop());\n        System.out.println(editor);\n        editor.restore(history.pop());\n        System.out.println(editor);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`Memento` is an empty public marker. The real snapshot is a private record, so only `Editor` can read its fields. Encapsulation is enforced by the compiler.","region":"originator"},{"note":"The caretaker stores opaque `Memento` values on a stack. It has no way to look inside, edit or fabricate a snapshot.","region":"caretaker"},{"note":"Snapshots are taken before each change. `save()` copies the text into an immutable `String`, so later typing cannot corrupt a stored memento.","region":"checkpoint"},{"note":"Restoring pops the latest snapshot and the editor rebuilds its own state from it. Each `pop` steps one checkpoint back.","region":"undo"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Rolling back a failed bulk edit in a diagram editor.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A user applies an auto-layout to a diagram with 2,000 shapes. The layout engine mutates positions in place and may fail halfway. Before running it, the editor takes a snapshot of positions and styles. On failure or on Ctrl-Z, it restores from the snapshot. Stored snapshots are compact (positions only), and the history panel can list them without understanding their contents.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Memento before = diagram.save();","try {","    layout.apply(diagram);","} catch (LayoutException e) {","    diagram.restore(before);","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: atomic, in-memory rollback with no change to the layout engine and no public setters on the diagram's internals.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"ct","label":"holds","rel":"aggregation","to":"mem"},{"from":"snap","rel":"realizes","to":"mem"},{"from":"orig","label":"creates, reads","rel":"dependency","to":"snap"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"ct","kind":"class","members":["- stack: Deque<Memento>","+ push(Memento)","+ pop(): Memento"],"name":"History","role":"Caretaker","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"orig","kind":"class","members":["- text, cursor","+ save(): Memento","+ restore(Memento)"],"name":"Editor","role":"Originator","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"mem","kind":"interface","members":[],"name":"Memento","role":"Memento (opaque)","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"snap","kind":"record","members":["text: String","cursor: int"],"name":"Snapshot","role":"Memento (private)","row":1}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"ct","label":"save()","style":"call","to":"orig"},{"from":"orig","label":"new Snapshot","style":"create","to":"m"},{"from":"orig","label":"memento","style":"return","to":"ct"},{"from":"ct","label":"restore(memento)","style":"call","to":"orig"},{"from":"orig","label":"read state","style":"call","to":"m"}],"participants":[{"id":"ct","name":"History","role":"Caretaker"},{"id":"orig","name":"Editor","role":"Originator"},{"id":"m","name":"Snapshot","role":"Memento"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["You need undo, checkpoints or rollback of an object's state.","Exposing the state through getters would break encapsulation.","State is small enough, or copy-on-write, to snapshot cheaply."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["State is huge and changes by small deltas: store the delta instead.","The object already is immutable; keep the old reference."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Shallow copies: mutable fields shared with the live object corrupt the snapshot.","Unbounded history exhausts memory; cap it or compact.","Mementos that hold external resources (files, connections) cannot be restored faithfully."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A nested private record is a compact, immutable memento.","With immutable state, the memento is the previous value itself: no pattern needed."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Undo with Command stores the inverse operation, which is cheap in memory. Memento stores the state itself, which is simpler when inverses are hard. Editors often mix both.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Both copy state. Prototype produces a new live object to use; a memento is an opaque snapshot that is only ever fed back to its originator.","ref":"prototype","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An iterator can save its position as a memento so a traversal can be resumed or rewound.","ref":"iterator","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Event sourcing rebuilds state from the full history of events, with snapshots as an optimization. Memento keeps only chosen snapshots.","ref":"event-sourcing","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"State changes behavior with the object's mode. Memento does not change behavior; it saves data to return to later.","ref":"state","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Serialization turns an object into an opaque byte snapshot that can be restored later; `ObjectOutputStream` writes it and `ObjectInputStream` reads it.","name":"java.io.Serializable","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Swing's undo framework stores `UndoableEdit`s that restore a document's earlier state.","name":"javax.swing.undo.UndoManager","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Hibernate keeps a snapshot of an entity's loaded state to detect dirty fields at flush.","name":"Persistence context loaded state","where":"Hibernate"},{"detail":"Each commit is an immutable snapshot of the working tree to which you can restore.","name":"Commit","where":"Git"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Canvas {","    private List<String> shapes = new ArrayList<>();","","    void draw(String s) { shapes.add(s); }","","    Object checkpoint() { return List.copyOf(shapes); }","","    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")","    void rollback(Object token) {","        shapes = new ArrayList<>((List<String>) token);","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"memento","why":"The object hands out an opaque copy of its state and rebuilds itself from it later."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Nothing creates a new live canvas; the copy is only fed back to the same object."},{"ref":"command","why":"No operation is being encapsulated or reversed; state, not an action, is stored."},{"ref":"state","why":"Behavior does not vary with a mode; the snapshot is data."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"memento","why":"Snapshots restore state regardless of how it was mutated, without exposing the cells' internals."},{"ref":"command","why":"Needs an inverse for every operation, which is difficult for sort and paste here."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Cloning produces new live objects; the need is restoring one object to an earlier state."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Notifying listeners of changes does not let you return to a prior state."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A spreadsheet needs multi-level undo. Cell state is private and mutated through many operations, some hard to invert, such as sort and paste with formatting. Sheets are small enough to copy."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The snapshot holds a reference to the live list, so later `add` calls change the saved state and `restore` even clears its own source. Save a copy: `new Snapshot(List.copyOf(lines))`.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Doc {","    private final List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();","    void add(String s) { lines.add(s); }","","    record Snapshot(List<String> lines) {}","","    Snapshot save() { return new Snapshot(lines); }","","    void restore(Snapshot s) {","        lines.clear();","        lines.addAll(s.lines());","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Memento pattern."},{"blocks":[["var history = new ArrayDeque<Editor.Memento>();"],["history.push(editor.save());"],["editor.type(\"!\");"],["editor.restore(history.pop());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","final class Editor { interface Memento {} void type(String s) {}","  Memento save() { return new Memento() {}; } void restore(Memento m) {} }","class Snippet { void run(Editor editor) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the store, snapshot before mutating, mutate, then restore from the snapshot. Saving after the edit would capture the wrong state.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the undo flow."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Originator creates and consumes snapshots","Caretaker stores them without looking inside","Snapshot is opaque and immutable","Must deep-copy mutable state","Command undo stores operations; Memento stores state"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"The originator creates an opaque snapshot of its own state and can restore itself from one. A caretaker stores snapshots, such as an undo stack, but cannot read them, so the originator's internals stay private. Snapshots must be immutable copies, not references into live state.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Memento in your own words: who creates snapshots, who stores them, and why it preserves encapsulation."}],"id":"memento"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let a subject notify any number of dependents when it changes, without knowing who they are.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **subject** keeps a list of **observers** that registered with it. When its state changes it calls each one through a small interface. Subject and observers are loosely coupled: observers can be added or removed at runtime, and the subject needs no knowledge of what they do.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Subject:** owns the state and the subscriber list.","**Observer:** reacts to a notification, through one interface.","**Direction:** push from subject, subscribe from observer."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Every observer you forget to unsubscribe is a memory leak (the lapsed listener problem).","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ALERT ACME at 120\n[ACME 100->105, ACME 105->120]","file":"Observer.java","regions":{"observer":[5,8],"subject":[10,31],"subscribe":[37,41],"unsubscribe":[44,45]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface PriceListener {\n    void onChange(String symbol, int oldPrice, int newPrice);\n}\n\nfinal class Ticker {\n    private final List<PriceListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n    private final String symbol;\n    private int price;\n\n    Ticker(String symbol, int price) {\n        this.symbol = symbol;\n        this.price = price;\n    }\n\n    Runnable subscribe(PriceListener l) {\n        listeners.add(l);\n        return () -> listeners.remove(l);\n    }\n\n    void set(int newPrice) {\n        if (newPrice == price) return;\n        int old = price;\n        price = newPrice;\n        for (PriceListener l : listeners) l.onChange(symbol, old, newPrice);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var ticker = new Ticker(\"ACME\", 100);\n        var log = new ArrayList<String>();\n        Runnable cancelLog = ticker.subscribe(\n            (s, o, n) -> log.add(s + \" \" + o + \"->\" + n));\n        ticker.subscribe((s, o, n) -> {\n            if (n > 110) System.out.println(\"ALERT \" + s + \" at \" + n);\n        });\n        ticker.set(105);\n        ticker.set(120);\n        cancelLog.run();\n        ticker.set(90);\n        System.out.println(log);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The observer is a one-method functional interface. The subject depends only on this contract, so a lambda is a complete observer.","region":"observer"},{"note":"`subscribe` returns a cancellation handle, which makes unsubscribing explicit. `CopyOnWriteArrayList` lets listeners subscribe or cancel during a notification without a concurrent modification error.","region":"subject"},{"note":"Two unrelated observers register. The ticker knows neither: one records history, the other raises alerts above a threshold.","region":"subscribe"},{"note":"Cancelling removes only the logger. The later change to `90` notifies the alert listener alone and the log stays unchanged.","region":"unsubscribe"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Reacting to configuration changes in a running service.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A config service reloads feature flags and rate limits from a remote store. The HTTP client pool, the cache and the metrics exporter each need to adapt when values change, but the service should not know about any of them. Each component subscribes to the keys it cares about and applies the change itself. Components that shut down cancel their subscription so they can be collected.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Runnable cancel = config.subscribe(\"http.maxConnections\", v -> {","    pool.resize(Integer.parseInt(v));","});","","// on shutdown","cancel.run();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: new consumers of a setting need no change to the config service, and settings update live with no restart.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"subj","label":"notifies","rel":"aggregation","to":"obs"},{"from":"log","rel":"realizes","to":"obs"},{"from":"alert","rel":"realizes","to":"obs"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"subj","kind":"class","members":["- listeners: List","+ subscribe(l): Runnable","+ set(price)"],"name":"Ticker","role":"Subject","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"obs","kind":"interface","members":["+ onChange(sym, old, new)"],"name":"PriceListener","role":"Observer","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"log","kind":"class","members":["+ onChange(...)"],"name":"HistoryLog","role":"ConcreteObserver","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"alert","kind":"class","members":["+ onChange(...)"],"name":"AlertRule","role":"ConcreteObserver","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"obs","text":"a lambda also implements it"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"a","label":"subscribe(this)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"b","label":"subscribe(this)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"c","label":"set(120)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"onChange(...)","style":"call","to":"a"},{"from":"s","label":"onChange(...)","style":"call","to":"b"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Client"},{"id":"s","name":"Ticker","role":"Subject"},{"id":"a","name":"HistoryLog","role":"Observer"},{"id":"b","name":"AlertRule","role":"Observer"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A change in one object must update several others you cannot enumerate.","Dependents come and go at runtime.","The subject should stay ignorant of what its listeners do."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["There is a single, fixed dependent: call it directly.","Order or atomicity across reactions matters; hidden chains are hard to reason about."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Lapsed listeners: forgotten subscriptions keep objects alive and leak memory.","A slow or throwing observer blocks or breaks notification for the rest.","Observers that mutate the subject cause re-entrant cascades and loops.","Notification order is unspecified; do not depend on it."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`java.util.Observable` and `Observer` are deprecated since Java 9; use your own interface or `java.beans.PropertyChangeListener`.","`java.util.concurrent.Flow` and reactive libraries add back-pressure to the same idea."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Observer is one-to-many broadcast with no coordination logic. A mediator knows its participants and holds the rules of who talks to whom. Mediators are often built on observers.","ref":"mediator","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A chain gives a request to one handler that may claim it. Observer informs all subscribers, and each reacts independently.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A domain event is a notification expressing something that happened in the business model, often published to many handlers inside or across boundaries.","ref":"domain-event","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"An iterator is pulled by the client. An observer is pushed to by the subject when something happens.","ref":"iterator","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Notifications can carry commands or events as payloads, which observers queue, log or replay.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Beans fire `PropertyChangeEvent`s to registered `PropertyChangeListener`s.","name":"java.beans.PropertyChangeSupport","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`Publisher` and `Subscriber` interfaces define asynchronous observation with back-pressure.","name":"java.util.concurrent.Flow","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Swing and AWT components notify registered listeners via `addActionListener`.","name":"java.awt.event.ActionListener","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Beans receive `ApplicationEvent`s published through `ApplicationEventPublisher`, including `@EventListener` methods.","name":"ApplicationListener","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Clients and controllers subscribe to resource changes and receive an event stream instead of polling.","name":"Watch API","where":"Kubernetes"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.function.Consumer;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Thermostat {","    private final List<Consumer<Double>> hooks = new ArrayList<>();","    private double celsius;","","    void onChange(Consumer<Double> hook) { hooks.add(hook); }","","    void set(double c) {","        celsius = c;","        for (var h : hooks) h.accept(c);","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"observer","why":"Callers register callbacks, and a state change pushes to all of them without the source knowing them."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"No central object coordinates multiple peers or holds interaction rules; it only broadcasts."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The callbacks are not alternatives for one algorithm; all of them run on every change."},{"ref":"command","why":"Nothing is queued or undone; callbacks are notified of a change as it happens."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"observer","why":"A one-to-many notification with subscribers added independently; the publisher stays unchanged."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"The consumers do not need to talk to each other; a mediator would centralize rules nobody asked for."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"Every consumer must react, not just the first one that handles the request."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"A fixed skeleton with inherited steps would force the order service to name every consumer."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"An order service must tell inventory, billing emails and analytics when an order is placed. New consumers appear every sprint, and the order service team does not want to change code or know about them."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Removing from the list while iterating throws `ConcurrentModificationException` and also silently drops observers after one notification. Keep subscriptions until cancelled; use `CopyOnWriteArrayList` if observers may unsubscribe during a callback.","flawLines":[11],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Stock {","    private final List<Runnable> listeners = new ArrayList<>();","    private int level;","","    void subscribe(Runnable r) { listeners.add(r); }","","    void set(int level) {","        this.level = level;","        for (Runnable r : listeners) {","            if (r != null) r.run();","            listeners.remove(r);","        }","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Observer pattern."},{"blocks":[["Runnable cancel = ticker.subscribe(listener);"],["ticker.set(105);"],["cancel.run();"],["ticker.set(90);"]],"compile":{"template":["interface PriceListener { void onChange(String s, int o, int n); }","final class Ticker { Runnable subscribe(PriceListener l) { return () -> {}; } void set(int p) {} }","class Snippet { void run(Ticker ticker, PriceListener listener) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A subscription must exist before it can receive events or be cancelled. Changes made after cancelling no longer reach the listener, so the last set comes last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lifecycle of a subscription."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Subject knows only the observer interface","Observers subscribe and unsubscribe at runtime","Push-based, one-to-many","Lapsed listeners leak memory","Mediator coordinates; Observer only notifies"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A subject keeps a list of observers behind a small interface and notifies them on change. The subject does not know what they do, and observers subscribe and unsubscribe at runtime. The classic risk is the lapsed listener: forgotten subscriptions leak memory, and slow or throwing observers affect others.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Observer in your own words: who knows whom, how observers come and go, and the biggest runtime risk."}],"id":"observer"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let an object change its behavior when its internal state changes, by delegating each event to a state object.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **context** holds a reference to its current **state**. Every event is forwarded to that object, which either handles it, rejects it, or returns the next state. The legal transitions live in the states, so the context carries no `switch` on a status field.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** behavior per lifecycle phase, and which events are legal.","**Stays stable:** the context's public API.","**Chosen by:** the machine itself, as events arrive."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Per-state data (a receipt, a tracking id) belongs in the state object. Fields that are meaningful in only some phases stop being nullable.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Paid[receipt=R-1]\nShipped[tracking=TRK-9]\ncancel not allowed in Shipped[tracking=TRK-9]","file":"State.java","regions":{"client":[36,45],"concrete":[11,23],"context":[25,32],"state":[1,9]},"role":"implementation","source":"sealed interface State permits Draft, Paid, Shipped, Cancelled {\n    default State pay(String receipt) { throw reject(\"pay\"); }\n    default State ship(String tracking) { throw reject(\"ship\"); }\n    default State cancel() { throw reject(\"cancel\"); }\n\n    private IllegalStateException reject(String event) {\n        return new IllegalStateException(event + \" not allowed in \" + this);\n    }\n}\n\nrecord Draft() implements State {\n    public State pay(String receipt) { return new Paid(receipt); }\n    public State cancel() { return new Cancelled(); }\n}\n\nrecord Paid(String receipt) implements State {\n    public State ship(String tracking) { return new Shipped(tracking); }\n    public State cancel() { return new Cancelled(); }\n}\n\nrecord Shipped(String tracking) implements State {}\n\nrecord Cancelled() implements State {}\n\nfinal class Order {\n    private State state = new Draft();\n\n    void pay(String receipt) { state = state.pay(receipt); }\n    void ship(String tracking) { state = state.ship(tracking); }\n    void cancel() { state = state.cancel(); }\n    State state() { return state; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var order = new Order();\n        order.pay(\"R-1\");\n        System.out.println(order.state());\n        order.ship(\"TRK-9\");\n        System.out.println(order.state());\n        try {\n            order.cancel();\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A sealed interface with a default for every event: reject. Each state overrides only the events it allows, so an illegal transition is the default, not an omission.","region":"state"},{"note":"Concrete states are records. Each returns the successor state, and `Paid` and `Shipped` carry data that exists only in that phase.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"The context only forwards the event and stores the result: `state = state.pay(...)`. No status enum, no `switch`.","region":"context"},{"note":"The client sees one `Order`. `cancel()` after shipping fails because `Shipped` never overrode it, with no check in `Order`.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Subscription billing lifecycle in a SaaS product.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A subscription is trialing, active, past due, suspended or cancelled. Each phase answers the same events differently: a failed charge during trial does nothing, in active it starts a grace period, in past due it suspends the account. Scattered `if (status == ...)` checks drifted apart across services. One state object per phase now owns its rules, and the transition table is readable in one place.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["sealed interface Sub permits Trialing, Active, PastDue, Suspended {","    default Sub chargeFailed(Clock c) { return this; }","}","record Active(Plan plan) implements Sub {","    public Sub chargeFailed(Clock c) { return new PastDue(plan, c.now()); }","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: adding a Paused phase touches that class and its neighbors' successors. The sealed hierarchy makes the compiler list every switch you must revisit.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"order","label":"state","rel":"aggregation","to":"state"},{"from":"draft","rel":"realizes","to":"state"},{"from":"paid","rel":"realizes","to":"state"},{"from":"draft","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"paid"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"order","kind":"class","members":["- state: State","+ pay(receipt)","+ cancel()"],"name":"Order","role":"Context","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"state","kind":"interface","members":["+ pay(receipt): State","+ cancel(): State"],"name":"State","role":"State","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"draft","kind":"record","members":["+ pay(receipt)"],"name":"Draft","role":"ConcreteState","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"paid","kind":"record","members":["+ ship(tracking)"],"name":"Paid","role":"ConcreteState","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"state","text":"returns the next state"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"pay(\"R-1\")","style":"call","to":"order"},{"from":"order","label":"pay(\"R-1\")","style":"call","to":"draft"},{"from":"draft","label":"new Paid","style":"create","to":"paid"},{"from":"draft","label":"Paid","style":"return","to":"order"},{"from":"client","label":"ship(\"TRK-9\")","style":"call","to":"order"},{"from":"order","label":"ship(\"TRK-9\")","style":"call","to":"paid"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"order","name":"Order","role":"Context"},{"id":"draft","name":"Draft","role":"State"},{"id":"paid","name":"Paid","role":"State"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Behavior depends on a lifecycle phase and many methods branch on a status field.","Some data or operations are valid in only some phases.","Illegal transitions must fail loudly and in one place."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Two or three states with trivial rules: an enum and a `switch` is clearer.","Transitions are configuration, edited by non-programmers: use a table-driven machine."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The context bypassing the state to set the next one, which voids the guards.","Mutable state objects shared by several contexts.","Transitions scattered over classes until nobody can draw the machine."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A class per state where each differs by one constant.","States that all delegate back to the context for every decision."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed interfaces and records make states exhaustive, immutable and data-carrying.","For a pure function of (state, event), a `switch` over sealed types can replace the objects."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Same shape, different intent. A strategy is chosen from outside and independent of its siblings. A state replaces itself in response to events and names its successors.","ref":"strategy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"States without data can be shared singletons, so one flyweight instance serves every context in that phase.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Observers are how outsiders learn that the machine moved, without the states knowing who is listening.","ref":"observer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Events are often modeled as commands queued to the context, which also gives an audit trail and replay.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`StateMachine<S, E>` configures states and transitions declaratively, with guards and actions per transition.","name":"Spring Statemachine","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Generic over a state type: `state()` and `checkpoint(S)` select which part of a frame the decoder parses next.","name":"ReplayingDecoder","where":"Netty"},{"detail":"State machines declared in Amazon States Language: each state decides what runs and which state follows.","name":"Step Functions","where":"AWS"},{"detail":"A pod moves through `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded` or `Failed`; the kubelet reacts differently in each phase.","name":"Pod phase","where":"Kubernetes"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Latch { Latch unlock(String key); Latch push(); Latch close(); }","class Locked implements Latch {","  public Latch unlock(String key) { return this; }","  public Latch push() { return this; }","  public Latch close() { return this; } }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Door {","    private Latch latch = new Locked();","","    void unlock(String key) { latch = latch.unlock(key); }","    void push()             { latch = latch.push(); }","    void close()            { latch = latch.close(); }","}","// Locked, Closed and Open each implement Latch","// and return the Latch that follows the event"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"state","why":"The context forwards each event to its current object, which returns the successor the context stores."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A strategy is injected from outside and never replaces itself; here each latch picks the next one."},{"ref":"memento","why":"Nothing captures a snapshot for later restore; the objects drive live behavior."},{"ref":"command","why":"The events are not objects that can be queued or undone; they are plain method calls."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"state","why":"Behavior and legal events depend on the phase, and each phase owns its data and its successors."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"There is no algorithm chosen from outside; the upload moves itself from phase to phase."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"A fixed skeleton with swappable steps does not model one object changing which events it accepts."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"Requests are not offered to a sequence of handlers; one object must remember where it is."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A video upload goes through uploading, transcoding, published and failed. A retry is allowed only after failure, a delete only after publish, and each phase stores different data: a temp path, a job id, a CDN url."},{"compile":{"template":["interface State {","  default State pay(String r) { return this; }","  default State ship(String t) { return this; }","  default State cancel() { return this; } }","record Draft() implements State {}","record Cancelled() implements State {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The context sets the next state itself, so a shipped order can be cancelled and the states' guards are bypassed. Forward the event: `state = state.cancel();`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Order {","    private State state = new Draft();","","    void pay(String receipt) { state = state.pay(receipt); }","","    void ship(String tracking) { state = state.ship(tracking); }","","    void cancel() { state = new Cancelled(); }","","    State state() { return state; }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the State pattern."},{"blocks":[["var order = new Order();"],["order.pay(\"R-1\");"],["order.ship(\"TRK-9\");"],["System.out.println(order.state());"]],"compile":{"template":["final class Order {","  private String s = \"DRAFT\";","  void pay(String r) { if (!s.equals(\"DRAFT\")) throw new IllegalStateException(); s = \"PAID\"; }","  void ship(String t) { if (!s.equals(\"PAID\")) throw new IllegalStateException(); s = \"SHIPPED\"; }","  String state() { return s; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A new order starts in Draft. Only Draft accepts pay, only Paid accepts ship, and printing reads the state those calls produced.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the calls so that no event is rejected by the current state."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One object per phase; the context delegates every event","The state returns or sets the next state, so transitions live in the states","Illegal events are rejected by default, not by checks in the context","Strategy is picked by the client and does not transition","Per-phase data lives in the state object"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"State moves the behavior that depends on a lifecycle phase into one object per phase. The context forwards each event to its current state, which handles it or returns the successor. Strategy has the same shape, but its strategies are chosen from outside and never replace themselves.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain State in your own words: who decides the next state, and how it differs from Strategy."}],"id":"state"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Define a family of interchangeable algorithms behind one interface, and let the caller choose one at runtime.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **context** holds a **strategy** and delegates one varying decision to it. The context never learns which concrete strategy it has, so a new algorithm means a new class or lambda, not an edit to the context.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** one algorithm or policy (pricing, sorting, retry, compression).","**Stays stable:** the code that uses it.","**Chosen by:** the client or configuration, usually once per use."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"In Java 8+ a one-method strategy is a functional interface, so a lambda or method reference is a complete strategy.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"NONE -> 50.00\nSPRING10 -> 45.00\nWELCOME -> 42.50","file":"Strategy.java","regions":{"client":[50,51],"concrete":[11,27],"context":[29,39],"lambda":[43,43],"select":[44,47],"strategy":[6,9]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.math.BigDecimal;\nimport java.math.RoundingMode;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface DiscountPolicy {\n    BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal subtotal);\n}\n\nrecord PercentOff(int percent) implements DiscountPolicy {\n    PercentOff {\n        if (percent < 0 || percent > 100) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"percent\");\n        }\n    }\n    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal subtotal) {\n        BigDecimal factor = BigDecimal.valueOf(100 - percent, 2);\n        return subtotal.multiply(factor).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n    }\n}\n\nrecord FlatOff(BigDecimal amount) implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal subtotal) {\n        return subtotal.subtract(amount).max(BigDecimal.ZERO);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Checkout {\n    private final DiscountPolicy policy;\n\n    Checkout(DiscountPolicy policy) { this.policy = policy; }\n\n    BigDecimal total(List<BigDecimal> prices) {\n        BigDecimal subtotal =\n            prices.stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);\n        return policy.apply(subtotal);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DiscountPolicy none = subtotal -> subtotal;\n        Map<String, DiscountPolicy> byCode = Map.of(\n            \"NONE\", none,\n            \"SPRING10\", new PercentOff(10),\n            \"WELCOME\", new FlatOff(new BigDecimal(\"7.50\")));\n        var prices = List.of(new BigDecimal(\"19.99\"), new BigDecimal(\"30.01\"));\n        for (String code : List.of(\"NONE\", \"SPRING10\", \"WELCOME\")) {\n            var checkout = new Checkout(byCode.get(code));\n            System.out.println(code + \" -> \" + checkout.total(prices));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The strategy interface: one method. `@FunctionalInterface` lets lambdas implement it and makes the compiler enforce that.","region":"strategy"},{"note":"Concrete strategies as records: immutable, validated in the compact constructor, each knowing only its own rule.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"The context takes a strategy in its constructor and delegates the one varying step. No `switch`, no `instanceof`.","region":"context"},{"note":"A lambda is a full strategy. Trivial rules need no class at all.","region":"lambda"},{"note":"Selection lives at the edge, in a map from promo code to policy. A new promo touches this map, never `Checkout`.","region":"select"},{"note":"The client pairs context with strategy and calls the context. The same `Checkout` prints three different totals.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Shipping quotes in an online shop.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each carrier prices parcels differently: weight tables, zones, surcharges. The customer picks a carrier at checkout, finance wants to A/B test a flat rate, and a new carrier arrives every quarter. Each rule becomes a `ShippingQuote` strategy with its own unit tests, and the checkout service stays unchanged.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface ShippingQuote { Money quote(Parcel p, Address to); }","","ShippingQuote dhl  = new DhlTableRate(rates);","ShippingQuote flat = (p, to) -> Money.eur(\"4.90\");","ShippingQuote chosen = flags.on(\"flat-shipping\", user) ? flat : dhl;","Money price = checkout.quoteWith(chosen, cart, address);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a new carrier is a new class plus one registry line. The flat-rate experiment is one lambda behind a flag.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"checkout","label":"policy","rel":"aggregation","to":"policy"},{"from":"pct","rel":"realizes","to":"policy"},{"from":"flat","rel":"realizes","to":"policy"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"checkout","kind":"class","members":["- policy: DiscountPolicy","+ total(prices): BigDecimal"],"name":"Checkout","role":"Context","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"policy","kind":"interface","members":["+ apply(subtotal): BigDecimal"],"name":"DiscountPolicy","role":"Strategy","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"pct","kind":"record","members":["+ apply(subtotal)"],"name":"PercentOff","role":"ConcreteStrategy","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"flat","kind":"record","members":["+ apply(subtotal)"],"name":"FlatOff","role":"ConcreteStrategy","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"policy","text":"a lambda also implements it"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"new Checkout(policy)","style":"create","to":"checkout"},{"from":"client","label":"total(prices)","style":"call","to":"checkout"},{"from":"checkout","label":"apply(subtotal)","style":"call","to":"policy"},{"from":"policy","label":"discounted","style":"return","to":"checkout"},{"from":"checkout","label":"total","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"checkout","name":"Checkout","role":"Context"},{"id":"policy","name":"PercentOff","role":"Strategy"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Several variants of one algorithm must be swappable at runtime.","A growing `switch` on a type or code picks behaviour.","You want each rule unit-tested in isolation."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["There are two variants that will never change: an `if` is clearer.","Variants need different inputs, so one interface cannot fit them all."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Context checking `instanceof` on its strategy, which defeats the pattern.","Clients must now know every strategy; hide the choice behind a registry or factory.","Stateful strategies shared across threads."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An interface plus classes for a rule that a lambda would cover.","A `StrategyFactoryProvider` with a single implementation."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Functional interfaces and lambdas replace most strategy class hierarchies.","For a closed set of data-driven rules, a sealed interface plus `switch` pattern matching can be simpler."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Same shape, different intent. With State the object switches its own state as events arrive and the states know each other. With Strategy the client chooses and strategies are independent.","ref":"state","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Template Method varies steps through inheritance, fixed at compile time. Strategy varies the whole algorithm through composition, at runtime.","ref":"template-method","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A command packages a request to run, queue or undo later. A strategy answers *how* to do one job right now.","ref":"command","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Decorator changes an object's behaviour by wrapping it from the outside; Strategy replaces its internals.","ref":"decorator","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A factory or registry often picks the strategy from configuration, and DI injects it.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Passed to `List.sort`, `TreeMap` and `Collections.max` to choose the ordering algorithm.","name":"java.util.Comparator","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`ThreadPoolExecutor` delegates overflow to a policy: `AbortPolicy`, `CallerRunsPolicy`, `DiscardPolicy`.","name":"RejectedExecutionHandler","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`BCryptPasswordEncoder` and `Argon2PasswordEncoder` are interchangeable; `DelegatingPasswordEncoder` picks one per stored hash.","name":"PasswordEncoder","where":"Spring Security"},{"detail":"`SNAKE_CASE` and `KEBAB_CASE` change how property names map to JSON.","name":"PropertyNamingStrategies","where":"Jackson"},{"detail":"`RollingUpdate` or `Recreate`: the same controller with a different rollout algorithm.","name":"Deployment spec.strategy","where":"Kubernetes"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.nio.file.*;","interface Codec { String extension(); byte[] encode(byte[] raw); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class BackupJob {","    private final Codec codec;","    BackupJob(Codec codec) { this.codec = codec; }","","    void run(Path file) throws IOException {","        byte[] raw = Files.readAllBytes(file);","        Path out = file.resolveSibling(file.getFileName() + codec.extension());","        Files.write(out, codec.encode(raw));","    }","}","// GzipCodec, ZstdCodec, NoopCodec; chosen from the --codec flag"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"strategy","why":"The job delegates one varying algorithm (encoding) to an injected, interchangeable `Codec`."},{"ref":"state","why":"Nothing transitions: the codec is fixed for the job's life and never replaces itself."},{"ref":"bridge","why":"There is no second abstraction hierarchy being developed independently; it is one job with one pluggable step."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"There is no abstract superclass with overridable steps; the variation comes through composition."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"strategy","why":"One varying algorithm, selected from outside per request, with new variants added without touching the engine."},{"ref":"state","why":"There is no lifecycle and no transitions; the rule does not change itself as events arrive."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"A subclass per country fixes the choice at compile time and couples every rule to one base-class skeleton."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"The responsible rule is known up front from the address; there is nothing to pass along a chain."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A tax engine computes VAT differently per country. The rule is chosen at checkout from the shipping address, new countries are added monthly, and a rule never changes during one calculation."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.math.BigDecimal;","interface DiscountPolicy { BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal s); }","record PercentOff(int percent) implements DiscountPolicy { public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal s) { return s; } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The context inspects a concrete strategy, so every new policy can force an edit here. Put the cap in a decorating policy, e.g. `capped(policy, 50)`, or in the strategy itself.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Checkout {","    private final DiscountPolicy policy;","","    Checkout(DiscountPolicy policy) { this.policy = policy; }","","    BigDecimal total(BigDecimal subtotal) {","        if (policy instanceof PercentOff p && p.percent() > 50) {","            return subtotal; // block deep discounts","        }","        return policy.apply(subtotal);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Strategy pattern."},{"blocks":[["String code = request.promoCode();"],["DiscountPolicy policy = policies.getOrDefault(code, s -> s);"],["Checkout checkout = new Checkout(policy);"],["BigDecimal total = checkout.total(cart.prices());"],["receipt.print(total);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.math.BigDecimal;","import java.util.*;","interface DiscountPolicy { BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal s); }","final class Checkout { private final DiscountPolicy p; Checkout(DiscountPolicy p) { this.p = p; }","  BigDecimal total(List<BigDecimal> xs) { return p.apply(xs.stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)); } }","record Request(String promoCode) {}","record Cart(List<BigDecimal> prices) {}","interface Receipt { void print(BigDecimal total); }","class Snippet { void run(Request request, Map<String, DiscountPolicy> policies, Cart cart, Receipt receipt) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Read the input, resolve the strategy at the edge, inject it into the context, then delegate. The context never sees the promo code.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the request flow so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One varying algorithm behind one interface","Context delegates and never inspects the concrete type","The client or config chooses, often by injection","State changes itself and knows the transitions; Strategy is chosen from outside","In modern Java a lambda can be the strategy"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Strategy pulls one varying algorithm out of a class behind an interface. The context delegates to whatever strategy it was given, and the client or configuration chooses it, usually at construction. Unlike State, strategies don't know each other and the context doesn't swap them in response to its own events.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Strategy in your own words: what varies, who chooses, and how it differs from State."}],"id":"strategy"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Fix the skeleton of an algorithm in a base class and let subclasses fill in individual steps.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The base class owns the **template method**: a `final` method that calls steps in a fixed order. Subclasses override **abstract steps** they must supply and optional **hooks** they may refine. The base class calls the subclass, not the other way round, which is the Hollywood principle.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** individual steps, supplied by subclasses.","**Stays stable:** the order and the control flow.","**Chosen by:** inheritance, fixed at compile time."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Make the template `final`. If subclasses can override the skeleton, the invariant that justified the pattern is gone.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"open\nsaved 2\n[User[name=ana, age=31], User[name=bo, age=40]]","file":"TemplateMethod.java","regions":{"client":[42,45],"concrete":[28,38],"hooks":[21,24],"steps":[18,19],"template":[4,16]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nabstract class Importer<T> {\n    public final int run(List<String> lines) {\n        open();\n        int saved = 0;\n        for (String line : lines) {\n            T row = parse(line);\n            if (!accept(row)) continue;\n            store(row);\n            saved++;\n        }\n        close();\n        return saved;\n    }\n\n    protected abstract T parse(String line);\n    protected abstract void store(T row);\n\n    protected boolean accept(T row) { return true; }\n    protected void open() {}\n    protected void close() {}\n}\n\nrecord User(String name, int age) {}\n\nfinal class UserImporter extends Importer<User> {\n    final List<User> saved = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    @Override protected User parse(String line) {\n        String[] f = line.split(\",\");\n        return new User(f[0].trim(), Integer.parseInt(f[1].trim()));\n    }\n    @Override protected void store(User u) { saved.add(u); }\n    @Override protected boolean accept(User u) { return u.age() >= 18; }\n    @Override protected void open() { System.out.println(\"open\"); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var importer = new UserImporter();\n        int n = importer.run(List.of(\"ana, 31\", \"kid, 9\", \"bo, 40\"));\n        System.out.println(\"saved \" + n);\n        System.out.println(importer.saved);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`run` is `public final`: it fixes the sequence open, parse, filter, store, close. Subclasses cannot reorder it or skip a step.","region":"template"},{"note":"Abstract steps are the extension points a subclass must implement. They are `protected`, since only the template should call them.","region":"steps"},{"note":"Hooks have a default: `accept` keeps every row, `open` and `close` do nothing. A subclass overrides them only when it needs to.","region":"hooks"},{"note":"The subclass fills in the steps. `@Override` makes the compiler catch a misspelled hook that would otherwise silently never run.","region":"concrete"},{"note":"The client calls the template. The importer calls back into its own overrides, and the underage row is filtered by the hook.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Payment provider integrations behind one gateway.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Every provider needs the same envelope: validate the request, take an idempotency key, call the provider, map its error codes, write an audit record. Only the call and the error mapping differ. A `PaymentGateway.charge` template guarantees that no provider integration forgets the audit record or the idempotency check, and a new provider is a subclass of two methods.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["public final Receipt charge(Charge c) {","    validate(c);","    var key = idempotencyKey(c);","    var raw = call(c, key);          // abstract","    audit.record(c, raw);","    return map(raw);                 // abstract","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: cross-cutting rules are written once, in one `final` method, so a new provider cannot skip them.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"users","rel":"extends","to":"base"},{"from":"orders","rel":"extends","to":"base"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"base","kind":"abstract","members":["+ run(lines): int","# parse(line): T","# store(row)","# accept(row): boolean"],"name":"Importer<T>","role":"AbstractClass","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"users","kind":"class","members":["# parse(line)","# store(row)","# accept(row)"],"name":"UserImporter","role":"ConcreteClass","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"orders","kind":"class","members":["# parse(line)","# store(row)"],"name":"OrderImporter","role":"ConcreteClass","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"base","text":"run() is final"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"run(lines)","style":"call","to":"imp"},{"from":"imp","label":"open()","style":"call","to":"imp"},{"from":"imp","label":"parse(line)","style":"call","to":"imp"},{"from":"imp","label":"accept(row)","style":"call","to":"imp"},{"from":"imp","label":"store(row)","style":"call","to":"imp"},{"from":"imp","label":"saved","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"imp","name":"UserImporter","role":"ConcreteClass"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Several classes share an algorithm and differ in a few steps.","An order or invariant must hold for every variant, such as audit or cleanup.","A framework needs defined extension points."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Variants must be chosen or swapped at runtime.","Behavior varies along several independent axes: subclass counts multiply."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Calling an overridable step from the base constructor: the subclass fields are not initialized yet.","Subclasses violating the contract of a step, which breaks substitutability.","Too many hooks, so nobody can state what the template guarantees."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A deep hierarchy built only to reuse the skeleton.","Hooks that every subclass overrides identically."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Passing steps as lambdas to a method or constructor is Strategy, and often simpler.","Interface `default` methods can host a template without a base class."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Template Method varies steps through inheritance, fixed at compile time. Strategy varies the whole algorithm through composition and can be swapped at runtime.","ref":"strategy","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A factory method is a template step whose job is to create an object. Template Method is the broader skeleton that may contain it.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Template Method is the textbook inheritance-based pattern. Prefer composition once the skeleton needs to change at runtime or subclasses multiply.","ref":"composition-over-inheritance","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Overridden steps and hooks must honor the base class's contract, or the template's guarantees silently break.","ref":"lsp","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`service` dispatches to `doGet`, `doPost` and the other hooks that you override.","name":"HttpServlet","where":"Jakarta Servlet"},{"detail":"`read(byte[], int, int)` is implemented in terms of the single abstract `read()`.","name":"java.io.InputStream","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`indexOf`, `iterator` and others are built on the abstract `get(int)` and `size()`.","name":"java.util.AbstractList","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`doFilter` guarantees once-per-request execution and delegates to the abstract `doFilterInternal`.","name":"OncePerRequestFilter","where":"Spring"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["abstract class Report {","    public final String render() {","        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(header());","        for (String row : rows()) sb.append(row).append('\\n');","        return sb.append(footer()).toString();","    }","    protected abstract String header();","    protected abstract List<String> rows();","    protected String footer() { return \"\"; }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"template-method","why":"A final method fixes the sequence; abstract steps and an overridable default hook are filled in by subclasses."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"No collaborator is injected; variation arrives through subclassing, not composition."},{"ref":"builder","why":"The steps do not accumulate configuration for a product; they render once in a fixed order."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Nothing wraps another `Report` instance with the same interface."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"template-method","why":"A fixed lifecycle that must hold for every job, with one varying step, chosen at compile time."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"It could work, but nothing then forces each job to keep the shared lifecycle, and no runtime swapping is needed."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Jobs do not broadcast changes to dependents; they need a guaranteed sequence."},{"ref":"visitor","why":"There is no class hierarchy of elements needing new operations."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Five batch jobs each read a file, transform rows and write results. All must log start and end, open and close the same resources, and fail the same way. Only the transform differs, and the job type is known at deploy time."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The template is overridable, so a subclass can drop the `finally` and leak the resource. Declare it `public final void run()`.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["abstract class Job {","    public void run() {","        open();","        try {","            work();","        } finally {","            close();","        }","    }","    protected abstract void work();","    protected void open() {}","    protected void close() {}","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Template Method pattern."},{"blocks":[["open();"],["T row = parse(line);"],["if (!accept(row)) continue;"],["store(row);"],["close();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","abstract class Importer<T> {","  abstract T parse(String s); abstract void store(T t); boolean accept(T t) { return true; }","  void open() {} void close() {}","  void run(List<String> lines) {","    open();","    for (String line : lines) {","{{code}}","    }","  } }"]},"explanation":"Resources open before use, a row must be parsed before it is filtered, and only accepted rows are stored. Closing comes after the loop work.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the template so that each step has what it needs."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Final template method fixes the order and invariants","Subclasses supply abstract steps and optional hooks","The base class calls subclass code (Hollywood principle)","Compile-time inheritance; Strategy is runtime composition","Never call overridable steps from a constructor"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A base class defines a final method with the algorithm's fixed order and calls abstract steps and optional hooks that subclasses implement. The base calls the subclass. It is inheritance-based and decided at compile time; Strategy gives the same variation through composition and can change at runtime.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Template Method in your own words: what is fixed, what varies, and why prefer Strategy sometimes."}],"id":"template-method"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Add new operations to a class hierarchy without changing its classes, by moving each operation into a separate visitor.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each element has one `accept(visitor)` method that calls back the visitor method for its own type. That is **double dispatch**: the operation depends on both the visitor and the element's runtime class, which Java's single dispatch cannot do on its own.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Varies:** the operation (evaluate, print, type-check).","**Stays stable:** the set of element types.","**Cost:** adding an element type forces every visitor to change."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Visitor makes new operations cheap and new element types expensive. Use it only when the hierarchy is stable and the operations keep growing.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"(2 + 3) * 4 = 20","file":"Visitor.java","regions":{"client":[46,47],"elements":[7,21],"eval":[23,31],"show":[33,42],"visitor":[1,5]},"role":"implementation","source":"interface Visitor<R> {\n    R visitNum(Num n);\n    R visitAdd(Add a);\n    R visitMul(Mul m);\n}\n\nsealed interface Expr permits Num, Add, Mul {\n    <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v);\n}\n\nrecord Num(int value) implements Expr {\n    public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitNum(this); }\n}\n\nrecord Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {\n    public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitAdd(this); }\n}\n\nrecord Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {\n    public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitMul(this); }\n}\n\nfinal class Eval implements Visitor<Integer> {\n    public Integer visitNum(Num n) { return n.value(); }\n    public Integer visitAdd(Add a) {\n        return a.left().accept(this) + a.right().accept(this);\n    }\n    public Integer visitMul(Mul m) {\n        return m.left().accept(this) * m.right().accept(this);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Show implements Visitor<String> {\n    public String visitNum(Num n) { return String.valueOf(n.value()); }\n    public String visitAdd(Add a) {\n        return \"(\" + a.left().accept(this) + \" + \"\n            + a.right().accept(this) + \")\";\n    }\n    public String visitMul(Mul m) {\n        return m.left().accept(this) + \" * \" + m.right().accept(this);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Expr e = new Mul(new Add(new Num(2), new Num(3)), new Num(4));\n        System.out.println(e.accept(new Show()) + \" = \" + e.accept(new Eval()));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"One `visit` method per element type, generic in the result `R`. This interface is the complete list of element types the operations must handle.","region":"visitor"},{"note":"Each element's `accept` calls the method for its own type, passing `this`. The static type of `this` is exact there, so the right overload runs.","region":"elements"},{"note":"An operation is one class. Recursion goes through `accept(this)`, never `instanceof`, so every node dispatches on its real type.","region":"eval"},{"note":"A second operation is added without touching `Expr`, `Num`, `Add` or `Mul`. Each visitor chooses its own result type.","region":"show"},{"note":"The client builds one tree and runs two unrelated operations over it. The sealed `Expr` lets the compiler know the closed set.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Static analysis passes over a compiler's syntax tree.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A compiler has maybe 40 stable node types, but its passes keep growing: name resolution, type checking, constant folding, lint rules, code generation, each maintained by a different team. Putting every pass inside the node classes makes the nodes unreadable. One visitor per pass keeps each algorithm in one file, and a new lint rule is a new class with no merge conflicts in the tree definition.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface NodeVisitor<R> {","    R visitCall(CallNode n);","    R visitLiteral(LiteralNode n);","    R visitAssign(AssignNode n);","}","unit.accept(new UnusedVariableLint(reporter));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: each pass is a self-contained class, tested alone, and the node classes stay small.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"eval","rel":"realizes","to":"visitor"},{"from":"num","rel":"realizes","to":"expr"},{"from":"add","rel":"realizes","to":"expr"},{"from":"expr","label":"accept","rel":"dependency","to":"visitor"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"visitor","kind":"interface","members":["+ visitNum(Num): R","+ visitAdd(Add): R"],"name":"Visitor<R>","role":"Visitor","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"eval","kind":"class","members":["+ visitNum(Num)","+ visitAdd(Add)"],"name":"Eval","role":"ConcreteVisitor","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"expr","kind":"interface","members":["+ accept(Visitor<R>): R"],"name":"Expr","role":"Element","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"num","kind":"record","members":["+ accept(v)"],"name":"Num","role":"ConcreteElement","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"add","kind":"record","members":["+ accept(v)"],"name":"Add","role":"ConcreteElement","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"accept(eval)","style":"call","to":"add"},{"from":"add","label":"visitAdd(this)","style":"call","to":"eval"},{"from":"eval","label":"left.accept(this)","style":"call","to":"num"},{"from":"num","label":"visitNum(this)","style":"call","to":"eval"},{"from":"eval","label":"result","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"add","name":"Add","role":"Element"},{"id":"eval","name":"Eval","role":"Visitor"},{"id":"num","name":"Num","role":"Element"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The element types are stable but operations keep being added.","An operation needs per-type logic across many unrelated classes.","You want an operation's code in one place instead of spread over the hierarchy."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["New element types appear often: every visitor must change.","The hierarchy is small; a sealed type with a `switch` is lighter."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Visitors need access to element internals, which pressures encapsulation.","Recursion with `instanceof` instead of `accept` silently skips dispatch.","Traversal logic duplicated in every visitor; centralize it or use a base visitor."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A visitor for a hierarchy with two subtypes.","Visitors that only ever return `void` and mutate shared state."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed interfaces with `switch` pattern matching give the same open-operations axis with no `accept` boilerplate and exhaustiveness checks.","Classic Visitor still wins across module boundaries, where the library owns the hierarchy."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Composite builds the tree; Visitor adds operations over it. The two are used together so often that a visitor usually owns the traversal.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An iterator hands you elements one by one of a uniform type. A visitor handles each concrete type differently and can return results while traversing.","ref":"iterator","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Interpreter puts the operation inside each node, so adding an operation edits every class. Visitor externalizes it, trading that for the cost of adding node types.","ref":"interpreter","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Visitor is open for new operations and closed for new element types. It applies the open-closed principle along one axis only.","ref":"ocp","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Passed to `Files.walkFileTree`; `SimpleFileVisitor` supplies defaults for `visitFile`, `preVisitDirectory` and the rest.","name":"java.nio.file.FileVisitor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Annotation processors visit the program model with it; `SimpleElementVisitor14` is a ready base class.","name":"javax.lang.model.element.ElementVisitor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Reads and rewrites bytecode through `visitMethod`, `visitField` and related callbacks.","name":"ClassVisitor","where":"ASM"},{"detail":"Generated visitors give one `visitRule` method per grammar rule, to walk the parse tree.","name":"ParseTreeVisitor","where":"ANTLR"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface Shape { <R> R apply(Op<R> op); }","interface Op<R> { R onCircle(Circle c); R onRect(Rect r); }","","record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {","    public <R> R apply(Op<R> op) { return op.onCircle(this); }","}","record Rect(double w, double h) implements Shape {","    public <R> R apply(Op<R> op) { return op.onRect(this); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"visitor","why":"Each shape calls back the method for its own type, so an operation is chosen by both the op and the shape's class."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A strategy is a single interchangeable algorithm; here the interface has one method per element type."},{"ref":"state","why":"Nothing changes which object handles later calls; the shapes are immutable data."},{"ref":"composite","why":"There are no children or recursive structure, only a double-dispatch hook."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"visitor","why":"A stable hierarchy with a growing set of operations: each new operation is one class and no node changes."},{"ref":"interpreter","why":"Each operation would be a method added to all 12 node classes, owned by the model team."},{"ref":"template-method","why":"It varies steps of one algorithm; these are unrelated operations over the same structure."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A strategy swaps one algorithm and cannot dispatch on the type of each node it meets."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A document model has 12 stable node types. Over the next year, different teams will add spell-check, HTML export, PDF export, accessibility audit and word count. Nobody expects new node types."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Visitor<R> { R visitNum(Num n); R visitAdd(Add a); R visitMul(Mul m); }","interface Expr { <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v); }","record Num(int value) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitNum(this); } }","record Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitAdd(this); } }","record Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitMul(this); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The left child is inspected with `instanceof`, so a nested `Add` or `Mul` evaluates to 0. Recurse through `a.left().accept(this)` so every node dispatches on its real type.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Eval implements Visitor<Integer> {","    public Integer visitNum(Num n) { return n.value(); }","","    public Integer visitAdd(Add a) {","        int l = a.left() instanceof Num n ? n.value() : 0;","        int r = a.right().accept(this);","        return l + r;","    }","","    public Integer visitMul(Mul m) {","        return m.left().accept(this) * m.right().accept(this);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Visitor pattern."},{"blocks":[["Expr two = new Num(2);"],["Expr sum = new Add(two, new Num(3));"],["Expr product = new Mul(sum, new Num(4));"],["int value = product.accept(new Eval());"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Visitor<R> { R visitNum(Num n); R visitAdd(Add a); R visitMul(Mul m); }","interface Expr { <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v); }","record Num(int value) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitNum(this); } }","record Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitAdd(this); } }","record Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr { public <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v) { return v.visitMul(this); } }","final class Eval implements Visitor<Integer> {","  public Integer visitNum(Num n) { return n.value(); }","  public Integer visitAdd(Add a) { return a.left().accept(this) + a.right().accept(this); }","  public Integer visitMul(Mul m) { return m.left().accept(this) * m.right().accept(this); } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Children must exist before the nodes that hold them, and the operation runs only on the finished root.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so that each uses only what exists."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["accept calls back visitX(this) for the element's own type","Double dispatch: runtime types of both visitor and element decide","New operations are one new class; no element changes","New element types break every existing visitor","Sealed types and switch patterns are the modern alternative"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Visitor moves operations out of a hierarchy into visitor classes. Each element's accept calls the visit method for its own type, so the code that runs depends on both the visitor and the element class: double dispatch. New operations are cheap; new element types force every visitor to change.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Visitor in your own words: what is double dispatch, and what does the pattern cost?"}],"id":"visitor"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Share state safely between threads by never changing it: every update produces a new object.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An **immutable object** has a state fixed at construction. With no writes to race on, it needs no locks and can be shared freely. Java backs this with a memory-model rule: after the constructor finishes, a thread that sees the reference also sees the values of its **`final` fields**, even if the reference was published through a data race.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Requires:** `final` fields, no setters, a `final` class or private constructor.","**Deep immutability:** fields must reference immutable objects, or be copied defensively.","**Update:** return a new instance (a *wither*)."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The `final` guarantee holds only if `this` does not escape during construction, and only for state reachable through final fields.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Route[name=R1, stops=[A, B]]\n[A, B, S0]\n[A, B, S1]\n[A, B, S2]\n[X]","file":"ImmutableObject.java","regions":{"client":[28,33],"copy":[5,7],"shared":[17,17],"threads":[20,27],"wither":[9,13]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nrecord Route(String name, List<String> stops) {\n    Route {\n        stops = List.copyOf(stops);\n    }\n\n    Route withStop(String stop) {\n        var next = new ArrayList<>(stops);\n        next.add(stop);\n        return new Route(name, next);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static final Route BASE = new Route(\"R1\", List.of(\"A\", \"B\"));\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        var results = new Route[3];\n        var threads = new ArrayList<Thread>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {\n            int id = i;\n            threads.add(Thread.ofVirtual().start(\n                () -> results[id] = BASE.withStop(\"S\" + id)));\n        }\n        for (Thread t : threads) t.join();\n        System.out.println(BASE);\n        for (Route r : results) System.out.println(r.stops());\n        var src = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"X\"));\n        var copy = new Route(\"tmp\", src);\n        src.add(\"Y\");\n        System.out.println(copy.stops());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A record's fields are `final`. The compact constructor replaces the incoming list with an unmodifiable copy, so later changes by the caller cannot leak in.","region":"copy"},{"note":"An update never mutates `this`: it builds a modified copy and returns a new `Route`. The old value stays valid for anyone still holding it.","region":"wither"},{"note":"One instance is shared by every thread through a `static final` field. Class initialization safely publishes it.","region":"shared"},{"note":"Three virtual threads derive new routes from the same base with no lock. Each `join` gives a happens-before edge, so reading `results` afterwards is safe.","region":"threads"},{"note":"The base is unchanged, and mutating the original source list after construction does not affect the copy.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A hot-reloadable configuration read by every request.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A service reads feature flags and rate limits on every request, and an admin endpoint reloads them from a file. Guarding a mutable map with a lock puts every request on one monitor. Instead the config is an immutable `Settings` record, and the reload builds a complete new one and swaps a single `volatile` reference. A request sees either the old or the new snapshot, never a half-updated mix.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["private volatile Settings current = Settings.defaults();","","void reload(Path file) {","    current = Settings.parse(file);   // new snapshot","}","boolean allowed(Request r) {","    return current.limits().allow(r);  // one read","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: readers take no lock, see a consistent snapshot, and a failed parse leaves the old settings in place.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"route","label":"stops","rel":"composition","to":"list"},{"from":"route","label":"withStop","rel":"dependency","to":"next"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"route","kind":"record","members":["- name: String (final)","- stops: List (final)","+ withStop(s): Route"],"name":"Route","role":"ImmutableObject","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"list","kind":"interface","members":["copied by List.copyOf"],"name":"List<String>","role":"ImmutableState","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"next","kind":"record","members":["new instance"],"name":"Route'","role":"New version","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"route","text":"no setters, never mutated"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["State is read by many threads and rarely changes.","Values are map keys, cached, or passed between components.","You want an object that is correct without synchronization."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Large objects change often: each update copies everything.","You need in-place identity-based updates, such as counters on a hot path."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Shallow immutability: a `final` field holding an `ArrayList` is still mutable.","Letting `this` escape from the constructor voids the final-field guarantee.","Returning internal arrays or collections instead of copies or unmodifiable views."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Copying a large collection on every single-element update, when a persistent structure would do.","Immutable wrapper around a mutable service object."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records give shallow immutability for free; copy collections in the compact constructor.","`List.of`, `Map.of` and `List.copyOf` are immutable; `Collections.unmodifiableList` is only a view."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A builder is mutable scratch space that ends in an immutable product. It is the usual way to construct objects with many fields.","ref":"builder","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Flyweights are shared across clients, which is only safe when the shared part is immutable.","ref":"flyweight","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A read-write lock lets many readers share a mutable object. An immutable snapshot removes the lock, at the cost of copying on write.","ref":"read-write-lock","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A memento is an immutable snapshot of another object's state, kept so that state can be restored.","ref":"memento","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Immutable, so safe to share, cache its hash code, and use as a map key.","name":"java.lang.String","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`LocalDate`, `Instant` and `Duration` are immutable and thread-safe; `plusDays` returns a new instance.","name":"java.time","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Return unmodifiable lists; mutators throw `UnsupportedOperationException`.","name":"List.of / List.copyOf","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Every arithmetic method returns a new `BigDecimal`.","name":"java.math.BigDecimal","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Immutable collections with builders, rejecting nulls.","name":"ImmutableList","where":"Guava"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Range {","    private final int lo;","    private final int hi;","","    Range(int lo, int hi) {","        if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException();","        this.lo = lo;","        this.hi = hi;","    }","    Range shift(int by) { return new Range(lo + by, hi + by); }","    int lo() { return lo; }","    int hi() { return hi; }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"immutable-object","why":"Final fields, no setters, and shift returns a new instance rather than changing this one."},{"ref":"prototype","why":"Nothing clones a template to make configured variants; shift derives a value, not a copy of a prototype."},{"ref":"builder","why":"There is no step-by-step assembly or separate mutable builder."},{"ref":"flyweight","why":"Instances are not pooled or shared by key, though immutability would permit it."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"immutable-object","why":"Build a new immutable table and swap one volatile reference: no locks for readers and no torn state."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"It works, but every read still takes the lock and the table must be mutated in place."},{"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"Nobody needs to wait for a condition; readers should proceed immediately."},{"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"It is for lazy one-time initialization, not for repeated replacement of shared data."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A pricing table is read by hundreds of request threads on every call and replaced wholesale a few times a day by a reload job. Readers must never see a half-updated table."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The constructor keeps the caller's list, so the caller can still mutate it. The field is only shallowly final. Copy it: `this.names = List.copyOf(names);`.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Roster {","    private final List<String> names;","","    Roster(List<String> names) {","        this.names = names;","    }","","    Roster with(String name) {","        var next = new ArrayList<>(names);","        next.add(name);","        return new Roster(next);","    }","","    List<String> names() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(names); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Immutable Object pattern."},{"blocks":[["var next = new ArrayList<>(stops);"],["next.add(stop);"],["Route moved = new Route(name, next);"],["return moved;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Route(String name, List<String> stops) {","  Route { stops = List.copyOf(stops); }","  Route withStop(String stop) {","{{code}}","  } }"]},"explanation":"Copy first so the original is untouched, modify the copy, wrap it in a new instance, then return it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of the wither so that it never changes this."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["No writes after construction means no races","final fields are safely visible once the constructor completes","Final reference to a mutable object is only shallow immutability","Copy defensively; do not leak this from the constructor","Updates return new instances, often swapped via a volatile reference"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"With no writes after construction there is nothing to race on. The memory model guarantees that any thread seeing the reference sees the final fields as set by the constructor. That needs deep immutability: copy mutable inputs and never let this escape during construction.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain why immutable objects are thread-safe, and what is needed beyond declaring fields final."}],"id":"immutable-object"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Make a thread wait until a condition on shared state holds before it proceeds.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **guarded method** checks a **precondition** under a lock. While it is false, the thread **suspends** and releases the lock. Another thread changes the state and **signals**; the waiter wakes, reacquires the lock, and checks the guard again. State and guard live together in one monitor.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Guard:** a boolean condition over state protected by the lock.","**Wait in a loop:** `while`, never `if`; wakeups can be spurious or stolen.","**Signal on every change** that can make some guard true."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"`wait` releases the monitor and reacquires it before returning. Writes made before `notifyAll` by the signaler are visible to the waiter afterwards.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"[m1, m2, m3]","file":"GuardedSuspension.java","regions":{"consumer":[30,37],"join":[39,40],"producer":[38,38],"put":[17,23],"take":[7,15]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nfinal class Mailbox<T> {\n    private T item; // guarded by this\n\n    synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {\n        while (item == null) {\n            wait();\n        }\n        T value = item;\n        item = null;\n        notifyAll();\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    synchronized void put(T value) throws InterruptedException {\n        while (item != null) {\n            wait();\n        }\n        item = value;\n        notifyAll();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        var box = new Mailbox<String>();\n        var received = new ArrayList<String>();\n        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {\n            try {\n                for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) received.add(box.take());\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n            }\n        });\n        consumer.start();\n        for (String m : List.of(\"m1\", \"m2\", \"m3\")) box.put(m);\n        consumer.join();\n        System.out.println(received);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The guard `item == null` is rechecked in a `while` loop after every wakeup. `wait()` releases the monitor, so a producer can enter `put`.","region":"take"},{"note":"The producer has its own guard: the slot must be empty. Changing `item` and then calling `notifyAll()` wakes threads whose guard may now hold.","region":"put"},{"note":"The consumer starts first and blocks on the empty slot. Restoring the interrupt flag keeps cancellation visible to callers.","region":"consumer"},{"note":"The main thread produces. Whatever order the scheduler picks, each guard is evaluated under the lock, so the result is the same.","region":"producer"},{"note":"`join` gives a happens-before edge, so reading `received` here is safe. The list needed no extra synchronization.","region":"join"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Borrowing a connection from a database pool.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A request thread calls `borrow()` when all connections are in use. It must block until one is returned, but not forever: after 30 seconds the call fails so the request can return an error instead of hanging. The guard is `!idle.isEmpty()`, `return()` signals, and the wait is a timed `awaitNanos` that survives spurious wakeups by recomputing the remaining time.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["lock.lock(); try {","    long left = timeout.toNanos();","    while (idle.isEmpty()) {","        if (left <= 0) throw new TimeoutException();","        left = notEmpty.awaitNanos(left);","    }","    return idle.removeFirst();","} finally { lock.unlock(); }"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: waiters sleep without burning CPU, and the bounded wait turns pool exhaustion into a fast, handled failure.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"cons","label":"take","rel":"dependency","to":"box"},{"from":"prod","label":"put","rel":"dependency","to":"box"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"box","kind":"class","members":["- item: T","+ take(): T","+ put(value)"],"name":"Mailbox<T>","role":"GuardedObject","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"cons","kind":"class","members":["blocks until item != null"],"name":"Consumer","role":"WaitingThread","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"prod","kind":"class","members":["sets item, notifyAll()"],"name":"Producer","role":"SignalingThread","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"box","text":"guard + state share one lock"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"cons","label":"take()","style":"call","to":"box"},{"from":"box","label":"item == null: wait()","style":"call","to":"box"},{"from":"prod","label":"put(v)","style":"call","to":"box"},{"from":"box","label":"item = v; notifyAll()","style":"call","to":"box"},{"from":"box","label":"recheck guard: true","style":"call","to":"box"},{"from":"box","label":"v","style":"return","to":"cons"}],"participants":[{"id":"cons","name":"Consumer"},{"id":"box","name":"Mailbox","role":"GuardedObject"},{"id":"prod","name":"Producer"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A thread cannot proceed until another thread changes shared state.","The wait should consume no CPU, unlike polling.","Several conditions share one lock and need separate wait sets."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A `BlockingQueue`, `CountDownLatch` or `Future` already expresses the wait.","The caller can proceed without the result: use a callback or a non-blocking check."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Using `if` instead of `while`: spurious or stolen wakeups break the guard.","Forgetting to signal after a state change, which leaves waiters stuck forever.","Unbounded waits and swallowed `InterruptedException`."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Hand-written `wait`/`notify` where a `java.util.concurrent` class fits.","One lock with many unrelated guards and `notifyAll` storming every waiter."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`ReentrantLock` with `Condition` gives several wait sets and timed waits.","Blocking inside `synchronized` pins a virtual thread in Java 21; prefer `ReentrantLock` conditions or `java.util.concurrent` types there."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A blocking queue is guarded suspension on both ends: `take` waits while empty, `put` waits while full.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A future packages one result and lets the caller wait for it, or compose without waiting. Guarded suspension waits on arbitrary shared state, repeatedly.","ref":"future-promise","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A guard should be bounded. A timed wait with remaining-time bookkeeping stops a missed signal becoming a hung thread.","ref":"timeout","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An active object takes the request on its own thread, so the caller need not block on a condition at all.","ref":"active-object","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The primitive form: `wait` releases the monitor, and the javadoc requires calling it in a loop.","name":"Object.wait / notifyAll","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`await`, `awaitNanos` and `signalAll` provide multiple wait sets per `ReentrantLock`.","name":"java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`take` and `put` wait on `Condition` objects (`notEmpty`, `notFull`) guarded by one lock.","name":"ArrayBlockingQueue","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`await` blocks until the count reaches zero, a one-shot guard.","name":"CountDownLatch","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Gate {","    private boolean open;","","    synchronized void pass() throws InterruptedException {","        while (!open) wait();","    }","    synchronized void release() {","        open = true;","        notifyAll();","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"Threads block while a condition on shared state is false and are woken when another thread changes it."},{"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"It looks like a repeated check, but this waits for a signal; DCL avoids locking for one-time initialization."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"There is no queue of work items moving between threads, only a single flag."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"All access takes the same exclusive monitor; there is no shared read mode."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"The worker waits on a condition over shared state and is signaled when the handshake changes it."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A pool reuses threads for tasks; it does not make one thread wait for a condition."},{"ref":"immutable-object","why":"Immutable data needs no waiting, but here the point is waiting for a change."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"It governs concurrent access modes and has no way to wait until a condition becomes true."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A worker thread must not start sending until a separate thread finishes the handshake. The handshake flag is shared state; the worker should sleep, not poll, and may be reused for many handshakes."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The guard is checked once. After a spurious wakeup, or if another consumer took the item first, the thread proceeds with `null`. Use `while (item == null) wait();`.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Slot<T> {","    private T item;","","    synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {","        if (item == null) wait();","        T value = item;","        item = null;","        return value;","    }","","    synchronized void put(T value) {","        item = value;","        notifyAll();","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks Guarded Suspension."},{"blocks":[["while (item == null) wait();"],["T value = item;"],["item = null;"],["notifyAll();"],["return value;"]],"compile":{"template":["final class Slot<T> {","  private T item;","  synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {","{{code}}","  } }"]},"explanation":"Wait for the guard first, read the item, then clear it. Signal after the state change so woken threads see it, and return last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of the guarded take so that the state stays consistent."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Guard and state protected by the same lock","wait releases the lock and reacquires it before returning","Always recheck the guard in a while loop","Signal after every state change that can satisfy a guard","Bound the wait and handle interruption"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A thread tests a condition on shared state under a lock and, if false, waits, which releases the lock. Another thread changes the state and signals. The waiter reacquires the lock and rechecks in a loop, because wakeups can be spurious or the condition may already be consumed.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Guarded Suspension in your own words: why a loop, and what wait does with the lock."}],"id":"guarded-suspension"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Decouple the threads that create work from the threads that perform it by passing the work through a bounded queue.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Producers `put` items on a **bounded blocking queue**; consumers `take` them. The queue is the only shared state, so neither side needs to know how many peers exist or how fast they are. When the queue is full, `put` blocks the producer. That is **backpressure**: the system slows at its source instead of exhausting memory.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Decouples:** the rate, count and lifetime of producers and consumers.","**Bounds:** queue capacity caps memory and queueing delay.","**Ends by:** a poison pill, or by closing the source."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Java's memory-consistency rule for `BlockingQueue`: whatever a producer did before `put` happens-before what the consumer does after taking that element.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"jobs=10 total=55\nqueue drained=true","file":"ProducerConsumer.java","regions":{"consume":[18,26],"message":[7,9],"produce":[27,31],"queue":[13,13],"result":[33,34]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\n\nsealed interface Msg permits Job, Stop {}\nrecord Job(int amount) implements Msg {}\nrecord Stop() implements Msg {}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BlockingQueue<Msg> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);\n        var total = new AtomicLong();\n        var jobs = new AtomicInteger();\n        int consumers = 3;\n        try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            for (int c = 0; c < consumers; c++) {\n                exec.submit(() -> {\n                    while (queue.take() instanceof Job job) {\n                        total.addAndGet(job.amount());\n                        jobs.incrementAndGet();\n                    }\n                    return null;\n                });\n            }\n            exec.submit(() -> {\n                for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) queue.put(new Job(i));\n                for (int c = 0; c < consumers; c++) queue.put(new Stop());\n                return null;\n            });\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"jobs=\" + jobs + \" total=\" + total);\n        System.out.println(\"queue drained=\" + queue.isEmpty());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A sealed message type makes end-of-stream part of the protocol. `take()` returns `Msg`, and a type pattern separates work from `Stop`.","region":"message"},{"note":"Capacity 2 is deliberate. Once two jobs wait, `put` blocks the producer until a consumer frees a slot: backpressure with no extra code.","region":"queue"},{"note":"Each consumer loops on `take()`, which blocks while the queue is empty. The `instanceof Job` pattern ends the loop on the first `Stop`.","region":"consume"},{"note":"One producer enqueues ten jobs, then one `Stop` per consumer. FIFO order puts every job ahead of every stop, so no job is lost.","region":"produce"},{"note":"Closing the executor waits for the producer and all three consumers, so the totals are final and the output is deterministic.","region":"result"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Thumbnail generation behind a photo upload endpoint.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Uploads arrive in bursts, but thumbnailing is CPU heavy. The request thread stores the original, offers a `ThumbJob` to a bounded queue of 200 and returns. A fixed set of workers sized to the cores drains it. When a burst fills the queue, the endpoint uses `offer` with a short timeout and answers 503, instead of letting memory grow. Throughput is set by the workers, latency by the queue length, and a slow worker never blocks an upload that still has room.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<ThumbJob>(200);","// request thread","if (!queue.offer(job, 50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) return tooBusy();","// worker thread","while (true) thumbnailer.render(queue.take());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: bursts are absorbed, memory stays bounded, and overload becomes an explicit 503 rather than an OutOfMemoryError.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"prod","label":"put","rel":"dependency","to":"q"},{"from":"cons","label":"take","rel":"dependency","to":"q"},{"from":"abq","rel":"realizes","to":"q"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"prod","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"Producer","role":"Producer","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"cons","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"Consumer","role":"Consumer","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"q","kind":"interface","members":["+ put(msg)","+ take(): Msg"],"name":"BlockingQueue<Msg>","role":"Channel","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"abq","kind":"class","members":["- capacity: int"],"name":"ArrayBlockingQueue","role":"BoundedBuffer","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"q","text":"put blocks when full, take when empty"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"cons","label":"take()","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"prod","label":"put(job)","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"q","label":"job","style":"return","to":"cons"},{"from":"prod","label":"put(stop)","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"cons","label":"take()","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"q","label":"stop","style":"return","to":"cons"}],"participants":[{"id":"prod","name":"Producer"},{"id":"q","name":"Queue","role":"Channel"},{"id":"cons","name":"Consumer"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Producers and consumers run at different or bursty rates.","You need backpressure to bound memory under load.","Items are independent, so any consumer can take any item."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The caller needs the result right away: use a plain call or a future.","Strict global ordering is required across several consumers."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["An unbounded queue hides overload until an OutOfMemoryError.","Too few poison pills: a consumer never gets one and waits forever.","A consumer that dies on an exception silently stops draining.","A consumer that also produces into its own bounded queue can deadlock."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads make a blocking `take()` cheap, so a consumer per task is affordable.","`Flow` and `SubmissionPublisher` add demand signalling for streaming pipelines."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A thread pool is this pattern packaged: callers produce `Runnable`s, the workers are interchangeable consumers of one shared queue.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Guarded suspension makes one thread wait for a condition on a shared object. Here the queue encapsulates that wait and decouples two roles.","ref":"guarded-suspension","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"The same shape across processes, with a broker as the queue. It adds acknowledgements, redelivery and idempotency concerns that an in-memory queue lacks.","ref":"competing-consumers","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An active object is one consumer whose queue carries method requests, with futures returned to the callers.","ref":"active-object","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Observer pushes events synchronously to subscribers it knows. A queue buffers them and lets the consumer pull at its own pace.","ref":"observer","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`ArrayBlockingQueue`, `LinkedBlockingQueue` and `SynchronousQueue` implement the bounded buffer; `put` and `take` block.","name":"java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Its `workQueue` is a `BlockingQueue<Runnable>` that worker threads `take` from.","name":"ThreadPoolExecutor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Application threads enqueue log events on a bounded queue (default size 256); one worker thread writes them.","name":"AsyncAppender","where":"Logback"},{"detail":"The pattern at distributed scale, with a durable partitioned log as the buffer.","name":"KafkaProducer / KafkaConsumer","where":"Apache Kafka"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.nio.file.Path;","import java.util.concurrent.*;","interface Parser { void parse(Path p); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Ingest {","    private final BlockingQueue<Path> inbox = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100);","","    void accept(Path file) throws InterruptedException {","        inbox.put(file);","    }","","    void startWorker(Parser parser) {","        Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> {","            try { while (true) parser.parse(inbox.take()); }","            catch (InterruptedException e) { }","        });","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"Callers hand data to a bounded queue and a separate thread drains it. `put` blocks when full, which is the backpressure."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"There is one dedicated worker running a fixed operation on typed data, not a set of interchangeable workers executing arbitrary tasks."},{"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"The wait is a means to a hand-off between two roles; the point is decoupling them through a buffer, not one object waiting for its own state."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"Callers do not invoke methods on an object and get futures back; they only drop data into a queue."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"A bounded queue absorbs the burst, writer threads drain it at their own rate, and a full queue pushes back on the endpoint."},{"ref":"future-promise","why":"A future stands for one pending result. Nothing here waits for a result, and a future does nothing to bound the backlog."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"There is no shared mutable structure to protect; the problem is a rate mismatch between two sides."},{"ref":"fork-join","why":"It splits one large computation into parts. These are independent events arriving continuously, with nothing to recombine."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"An endpoint receives bursts of 5,000 audit events per second, but the database sustains about 800 writes per second. Events may be written in any order, and the endpoint must not run out of memory during a burst."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","record Event() {}","interface Db { void write(Event e); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`add` throws IllegalStateException when the queue is full, so overload becomes a crash instead of pressure. Use `put` to block the caller, or `offer` with a timeout to shed load deliberately.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class AuditSink {","    private final BlockingQueue<Event> queue =","        new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);","","    void record(Event e) {","        queue.add(e);","    }","","    void drain(Db db) throws InterruptedException {","        while (true) db.write(queue.take());","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the pattern's backpressure."},{"blocks":[["BlockingQueue<Integer> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);"],["Thread worker = Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> drain(queue));"],["for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) queue.put(i);"],["queue.put(-1); // stop signal"],["worker.join();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","class Snippet {","  static void drain(BlockingQueue<Integer> q) {","    try { while (q.take() != -1) { } } catch (InterruptedException e) { }","  }","  void run() throws InterruptedException {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"The queue must exist first. With capacity 2, putting three jobs before a consumer runs would block forever. The stop signal follows the last job, and join waits for the drain to finish.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so the producer never blocks forever and the consumer ends."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["The queue is the only shared state; it decouples rate and count","Bounded capacity gives backpressure instead of unbounded memory","put blocks when full, take blocks when empty","Shutdown by a poison pill per consumer, queued last","Unbounded queues and lost pills are the classic bugs"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Producers and consumers share only a blocking queue, so they can differ in speed, count and lifetime. Bounding the queue turns a full buffer into backpressure that blocks producers instead of growing memory. Consumers stop on a poison pill, one per consumer, queued after the last item.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Producer-Consumer in your own words: what the queue decouples, why it should be bounded, and how consumers know when to stop."}],"id":"producer-consumer"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Reuse a fixed set of long-lived worker threads to run many short tasks, instead of starting a thread per task.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **thread pool** couples a task queue with N workers. Submitting a task is an enqueue; a free worker dequeues and runs it. Thread creation is paid once, and the pool size caps concurrency, so a flood of work waits in the queue instead of overwhelming the CPU, or the database behind it.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Sizing:** about the core count for CPU-bound work; larger only when tasks block.","**Queue and rejection policy** decide what happens under overload.","**Lifecycle:** shut the pool down, or its non-daemon threads keep the JVM alive."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"With virtual threads, one thread per task is cheap again for blocking I/O. Use a pool to cap concurrency or run CPU-bound work, not merely to save thread creation.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"[0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49]\nthreads used <= 3: true","file":"ThreadPool.java","regions":{"join":[61,63],"pool":[11,31],"shutdown":[43,46],"submit":[54,60],"worker":[33,41]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Arrays;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executor;\nimport java.util.concurrent.RejectedExecutionException;\n\nfinal class MiniPool implements Executor {\n    private static final Runnable STOP = () -> { };\n    private final BlockingQueue<Runnable> queue;\n    private final List<Thread> workers = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    MiniPool(int threads, int capacity) {\n        queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(capacity);\n        for (int i = 0; i < threads; i++) {\n            workers.add(Thread.ofPlatform().start(this::loop));\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void execute(Runnable task) {\n        try {\n            queue.put(task);\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n            throw new RejectedExecutionException(e);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void loop() {\n        try {\n            for (Runnable r; (r = queue.take()) != STOP; ) {\n                try { r.run(); } catch (RuntimeException e) { /* log */ }\n            }\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    void shutdown() throws InterruptedException {\n        for (int i = 0; i < workers.size(); i++) queue.put(STOP);\n        for (Thread w : workers) w.join();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        var pool = new MiniPool(3, 4);\n        int[] squares = new int[8];\n        Set<String> used = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();\n        for (int i = 0; i < squares.length; i++) {\n            int n = i;\n            pool.execute(() -> {\n                squares[n] = n * n;\n                used.add(Thread.currentThread().getName());\n            });\n        }\n        pool.shutdown();\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(squares));\n        System.out.println(\"threads used <= 3: \" + (used.size() <= 3));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The pool is an `Executor`: a bounded queue plus a fixed set of platform threads started in the constructor and reused for every task.","region":"pool"},{"note":"Each worker blocks on `take()` and runs tasks until it dequeues `STOP`. Catching `RuntimeException` keeps one failing task from killing its worker.","region":"worker"},{"note":"One `STOP` per worker is queued behind the pending tasks, so accepted work finishes first. Then every worker is joined.","region":"shutdown"},{"note":"Eight tasks, three workers. `execute` blocks when the 4-slot queue is full, throttling the submitter. `put` happens-before the worker's `take`.","region":"submit"},{"note":"`Thread.join` makes the workers' writes to `squares` visible here, so the plain array is safe to read. Which worker ran which task is never printed.","region":"join"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A report service rendering PDFs with a licensed library.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each render is CPU heavy, and the vendor library tolerates at most eight concurrent calls. A pool of eight workers with a queue of 50 enforces that limit by construction. When the queue is full, `CallerRunsPolicy` makes the submitting request thread render the PDF itself, which slows new arrivals instead of failing them or buffering without limit. The pool is built once at startup and shared by all request threads.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(","    8, 8, 0, TimeUnit.SECONDS,","    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(50),","    new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());","Future<Pdf> pdf = pool.submit(() -> renderer.render(order));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the vendor limit holds under any load, and overload slows callers down rather than crashing the service.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"submits to","rel":"dependency","to":"exec"},{"from":"pool","rel":"realizes","to":"exec"},{"from":"pool","label":"tasks","rel":"composition","to":"queue"},{"from":"pool","label":"N workers","rel":"composition","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","label":"take","rel":"dependency","to":"queue"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ execute(task)"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"exec","kind":"interface","members":["+ execute(Runnable)"],"name":"Executor","role":"Pool","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"pool","kind":"class","members":["- workers: List<Thread>","+ shutdown()"],"name":"ThreadPool","role":"Pool","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"queue","kind":"interface","members":["+ put(task)","+ take(): Runnable"],"name":"BlockingQueue","role":"TaskQueue","row":3},{"col":1,"id":"worker","kind":"class","members":["+ loop()"],"name":"Worker","role":"Worker","row":3}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"execute(task)","style":"call","to":"pool"},{"from":"pool","label":"put(task)","style":"call","to":"queue"},{"from":"worker","label":"take()","style":"call","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","label":"task","style":"return","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","label":"take()","style":"call","to":"queue"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"pool","name":"Pool","role":"Pool"},{"id":"queue","name":"Queue","role":"TaskQueue"},{"id":"worker","name":"Worker","role":"Worker"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Many short tasks would otherwise spawn unbounded threads.","You need a hard cap on concurrency toward a database or API.","CPU-bound work should run with about one thread per core."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Tasks block for long on I/O: prefer one virtual thread per task.","Tasks wait on other tasks submitted to the same pool."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Pool starvation deadlock: a task blocks on a subtask queued behind it.","`newFixedThreadPool` has an unbounded queue, so overload becomes memory growth.","A forgotten `shutdown()` leaves non-daemon workers running.","`submit` stores exceptions in the `Future`; nobody reads them."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` replaces pools for blocking tasks; cap with a `Semaphore`.","`ExecutorService` is `AutoCloseable` since Java 19: try-with-resources waits for tasks."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A pool is Producer-Consumer packaged: submitters produce `Runnable`s onto one queue, interchangeable workers consume them.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A fork-join pool gives each worker its own deque and steals work, which suits recursive subtasks. A plain pool shares one queue and suits independent tasks.","ref":"fork-join","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A single-thread executor is the engine of an active object: one thread owning one object's state, rather than N workers sharing a load.","ref":"active-object","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"`submit` returns a `Future`, the handle through which a caller collects the result or failure of a pooled task.","ref":"future-promise","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Separate pools per dependency isolate failures: a slow payment API can exhaust only its own threads.","ref":"bulkhead","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Core and maximum size, a work queue and a `RejectedExecutionHandler`, with the policy classes `AbortPolicy` and `CallerRunsPolicy`.","name":"java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A pool whose queue is ordered by trigger time; behind `Executors.newScheduledThreadPool`.","name":"ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Spring's configurable wrapper over `ThreadPoolExecutor`, used by `@Async` when configured.","name":"ThreadPoolTaskExecutor","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"The size of the request-processing thread pool; the default is 200.","name":"Connector maxThreads","where":"Apache Tomcat"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Batcher {","    private final BlockingQueue<Runnable> inbox = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(64);","","    Batcher(int n) {","        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) Thread.ofPlatform().start(this::run);","    }","","    private void run() {","        try { while (true) inbox.take().run(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }","    }","","    void submit(Runnable job) throws InterruptedException { inbox.put(job); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"thread-pool","why":"N interchangeable workers share one queue of arbitrary tasks and run them. That is an executor."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"It is the underlying mechanism, but here the queue carries executable tasks, not data for one typed consumer. The shape is an executor."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"Several anonymous workers share the load; no single thread owns an object's state, and callers get no futures."},{"ref":"reactor","why":"Nothing waits on I/O readiness events; workers just run queued tasks."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A pool of 8 workers enforces the concurrency limit by construction, and a queue holds the overflow."},{"ref":"reactor","why":"It multiplexes I/O events on few threads. CPU-heavy renders would block the event loop for everyone."},{"ref":"fork-join","why":"It splits one large job into recursive parts. These are independent requests with no subtasks to combine."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"It serializes everything onto one thread, giving a concurrency of 1 instead of 8."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A service calls a licensed PDF library that tolerates at most 8 concurrent renders. Requests are CPU heavy, arrive in bursts, and extra requests should wait their turn rather than fail."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.concurrent.*;","class Snippet { void run() throws Exception {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Both workers sit in `part.get()` waiting for subtasks that are queued behind them, and nobody is left to run them. Compute the part inline, or use a separate pool or non-blocking composition.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);","List<Future<Integer>> results = new ArrayList<>();","for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {","    results.add(pool.submit(() -> {","        Future<Integer> part = pool.submit(() -> 21);","        return part.get() * 2;","    }));","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that can deadlock the pool."},{"blocks":[["var seen = new AtomicInteger();","var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);"],["pool.submit(() -> seen.incrementAndGet());"],["pool.shutdown();"],["boolean finished = pool.awaitTermination(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;","class Snippet { void run() throws InterruptedException {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create before submit. A shut-down pool rejects new tasks, so shutdown follows the submit. awaitTermination blocks until its timeout unless shutdown was already requested.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines of a clean pool lifecycle."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Reuses N long-lived workers fed from a queue","The size caps concurrency; the queue absorbs bursts","An unbounded queue or missing rejection policy hides overload","Tasks waiting on same-pool tasks cause starvation deadlock","Virtual threads replace pools for blocking I/O, not for limits"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A pool keeps N workers alive and feeds them from a queue, so creating threads is paid once and concurrency is capped at N. Overload then shows up in the queue, so its bound and the rejection policy matter. A task that blocks on another task in the same pool can starve it.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain a thread pool in your own words: what it saves, what it bounds, and what can go wrong under overload."}],"id":"thread-pool"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Represent a result that is not ready yet, and compose what happens when it arrives without blocking a thread.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **future** is a read-only handle to a pending result; a **promise** is the writable side that completes it. In Java, `CompletableFuture` is both. You attach stages with `thenApply`, `thenCompose` and `thenCombine`; each runs when its inputs complete, and a failure travels down the chain until some stage handles it.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Completes once:** with a value or an exception, never both, never twice.","**Compose, don't wait:** `get()` blocks a thread; `thenX` registers a continuation.","**Where it runs:** async stages default to the common pool; pass an executor for I/O."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Memory model: actions taken by the computation happen-before the actions that follow the corresponding `get()` in the waiting thread. No extra synchronization is needed.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"user-7 owes 36\nuser-3+user-4\nfallback: no user 0\npromise: 42","file":"FuturePromise.java","regions":{"compose":[19,25],"failure":[26,31],"promise":[33,36],"source":[6,15]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executor;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\n\nclass Main {\n    static CompletableFuture<String> userName(Executor io, int id) {\n        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {\n            if (id <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"no user \" + id);\n            return \"user-\" + id;\n        }, io);\n    }\n\n    static CompletableFuture<Integer> balance(Executor io, String user) {\n        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> user.length() * 6, io);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        try (var io = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            var owed = userName(io, 7)\n                .thenCompose(u -> balance(io, u)\n                    .thenApply(b -> u + \" owes \" + b));\n            var both = userName(io, 3)\n                .thenCombine(userName(io, 4), (a, b) -> a + \"+\" + b);\n            System.out.println(owed.get());\n            System.out.println(both.get());\n            var safe = userName(io, 0)\n                .thenCompose(u -> balance(io, u))\n                .handle((value, error) -> error == null\n                    ? \"ok \" + value\n                    : \"fallback: \" + error.getCause().getMessage());\n            System.out.println(safe.get());\n        }\n        var promise = new CompletableFuture<Integer>();\n        var doubled = promise.thenApply(x -> x * 2);\n        promise.complete(21);\n        System.out.println(\"promise: \" + doubled.get());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Each call returns a `CompletableFuture` at once; the lambda runs on the supplied executor. A thrown exception completes the future exceptionally.","region":"source"},{"note":"`thenCompose` flattens a dependent call (the balance needs the user). `thenCombine` joins two independent futures that run in parallel.","region":"compose"},{"note":"`handle` receives value or error. Through a dependent stage the error arrives wrapped in `CompletionException`, so the message is read from `getCause()`.","region":"failure"},{"note":"A bare `new CompletableFuture<>()` is a promise: nothing computes it. The dependent stage attached first fires when `complete(21)` is called.","region":"promise"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A product page aggregating pricing, stock and reviews.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The page needs three independent backend calls. Run one after another they cost the sum of their latencies; started as futures they cost about the slowest. Each call carries its own timeout, and reviews fall back to an empty list, so a slow reviews service degrades the page instead of failing it. The combined future completes once all three inputs have, and the page assembler runs a single time.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var price = pricing.fetch(id).orTimeout(300, MILLISECONDS);","var stock = stocks.fetch(id).orTimeout(300, MILLISECONDS);","var reviews = reviewsApi.fetch(id)","    .completeOnTimeout(List.of(), 300, MILLISECONDS);","return price.thenCombine(stock, Offer::new)","    .thenCombine(reviews, Page::new);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: latency is the slowest call, not the sum, and timeouts and fallbacks are part of the chain.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"task","label":"complete","rel":"dependency","to":"cf"},{"from":"caller","label":"then...","rel":"dependency","to":"stage"},{"from":"caller","label":"get","rel":"dependency","to":"fut"},{"from":"cf","rel":"realizes","to":"stage"},{"from":"cf","rel":"realizes","to":"fut"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"task","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"Task","role":"Producer","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"caller","kind":"class","members":["+ onResult(v)"],"name":"Caller","role":"Consumer","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"stage","kind":"interface","members":["+ thenApply(f)","+ thenCompose(f)"],"name":"CompletionStage<T>","role":"Future","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"fut","kind":"interface","members":["+ get(): T"],"name":"Future<T>","role":"Future","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"cf","kind":"class","members":["+ complete(v): boolean","+ completeExceptionally(e)"],"name":"CompletableFuture<T>","role":"Promise","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"cf","text":"write side: the promise"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"caller","label":"new CompletableFuture","style":"create","to":"cf"},{"from":"caller","label":"start","style":"async","to":"task"},{"from":"caller","label":"thenApply(f)","style":"call","to":"cf"},{"from":"task","label":"complete(v)","style":"call","to":"cf"},{"from":"cf","label":"f(v) runs","style":"async","to":"caller"}],"participants":[{"id":"caller","name":"Caller"},{"id":"task","name":"Task"},{"id":"cf","name":"Promise","role":"Promise"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Independent calls can run in parallel and be combined.","A result feeds follow-up steps without parking a thread per step.","Timeouts and fallbacks belong in the chain."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Straight-line blocking code on virtual threads is simpler to read and debug.","You need cancellation that stops the work: `cancel` does not interrupt it."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["`get()` or `join()` inside a stage blocks a pool thread.","Without an executor, blocking I/O lands on the common pool.","An unhandled exception vanishes if nobody calls `get`, `join` or `handle`.","`allOf` returns `CompletableFuture<Void>`: collect the values yourself."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["On virtual threads, plain blocking code often replaces long chains.","`StructuredTaskScope` (preview in Java 21) scopes forked subtasks and cancels siblings on failure."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The pool supplies the threads that run asynchronous stages; the future is the handle through which results and failures come back.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A continuation is a one-shot observer of a single completion. Observer keeps a list of subscribers notified on every event.","ref":"observer","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Guarded suspension parks a thread until a condition holds. A future lets you register what happens next and keep the thread free.","ref":"guarded-suspension","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"An active object's asynchronous methods return futures; the caller composes on them while the servant works.","ref":"active-object","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The standard promise: `supplyAsync`, `thenCompose`, `orTimeout`, `completeOnTimeout`, `allOf`.","name":"java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`java.net.http.HttpClient` returns `CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<T>>` for non-blocking requests.","name":"HttpClient.sendAsync","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A writable future (`setSuccess`, `setFailure`) used throughout Netty's channel operations.","name":"io.netty.util.concurrent.Promise","where":"Netty"},{"detail":"A Guava `ListenableFuture` whose result you set by hand, often to bridge a callback API.","name":"SettableFuture","where":"Guava"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.concurrent.*;","record Profile(String user, List<String> orders) {","  static Profile anonymous() { return new Profile(\"?\", List.of()); } }","interface Db { String load(long id); }","interface Api { List<String> orders(long id); }","class Svc { Db db; Api api; Executor io;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["CompletableFuture<Profile> profile(long id) {","    var user = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> db.load(id), io);","    var orders = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> api.orders(id), io);","    return user.thenCombine(orders, Profile::new)","        .exceptionally(e -> Profile.anonymous());","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"future-promise","why":"Pending results are returned immediately, combined with continuations, and failures are handled in the chain."},{"ref":"fork-join","why":"Fork-join recursively splits one problem and joins the halves. Here two unrelated calls are composed with continuations."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"There is no queue between roles; each call yields a result handle for the caller."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"No handler decides whether to pass a request along; every stage always runs when its inputs complete."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"future-promise","why":"Start both calls as futures, combine them when both complete, and attach timeouts or fallbacks to the chain."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"A queue and consumer could run the calls, but it gives the caller no handle to get two results back and combine them."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"It serializes calls on one thread, so the two independent calls would still run one after the other."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"No shared state is contended; the problem is waiting on two slow remote calls."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A checkout step needs a price from one service and a stock level from another. The calls are independent, each takes up to 300 ms, and the next step needs both results. Calling them one after another doubles the latency."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","class Snippet {","  static String fetchName() { return \"Ada\"; }","  static CompletableFuture<String> fetchTitle(String n) { return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(\"Dr\"); }","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"`join()` blocks a pool thread inside a stage, and with a small pool it can starve the very task it waits on. Use `thenCompose(name -> fetchTitle(name).thenApply(...))` to flatten the dependent future.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["CompletableFuture<String> greeting(Executor io) {","    return CompletableFuture","        .supplyAsync(() -> fetchName(), io)","        .thenApply(name -> {","            String title = fetchTitle(name).join();","            return title + \" \" + name;","        });","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that defeats the non-blocking chain."},{"blocks":[["var promise = new CompletableFuture<String>();"],["var shout = promise.thenApply(String::toUpperCase);"],["promise.complete(\"done\");"],["String result = shout.get();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","class Snippet { void run() throws Exception {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The promise must exist before anything attaches to it. Reading with `get()` before `complete` would block forever, so the read comes last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines so the dependent stage is attached before completion and the result is read last."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Promise completes once; future is the read-only handle","thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine chain without blocking","Errors flow down the chain to handle or exceptionally","Pass an executor for blocking work, not the common pool","get() or join() inside a stage reintroduces blocking"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A promise is the writable side that completes a pending result once, with a value or an error; the future is the read side others hold. Instead of blocking with get, you attach stages that run on completion and propagate failures. Async stages run on an executor, by default the common pool, so pass one for blocking work.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Future/Promise in your own words: the two sides, why you compose instead of calling get(), and where a stage runs."}],"id":"future-promise"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let many threads read shared state at the same time, while a writer gets exclusive access.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **read-write lock** has two modes. Any number of threads may hold the **read lock** together; the **write lock** excludes everyone, readers included. It pays off when reads dominate and the guarded section is long enough that serializing readers would hurt. For tiny sections a plain lock is often faster, because the extra bookkeeping costs more than the parallelism saves.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Read lock:** shared with other readers, excluded by a writer.","**Write lock:** exclusive, and sees every earlier write.","**No upgrade:** holding read and asking for write deadlocks; downgrading is allowed."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Memory model: releasing the write lock happens-before the next successful acquisition of either lock, so readers always see completed writes.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"readers inside: 2\nwriter could enter: false\nwriter could enter: true","file":"ReadWriteLock.java","regions":{"after":[66,67],"check":[60,64],"guarded":[9,29],"probe":[30,36],"readers":[53,59]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\nimport java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;\nimport java.util.function.Consumer;\nimport java.util.function.Function;\n\nfinal class PriceBook {\n    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();\n    private final Map<String, Integer> prices = new HashMap<>();\n\n    <R> R read(Function<Map<String, Integer>, R> query) {\n        lock.readLock().lock();\n        try {\n            return query.apply(prices);\n        } finally {\n            lock.readLock().unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    void write(Consumer<Map<String, Integer>> update) {\n        lock.writeLock().lock();\n        try {\n            update.accept(prices);\n        } finally {\n            lock.writeLock().unlock();\n        }\n    }\n    int readers() { return lock.getReadLockCount(); }\n\n    boolean writerCouldEnter() {\n        boolean ok = lock.writeLock().tryLock();\n        if (ok) lock.writeLock().unlock();\n        return ok;\n    }\n}\nclass Main {\n    static void await(CountDownLatch latch) {\n        try {\n            latch.await();\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        var book = new PriceBook();\n        book.write(m -> { m.put(\"tea\", 3); m.put(\"jam\", 5); });\n        var inside = new CountDownLatch(2);\n        var release = new CountDownLatch(1);\n        try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            for (String item : new String[] {\"tea\", \"jam\"}) {\n                exec.submit(() -> book.read(m -> {\n                    inside.countDown();\n                    await(release);\n                    return m.get(item);\n                }));\n            }\n            inside.await();\n            System.out.println(\"readers inside: \" + book.readers());\n            System.out.println(\"writer could enter: \"\n                + book.writerCouldEnter());\n            release.countDown();\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"writer could enter: \"\n            + book.writerCouldEnter());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"All access goes through a lock mode. Callers pass a query or an update, never touching the map directly, and `finally` guarantees release.","region":"guarded"},{"note":"Test hooks: `getReadLockCount` reports concurrent readers, and `tryLock` on the write side shows whether a writer could enter right now.","region":"probe"},{"note":"Two readers enter `read` and park inside it on a latch. If readers excluded each other the second could never enter, and the run would hang.","region":"readers"},{"note":"With both readers inside, the count is 2 and a writer cannot enter. Releasing the latch lets them finish.","region":"check"},{"note":"Once the readers have left, try-locking the write side succeeds. Waiting for them was done by closing the executor.","region":"after"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A routing table consulted on every packet.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A gateway looks up routes for each request on 32 threads, while a control thread applies a route update about once a minute. The table is too large to copy per update, and an update touches many entries that lookups must see all-or-nothing. Lookups take the read lock and run in parallel; the control thread takes the write lock, so a lookup sees the table either before the update or after it, never half applied.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Route hit = table.read(m -> m.lookup(dest));","// control thread, once a minute","table.write(m -> m.apply(update));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: lookups never wait on each other, and updates are atomic to every reader without copying the table.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"reader","label":"read","rel":"dependency","to":"book"},{"from":"writer","label":"write","rel":"dependency","to":"book"},{"from":"book","label":"lock","rel":"composition","to":"rw"},{"from":"impl","rel":"realizes","to":"rw"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"reader","kind":"class","members":["+ query()"],"name":"Reader","role":"Reader","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"writer","kind":"class","members":["+ update()"],"name":"Writer","role":"Writer","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"book","kind":"class","members":["- prices: Map","+ read(query)","+ write(update)"],"name":"PriceBook","role":"GuardedResource","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"rw","kind":"interface","members":["+ readLock(): Lock","+ writeLock(): Lock"],"name":"ReadWriteLock","role":"Lock","row":2},{"col":0.5,"id":"impl","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"ReentrantReadWriteLock","role":"Lock","row":3}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"r1","label":"readLock().lock()","style":"call","to":"lock"},{"from":"r2","label":"readLock().lock()","style":"call","to":"lock"},{"from":"w","label":"writeLock().lock()","style":"call","to":"lock"},{"from":"r1","label":"unlock()","style":"call","to":"lock"},{"from":"r2","label":"unlock()","style":"call","to":"lock"},{"from":"lock","label":"acquired","style":"return","to":"w"}],"participants":[{"id":"r1","name":"Reader 1"},{"id":"r2","name":"Reader 2"},{"id":"w","name":"Writer"},{"id":"lock","name":"Lock","role":"Lock"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Reads vastly outnumber writes.","Read sections are long enough to amortize the lock's overhead.","A multi-field update must never be seen half done."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Sections are tiny: `synchronized` or an immutable snapshot is cheaper.","Writes are frequent, so readers queue behind them anyway."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Read-to-write upgrade deadlocks: release the read lock first, then re-check.","\"Read-only\" operations that mutate, such as `get` on an access-ordered `LinkedHashMap`.","Unlock outside `finally` leaks the lock on exceptions.","Fairness mode trades throughput for starvation guarantees; know which you run."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`StampedLock` offers optimistic reads that take no lock and are validated afterwards.","For rarely changed data, a `volatile` immutable snapshot gives readers no lock at all.","Java 21 virtual threads pin their carrier inside `synchronized`; lock classes do not."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Publish a new immutable snapshot through a `volatile` field and readers need no lock. Read-write locks suit data too big or too hot to copy.","ref":"immutable-object","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Both avoid locking on the read path, but DCL only guards one-time lazy initialization. A read-write lock guards ongoing reads and writes.","ref":"double-checked-locking","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"An in-process cache is a classic client: lookups take the read lock, and a miss refills under the write lock.","ref":"cache-aside","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Confine the state to one thread and send it requests, instead of sharing it under locks. Simpler reasoning, but readers are serialized.","ref":"active-object","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The standard implementation; supports reentrancy, fairness and write-to-read downgrading.","name":"java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Adds optimistic reads via `tryOptimisticRead` and `validate`; it is not reentrant.","name":"java.util.concurrent.locks.StampedLock","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The standard library's reader/writer mutex: `RLock`, `RUnlock`, `Lock`, `Unlock`.","name":"sync.RWMutex","where":"Go"},{"detail":"The C-level primitive behind many runtimes: `pthread_rwlock_rdlock` and `pthread_rwlock_wrlock`.","name":"pthread_rwlock_t","where":"POSIX"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.concurrent.locks.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Registry {","    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock gate = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();","    private final Map<String, String> entries = new HashMap<>();","    String lookup(String key) {","        gate.readLock().lock();","        try { return entries.get(key); } finally { gate.readLock().unlock(); }","    }","    void register(String key, String value) {","        gate.writeLock().lock();","        try { entries.put(key, value); } finally { gate.writeLock().unlock(); }","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"Lookups share the read lock while registrations take the exclusive write lock around the same map."},{"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"No caller waits for a condition to become true; the locks only exclude, they never block on state."},{"ref":"immutable-object","why":"The map is mutated in place; there is no immutable snapshot being swapped."},{"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"There is no lazy initialization and no unlocked first check."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"Lookups run in parallel under the shared lock, and the rare update gets exclusive, atomic access."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"Funnelling all 32 threads' lookups through one owner thread serializes reads that could run in parallel."},{"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"It guards one-time lazy initialization. The table already exists and keeps changing."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"There is no stream of work items to buffer; threads query shared state directly."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A routing table with 50,000 entries is consulted on every packet by 32 threads and updated in place by one control thread about once a minute. A lookup reads several fields, and an update touches many entries that must appear atomically. The table is too large to copy per update."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.concurrent.locks.*;","class Snippet {","  ReentrantReadWriteLock cache = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();","  Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();","  String load(String k) { return k; }","  String get(String k) {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"A thread holding the read lock cannot acquire the write lock: it waits for all readers, itself included, to leave. Release the read lock first, take the write lock, and re-check the condition.","flawLines":[4],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["cache.readLock().lock();","try {","    if (!map.containsKey(k)) {","        cache.writeLock().lock();","        try { map.put(k, load(k)); } finally { cache.writeLock().unlock(); }","    }","    return map.get(k);","} finally { cache.readLock().unlock(); }"],"prompt":"Tap the line that deadlocks."},{"blocks":[["lock.writeLock().lock();"],["try {","    map.put(key, value);","    lock.readLock().lock(); // downgrade"],["} finally {","    lock.writeLock().unlock();","}"],["try { return map.get(key); } finally { lock.readLock().unlock(); }"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.concurrent.locks.*;","class Snippet {","  ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();","  Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();","  String run(String key, String value) {","{{code}}","  }","}"]},"explanation":"Take the read lock while still holding the write lock, then release the write lock. No other writer can slip in between, and the final read sees the value just written.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines of a write-to-read downgrade."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Many concurrent readers, one exclusive writer","Wins with read-heavy load and non-trivial read sections","Plain lock or immutable snapshot often wins for tiny sections","Read-to-write upgrade deadlocks; write-to-read downgrade is allowed","Release in finally; never mutate under the read lock"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"It lets any number of readers share the lock while a writer excludes everyone, so read-heavy state with non-trivial read sections scales across cores. For tiny sections or frequent writes a plain lock is simpler and often faster. Never try to upgrade from read to write while holding the read lock: it deadlocks.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain a read-write lock in your own words: when it beats a plain lock, and the one thing you must never do while holding the read lock."}],"id":"read-write-lock"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Check a lazily created shared object without locking, and lock only to create it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Test the field without a lock; if it is `null`, enter a `synchronized` block, test **again**, and only then construct. After creation every caller takes the unsynchronized fast path. It is correct in Java only if the field is **volatile** (or the object is built from final fields only).","type":"p"},{"items":["**Goal:** avoid paying for a lock on every access.","**First check:** cheap and racy by design.","**Second check:** under the lock, to stop duplicate construction."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Without `volatile` it is broken: another thread can see the reference before the object's fields are visible.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"same instance: true\nconstructed: 1\nmode: prod\nholder: true","file":"DoubleCheckedLocking.java","regions":{"dcl":[5,29],"holder":[31,39],"race":[45,50],"report":[51,56]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\n\nfinal class Config {\n    static final AtomicInteger CREATED = new AtomicInteger();\n    private static volatile Config instance;\n    private final Map<String, String> settings;\n\n    private Config() {\n        CREATED.incrementAndGet();\n        settings = Map.of(\"mode\", \"prod\");\n    }\n\n    static Config get() {\n        Config local = instance;\n        if (local == null) {\n            synchronized (Config.class) {\n                local = instance;\n                if (local == null) {\n                    instance = local = new Config();\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return local;\n    }\n\n    String mode() { return settings.get(\"mode\"); }\n}\n\nfinal class Registry {\n    private Registry() { }\n\n    private static final class Holder {\n        static final Registry INSTANCE = new Registry();\n    }\n\n    static Registry get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        var seen = new Config[8];\n        var threads = new ArrayList<Thread>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < seen.length; i++) {\n            int n = i;\n            threads.add(Thread.ofPlatform()\n                .start(() -> seen[n] = Config.get()));\n        }\n        for (Thread t : threads) t.join();\n        boolean same = true;\n        for (Config c : seen) same &= c == seen[0];\n        System.out.println(\"same instance: \" + same);\n        System.out.println(\"constructed: \" + Config.CREATED.get());\n        System.out.println(\"mode: \" + seen[0].mode());\n        System.out.println(\"holder: \" + (Registry.get() == Registry.get()));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"`instance` is `volatile`. The write that publishes it happens-before any later read that sees it, so all constructor writes are visible.","region":"dcl"},{"note":"The holder idiom gets lazy, thread-safe initialization from class loading. The JVM runs `Holder`'s initializer once, on first use, with no locks in your code.","region":"holder"},{"note":"Eight threads race to call `get`. `join` makes their writes to the array visible to the main thread.","region":"race"},{"note":"Exactly one construction happened and every thread got the same instance. The holder gives the same guarantee with less code.","region":"report"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A reporting service builds an expensive template engine.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Compiling thousands of report templates takes seconds, and most requests never need the engine. A `TemplateEngineHolder` creates it on first use. Request threads read the cached engine on every call, so a lock per read would be a visible hotspot. DCL with a volatile field keeps reads lock-free after startup.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["private static volatile Engine engine;","static Engine engine() {","    Engine e = engine;","    if (e == null) {","        synchronized (Holder.class) { /* re-check, build */ }","    }","    return e;","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the lock is taken at most a few times, then every read is a single volatile load.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"get()","rel":"dependency","to":"lazy"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ call get()"],"name":"Client threads","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"lazy","kind":"class","members":["- volatile instance","- lock: Config.class","+ get(): Config"],"name":"Config","role":"LazyHolder","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"lazy","text":"volatile publishes safely"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"a","label":"get(): read, null","style":"call","to":"cfg"},{"from":"a","label":"lock, re-check, create","style":"create","to":"cfg"},{"from":"a","label":"volatile write instance","style":"call","to":"cfg"},{"from":"b","label":"get(): volatile read","style":"call","to":"cfg"},{"from":"cfg","label":"instance, fully visible","style":"return","to":"b"}],"participants":[{"id":"a","name":"Thread A"},{"id":"cfg","name":"Config","role":"LazyHolder"},{"id":"b","name":"Thread B"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A rarely created object is read very often by many threads.","Initialization is costly and you cannot use a holder class."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Creation is cheap: initialize eagerly in a `static final` field.","A holder class or enum works; they are simpler and provably correct."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Forgetting `volatile` compiles, usually works, and fails rarely on weak memory models.","Before Java 5, `volatile` was too weak to fix it, so old advice is wrong.","Reading the field twice on the fast path costs an extra volatile read."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["The initialization-on-demand holder idiom is lazy, thread-safe and lock-free in your code.","An `enum` singleton or a `static final` field covers most needs; reach for DCL last."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Singleton says there is one instance. DCL is just one way to create it lazily; a holder class or enum usually does the same job better.","ref":"singleton","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"If the object has only `final` fields, the JMM publishes them safely even through a racy read. Mutable state is what makes `volatile` necessary.","ref":"immutable-object","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A read lock still costs a memory write per read. DCL's fast path is a plain volatile read, but it only covers one-time initialization.","ref":"read-write-lock","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Guarded suspension makes threads wait until a condition holds. DCL threads never wait for a condition; they only contend once, to create the object.","ref":"guarded-suspension","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Several JDK classes use a private nested holder class, such as lazily initialized caches built on class initialization.","name":"Holder idiom in the JDK","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`updateAndGet` or `compareAndSet` achieve lock-free lazy publication when duplicate construction is tolerable.","name":"java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Wraps a `Supplier` and computes its value once, using double-checked locking with a volatile field.","name":"Suppliers.memoize","where":"Guava"},{"detail":"The revised memory model gave `volatile` acquire/release semantics, which made volatile-based DCL correct.","name":"JSR-133 (Java 5)","where":"JMM"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["final class Settings { }","class Snippet {","    static volatile Settings cached;","    static Settings load() { return new Settings(); }","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["static Settings get() {","    Settings s = cached;","    if (s == null) {","        synchronized (Settings.class) {","            s = cached;","            if (s == null) {","                cached = s = load();","            }","        }","    }","    return s;","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"An unlocked check, then a lock and a second check before creating, so later calls skip the lock."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"Singleton states the goal of one instance. This is the specific locking protocol used to create it lazily."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"There is no reader/writer lock; one plain monitor is used, and only for creation."},{"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"No thread waits for a condition to become true; each simply creates or reads the value."}],"prompt":"Which technique is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"double-checked-locking","why":"Lock-free reads after creation with a volatile field; the lock is taken only while the first caller builds it."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"It names the goal of one instance but gives no safe, cheap way to create it lazily."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"Even a read lock adds a memory write and contention on every hot-path read, for a value created once."},{"ref":"guarded-suspension","why":"Nobody needs to wait for a condition; callers simply create or read the value."}],"prompt":"Which technique fits best?","scenario":"A shared client is built on first use from a path known only at runtime, so a static holder class cannot construct it. Thousands of threads read it on a hot path, and it must be created exactly once."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Without `volatile`, nothing orders the write of `instance` against the constructor's writes, so a thread can see a non-null reference with `retries` still 0. Declare it `volatile`, or use a holder class.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Config {","    private static Config instance;","    private int retries = 3;","","    static Config get() {","        if (instance == null) {","            synchronized (Config.class) {","                if (instance == null) {","                    instance = new Config();","                }","            }","        }","        return instance;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the technique."},{"blocks":[["Config local = instance;"],["if (local == null) {"],["    synchronized (Config.class) {"],["        local = instance;"],["        if (local == null) instance = local = new Config();"],["    }","}"],["return local;"]],"compile":{"template":["final class Config {","    private static volatile Config instance;","    static Config get() {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Read once into a local, check without the lock, lock, re-read and re-check under the lock, create and publish, then close both blocks and return the local.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the lines of get() so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Unlocked first check, locked second check, create once","The unlocked read is a data race without volatile","Volatile gives a happens-before edge from publish to read (JMM, Java 5)","Partially visible objects are the failure, not double creation","Holder class or enum is the usual modern replacement"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"The first check reads the field without a lock, so it races with the creating thread. Without volatile there is no happens-before edge between the publishing write and that read, so a thread can see the reference but stale fields. Volatile adds the edge (correct since Java 5). A holder class or enum is simpler.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain why double-checked locking needs volatile, and what usually replaces it today."}],"id":"double-checked-locking"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give an object its own thread and request queue, so callers invoke it asynchronously and get futures back.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Method calls become **requests** placed on a queue. A single **scheduler** thread takes them one at a time and runs them on a plain, unsynchronized **servant**. The caller gets a future immediately and never touches the servant's state.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Concurrency control:** thread confinement, so the servant needs no locks.","**Caller experience:** an ordinary method that returns a future.","**Ordering:** requests run in the order they were enqueued."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The actor model is this idea scaled up: many active objects, each with a mailbox, communicating only by messages.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"1 3 6\ntotal = 406","file":"ActiveObject.java","regions":{"active":[13,30],"async":[54,57],"callers":[58,65],"loop":[32,48],"servant":[7,11]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\nimport java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\nfinal class Counter {\n    private int total;\n\n    int add(int n) { return total += n; }\n}\n\nfinal class ActiveCounter implements AutoCloseable {\n    private static final Runnable STOP = () -> { };\n    private final Counter servant = new Counter();\n    private final BlockingQueue<Runnable> inbox = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();\n    private final Thread scheduler = Thread.ofPlatform().start(this::loop);\n\n    CompletableFuture<Integer> add(int n) {\n        return enqueue(() -> servant.add(n));\n    }\n\n    private <T> CompletableFuture<T> enqueue(Supplier<T> request) {\n        var result = new CompletableFuture<T>();\n        inbox.add(() -> {\n            try { result.complete(request.get()); }\n            catch (RuntimeException e) { result.completeExceptionally(e); }\n        });\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void loop() {\n        try {\n            for (Runnable r; (r = inbox.take()) != STOP; ) r.run();\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void close() {\n        inbox.add(STOP);\n        try {\n            scheduler.join();\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        try (var counter = new ActiveCounter()) {\n            var a = counter.add(1);\n            var b = counter.add(2);\n            var c = counter.add(3);\n            System.out.println(a.get() + \" \" + b.get() + \" \" + c.get());\n            var callers = new ArrayList<Thread>();\n            for (int t = 0; t < 4; t++) {\n                callers.add(Thread.ofPlatform().start(() -> {\n                    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) counter.add(1);\n                }));\n            }\n            for (Thread t : callers) t.join();\n            System.out.println(\"total = \" + counter.add(0).get());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The servant is plain, single-threaded code. It has no locks and no `volatile`, because only one thread ever runs it.","region":"servant"},{"note":"The proxy turns a call into a request: a `Runnable` that completes a future. `enqueue` is the only way in, and the queue is the activation queue.","region":"active"},{"note":"The scheduler thread takes requests in FIFO order and runs each. A poison pill stops it; `close` then joins the thread.","region":"loop"},{"note":"Calls return at once with futures. Results arrive in submission order: 1, 3, 6.","region":"async"},{"note":"Four threads call concurrently, yet no total is lost. `put` on the queue happens-before `take`, and the servant is touched by one thread.","region":"callers"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A device driver that allows only one command at a time.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A lab-automation service talks to a robotic arm over a serial line. The vendor library is not thread-safe and each command takes seconds. Many HTTP threads want to issue commands. A `RobotArm` active object queues every command, runs them one by one on its own thread, and hands each caller a future for the outcome.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface RobotArm {","    CompletableFuture<Pose> moveTo(Pose target);","    CompletableFuture<Void> grip(boolean closed);","}","","arm.moveTo(shelf).thenCompose(p -> arm.grip(true));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the unsafe driver is never shared, request threads never block on hardware, and command order is explicit.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"calls","rel":"dependency","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","label":"enqueue","rel":"dependency","to":"queue"},{"from":"proxy","label":"returns","rel":"dependency","to":"future"},{"from":"sched","label":"take","rel":"dependency","to":"queue"},{"from":"sched","label":"invoke","rel":"dependency","to":"servant"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ add(n): Future"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"proxy","kind":"class","members":["+ add(n): Future","- enqueue(request)"],"name":"ActiveCounter","role":"Proxy","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"future","kind":"class","members":["+ get(): T"],"name":"CompletableFuture","role":"Future","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"queue","kind":"interface","members":["+ add(request)","+ take(): request"],"name":"BlockingQueue","role":"ActivationQueue","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"sched","kind":"class","members":["- loop()"],"name":"Scheduler thread","role":"Scheduler","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"servant","kind":"class","members":["+ add(n): int"],"name":"Counter","role":"Servant","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"servant","text":"no locks needed"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"add(5)","style":"call","to":"proxy"},{"from":"proxy","label":"enqueue request","style":"async","to":"sched"},{"from":"proxy","label":"future","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"sched","label":"add(5)","style":"call","to":"servant"},{"from":"servant","label":"5","style":"return","to":"sched"},{"from":"sched","label":"complete future","style":"async","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"proxy","name":"ActiveCounter","role":"Proxy"},{"id":"sched","name":"Scheduler","role":"Scheduler"},{"id":"servant","name":"Counter","role":"Servant"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A non-thread-safe resource must be shared by many callers.","Callers should not block on slow work.","You want serialized access without lock reasoning."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Calls are fast and a `synchronized` block or lock is simpler.","Callers need the result immediately on the same thread."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["An unbounded queue hides overload and grows until memory runs out.","A request that blocks stalls every request behind it.","Never touch the servant from outside; one leaking reference breaks confinement."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One thread per tiny object, which wastes memory and context switches.","Futures that every caller immediately blocks on."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor()` plus `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(task, executor)` is a compact active object.","A virtual thread draining a queue makes one active object per entity cheap."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Producer-consumer decouples any producers from any consumers through a queue. An active object is one consumer whose queue carries method requests and returns futures.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"A pool runs tasks on any worker, so they may overlap. An active object funnels all requests for one servant through exactly one thread, giving mutual exclusion.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"The future is how the active object returns results. The pattern adds the queue, the scheduler and the confinement of state.","ref":"future-promise","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The caller-facing object is a proxy, but it forwards through a queue to another thread instead of delegating directly.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Each queued request is a command object, here a `Runnable` closure, that the scheduler executes later.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Each actor has a mailbox and processes one message at a time; `ask` returns a future, as in an active object.","name":"Actor / ActorRef","where":"Akka"},{"detail":"One thread plus an unbounded queue, with `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(task, executor)` for the proxy side.","name":"Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A looper thread drains a message queue; posted work runs serially on that thread.","name":"HandlerThread and Handler","where":"Android"},{"detail":"Requests are queued to the single event dispatch thread, which owns all UI state.","name":"SwingUtilities.invokeLater","where":"Swing"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Ledger {","    private final ExecutorService loop =","        Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();","    private long balance;","","    CompletableFuture<Long> deposit(long cents) {","        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(","            () -> balance += cents, loop);","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"active-object","why":"State is confined to one thread; method calls become queued requests that return futures."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A pool spreads independent tasks across workers. Here one thread owns the state, so the calls are serialized on purpose."},{"ref":"future-promise","why":"Futures are returned, but the defining feature is the single owning thread and request queue."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"A proxy would delegate on the caller's thread; this hands the work to another thread."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"active-object","why":"One owning thread runs queued requests in order, and callers receive futures instead of waiting."},{"ref":"read-write-lock","why":"Every print is a write, so callers would still queue up and block, and the lock gives no ordering guarantee."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A pool runs tasks in parallel, which would call the unsafe SDK from several threads at once."},{"ref":"immutable-object","why":"Immutability removes shared mutable state; the printer is inherently stateful and cannot be made immutable."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A vendor SDK for a label printer is not thread-safe and each print takes about 300 ms. Forty request threads need to print labels. They should not block, and prints must happen one at a time in arrival order."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.*;","final class Counter { private int t; int add(int n) { return t += n; } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`peek` runs on the caller's thread, so it races with the scheduler thread on the servant. Route it through the queue too and return a future.","flawLines":[11],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class ActiveCounter {","    private final Counter servant = new Counter();","    private final ExecutorService loop =","        Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();","","    CompletableFuture<Integer> add(int n) {","        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(","            () -> servant.add(n), loop);","    }","","    int peek() { return servant.add(0); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the pattern."},{"blocks":[["var counter = new ActiveCounter();"],["var first = counter.add(5);","var second = counter.add(7);"],["int total = second.get();"],["System.out.println(total + \" \" + first.get());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;","final class ActiveCounter { private int t; CompletableFuture<Integer> add(int n) { return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(t += n); } }","class Snippet { void run() throws Exception {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the object, enqueue requests and keep their futures, wait on a result, then use it. Requests run in enqueue order, so the second future implies the first.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the caller's code so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Calls become queued requests, executed by one thread","Callers get futures and do not block","State is confined to the scheduler thread, so no locks","FIFO order per object","A pool shares tasks across threads; an active object serializes one servant"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"An active object owns one thread and a queue. Calls become requests on the queue and return futures, and the scheduler thread runs them one at a time on a plain servant. Because only that thread touches the state, no locks are needed. A thread pool instead runs unrelated tasks in parallel on any worker.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Active Object in your own words: how does it avoid locks, and how does it differ from a thread pool?"}],"id":"active-object"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Handle many I/O sources on few threads: wait for readiness, then dispatch each ready event to its handler.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **demultiplexer** blocks until some sources are ready and returns them. The **reactor** loop then calls the handler registered for each event, on a single thread. Handlers must be short and non-blocking, because they run on the loop itself.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Inverts control:** you register interest; the loop calls you.","**Scales:** thousands of idle connections cost memory, not threads.","**Contract:** a handler that blocks stalls every other source."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"This is the model behind `java.nio.channels.Selector`, Netty and Node.js: one loop, many channels.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"accept a\naccept b\nread b: hello\nread a: ping\nclose a open=[b]\nread b: bye\nclose b open=[]","file":"Reactor.java","regions":{"events":[6,12],"reactor":[14,38],"wire":[53,64]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.EnumMap;\nimport java.util.LinkedHashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nenum Op { ACCEPT, READ, CLOSE }\n\nrecord Ready(String channel, Op op, String data) { }\n\ninterface Demultiplexer {\n    List<Ready> select();\n}\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface Handler {\n    void handle(Ready event);\n}\n\nfinal class Reactor {\n    private final Demultiplexer demux;\n    private final Map<Op, Handler> handlers = new EnumMap<>(Op.class);\n\n    Reactor(Demultiplexer demux) { this.demux = demux; }\n\n    Reactor on(Op op, Handler handler) {\n        handlers.put(op, handler);\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    void run() {\n        List<Ready> batch;\n        while (!(batch = demux.select()).isEmpty()) {\n            for (Ready event : batch) {\n                handlers.get(event.op()).handle(event);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static Ready ev(String ch, Op op, String data) {\n        return new Ready(ch, op, data);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var script = List.of(\n            List.of(ev(\"a\", Op.ACCEPT, \"\"), ev(\"b\", Op.ACCEPT, \"\")),\n            List.of(ev(\"b\", Op.READ, \"HELLO\"), ev(\"a\", Op.READ, \"PING\")),\n            List.of(ev(\"a\", Op.CLOSE, \"\")),\n            List.of(ev(\"b\", Op.READ, \"BYE\"), ev(\"b\", Op.CLOSE, \"\")));\n        var open = new LinkedHashSet<String>();\n        var batches = script.iterator();\n        new Reactor(() -> batches.hasNext() ? batches.next() : List.of())\n            .on(Op.ACCEPT, e -> {\n                open.add(e.channel());\n                System.out.println(\"accept \" + e.channel());\n            })\n            .on(Op.READ, e -> System.out.println(\n                \"read \" + e.channel() + \": \" + e.data().toLowerCase()))\n            .on(Op.CLOSE, e -> {\n                open.remove(e.channel());\n                System.out.println(\"close \" + e.channel() + \" open=\" + open);\n            })\n            .run();\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The vocabulary: an operation kind, a ready event naming its channel, and a demultiplexer whose `select` returns the next batch of ready events.","region":"events"},{"note":"The loop selects, then dispatches each event to the handler registered for its operation. An empty batch ends the loop. No other threads exist.","region":"reactor"},{"note":"A lambda replays scripted batches, standing in for the OS; a real one blocks in `Selector.select()`. Handlers share `open` without locks: only the loop thread runs them.","region":"wire"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A chat gateway holding 100,000 mostly idle WebSocket connections.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Almost every connection is silent at any moment. A thread per connection would burn memory on blocked stacks. The gateway runs a few event loops, each owning a share of the sockets. A loop wakes when bytes arrive, decodes the frame, and calls the pipeline; slow work, such as a database lookup, is handed to a separate executor.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["EventLoopGroup loops = new NioEventLoopGroup(4);","ServerBootstrap b = new ServerBootstrap()","    .group(loops)","    .channel(NioServerSocketChannel.class)","    .childHandler(new FrameHandlerInit());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: four threads serve 100,000 connections, and each connection's handlers run on one loop without locks.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"reactor","label":"select","rel":"association","to":"demux"},{"from":"reactor","label":"handlers","rel":"aggregation","to":"handler"},{"from":"accept","rel":"realizes","to":"handler"},{"from":"read","rel":"realizes","to":"handler"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"reactor","kind":"class","members":["- handlers: Map<Op, Handler>","+ on(op, handler)","+ run()"],"name":"Reactor","role":"Dispatcher","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"demux","kind":"interface","members":["+ select(): List<Ready>"],"name":"Demultiplexer","role":"SynchronousDemultiplexer","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"handler","kind":"interface","members":["+ handle(event)"],"name":"Handler","role":"EventHandler","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"accept","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(event)"],"name":"AcceptHandler","role":"ConcreteHandler","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"read","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(event)"],"name":"ReadHandler","role":"ConcreteHandler","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"demux","text":"Selector.select() blocks"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"reactor","label":"select()","style":"call","to":"demux"},{"from":"os","label":"channels ready","style":"async","to":"demux"},{"from":"demux","label":"batch of events","style":"return","to":"reactor"},{"from":"reactor","label":"handle(event)","style":"call","to":"h"},{"from":"h","label":"return fast","style":"return","to":"reactor"},{"from":"reactor","label":"select() again","style":"call","to":"demux"}],"participants":[{"id":"os","name":"OS / channels"},{"id":"demux","name":"Demultiplexer","role":"SynchronousDemultiplexer"},{"id":"reactor","name":"Reactor","role":"Dispatcher"},{"id":"h","name":"Handler","role":"EventHandler"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Many connections are mostly idle and work per event is small.","You want few threads and handlers that need no locks."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Each event needs long computation or blocking calls on the loop thread.","Concurrency is modest and a thread (or virtual thread) per connection reads simply."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Blocking in a handler stalls every channel on that loop.","Callback chains scatter one logical flow across handlers and make errors hard to trace.","A busy loop with unbounded work per event starves other channels."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads make blocking thread-per-connection cheap enough for many workloads that once required a reactor.","Reactors remain the base of Netty and most async servers, but you rarely hand-roll one."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Observer notifies subscribers of state changes in-process. A reactor waits on I/O readiness from the OS and dispatches by event type, usually to one handler.","ref":"observer","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Producer-consumer passes work between threads through a queue. A reactor has no hand-off: the same thread detects the event and runs its handler.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Handlers that must block are offloaded to a pool so the loop stays responsive. Netty and Tomcat's NIO connector use this split.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An active object serializes requests to one object on its own thread. A reactor serializes I/O events for many channels on one loop.","ref":"active-object","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Netty's channel pipeline is a chain of handlers that each event passes through after the loop has dispatched it.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`select()` blocks until registered channels are ready, then `selectedKeys()` yields them for dispatch.","name":"java.nio.channels.Selector","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Each channel is bound to one event loop thread, and its pipeline handlers run there.","name":"EventLoop / NioEventLoopGroup","where":"Netty"},{"detail":"A single-threaded loop dispatches I/O callbacks via epoll, kqueue or IOCP.","name":"libuv event loop","where":"Node.js"},{"detail":"Verticles run on event loop threads and must not block them.","name":"Vertx event loop","where":"Vert.x"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.nio.channels.Selector;","class Snippet {","    Selector selector;","    boolean running;","    void loop() throws IOException {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["while (running) {","    selector.select();","    var keys = selector.selectedKeys();","    for (var key : keys) {","        ((Runnable) key.attachment()).run();","    }","    keys.clear();","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"reactor","why":"One thread waits for readiness on many channels and dispatches each ready one to its handler."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Observers are called when a subject changes. Here a blocking wait on the OS decides what is ready."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"There is no queue between threads: the same thread detects the event and handles it."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"No worker threads or task queue appear; everything runs on the loop thread."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"reactor","why":"A few loops wait on readiness for all sockets and dispatch tiny handlers, so idle connections cost no threads."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A thread per connection would be blocked on reads almost all the time, wasting thousands of threads."},{"ref":"fork-join","why":"The work is not recursive CPU-bound splitting; it is many sources waiting for I/O."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"One queue and thread per connection would reintroduce thousands of threads and add futures nobody needs."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A metrics collector accepts 20,000 long-lived TCP connections. Each sends a tiny packet every 30 seconds. The team wants a handful of threads and per-packet work takes a few microseconds."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","enum Op { READ }","record Ready(String channel, Op op) { }","interface Handler { void handle(Ready e); }","interface Demultiplexer { List<Ready> select(); }","interface Db { String findUser(String id); }","class Snippet {","    Demultiplexer demux;","    Db db;","    Map<Op, Handler> handlers = new EnumMap<>(Op.class);","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"A blocking database call on the loop thread stalls every other channel while it waits. Hand slow work to an executor and post its result back as a new event.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["void run() {","    List<Ready> batch;","    while (!(batch = demux.select()).isEmpty()) {","        for (Ready event : batch) {","            var user = db.findUser(event.channel());","            handlers.get(event.op()).handle(event);","        }","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the pattern."},{"blocks":[["var reactor = new Reactor(demux);"],["reactor.on(Op.ACCEPT, accepts);","reactor.on(Op.READ, reads);"],["reactor.run();"],["System.out.println(\"stopped\");"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","enum Op { ACCEPT, READ }","record Ready(String channel, Op op) { }","interface Handler { void handle(Ready e); }","interface Demultiplexer { List<Ready> select(); }","final class Reactor {","    Reactor(Demultiplexer d) { }","    Reactor on(Op op, Handler h) { return this; }","    void run() { }","}","class Snippet {","    void go(Demultiplexer demux, Handler accepts, Handler reads) {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Create the reactor, register handlers before the loop starts dispatching, run the loop, and report only after it returns. `run` blocks until the demultiplexer is exhausted.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the setup so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One loop waits for readiness on many sources","The same thread dispatches to registered handlers","Handlers run on the loop and must not block","Offload slow work to an executor","Virtual threads reduce, but do not remove, the need for it"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A reactor thread blocks in a demultiplexer, such as `Selector.select`, until some channels are ready. It then calls the handler registered for each ready event, on that same thread. A few threads can serve thousands of connections. Handlers must never block or run long, since that stalls every other channel.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Reactor pattern in your own words: who waits, who runs the handlers, and what must a handler never do?"}],"id":"reactor"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Recursively split a problem until the pieces are small, solve them in parallel, then combine the results.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **task** either computes directly (below a **threshold**) or splits itself in two, **forks** one half onto the pool, computes the other itself, and **joins**. A `ForkJoinPool` runs these tasks with **work stealing**: idle workers take tasks from the tails of busy workers' deques.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Fits:** CPU-bound, divisible, side-effect-free work.","**Threshold:** too small and overhead dominates; too large and cores sit idle.","**Combine:** results must be mergeable, such as sums, merges or counts."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Parallel streams and `Arrays.parallelSort` run on the common fork-join pool.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"parallelism: 4\nsum: 49500000\nmatches: true","file":"ForkJoin.java","regions":{"compute":[16,29],"invoke":[38,43],"task":[4,14]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;\nimport java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;\n\n@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")\nfinal class SumTask extends RecursiveTask<Long> {\n    private static final int THRESHOLD = 1_000;\n    private final int[] data;\n    private final int from, to;\n\n    SumTask(int[] data, int from, int to) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.from = from;\n        this.to = to;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected Long compute() {\n        if (to - from <= THRESHOLD) {\n            long sum = 0;\n            for (int i = from; i < to; i++) sum += data[i];\n            return sum;\n        }\n        int mid = (from + to) >>> 1;\n        var left = new SumTask(data, from, mid);\n        var right = new SumTask(data, mid, to);\n        left.fork();\n        long r = right.compute();\n        return left.join() + r;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = new int[1_000_000];\n        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) data[i] = i % 100;\n        long sequential = 0;\n        for (int v : data) sequential += v;\n        try (var pool = new ForkJoinPool(4)) {\n            long parallel = pool.invoke(new SumTask(data, 0, data.length));\n            System.out.println(\"parallelism: \" + pool.getParallelism());\n            System.out.println(\"sum: \" + parallel);\n            System.out.println(\"matches: \" + (parallel == sequential));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A `RecursiveTask<Long>` describes a slice of the array. It holds indices, not copies, so splitting costs nothing.","region":"task"},{"note":"Below the threshold it sums directly. Otherwise it forks the left half, computes the right half itself, then joins. Computing one half inline avoids queuing a task just to wait for it.","region":"compute"},{"note":"`invoke` submits the root task and waits. Each task's fork and join is a happens-before edge, so results are visible without extra synchronization.","region":"invoke"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Counting error lines across a large set of log files.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A nightly job scans thousands of log files of very different sizes. Splitting by file leaves cores idle when one file is huge. A task over a list of files splits the list; a task over one large file splits its byte range. Work stealing keeps all cores busy, and the counts are merged by addition.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["record Scan(List<Path> files) extends RecursiveTask<Long> { /* ... */ }","","long errors = pool.invoke(new Scan(allFiles));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: near-linear speedup on skewed input without hand-tuning how files are assigned to threads.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"pool","label":"runs","rel":"dependency","to":"task"},{"from":"sum","rel":"extends","to":"task"},{"from":"sum","label":"subtasks","rel":"composition","to":"sum"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"pool","kind":"class","members":["+ invoke(task): T","- work-stealing deques"],"name":"ForkJoinPool","role":"Pool","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"task","kind":"abstract","members":["# compute(): V","+ fork()","+ join(): V"],"name":"RecursiveTask<V>","role":"Task","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"sum","kind":"class","members":["- from, to: int","- THRESHOLD: int"],"name":"SumTask","role":"ConcreteTask","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"sum","text":"split below THRESHOLD? no: compute"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"root","label":"fork()","style":"async","to":"left"},{"from":"root","label":"compute()","style":"call","to":"right"},{"from":"right","label":"partial sum","style":"return","to":"root"},{"from":"root","label":"join()","style":"call","to":"left"},{"from":"left","label":"partial sum","style":"return","to":"root"}],"participants":[{"id":"root","name":"Root task","role":"Task"},{"id":"left","name":"Left half","role":"Subtask"},{"id":"right","name":"Right half","role":"Subtask"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The problem divides into independent, similar subproblems.","Work is CPU-bound and sizes are uneven, so stealing helps."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Tasks block on I/O or locks; this starves the pool's few threads.","The data is small: splitting overhead exceeds the work."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Calling `join()` on the first fork before computing the other half serializes the work.","Shared mutable state between subtasks needs care; prefer returning values.","A poor threshold yields tiny tasks or too few of them."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Parallel streams and `Arrays.parallelSort` use the common pool, so a hand-written task is often unnecessary.","Virtual threads suit blocking I/O; fork-join remains the tool for CPU-bound divide and conquer."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A fork-join pool is a thread pool specialized for tasks that spawn and join subtasks, with per-worker deques and work stealing.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"`ForkJoinTask` is a future: `fork` starts it and `join` waits for the result. The pattern adds recursive splitting.","ref":"future-promise","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Producer-consumer streams independent items through a queue. Fork-join breaks one large item into pieces and merges the answers.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Both recurse over a tree of parts. Composite is about uniform treatment of structure; fork-join is about parallel execution and combining.","ref":"composite","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`commonPool()` is shared by parallel streams, `CompletableFuture` async methods without an executor, and `parallelSort`.","name":"java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The base classes for value-returning and void divide-and-conquer tasks.","name":"RecursiveTask / RecursiveAction","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Splits the array, sorts the pieces in parallel on the common pool, and merges them.","name":"Arrays.parallelSort","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"The stream is split with spliterators and the pieces are processed as fork-join tasks.","name":"Stream.parallel()","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")","class Scan extends RecursiveTask<Long> {","    final int lo, hi;","    Scan(int lo, int hi) { this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; }","    long scanSequentially(int a, int b) { return b - a; }","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["protected Long compute() {","    if (hi - lo <= 2_000) {","        return scanSequentially(lo, hi);","    }","    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;","    var left = new Scan(lo, mid);","    var right = new Scan(mid, hi);","    left.fork();","    return right.compute() + left.join();","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"fork-join","why":"The task splits itself, forks one half, computes the other, and joins to combine results."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A pool runs tasks, but this task creates and waits for its own subtasks, which a plain pool task should not."},{"ref":"composite","why":"Composite treats a tree of parts uniformly. Here the recursion exists to run parts in parallel."},{"ref":"future-promise","why":"The fork returns a future-like handle, but the defining feature is recursive split and combine."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"fork-join","why":"Split the range recursively, compute partial maxima in parallel, and combine with `max`."},{"ref":"producer-consumer","why":"There is no stream of independent jobs; one large input has to be divided."},{"ref":"active-object","why":"Funneling every request through one thread would use a single core."},{"ref":"reactor","why":"The work is CPU-bound, not waiting on I/O readiness; a single event loop would run on one core."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A service must find the maximum value in a 200-million-element array and wants all cores used. The data is in memory, the work is pure computation, and results from parts combine with `Math.max`."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")","class SumTask extends RecursiveTask<Long> {","    static final int THRESHOLD = 1000;","    int[] data; int from, to;","    SumTask(int[] d, int f, int t) { data = d; from = f; to = t; }","    long sequentialSum(int a, int b) { return b - a; }","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"Joining the left half before computing the right half makes the current thread wait, so the halves run one after the other. Compute the right half first, then join the left.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["protected Long compute() {","    if (to - from <= THRESHOLD) {","        return sequentialSum(from, to);","    }","    int mid = (from + to) >>> 1;","    var left = new SumTask(data, from, mid);","    var right = new SumTask(data, mid, to);","    left.fork();","    long l = left.join();","    long r = right.compute();","    return l + r;","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that defeats the parallelism."},{"blocks":[["int mid = (from + to) >>> 1;"],["var left = new SumTask(data, from, mid);","var right = new SumTask(data, mid, to);"],["left.fork();"],["return right.compute() + left.join();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;","@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")","class SumTask extends RecursiveTask<Long> {","    int[] data; int from, to;","    SumTask(int[] d, int f, int t) { data = d; from = f; to = t; }","    protected Long compute() {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Find the midpoint, build both halves, start one asynchronously, then compute the other inline and join the forked one last. A join before the fork would wait on an unstarted task.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the split step so that each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Split until below a threshold, then compute directly","Fork one half, compute the other inline, then join","Work stealing balances uneven subtasks across workers","Threshold trades task overhead against parallelism","Best for CPU-bound, side-effect-free work"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A task solves small inputs directly and otherwise splits in two, forks one half, computes the other, and joins to combine. Each worker has a deque of tasks; idle workers steal from busy ones, balancing uneven work. The threshold balances overhead against parallelism: small enough to spread across cores, large enough to amortize task costs.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Fork-Join in your own words: how a task splits, what work stealing does, and how to choose a threshold."}],"id":"fork-join"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Split a system into horizontal layers, each depending only on the layer directly beneath it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Typical layers are **presentation**, **business** (service/domain) and **data access**. A call travels down and a result travels back up. Dependencies point one way, so you can replace or test a layer without touching the ones above it.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Strict layering:** a layer may use only the one directly below.","**Relaxed layering:** a layer may skip down, trading isolation for fewer pass-through methods.","**Never:** a lower layer referring to a higher one."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Layers are about dependency direction, not deployment. Three layers in one JVM are still layered; three services are not automatically layered.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"200 Refactoring\n409 already on loan\n404 unknown book","file":"LayeredArchitecture.java","regions":{"data":[6,16],"domain":[4,4],"presentation":[33,47],"service":[18,31],"wiring":[51,53]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nrecord Book(String isbn, String title, boolean onLoan) {}\n\ninterface BookRepository {\n    Book find(String isbn);\n    void save(Book book);\n}\n\nfinal class InMemoryBookRepository implements BookRepository {\n    private final Map<String, Book> rows = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public Book find(String isbn) { return rows.get(isbn); }\n    public void save(Book book) { rows.put(book.isbn(), book); }\n}\n\nfinal class LoanService {\n    private final BookRepository books;\n\n    LoanService(BookRepository books) { this.books = books; }\n\n    Book lend(String isbn) {\n        Book book = books.find(isbn);\n        if (book == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"unknown book\");\n        if (book.onLoan()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"already on loan\");\n        Book lent = new Book(book.isbn(), book.title(), true);\n        books.save(lent);\n        return lent;\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class LoanController {\n    private final LoanService service;\n\n    LoanController(LoanService service) { this.service = service; }\n\n    String lend(String isbn) {\n        try {\n            return \"200 \" + service.lend(isbn).title();\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            return \"404 \" + e.getMessage();\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            return \"409 \" + e.getMessage();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var books = new InMemoryBookRepository();\n        books.save(new Book(\"111\", \"Refactoring\", false));\n        var controller = new LoanController(new LoanService(books));\n        System.out.println(controller.lend(\"111\"));\n        System.out.println(controller.lend(\"111\"));\n        System.out.println(controller.lend(\"999\"));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A plain record that every layer may use. It carries data and no framework or persistence type.","region":"domain"},{"note":"The bottom layer exposes `BookRepository` and hides storage behind it. Swapping the map for JDBC changes only this layer.","region":"data"},{"note":"The business layer holds the lending rule. It knows the repository interface but nothing about HTTP, status codes or views.","region":"service"},{"note":"The top layer translates between the outside protocol and the service: parsing input, mapping exceptions to status codes.","region":"presentation"},{"note":"Construction runs bottom-up, matching the dependency direction. Each layer receives only the layer below it.","region":"wiring"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A claims back office maintained by rotating teams.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Adjusters use a web UI to register and approve insurance claims. Validation and approval limits live in a service layer, persistence in a data layer, and the UI team never writes SQL. A new engineer can predict where a change goes: a screen change is presentation, a payout limit is business, a new index is data.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["claims-web      // controllers, forms, templates","claims-service  // ApprovalPolicy, ClaimService","claims-data     // ClaimRepository, JDBC adapter","// claims-web depends on claims-service, never the reverse"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: predictable change locations, and service rules can be tested with a fake repository, no web server or database.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"ctl","rel":"dependency","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","rel":"dependency","to":"repo"},{"from":"mem","rel":"realizes","to":"repo"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"ctl","kind":"class","members":["+ lend(isbn): String"],"name":"LoanController","role":"Presentation","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ lend(isbn): Book"],"name":"LoanService","role":"Business","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"repo","kind":"interface","members":["+ find(isbn): Book","+ save(book)"],"name":"BookRepository","role":"Data access","row":2},{"col":0.5,"id":"mem","kind":"class","members":["- rows: Map"],"name":"InMemoryBookRepository","role":"Data access","row":3}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"svc","text":"knows nothing about HTTP"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"lend(isbn)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"find(isbn)","style":"call","to":"r"},{"from":"r","label":"book","style":"return","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"save(lent)","style":"call","to":"r"},{"from":"s","label":"book","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Controller","role":"Presentation"},{"id":"s","name":"LoanService","role":"Business"},{"id":"r","name":"Repository","role":"Data"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The team is small to mid-sized and the domain is mostly CRUD plus validation.","You want a structure every newcomer already recognizes.","Teams can specialize by layer, such as UI and persistence."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Business rules are rich and long-lived: layers tie them to the database.","Many integrations come and go; consider [Hexagonal Architecture](kata:hexagonal)."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The domain ends up depending on persistence classes, since data access sits below it.","Pass-through methods: a service that only forwards to a repository.","Entities or ORM proxies leaking into the presentation layer."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Every layer has an identical mirror class: `UserEntity`, `UserModel`, `UserDto`, `UserView`.","One feature requires edits in five packages."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Java modules (`module-info.java`) and ArchUnit rules can enforce the dependency direction at build time.","Records give every layer cheap, immutable data carriers across layer boundaries."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Layers point down toward storage, so the domain depends on persistence. Hexagonal points every dependency toward the domain, which owns the interfaces that infrastructure implements.","ref":"hexagonal","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"MVC splits the presentation layer into model, view and controller roles. Layering stacks whole responsibilities; MVC divides one of them.","ref":"mvc","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"The repository is the usual contract of the data layer, giving the business layer a collection-like view of storage.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A service layer often acts as a facade over the domain. The facade simplifies access; layering also restricts who may depend on whom.","ref":"facade","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Stereotype annotations in `org.springframework.stereotype` that name the three classic layers.","name":"@Controller, @Service, @Repository","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Fluent API that fails the build when a lower layer imports a higher one.","name":"Architectures.layeredArchitecture()","where":"ArchUnit"},{"detail":"The module system rejects compilation when a module uses a package it does not require, enforcing direction.","name":"module-info.java requires","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Application, transport, internet and link layers each use only the one below.","name":"TCP/IP protocol stack","where":"Networking"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["record Order() {}","class JdbcOrderRepo { Order save(Order o) { return o; } }","class Snippet {","  static Order parse(String s) { return new Order(); }","  static void validate(Order o) {}","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["class OrderEndpoint {","    private final OrderService service;","    OrderEndpoint(OrderService s) { this.service = s; }","    String post(String body) {","        return service.place(parse(body)).toString();","    }","}","class OrderService {","    private final JdbcOrderRepo repo = new JdbcOrderRepo();","    Order place(Order o) { validate(o); return repo.save(o); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"layered-architecture","why":"Each class talks only to the one below: endpoint to service to repository, with no references upward."},{"ref":"hexagonal","why":"The service constructs a concrete `JdbcOrderRepo`; in hexagonal the core owns a port and an adapter implements it."},{"ref":"mvc","why":"There is no view or model-change notification; this is a call chain, not three cooperating roles."},{"ref":"facade","why":"The service adds rules and persistence calls; it is not just a simplified front for a subsystem."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"layered-architecture","why":"Simple, familiar structure that matches a CRUD-shaped problem without extra indirection."},{"ref":"hexagonal","why":"Ports for integrations that do not exist add interfaces and mapping with no payoff here."},{"ref":"cqrs","why":"Separate read and write models solve scaling and complexity problems this tool does not have."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"Storing events adds replay and versioning cost for a system that only needs current state."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A 4-person team builds an internal admin tool: forms over one relational database, a few validation rules, no external integrations, expected lifetime of years. New hires should find their way quickly."},{"compile":{"template":["interface HttpRequest { String param(String k); }","record Book(boolean onLoan) { Book lent() { return new Book(true); } }","interface BookRepository { Book find(String i); void save(Book b); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The service accepts a web-layer type, so the business layer now depends upward. Take plain arguments, `lend(String isbn)`, and let the controller extract them from the request.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class LoanService {","    private final BookRepository books;","","    LoanService(BookRepository b) { this.books = b; }","","    Book lend(HttpRequest request) {","        String isbn = request.param(\"isbn\");","        Book book = books.find(isbn);","        if (book.onLoan()) throw new IllegalStateException();","        books.save(book.lent());","        return book;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Layered Architecture pattern."},{"blocks":[["var repo = new InMemoryBookRepository();"],["var service = new LoanService(repo);"],["var controller = new LoanController(service);"],["String response = controller.lend(\"111\");"],["System.out.println(response);"]],"compile":{"template":["class InMemoryBookRepository {}","class LoanService { LoanService(InMemoryBookRepository r) {} }","class LoanController { LoanController(LoanService s) {} String lend(String i) { return i; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Build bottom-up: each layer is constructed from the layer below it, and the request is sent through the top.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Dependencies point strictly downward","Typical layers: presentation, business, data","A layer is replaceable behind its interface","Weakness: domain depends on data access","Differs from MVC: layers stack, MVC roles cooperate"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Code is arranged in layers, typically presentation, business and data access, and each layer may depend only on the one below it. That isolates responsibilities and makes layers replaceable. The weakness is that the business layer ends up depending on persistence, and rules of thumb like this erode into pass-through code.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Layered Architecture: what rule defines it, and what is its main weakness?"}],"id":"layered-architecture"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Put the application core in the center and talk to everything outside it through ports implemented by adapters.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The core defines **ports**: interfaces for what it offers (inbound) and what it needs (outbound). **Adapters** translate between a port and a technology such as HTTP, SQL or a message broker. Dependencies always point inward, so the core compiles and runs with no framework.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Driving adapters:** call the core (REST, CLI, scheduler).","**Driven adapters:** the core calls them (database, mail, payments).","**Test seam:** replace any adapter with a fake."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The same idea is called Ports and Adapters, Onion or Clean Architecture; the details differ but the inward dependency rule is shared.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ana@x.org -> registered\nbad -> invalid\nana@x.org -> duplicate\nmails: [ana@x.org]","file":"Hexagonal.java","regions":{"adapters":[37,48],"assembly":[53,53],"core":[19,35],"driver":[54,56],"ports":[6,17]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\ninterface RegisterUser {\n    String register(String email);\n}\n\ninterface UserStore {\n    boolean exists(String email);\n    void add(String email);\n}\n\ninterface Mailer {\n    void welcome(String email);\n}\n\nfinal class Registration implements RegisterUser {\n    private final UserStore store;\n    private final Mailer mailer;\n\n    Registration(UserStore store, Mailer mailer) {\n        this.store = store;\n        this.mailer = mailer;\n    }\n\n    public String register(String email) {\n        if (!email.contains(\"@\")) return \"invalid\";\n        if (store.exists(email)) return \"duplicate\";\n        store.add(email);\n        mailer.welcome(email);\n        return \"registered\";\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class MemoryUserStore implements UserStore {\n    private final Set<String> emails = new HashSet<>();\n\n    public boolean exists(String email) { return emails.contains(email); }\n    public void add(String email) { emails.add(email); }\n}\n\nfinal class RecordingMailer implements Mailer {\n    final List<String> sent = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void welcome(String email) { sent.add(email); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var mailer = new RecordingMailer();\n        RegisterUser app = new Registration(new MemoryUserStore(), mailer);\n        for (String line : List.of(\"ana@x.org\", \"bad\", \"ana@x.org\")) {\n            System.out.println(line + \" -> \" + app.register(line));\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"mails: \" + mailer.sent);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Three interfaces owned by the core. `RegisterUser` is inbound; `UserStore` and `Mailer` are outbound and describe needs in the core's own words.","region":"ports"},{"note":"The use case implements the inbound port and uses only outbound ports. It imports no framework and no adapter.","region":"core"},{"note":"Driven adapters implement outbound ports. Here they are in-memory; a JDBC store or SMTP mailer would replace them without touching the core.","region":"adapters"},{"note":"Assembly is the only place that sees both sides, choosing which adapters plug into the core.","region":"assembly"},{"note":"A driving adapter turns an outside stimulus into a port call. A REST controller or message listener would play this role.","region":"driver"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Ride dispatch that must survive swapping map providers.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A dispatch core assigns drivers to riders using ETA and price rules. It asks a `RoutingPort` for travel times and a `DriverLocations` port for positions. One adapter calls a commercial maps API, another replays recorded traffic for tests, a third reads a cache. REST, gRPC and a Kafka listener all drive the same `AssignDriver` port.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface RoutingPort { Duration eta(Point a, Point b); }","// adapters: GoogleMapsRouting, CachedRouting, RecordedRouting","interface AssignDriver { Assignment assign(RideRequest r); }","// driving: RestAssign, KafkaAssign"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: changing map vendors or transport protocols never touches dispatch rules, and rules are tested at memory speed.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"http","rel":"association","to":"in"},{"from":"core","rel":"realizes","to":"in"},{"from":"core","rel":"association","to":"store"},{"from":"core","rel":"association","to":"mail"},{"from":"mem","rel":"realizes","to":"store"},{"from":"rec","rel":"realizes","to":"mail"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"http","kind":"class","members":["+ post(email)"],"name":"HttpAdapter","role":"Driving adapter","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"in","kind":"interface","members":["+ register(email)"],"name":"RegisterUser","role":"Inbound port","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"core","kind":"class","members":["- store: UserStore","- mailer: Mailer"],"name":"Registration","role":"Application core","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"store","kind":"interface","members":["+ exists(email)","+ add(email)"],"name":"UserStore","role":"Outbound port","row":3},{"col":1,"id":"mail","kind":"interface","members":["+ welcome(email)"],"name":"Mailer","role":"Outbound port","row":3},{"col":0,"id":"mem","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"MemoryUserStore","role":"Driven adapter","row":4},{"col":1,"id":"rec","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"RecordingMailer","role":"Driven adapter","row":4}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"core","text":"imports no adapter"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"h","label":"register(email)","style":"call","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"exists(email)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"false","style":"return","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"add(email)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"c","label":"welcome(email)","style":"call","to":"m"},{"from":"c","label":"registered","style":"return","to":"h"}],"participants":[{"id":"h","name":"HttpAdapter","role":"Driving"},{"id":"c","name":"Registration","role":"Core"},{"id":"s","name":"UserStore","role":"Port"},{"id":"m","name":"Mailer","role":"Port"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The domain has real rules worth protecting from framework and vendor churn.","Several entry points drive the same use cases: REST, CLI, events.","You want core tests that need no database or network."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The app is thin CRUD: ports would mirror the framework one to one.","The team is small and the infrastructure will not change."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Ports shaped like the technology (`SqlPort`) instead of the core's needs.","Domain objects annotated with persistence or JSON annotations, quietly coupling the core.","Mapping at every boundary becomes a tax if overdone."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One interface per class with a single implementation and no second adapter in sight.","Use cases that only forward to one outbound call."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and sealed interfaces make small, immutable port messages cheap.","Java modules can make adapters depend on the core, never the reverse."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Layering points dependencies down to storage. Hexagonal points them inward and lets the core own the interfaces that infrastructure implements.","ref":"layered-architecture","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Hexagonal applies the Dependency Inversion Principle at architectural scale: the core defines abstractions, outer code depends on them.","ref":"dip","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Each driven or driving adapter is a GoF Adapter converting between the core's port and an external API.","ref":"adapter","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A repository interface is a typical outbound port, expressed in domain terms and implemented by a persistence adapter.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Injection wires adapters into the core at the composition root, so the core never builds its own adapters.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Applications code against the JDBC API (port); each vendor driver is an adapter.","name":"JDBC java.sql.Driver and DataSource","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Code logs through the API; Logback, Log4j 2 or `java.util.logging` bindings adapt it.","name":"org.slf4j.Logger","where":"SLF4J"},{"detail":"Application logic uses channels; Kafka and RabbitMQ binders adapt the broker.","name":"Binder","where":"Spring Cloud Stream"},{"detail":"Rule builder that checks domain, application and adapter packages for inward-only dependencies.","name":"Architectures.onionArchitecture()","where":"ArchUnit"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["record Money() {}","record Receipt() {}","record Cart() { Money total() { return new Money(); } }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface PlaceOrder { Receipt place(Cart cart); }","interface Payments { Receipt charge(Money amount); }","","final class OrderCore implements PlaceOrder {","    private final Payments payments;","    OrderCore(Payments payments) { this.payments = payments; }","    public Receipt place(Cart cart) {","        return payments.charge(cart.total());","    }","}","final class HttpOrders { HttpOrders(PlaceOrder core) { /* ... */ } }","final class CardGatewayPayments implements Payments {","    public Receipt charge(Money m) { return new Receipt(); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"hexagonal","why":"The core offers one interface, needs another, and adapters sit on both sides, each depending inward."},{"ref":"layered-architecture","why":"Layering would have the core call a concrete lower layer; here the core owns `Payments` and the outside implements it."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"`CardGatewayPayments` is an adapter, but the snippet's shape is the whole inward-pointing arrangement, not a single wrapper."},{"ref":"facade","why":"`OrderCore` hides no subsystem behind a simplified API; it is the domain logic itself."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"hexagonal","why":"Rules sit in a core with ports; transports and providers are adapters you can swap and fake."},{"ref":"layered-architecture","why":"Rules would depend on persistence and transport-shaped layers, making swaps and fast tests harder."},{"ref":"mvc","why":"MVC separates UI roles; it says nothing about swapping providers or driving the core from events."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade simplifies access to a subsystem but doesn't invert dependencies away from vendors."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Your pricing engine must run behind REST today and Kafka next quarter, switch payment providers per market, and be tested without any container. Business rules change weekly."},{"compile":{"template":["record Money() {}","record Receipt() {}","record Cart() { Money total() { return new Money(); } }","interface PlaceOrder { Receipt place(Cart c); }","interface Orders { void save(Cart c, Receipt r); }","class StripeClient { Receipt charge(Money m) { return new Receipt(); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The core constructs a concrete vendor client, so it depends outward and can't be tested or switched. Depend on a `Payments` port and inject the adapter.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Checkout implements PlaceOrder {","    private final StripeClient stripe = new StripeClient();","    private final Orders orders;","","    Checkout(Orders orders) { this.orders = orders; }","","    public Receipt place(Cart cart) {","        Receipt r = stripe.charge(cart.total());","        orders.save(cart, r);","        return r;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Hexagonal pattern."},{"blocks":[["var store = new MemoryUserStore();","var mailer = new RecordingMailer();"],["RegisterUser app = new Registration(store, mailer);"],["String result = app.register(\"ana@x.org\");"],["System.out.println(result + \" \" + mailer.sent);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface RegisterUser { String register(String e); }","class MemoryUserStore {}","class RecordingMailer { List<String> sent = new ArrayList<>(); }","class Registration implements RegisterUser { Registration(MemoryUserStore s, RecordingMailer m) {} public String register(String e) { return e; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the driven adapters first, hand them to the core through its constructor, then drive the core through its inbound port.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Core owns the ports","Inbound ports are driven by adapters, outbound ports are implemented by adapters","Dependencies point inward only","Core runs and tests without frameworks","Contrast: layered depends toward the database"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"The application core defines ports, interfaces for what it offers and what it needs. Adapters implement or call those ports for a specific technology, such as HTTP or SQL. Every dependency points inward at the core, which has no framework imports. That inverts the layered style and lets you swap or fake any adapter.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Hexagonal Architecture: what are ports and adapters, and which way do dependencies point?"}],"id":"hexagonal"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Split an interactive feature into a model for state and rules, a view for display, and a controller for input.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **model** owns data and business rules and knows nothing about the screen. The **view** renders model state. The **controller** translates user input into model operations. A change in the model reaches the view through notification, not through the controller.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Model:** state plus rules, UI-free.","**View:** output only, reads the model.","**Controller:** input in, model calls out."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Server-side web frameworks use a variant: the controller selects a model and a view per request, with no live notifications.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Target: 30C\nTarget: 30C\nTarget: 29C\nUnknown: sing","file":"Mvc.java","regions":{"controller":[28,40],"model":[4,20],"view":[22,26],"wiring":[44,46]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface ModelListener {\n    void changed(int target);\n}\n\nfinal class Thermostat {\n    private final List<ModelListener> listeners = new ArrayList<>();\n    private int target;\n\n    Thermostat(int target) { this.target = target; }\n\n    void addListener(ModelListener l) { listeners.add(l); }\n\n    void adjust(int delta) {\n        target = Math.max(10, Math.min(30, target + delta));\n        listeners.forEach(l -> l.changed(target));\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class ConsoleView implements ModelListener {\n    public void changed(int target) {\n        System.out.println(\"Target: \" + target + \"C\");\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Controller {\n    private final Thermostat model;\n\n    Controller(Thermostat model) { this.model = model; }\n\n    void onInput(String command) {\n        switch (command) {\n            case \"up\" -> model.adjust(1);\n            case \"down\" -> model.adjust(-1);\n            default -> System.out.println(\"Unknown: \" + command);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var model = new Thermostat(29);\n        model.addListener(new ConsoleView());\n        var controller = new Controller(model);\n        for (String input : List.of(\"up\", \"up\", \"down\", \"sing\")) {\n            controller.onInput(input);\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The model owns the target temperature and the clamping rule. It announces changes through a listener interface and has no import of any view.","region":"model"},{"note":"The view implements the listener and renders. It holds no rules and sends no commands to the model.","region":"view"},{"note":"The controller maps raw input to model operations. It does not format output or repeat the clamping rule.","region":"controller"},{"note":"Wiring registers the view as a model listener and gives the controller the model. The view and controller never reference each other.","region":"wiring"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Server-rendered account pages in a web portal.","type":"lead"},{"text":"In a request-driven MVC framework the controller receives `GET /accounts/42`, asks the domain model for the account, puts it into a view model and names a template. The template only renders. Changing the page layout never touches the account rules, and a JSON endpoint reuses the same model with a different view.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["@GetMapping(\"/accounts/{id}\")","String show(@PathVariable long id, Model m) {","    m.addAttribute(\"account\", accounts.find(id));","    return \"account/show\";   // view name","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: HTML, JSON and PDF views share one model, and account rules are testable without a servlet container.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"ctl","label":"updates","rel":"association","to":"model"},{"from":"model","label":"notifies","rel":"aggregation","to":"lis"},{"from":"view","rel":"realizes","to":"lis"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"ctl","kind":"class","members":["+ onInput(command)"],"name":"Controller","role":"Controller","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"view","kind":"class","members":["+ changed(target)"],"name":"ConsoleView","role":"View","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"model","kind":"class","members":["- target: int","+ adjust(delta)"],"name":"Thermostat","role":"Model","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"lis","kind":"interface","members":["+ changed(target)"],"name":"ModelListener","role":"Observer","row":1}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"u","label":"onInput(\"up\")","style":"call","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"adjust(1)","style":"call","to":"m"},{"from":"m","label":"changed(30)","style":"call","to":"v"},{"from":"m","label":"done","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"u","name":"User"},{"id":"c","name":"Controller","role":"Controller"},{"id":"m","name":"Thermostat","role":"Model"},{"id":"v","name":"ConsoleView","role":"View"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The same state needs several presentations or input paths.","UI-free rules should be tested without a screen.","A framework already structures requests as controller plus view."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A reactive UI with rich bindings fits [MVVM](kata:mvvm) better.","The screen is trivial and has no rules worth isolating."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The controller grows into a God class that holds business rules.","The view reads from many models and recomputes rules.","The model calling a view method directly, which breaks the dependency direction."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One controller, view and model per button.","Controllers that only forward to a service and return a view name."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records make fine immutable view models. Listener interfaces are often replaced by `java.util.concurrent.Flow` or reactive streams.","Pattern-matching `switch` over sealed command types gives controllers exhaustive input handling."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"MVVM replaces the controller with a view model that exposes state and commands, and the view binds to it declaratively. MVC's view is usually updated through code.","ref":"mvvm","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"MVC uses Observer so the model can notify views without referencing them. Observer is one mechanism; MVC is the three-role split.","ref":"observer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A controller coordinates model and view like a mediator, but its main job is translating input; a mediator manages peer-to-peer collaboration.","ref":"mediator","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"MVC usually lives inside the presentation layer, with the model drawing on the business layer below.","ref":"layered-architecture","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Spring MVC routes requests to controllers, fills a `Model`, and resolves a view.","name":"DispatcherServlet, @Controller, Model","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Action-based MVC specification for Jakarta REST, with Eclipse Krazo as an implementation.","name":"Jakarta MVC (jakarta.mvc.Controller)","where":"Jakarta EE"},{"detail":"The table component renders a model it observes; `AbstractTableModel.fireTableDataChanged` notifies it.","name":"javax.swing.table.TableModel and JTable","where":"JDK Swing"},{"detail":"Same split: the list view listens to a model, input handlers change the model.","name":"javax.swing.ListModel and JList","where":"JDK Swing"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.function.IntConsumer;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Counter {","    private int n;","    private final List<IntConsumer> watchers = new ArrayList<>();","    void watch(IntConsumer w) { watchers.add(w); }","    void add(int d) { n += d; watchers.forEach(w -> w.accept(n)); }","}","final class Screen {","    Screen(Counter c) { c.watch(n -> System.out.println(\"count=\" + n)); }","}","final class KeyHandler {","    private final Counter c;","    KeyHandler(Counter c) { this.c = c; }","    void onKey(char k) { if (k == '+') c.add(1); else if (k == '-') c.add(-1); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mvc","why":"State with rules, a display that observes it, and a separate input translator that mutates it."},{"ref":"mvvm","why":"No presentation state is exposed for binding; the screen subscribes directly to the domain object."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Observer is only the notification mechanism here; the input-handling role makes it the full three-part split."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"`KeyHandler` does not coordinate peers; it forwards one kind of input to one model."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mvc","why":"Controllers handle the request, the model supplies data and a template renders it: the request-driven variant fits."},{"ref":"mvvm","why":"MVVM depends on long-lived bindings between view and view model, which a stateless request cycle lacks."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Nothing needs to be pushed to subscribers; each page is built once per request."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"There are no peer components to decouple; the problem is separating roles in the request flow."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A server-rendered admin portal: each request needs to read some domain state, choose a template and return HTML. There is no client-side data binding and pages are stateless between requests."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.function.IntConsumer;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The model prints, so it now knows about one particular presentation. Remove the line and let a view subscribe through `listen`.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Counter {","    private final List<IntConsumer> listeners = new ArrayList<>();","    private int n;","","    void listen(IntConsumer l) { listeners.add(l); }","","    void add(int delta) {","        n += delta;","        System.out.println(\"count=\" + n);","        listeners.forEach(l -> l.accept(n));","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the MVC pattern."},{"blocks":[["var model = new Thermostat(20);"],["model.addListener(new ConsoleView());"],["var controller = new Controller(model);"],["controller.onInput(\"up\");"]],"compile":{"template":["interface ModelListener { void changed(int t); }","class Thermostat { Thermostat(int t) {} void addListener(ModelListener l) {} }","class ConsoleView implements ModelListener { public void changed(int t) {} }","class Controller { Controller(Thermostat m) {} void onInput(String c) {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The model must exist before a view can listen to it or a controller can be given it; input comes last, once both are connected.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Model: state and rules, UI-free","View: renders, no rules","Controller: input to model operations","Changes reach the view via notification","Server frameworks use a request-driven variant"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"The model holds state and rules and has no UI knowledge. The view renders model state. The controller turns user input into model calls. The model notifies registered views when it changes, so the view never depends on the controller and the model never depends on the view.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain MVC: what each role owns and how the view finds out about changes."}],"id":"mvc"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give each screen a view model that exposes observable state and commands, and let a thin view bind to it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **view model** holds presentation state (field values, enabled flags, messages) and the actions the user can trigger. It imports no UI classes. The **view** only binds: widgets observe properties, and user events call commands. The UI logic therefore becomes plain objects that tests drive directly.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Model:** domain and services.","**ViewModel:** state + commands, UI-free.","**View:** declarative binding, no logic."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Binding needs an observable type. Most platforms provide one: JavaFX properties, Android `StateFlow`, SwiftUI `@Published`.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"submit enabled=false\nstatus=\nsubmit enabled=true\nstatus=Welcome\nsubmit enabled=false","file":"Mvvm.java","regions":{"binding":[55,56],"input":[57,61],"observable":[7,25],"viewmodel":[27,50]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Objects;\nimport java.util.function.BiPredicate;\nimport java.util.function.Consumer;\n\nfinal class Observable<T> {\n    private final List<Consumer<T>> subscribers = new ArrayList<>();\n    private T value;\n\n    Observable(T initial) { this.value = initial; }\n\n    T get() { return value; }\n\n    void set(T next) {\n        if (Objects.equals(value, next)) return;\n        value = next;\n        subscribers.forEach(s -> s.accept(next));\n    }\n\n    void bind(Consumer<T> subscriber) {\n        subscribers.add(subscriber);\n        subscriber.accept(value);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class SignInViewModel {\n    final Observable<String> email = new Observable<>(\"\");\n    final Observable<String> password = new Observable<>(\"\");\n    final Observable<Boolean> canSubmit = new Observable<>(false);\n    final Observable<String> status = new Observable<>(\"\");\n    private final BiPredicate<String, String> auth;\n\n    SignInViewModel(BiPredicate<String, String> auth) {\n        this.auth = auth;\n        email.bind(e -> refresh());\n        password.bind(p -> refresh());\n    }\n\n    private void refresh() {\n        canSubmit.set(email.get().contains(\"@\")\n                && password.get().length() >= 8);\n    }\n\n    void submit() {\n        if (!canSubmit.get()) return;\n        boolean ok = auth.test(email.get(), password.get());\n        status.set(ok ? \"Welcome\" : \"Denied\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var vm = new SignInViewModel((e, p) -> p.startsWith(\"hunter\"));\n        vm.canSubmit.bind(on -> System.out.println(\"submit enabled=\" + on));\n        vm.status.bind(s -> System.out.println(\"status=\" + s));\n        vm.email.set(\"ana@x.org\");\n        vm.password.set(\"hunter2\");\n        vm.password.set(\"hunter22!\");\n        vm.submit();\n        vm.password.set(\"x\");\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A minimal observable value. `bind` delivers the current value immediately and every later change, and `set` ignores no-op writes.","region":"observable"},{"note":"The view model derives `canSubmit` from two inputs and exposes a command. It depends on a model-side `BiPredicate`, never on a widget.","region":"viewmodel"},{"note":"The view's whole job: subscribe widgets to properties. Println stands in for button and label updates.","region":"binding"},{"note":"User events are just `set` calls on properties. Only the password that crosses 8 characters flips `canSubmit`, so no redundant events appear.","region":"input"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A mobile checkout screen with asynchronous price updates.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The checkout view model exposes `lines`, `total`, `isLoading`, `error` and a `placeOrder` command. The price service updates `total`; the screen redraws because it observes state. Rotating the device recreates the view while the view model survives, and tests assert on `canPlaceOrder` without launching an emulator.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["class CheckoutViewModel : ViewModel() {","    val state: StateFlow<CheckoutState>","    fun placeOrder() { /* ... */ }","}","// the screen: collect state, call placeOrder()"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: screen logic is unit-tested as plain objects, and the view survives configuration changes without losing state.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"view","label":"binds to","rel":"association","to":"vm"},{"from":"vm","rel":"composition","to":"obs"},{"from":"vm","rel":"association","to":"auth"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"view","kind":"class","members":["+ bind(vm)"],"name":"SignInView","role":"View","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"vm","kind":"class","members":["+ email: Observable","+ canSubmit: Observable","+ submit()"],"name":"SignInViewModel","role":"ViewModel","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"obs","kind":"class","members":["+ get(): T","+ set(T)","+ bind(Consumer)"],"name":"Observable<T>","role":"Observable","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"auth","kind":"interface","members":["+ test(email, pw)"],"name":"BiPredicate","role":"Model","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"vm","text":"imports no widget type"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"v","label":"password.set(text)","style":"call","to":"vm"},{"from":"vm","label":"canSubmit=true","style":"return","to":"v"},{"from":"v","label":"submit()","style":"call","to":"vm"},{"from":"vm","label":"test(email, pw)","style":"call","to":"m"},{"from":"m","label":"true","style":"return","to":"vm"},{"from":"vm","label":"status=Welcome","style":"return","to":"v"}],"participants":[{"id":"v","name":"View","role":"View"},{"id":"vm","name":"SignInViewModel","role":"ViewModel"},{"id":"m","name":"Authenticator","role":"Model"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The UI framework supports data binding or reactive state.","Screen logic is substantial and deserves fast unit tests.","State must outlive the view, as on rotation."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The platform lacks binding and you'd hand-write synchronization everywhere.","The screen is static; a view model is ceremony."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["The view model holding a reference to the view or context, leaking it.","Binding logic hidden in markup, where tests can't reach it.","Exposing mutable state so the view can corrupt it."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A view model per widget instead of per screen.","Fields mirrored in model, view model and DTO with no transformation."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["JavaFX properties and `java.util.concurrent.Flow` are the standard JVM binding tools.","Records suit immutable state snapshots exposed by a view model."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"MVC's controller pushes updates into the view. MVVM's view pulls through binding, so the view model needs no reference to the view at all.","ref":"mvc","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Binding is Observer: the view subscribes to view-model properties. MVVM adds the role split around that mechanism.","ref":"observer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"View-model actions are often exposed as command objects that the view can invoke and enable or disable.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The view model adapts domain data to what a screen needs, but it also owns presentation state and behavior, which an adapter doesn't.","ref":"adapter","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Properties support `bind` and `addListener`, the basis of JavaFX view-model code.","name":"javafx.beans.property.StringProperty","where":"JavaFX"},{"detail":"Holds UI state across configuration changes and exposes it as `StateFlow` or `LiveData`.","name":"androidx.lifecycle.ViewModel","where":"Android"},{"detail":"Views re-render when published properties of a view model change.","name":"ObservableObject and @Published","where":"SwiftUI"},{"detail":"The interface that data binding uses to observe view-model changes.","name":"INotifyPropertyChanged","where":".NET WPF"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","import java.util.function.Consumer;","interface Api { void login(String e); }","final class Prop<T> { T v; Prop(T v) { this.v = v; } T get() { return v; } void set(T n) { v = n; } void listen(Consumer<T> c) {} }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class LoginState {","    final Prop<String> email = new Prop<>(\"\");","    final Prop<Boolean> enabled = new Prop<>(false);","    LoginState(Api api) {","        email.listen(e -> enabled.set(e.contains(\"@\")));","        this.api = api;","    }","    private final Api api;","    void submit() { if (enabled.get()) api.login(email.get()); }","}","// screen: state.enabled.listen(button::setEnabled);","// screen: field.onChange(state.email::set);"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mvvm","why":"Observable state plus a command, with a screen that merely subscribes and forwards events."},{"ref":"mvc","why":"No controller translates input and the screen is not updated by code from a controller; the screen subscribes to state."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Observer is the mechanism, but the class also owns presentation state and an action for the view."},{"ref":"state","why":"The enabled flag is data, not a swappable object that changes the class's behavior."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"mvvm","why":"A UI-free view model holds the state and survives view recreation, and the view just binds."},{"ref":"mvc","why":"A controller that pokes widgets ties logic to the view and makes rotation handling manual."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Observer is just the notification part; it doesn't say where screen state and commands live."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade would simplify the backend call, not organize presentation state."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A mobile form screen enables its button depending on four fields, shows async validation results, and must keep its state across rotation. The team wants most screen logic unit-tested without a device."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.Consumer;","interface TextField { String getText(); }","final class Prop<T> { T v; Prop(T v) { this.v = v; } T get() { return v; } void set(T n) { v = n; } void listen(Consumer<T> c) {} }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The view model accepts a widget, so it now depends on the UI toolkit and can't be unit-tested alone. Take `String text` and let the view pass the value.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class SignInViewModel {","    final Prop<String> email = new Prop<>(\"\");","    final Prop<Boolean> canSubmit = new Prop<>(false);","","    SignInViewModel() {","        email.listen(e -> canSubmit.set(e.contains(\"@\")));","    }","","    void onTextChanged(TextField field) {","        email.set(field.getText());","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the MVVM pattern."},{"blocks":[["var vm = new SignInViewModel((e, p) -> true);"],["vm.email.set(\"ana@x.org\");"],["vm.password.set(\"hunter22!\");"],["vm.submit();"],["System.out.println(vm.status.get());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.BiPredicate;","final class Prop<T> { T v; Prop(T v) { this.v = v; } T get() { return v; } void set(T n) { v = n; } }","final class SignInViewModel {","  Prop<String> email = new Prop<>(\"\"), password = new Prop<>(\"\"), status = new Prop<>(\"\");","  SignInViewModel(BiPredicate<String, String> a) {} void submit() {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"`submit()` is gated by `canSubmit`, which depends on both inputs, so both must be set before submitting. Status is read last.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["View model: observable state plus commands","No UI classes in the view model","View only binds and forwards events","Tests drive the view model directly","Differs from MVC: no controller pushing into the view"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"The view model holds presentation state as observable properties and exposes commands for user actions, with no reference to any widget. The view only binds widgets to those properties and forwards events. Because the view model is plain code, tests set inputs and assert on outputs without a UI.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain MVVM: what the view model contains, what the view does, and why it helps testing."}],"id":"mvvm"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give an object its collaborators from outside, usually through the constructor, instead of letting it create or look them up.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A class declares what it needs as constructor parameters typed by **interface**. A single **composition root**, usually `main` or a container, creates the objects and connects them. The class stays ignorant of concrete implementations and lifetimes, so tests pass fakes and configuration swaps real ones.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Constructor injection:** required, immutable, visible.","**Setter or field injection:** optional, mutable, easy to forget.","**Container:** automates the wiring, never required."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Dependency injection is a technique and needs no framework. A container only automates the composition root.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ana: A-1 overdue\nsent 1\nsent 2 [ana, bo]","file":"DependencyInjection.java","regions":{"consumer":[15,35],"contract":[11,13],"root":[47,49],"test":[50,53]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.time.Clock;\nimport java.time.Instant;\nimport java.time.LocalDate;\nimport java.time.ZoneOffset;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Objects;\n\nrecord Invoice(String id, String customer, LocalDate due) {}\n\ninterface Mailer {\n    void send(String to, String body);\n}\n\nfinal class InvoiceReminder {\n    private final Clock clock;\n    private final Mailer mailer;\n\n    InvoiceReminder(Clock clock, Mailer mailer) {\n        this.clock = Objects.requireNonNull(clock);\n        this.mailer = Objects.requireNonNull(mailer);\n    }\n\n    int remind(List<Invoice> invoices) {\n        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now(clock);\n        int count = 0;\n        for (Invoice inv : invoices) {\n            if (inv.due().isBefore(today)) {\n                mailer.send(inv.customer(), inv.id() + \" overdue\");\n                count++;\n            }\n        }\n        return count;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static Clock at(String isoDate) {\n        Instant start = Instant.parse(isoDate + \"T00:00:00Z\");\n        return Clock.fixed(start, ZoneOffset.UTC);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var invoices = List.of(\n            new Invoice(\"A-1\", \"ana\", LocalDate.parse(\"2026-03-01\")),\n            new Invoice(\"A-2\", \"bo\", LocalDate.parse(\"2026-03-20\")));\n        Mailer console = (to, body) -> System.out.println(to + \": \" + body);\n        var job = new InvoiceReminder(at(\"2026-03-10\"), console);\n        System.out.println(\"sent \" + job.remind(invoices));\n        var outbox = new ArrayList<String>();\n        var later = new InvoiceReminder(at(\"2026-04-01\"),\n                (to, body) -> outbox.add(to));\n        System.out.println(\"sent \" + later.remind(invoices) + \" \" + outbox);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The consumer depends on a small interface that it defines in its own vocabulary, not on an SMTP or HTTP class.","region":"contract"},{"note":"Collaborators arrive through the constructor and are null-checked. Time itself is injected as a `Clock`, so overdue logic is deterministic.","region":"consumer"},{"note":"The composition root builds the object graph with production-style collaborators: a fixed clock and a console mailer.","region":"root"},{"note":"A test wires the same class differently: a later clock and a capturing mailer. Nothing inside `InvoiceReminder` changed.","region":"test"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Testing a payment service against a fake gateway.","type":"lead"},{"text":"`PaymentService` needs a gateway, an idempotency store and a clock. In production a container injects an HTTP gateway client; in tests the constructor receives a scripted fake that fails twice, then succeeds. Retry logic, timeouts and expiry are tested in milliseconds without network or sleeping.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var gateway = new ScriptedGateway(FAIL, FAIL, OK);","var clock = Clock.fixed(T0, ZoneOffset.UTC);","var svc = new PaymentService(gateway, store, clock);","assertEquals(PAID, svc.charge(order).status());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: failure paths that are hard to provoke in integration environments become ordinary unit tests.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"main","label":"creates, injects","rel":"dependency","to":"rem"},{"from":"rem","rel":"aggregation","to":"mailer"},{"from":"rem","rel":"aggregation","to":"clock"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"main","kind":"class","members":["+ main(args)"],"name":"Main","role":"Composition root","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"rem","kind":"class","members":["- clock: Clock","- mailer: Mailer","+ remind(invoices)"],"name":"InvoiceReminder","role":"Client","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"mailer","kind":"interface","members":["+ send(to, body)"],"name":"Mailer","role":"Dependency","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"clock","kind":"class","members":["+ instant()"],"name":"Clock","role":"Dependency","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"rem","text":"never calls new on a collaborator"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A class has collaborators that tests must replace: time, network, storage.","Several environments need different implementations.","You want dependencies visible in the constructor signature."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A value is a trivial pure helper: a plain `static` call or `new` is clearer.","Wiring is a one-line obvious choice and nothing is ever replaced."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Constructors with eight or more parameters usually signal a class doing too much.","Injecting the container itself, which turns DI into a [Service Locator](kata:service-locator).","Circular dependencies hidden by setter injection."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An interface and a factory for every class with exactly one implementation.","Wiring spread across annotations, XML and code, so no one can see the graph."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`java.time.Clock` is the standard injectable clock.","Constructor injection with records or `final` fields makes required collaborators immutable."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"With a locator the class asks a global registry for its dependencies, hiding them. With DI they are pushed in and visible in the constructor.","ref":"service-locator","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"The Dependency Inversion Principle says depend on abstractions; DI is the common mechanism that supplies the concrete one.","ref":"dip","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A factory creates collaborators on demand inside the class. Injection hands them in ready, and the class never builds them.","ref":"abstract-factory","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Injecting a strategy is DI, but DI also supplies collaborators that are not interchangeable algorithms, like a clock or a repository.","ref":"strategy","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Containers manage lifetime, so a singleton scope replaces hand-written global `getInstance()` with an injected shared instance.","ref":"singleton","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The container resolves constructor parameters by type; `@Autowired` is optional on a single constructor.","name":"ApplicationContext and constructor injection","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Standard annotation for injection points, resolved by a CDI container.","name":"jakarta.inject.Inject (CDI)","where":"Jakarta EE"},{"detail":"Guice wires at runtime from modules; Dagger generates the wiring at compile time.","name":"Guice Injector and Dagger","where":"Google"},{"detail":"Abstract class designed as an injectable time source, with `Clock.fixed` for tests.","name":"java.time.Clock","where":"JDK"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Clock;","interface Sink { void write(String s); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Reporter {","    private final Clock clock;","    private final Sink sink;","","    Reporter(Clock clock, Sink sink) {","        this.clock = clock;","        this.sink = sink;","    }","","    void run() { sink.write(clock.instant().toString()); }","}","// elsewhere: new Reporter(Clock.systemUTC(), new FileSink(path))"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dependency-injection","why":"Collaborators come in through the constructor as interfaces; one outer place chooses the implementations."},{"ref":"service-locator","why":"Nothing is looked up from a registry; the dependencies are explicit constructor parameters."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"`Clock` and `Sink` are collaborators of different kinds, not interchangeable algorithms for one decision."},{"ref":"abstract-factory","why":"The class doesn't create anything, and no factory family appears."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dependency-injection","why":"Pass the connection source and clients in as interfaces; tests and environments differ only in the composition root."},{"ref":"service-locator","why":"A registry still hides the needs and fails at runtime if something is missing."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"A global instance would make tests share state and still wouldn't let staging differ."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"A subclass overriding a creation method would couple environment choice to inheritance."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"`ReportJob` opens its own JDBC connection and builds an HTTP client in the constructor. Tests hit real services, and the staging environment needs a different mail server than production."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.*;","interface Mailer { void send(String to, String body); }","class SmtpMailer implements Mailer { SmtpMailer(String host) {} public void send(String to, String body) {} }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The class builds its own mailer, so tests can't replace it and the host is hardcoded. Accept a `Mailer` in the constructor and construct `SmtpMailer` at the composition root.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class InvoiceReminder {","    private final Clock clock;","    private final Mailer mailer;","","    InvoiceReminder(Clock clock) {","        this.clock = clock;","        this.mailer = new SmtpMailer(\"smtp.internal\");","    }","","    void remind(String customer) {","        mailer.send(customer, \"Due \" + LocalDate.now(clock));","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Dependency Injection pattern."},{"blocks":[["Clock clock = Clock.systemUTC();","Mailer mailer = new SmtpMailer(\"smtp.internal\");"],["var job = new InvoiceReminder(clock, mailer);"],["int sent = job.remind(invoices);"],["System.out.println(sent);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Clock;","import java.util.List;","interface Mailer {}","class SmtpMailer implements Mailer { SmtpMailer(String h) {} }","class InvoiceReminder { InvoiceReminder(Clock c, Mailer m) {} int remind(List<String> l) { return 0; } }","class Snippet { void run(List<String> invoices) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The root builds leaves first, then passes them into the object that needs them, and only then runs the work.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Collaborators are passed in, usually via constructor","One composition root wires the graph","Dependencies are visible in the signature","Locator hides them and fails at runtime","A container is optional automation"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A class declares its collaborators as constructor parameters and never creates or finds them itself. One composition root builds the graph and chooses implementations. Unlike a service locator, the dependencies are explicit in the type signature, missing ones fail at compile or startup, and tests simply pass fakes.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain dependency injection: who creates dependencies, where, and how it differs from a service locator."}],"id":"dependency-injection"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let code look up the services it needs from a central registry, instead of receiving them.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **locator** maps a type or name to a service instance. A class calls `Locator.get(Mailer.class)` when it needs one. This decouples the class from the concrete implementation, but dependencies become **invisible**: nothing in the constructor says what the class needs, and a missing registration fails at runtime.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Benefit:** easy to retrofit, no constructor changes.","**Cost:** hidden dependencies and global state.","**Fit:** runtime discovery, framework edges."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Treat it as the pattern that dependency injection replaces. Prefer it only where the set of services is unknown at compile time.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Welcome, ana\nruntime: no service registered for Mailer","file":"ServiceLocator.java","regions":{"consumer":[28,34],"failure":[42,47],"locator":[4,22],"registration":[39,40]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\n\nfinal class Locator {\n    private static final Map<Class<?>, Object> SERVICES =\n            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    static <T> void register(Class<T> type, T service) {\n        SERVICES.put(type, service);\n    }\n\n    static <T> T get(Class<T> type) {\n        Object service = SERVICES.get(type);\n        if (service == null) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\n                \"no service registered for \" + type.getSimpleName());\n        }\n        return type.cast(service);\n    }\n\n    static void clear() { SERVICES.clear(); }\n}\n\ninterface Mailer {\n    void send(String to, String body);\n}\n\nfinal class SignupService {\n    // Nothing in the constructor or fields reveals the Mailer need.\n    void signUp(String email) {\n        Mailer mailer = Locator.get(Mailer.class);\n        mailer.send(email, \"Welcome\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var service = new SignupService();\n        Locator.register(Mailer.class,\n                (to, body) -> System.out.println(body + \", \" + to));\n        service.signUp(\"ana\");\n        Locator.clear();\n        try {\n            service.signUp(\"bo\");\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"runtime: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A static registry keyed by type. `get` fails loudly with a message naming the missing type, and a concurrent map keeps it safe across threads.","region":"locator"},{"note":"`SignupService` has no constructor parameters, so its dependency on `Mailer` is invisible until the method runs.","region":"consumer"},{"note":"Startup code registers implementations. Nothing forces this to happen before first use.","region":"registration"},{"note":"Remove the registration and the same call compiles but fails at runtime. This is the cost DI avoids by moving the failure to construction.","region":"failure"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A build tool discovering compiler plugins at startup.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The tool cannot know at compile time which plugins sit on the classpath. It asks the JDK's `ServiceLoader` for every `CompilerPlugin` implementation, each declared in a descriptor inside its own jar. Adding a plugin means dropping a jar in, with no change to the host. A locator fits because discovery, not wiring, is the problem.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var plugins = ServiceLoader.load(CompilerPlugin.class);","for (CompilerPlugin p : plugins) {","    p.configure(project);","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: extensibility without recompiling the host. Pair it with DI inside the plugin so its own collaborators stay explicit.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"main","label":"register","rel":"dependency","to":"loc"},{"from":"svc","label":"get(Class)","rel":"dependency","to":"loc"},{"from":"loc","label":"holds","rel":"association","to":"mail"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"main","kind":"class","members":["+ main(args)"],"name":"Main","role":"Bootstrap","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ signUp(email)"],"name":"SignupService","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"loc","kind":"class","members":["- SERVICES: Map","+ register(type, svc)","+ get(type): T"],"name":"Locator","role":"Registry","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"mail","kind":"interface","members":["+ send(to, body)"],"name":"Mailer","role":"Service","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"svc","text":"dependency not visible in its API"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"get(Mailer.class)","style":"call","to":"l"},{"from":"l","label":"mailer","style":"return","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"send(email, body)","style":"call","to":"m"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"SignupService","role":"Client"},{"id":"l","name":"Locator","role":"Registry"},{"id":"m","name":"Mailer","role":"Service"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Implementations are discovered at runtime, as with plugins and drivers.","Retrofitting legacy code that you can't give constructors yet.","At a framework boundary where object creation is out of your control."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["You control construction: use [Dependency Injection](kata:dependency-injection) instead.","You need compile-time or startup-time proof that dependencies exist."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Hidden dependencies make classes hard to understand and reuse.","Global mutable registry leaks state between tests.","Failures appear at the first call, possibly in production."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Business classes that call `Locator.get` several times per method.","Injecting the locator itself everywhere, which is DI in name only."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The class asks for services (pull) versus being handed them (push). Push makes dependencies explicit; pull hides them and fails later.","ref":"dependency-injection","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Both give global access. A singleton is one class controlling its own instance; a locator is a registry of many services replaceable by type.","ref":"singleton","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A factory creates a new object on request. A locator returns an already registered one, often shared.","ref":"factory-method","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A locator decouples from concrete classes, as DIP asks, but still couples every client to the locator and hides what it uses.","ref":"dip","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Discovers implementations of a service interface declared in `META-INF/services` or `module-info.java`.","name":"java.util.ServiceLoader","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Classic Jakarta EE way to find a DataSource or EJB by name.","name":"javax.naming.InitialContext.lookup","where":"JNDI"},{"detail":"Direct pull from the container; legitimate at edges, a smell inside beans.","name":"ApplicationContext.getBean","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Looks up platform services such as `ConnectivityManager` by name or class.","name":"Context.getSystemService","where":"Android"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.*;","interface Gateway { void pay(String a, Instant t); }","class Registry { static <T> T lookup(Class<T> c) { return null; } }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Billing {","    void charge(String accountId) {","        Gateway gateway = Registry.lookup(Gateway.class);","        Clock clock = Registry.lookup(Clock.class);","        gateway.pay(accountId, clock.instant());","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"service-locator","why":"The class pulls its collaborators from a global registry inside the method, so its needs are invisible from outside."},{"ref":"dependency-injection","why":"Injection would receive `Gateway` and `Clock` as constructor parameters instead of fetching them."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"`Registry` is global, but the point here is lookup of several services by type, not a class restricting its own instances."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"`lookup` doesn't create a product through an overridable creation step."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"service-locator","why":"Runtime discovery by interface is the locator's legitimate use, as `ServiceLoader` does."},{"ref":"dependency-injection","why":"Constructor wiring needs the implementations known to the composition root, which is exactly what isn't available."},{"ref":"factory-method","why":"A factory method needs a subclass per format known in advance."},{"ref":"singleton","why":"A global instance doesn't discover anything; it only restricts instance count."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A host application must load optional export formats from jars dropped into a folder by third parties. The host can't know the implementations at compile time and must find them at startup."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Returning null defers the failure to a distant NullPointerException. Throw an exception naming the missing type so the problem surfaces where the lookup happens.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Registry {","    private static final Map<Class<?>, Object> S = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();","","    static <T> void register(Class<T> type, T service) { S.put(type, service); }","","    static <T> T get(Class<T> type) {","        T service = type.cast(S.get(type));","        if (service == null) {","            return null;","        }","        return service;","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Service Locator pattern."},{"blocks":[["Mailer mailer = (to, body) -> System.out.println(body);"],["Locator.register(Mailer.class, mailer);"],["new SignupService().signUp(\"ana\");"],["Locator.clear();"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Mailer { void send(String to, String body); }","class Locator { static <T> void register(Class<T> c, T s) {} static void clear() {} }","class SignupService { void signUp(String e) {} }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A service must exist before it is registered, and registration must precede the first lookup. Clearing comes last, so earlier calls still find it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Registry code pulls services","Dependencies are hidden from the API","Missing registration fails at runtime","Global state hurts test isolation","Valid for plugin discovery such as ServiceLoader"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A service locator is a registry that code queries for the services it needs. It decouples callers from concrete classes, but the dependencies are hidden in method bodies, global state complicates tests, and a missing registration fails at runtime. Dependency injection makes them explicit parameters. Locators remain valid for runtime plugin discovery.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Service Locator pattern and why most teams prefer dependency injection."}],"id":"service-locator"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Hide storage behind a collection-like interface so domain code asks for objects, not queries.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **repository** exposes `findById`, `add`, `save` and a few intention-revealing queries for one aggregate. The domain depends on that interface only. SQL, an ORM, a document store or a `HashMap` for tests sit behind it, so storage can change without touching business rules.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Speaks:** the domain's language, in terms of aggregates and not tables.","**Hides:** query language, mapping, connections and caching.","**One per aggregate root,** not per table."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A repository is a port in the domain. Its implementation is an adapter that belongs to infrastructure.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ada -> 35\no-2 -> Optional[Order[id=OrderId[value=o-2], customer=bob, totalCents=900]]\no-9 -> Optional.empty","file":"Repository.java","regions":{"client":[48,55],"domain":[7,9],"memory":[17,32],"port":[11,15],"service":[34,44]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Optional;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\n\nrecord OrderId(String value) {}\n\nrecord Order(OrderId id, String customer, int totalCents) {}\n\ninterface OrderRepository {\n    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id);\n    List<Order> findByCustomer(String customer);\n    void save(Order order);\n}\n\nfinal class InMemoryOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {\n    private final Map<OrderId, Order> rows = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    public Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id) {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(rows.get(id));\n    }\n\n    public List<Order> findByCustomer(String customer) {\n        return rows.values().stream()\n            .filter(o -> o.customer().equals(customer))\n            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(o -> o.id().value()))\n            .toList();\n    }\n\n    public void save(Order order) { rows.put(order.id(), order); }\n}\n\nfinal class LoyaltyService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n\n    LoyaltyService(OrderRepository orders) { this.orders = orders; }\n\n    int points(String customer) {\n        return orders.findByCustomer(customer).stream()\n            .mapToInt(o -> o.totalCents() / 100)\n            .sum();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        OrderRepository orders = new InMemoryOrderRepository();\n        orders.save(new Order(new OrderId(\"o-1\"), \"ada\", 2550));\n        orders.save(new Order(new OrderId(\"o-2\"), \"bob\", 900));\n        orders.save(new Order(new OrderId(\"o-3\"), \"ada\", 1000));\n        var loyalty = new LoyaltyService(orders);\n        System.out.println(\"ada -> \" + loyalty.points(\"ada\"));\n        System.out.println(\"o-2 -> \" + orders.findById(new OrderId(\"o-2\")));\n        System.out.println(\"o-9 -> \" + orders.findById(new OrderId(\"o-9\")));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Plain domain types with no persistence annotations. The identity is a typed `OrderId`, not a bare `String`.","region":"domain"},{"note":"The repository is an interface owned by the domain. Queries are named for business meaning, and absence is `Optional`, never `null`.","region":"port"},{"note":"One adapter: a `ConcurrentHashMap`. A JDBC or JPA adapter implements the same three methods, and the sort makes results deterministic.","region":"memory"},{"note":"The service takes the interface in its constructor. It cannot see SQL, sessions or maps.","region":"service"},{"note":"Wiring happens at the edge. Swapping the adapter changes one line and no domain code.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Appointment booking in a clinic system.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The scheduling rules are the valuable part: no double booking, no overlap with a doctor's leave. Rule tests must run in milliseconds, but production stores appointments in PostgreSQL through JPA. The rules talk to `AppointmentRepository.overlapping(doctor, slot)`. Unit tests use an in-memory adapter and a few integration tests run the JPA adapter against a real database.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["interface AppointmentRepository {","    List<Appointment> overlapping(DoctorId d, TimeSlot s);","    void add(Appointment a);","}","","var repo = new InMemoryAppointments();","new Scheduler(repo).book(doctor, slot, patient);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: scheduling logic is tested without a database, and moving to a new store means writing one adapter.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"svc","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"repo"},{"from":"mem","rel":"realizes","to":"repo"},{"from":"jpa","rel":"realizes","to":"repo"},{"from":"repo","label":"returns","rel":"dependency","to":"order"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":1,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["- orders: OrderRepository","+ points(customer): int"],"name":"LoyaltyService","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"repo","kind":"interface","members":["+ findById(id): Optional","+ findByCustomer(c): List","+ save(order)"],"name":"OrderRepository","role":"Repository","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"mem","kind":"class","members":["- rows: Map"],"name":"InMemoryOrderRepo","role":"Adapter","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"jpa","kind":"class","members":["- em: EntityManager"],"name":"JpaOrderRepo","role":"Adapter","row":2},{"col":0,"id":"order","kind":"record","members":["id, customer, total"],"name":"Order","role":"Entity","row":0}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Domain logic must be testable without a database.","Storage technology may change, or differs per environment.","Queries repeat across services and deserve one named home."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The app is thin CRUD over tables; the layer adds ceremony.","A framework repository already is the abstraction and you never swap it."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["A leaky interface that returns query builders, `ResultSet` or `Map` internals.","Generic `findAll()` on huge tables, or one method per report.","Repositories per table instead of per aggregate root."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A repository that only forwards to `JpaRepository` with the same methods.","Dozens of `findByXAndYAndZ` methods; switch to [Specification](kata:specification)."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Spring Data and Jakarta Data generate the implementation from an interface, so you write only the port.","Return `Optional` for single results and immutable lists or `Stream` for collections."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The repository is the collection the domain sees; a mapper does the object-to-row translation inside its implementation.","ref":"data-mapper","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Active Record puts finders and save on the entity itself, tying it to storage. A repository keeps entities persistence-ignorant.","ref":"active-record","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Repositories fetch and register objects; the unit of work decides when changes are flushed together in one transaction.","ref":"unit-of-work","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Pass a specification to one `find(spec)` method instead of growing the interface with a method per filter.","ref":"specification","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"In hexagonal architecture a repository interface is the typical outbound port, and its implementation is a driven adapter.","ref":"hexagonal","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Declare an interface; Spring Data generates the implementation, including derived queries like `findByLastName`.","name":"CrudRepository / JpaRepository","where":"Spring Data"},{"detail":"Jakarta Data marks an interface as a repository with built-in CRUD and query methods.","name":"jakarta.data.repository.Repository","where":"Jakarta EE"},{"detail":"A stereotype that marks the persistence component and translates vendor exceptions into `DataAccessException`.","name":"@Repository","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Hibernate ORM with Panache repository interface offering `find`, `list` and `persist` for an entity.","name":"PanacheRepository","where":"Quarkus"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Book(String isbn, String author) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface Shelf {","    Optional<Book> byIsbn(String isbn);","    List<Book> byAuthor(String author);","    void add(Book book);","    void remove(String isbn);","}","// one implementation talks SQL, another wraps a HashMap for tests"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"repository","why":"A collection-like, domain-worded interface over storage, with interchangeable backends."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"A mapper translates between object and row shapes; this interface says nothing about mapping."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"The entity does not save or find itself; a separate object holds the access."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade simplifies a subsystem's API; this interface exists to abstract where data lives."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"repository","why":"A domain-owned, collection-like port with swappable adapters hides storage completely."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"Policies would carry find and save, binding domain rules to a concrete store and forcing a database in tests."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"Mapping is needed somewhere, but a mapper alone gives the domain no storage-agnostic interface to depend on."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade wraps a subsystem; it does not give the domain an abstraction in its own vocabulary."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A domain service loads and stores `Policy` aggregates. Tests must run without a database, and storage may move from PostgreSQL to DynamoDB next year. Business rules should not mention tables or queries."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record User(long id) {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Returning the internal map lets callers mutate storage directly and ties them to its shape. Expose intention-revealing queries or an immutable copy such as `List.copyOf(rows.values())`.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class UserRepository {","    private final Map<Long, User> rows = new HashMap<>();","","    Optional<User> findById(long id) {","        return Optional.ofNullable(rows.get(id));","    }","","    Map<Long, User> all() { return rows; }","","    void save(User user) { rows.put(user.id(), user); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Repository pattern."},{"blocks":[["var id = new OrderId(request.orderId());"],["Order order = orders.findById(id).orElseThrow();"],["order.cancel();"],["orders.save(order);"],["audit.record(order.id());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record OrderId(String v) {}","record Request(String orderId) {}","final class Order { OrderId id() { return null; } void cancel() {} }","interface Orders { Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id); void save(Order o); }","interface Audit { void record(OrderId id); }","class Snippet { void run(Request request, Orders orders, Audit audit) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Build the identity, fetch the aggregate, change it, persist it, then use the persisted result. Each line depends on the previous variable.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use case so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Collection-like interface in domain language","Hides storage, queries and mapping","One per aggregate root, owned by the domain","Implementation is a swappable adapter","Mapping and transactions are separate concerns"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A repository gives the domain a collection-like interface for aggregates: find, add, save, in domain vocabulary. It hides storage technology and mapping. The domain owns the interface and infrastructure implements it. A mapper only translates shapes, and a DAO often mirrors tables rather than aggregates.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Repository in your own words: what does it hide, and why is it not just a DAO or a mapper?"}],"id":"repository"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Track every change made during one business transaction, then write them all to storage together or not at all.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **unit of work** records objects that were created, modified or removed. Nothing hits storage until `commit()`, which writes all changes in the right order within one transaction. It also keeps each entity written once, however often it changed, and it is where rollback lives.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Tracks:** new, dirty and removed objects (`registerNew/Dirty/Removed`).","**Commits:** one batch, one transaction, in dependency order.","**Scope:** one request or business transaction, then discarded."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A unit of work is not thread-safe or long-lived. Share a repository, never a unit of work, across requests.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"committed 3 changes\n{a=60, c=10}\nrolled back: missing zzz\n{a=60, c=10}","file":"UnitOfWork.java","regions":{"changes":[5,8],"client":[57,63],"commit":[31,51],"register":[16,29]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.LinkedHashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\nsealed interface Change permits Insert, Update, Delete {}\nrecord Insert(String id, int value) implements Change {}\nrecord Update(String id, int value) implements Change {}\nrecord Delete(String id) implements Change {}\n\nfinal class UnitOfWork {\n    private final TreeMap<String, Integer> db;\n    private final Map<String, Change> pending = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n\n    UnitOfWork(TreeMap<String, Integer> db) { this.db = db; }\n\n    void registerNew(String id, int value) {\n        pending.put(id, new Insert(id, value));\n    }\n\n    void registerDirty(String id, int value) {\n        pending.merge(id, new Update(id, value), (old, now) ->\n            old instanceof Insert ? new Insert(id, value)\n            : old instanceof Update ? now : old);\n    }\n\n    void registerRemoved(String id) {\n        if (pending.get(id) instanceof Insert) pending.remove(id);\n        else pending.put(id, new Delete(id));\n    }\n\n    int commit() {\n        var copy = new TreeMap<>(db);\n        for (Change c : pending.values()) {\n            switch (c) {\n                case Insert i -> {\n                    if (copy.putIfAbsent(i.id(), i.value()) != null)\n                        throw new IllegalStateException(\"duplicate \" + i.id());\n                }\n                case Update u -> {\n                    if (copy.replace(u.id(), u.value()) == null)\n                        throw new IllegalStateException(\"missing \" + u.id());\n                }\n                case Delete d -> copy.remove(d.id());\n            }\n        }\n        db.clear();\n        db.putAll(copy);\n        int applied = pending.size();\n        pending.clear();\n        return applied;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var db = new TreeMap<>(Map.of(\"a\", 100, \"b\", 50));\n        var work = new UnitOfWork(db);\n        work.registerNew(\"c\", 10);\n        work.registerDirty(\"a\", 70);\n        work.registerDirty(\"a\", 60);\n        work.registerRemoved(\"b\");\n        System.out.println(\"committed \" + work.commit() + \" changes\");\n        System.out.println(db);\n        var bad = new UnitOfWork(db);\n        bad.registerDirty(\"a\", 1);\n        bad.registerDirty(\"zzz\", 5);\n        try {\n            bad.commit();\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rolled back: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        System.out.println(db);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Pending work as a sealed hierarchy of records. The compiler checks that commit handles every kind of change.","region":"changes"},{"note":"A map keyed by id coalesces repeats: two updates become one, an update after an insert stays an insert, and removing a new object cancels it.","region":"register"},{"note":"Apply changes to a copy, then swap it in. A failing change throws before anything is published, which stands in for a database rollback.","region":"commit"},{"note":"Three business operations, four registrations, one commit. `a` is written once with its final value.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Cancelling an order in a shop backend.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Cancelling must flip the order status, release reserved stock and credit the loyalty balance. Doing this with three independent saves leaves a half-cancelled order when the third fails. With a unit of work, the handler loads entities through it, mutates them as plain objects, and calls one `commit()`. Hibernate's persistence context detects the changes and flushes them in a single transaction.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["try (var work = factory.open()) {","    Order o = work.orders().get(id);","    o.cancel();","    work.stock().release(o.lines());","    work.loyalty().credit(o.customer(), o.points());","    work.commit();","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the three writes succeed or fail together, and each row is written at most once.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"svc","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"uow"},{"from":"uow","label":"pending","rel":"aggregation","to":"chg"},{"from":"uow","label":"flushes to","rel":"dependency","to":"db"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(id)"],"name":"CancelOrder","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"uow","kind":"class","members":["- pending: Map","+ registerNew(e)","+ registerDirty(e)","+ registerRemoved(e)","+ commit()"],"name":"UnitOfWork","role":"UnitOfWork","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"chg","kind":"interface","members":["Insert | Update | Delete"],"name":"Change","role":"Change","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"db","kind":"interface","members":["+ begin()","+ commit()","+ rollback()"],"name":"Database","role":"Resource","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"registerDirty(a)","style":"call","to":"u"},{"from":"c","label":"registerNew(c)","style":"call","to":"u"},{"from":"c","label":"commit()","style":"call","to":"u"},{"from":"u","label":"begin()","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"u","label":"insert, update, delete","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"u","label":"commit()","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"u","label":"3 changes","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Handler"},{"id":"u","name":"UnitOfWork","role":"UnitOfWork"},{"id":"d","name":"Database","role":"Resource"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Several entities change in one business operation and must succeed together.","You want to batch writes and write each changed entity once.","You need a single place for transaction boundaries."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Each request touches one row: a plain transactional `save` is enough.","Work spans services; a local transaction cannot cover it (see Saga)."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Long-lived units of work that hold stale objects and balloon in memory.","Sharing one across threads or requests.","Order of inserts and deletes violating foreign keys on flush."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Hand-writing change tracking when your ORM already provides it.","A custom unit of work wrapping a framework that already tracks changes."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Declarative `@Transactional` plus a JPA persistence context is a unit of work you rarely write by hand.","Try-with-resources on an `AutoCloseable` unit of work guarantees rollback when `commit()` is not reached."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Repositories read and register aggregates; the unit of work decides when those changes are written, and in what order.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The unit of work chooses what to write and when; mappers perform the actual row translation for each change.","ref":"data-mapper","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Active Record saves each object immediately on `save()`, so there is no shared transaction scope or batching unless you add one.","ref":"active-record","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A unit of work is one local transaction in one store. A saga coordinates several transactions across services with compensations.","ref":"saga","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Events raised by entities during the unit of work are usually dispatched after a successful commit.","ref":"domain-event","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The persistence context tracks managed entities, detects dirty ones and flushes them at commit.","name":"EntityManager and Session","where":"JPA / Hibernate"},{"detail":"Opens a transaction boundary around a method; the JPA unit of work is flushed when it commits.","name":"@Transactional","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Programmatic transaction scope: callback body runs, then commit or rollback.","name":"TransactionTemplate","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"A change tracker collects entity states and writes them in one transaction.","name":"DbContext.SaveChanges","where":"Entity Framework"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Db { void begin(); void insert(Object o); void update(Object o); void commit(); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Tx {","    private final List<Object> added = new ArrayList<>();","    private final List<Object> changed = new ArrayList<>();","","    void add(Object o) { added.add(o); }","    void touch(Object o) { if (!added.contains(o)) changed.add(o); }","","    void flush(Db db) {","        db.begin();","        added.forEach(db::insert);","        changed.forEach(db::update);","        db.commit();","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"unit-of-work","why":"It collects new and changed objects and writes them together inside one transaction."},{"ref":"command","why":"Operations are not reified as undoable request objects; it records object sets, not actions."},{"ref":"repository","why":"There is no collection-like query interface; the class batches writes."},{"ref":"transactional-outbox","why":"No message is written alongside the data; nothing is published afterwards."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"unit-of-work","why":"It collects changes across entities and commits them as a single transaction, coalescing repeated edits."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository gives access per aggregate but does not coordinate a cross-entity atomic commit."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"Each `save()` writes immediately, so a mid-way failure leaves partial changes."},{"ref":"saga","why":"Everything is in one database and one local transaction; compensation is unnecessary overhead."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"An application service cancels an order: it updates the order, releases stock and writes an audit row, all in one database. If any write fails, none may persist, and an entity touched twice should be written once."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Db { void write(String id, int v); void commit(); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Committing inside the loop makes each write its own transaction, so a failure halfway leaves earlier rows persisted. Write all entries, then call `db.commit()` once after the loop.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Work {","    private final Map<String, Integer> dirty = new LinkedHashMap<>();","    private final Db db;","    Work(Db db) { this.db = db; }","    void markDirty(String id, int v) { dirty.put(id, v); }","    void commit() {","        for (var e : dirty.entrySet()) {","            db.write(e.getKey(), e.getValue());","            db.commit();","        }","        dirty.clear();","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Unit of Work pattern."},{"blocks":[["var work = new UnitOfWork(db);"],["Order order = work.load(id);"],["order.cancel();"],["work.registerDirty(order);"],["work.commit();"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Db {}","final class Order { void cancel() {} }","final class UnitOfWork { UnitOfWork(Db db) {} Order load(String id) { return new Order(); } void registerDirty(Order o) {} void commit() {} }","class Snippet { void run(Db db, String id) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Create the scope, load through it, mutate, register the change, and commit last. Commit publishes everything registered before it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use case so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Tracks new, dirty and removed objects","Nothing is written until commit","One transaction, batched and ordered","Short-lived and request-scoped","Local to one store; a saga spans services"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A unit of work records new, changed and removed objects during one business transaction and writes them together at commit, in a valid order, in one transaction. It coalesces repeated edits and owns rollback. It is request-scoped and local to one store, so it is not a saga and not a cache.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Unit of Work in your own words: what does it track, when does it write, and what is it not?"}],"id":"unit-of-work"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Move data between domain objects and storage with a separate mapper, so neither side knows about the other.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **data mapper** translates between rich domain objects and the storage shape: rows, documents, columns with other names. The domain class has no `save` and no SQL. Storage has no business rules. Both can evolve independently, which is the whole value.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Domain side:** invariants, value objects, behavior, no persistence code.","**Storage side:** schema, column names, denormalization.","**Mapper:** the only code that knows both shapes."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"This is how JPA/Hibernate works. The mapping metadata plays the mapper's role.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Ada <ada@old.org>\nRow[id=1, name=Ada, emailAddr=ada@new.org]\ncorrupt row: broken","file":"DataMapper.java","regions":{"client":[57,61],"domain":[5,27],"guard":[62,66],"mapper":[31,49],"row":[29,29]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\nrecord Email(String value) {\n    Email {\n        if (!value.contains(\"@\")) throw new IllegalArgumentException(value);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Customer {\n    private final long id;\n    private final String name;\n    private Email email;\n\n    Customer(long id, String name, Email email) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.name = name;\n        this.email = email;\n    }\n\n    long id() { return id; }\n    String name() { return name; }\n    Email email() { return email; }\n    void changeEmail(Email next) { this.email = next; }\n    public String toString() { return name + \" <\" + email.value() + \">\"; }\n}\n\nrecord Row(long id, String name, String emailAddr) {}\n\nfinal class CustomerMapper {\n    private final Map<Long, Row> table;\n\n    CustomerMapper(Map<Long, Row> table) { this.table = table; }\n\n    Optional<Customer> find(long id) {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(table.get(id)).map(this::toDomain);\n    }\n\n    void save(Customer c) { table.put(c.id(), toRow(c)); }\n\n    private Customer toDomain(Row r) {\n        return new Customer(r.id(), r.name(), new Email(r.emailAddr()));\n    }\n\n    private Row toRow(Customer c) {\n        return new Row(c.id(), c.name(), c.email().value());\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var table = new HashMap<Long, Row>();\n        table.put(1L, new Row(1, \"Ada\", \"ada@old.org\"));\n        table.put(2L, new Row(2, \"Bob\", \"broken\"));\n        var mapper = new CustomerMapper(table);\n        Customer ada = mapper.find(1).orElseThrow();\n        System.out.println(ada);\n        ada.changeEmail(new Email(\"ada@new.org\"));\n        mapper.save(ada);\n        System.out.println(table.get(1L));\n        try {\n            mapper.find(2);\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"corrupt row: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A domain object with an invariant-checking value object. It imports nothing about storage and has no `save`.","region":"domain"},{"note":"The storage shape: a flat record whose field `emailAddr` differs from the domain's `Email` type, standing in for a table row.","region":"row"},{"note":"The mapper owns both directions. `find` hydrates a `Customer`; `save` flattens it to a `Row`. Private methods keep conversions in one place.","region":"mapper"},{"note":"The domain object is loaded, changed through its own behavior and handed back to the mapper. It never sees the table.","region":"client"},{"note":"Hydration goes through the domain constructors, so a corrupt row fails loudly instead of creating an invalid object.","region":"guard"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Clean domain model over a legacy billing table.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A twenty-year-old table has columns like `CUST_NM`, `STAT_CD` ('A', 'S', 'C') and a split address. The new domain wants a `Customer` with a `Status` enum and an `Address` value object. A mapper converts codes to enums and merges columns into value objects. When the schema is later refactored, only the mapper changes. MyBatis result maps do the same declaratively.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Customer toDomain(BillingRow r) {","    var status = Status.fromCode(r.statCd());","    var addr = new Address(r.street(), r.zip());","    return new Customer(r.id(), r.custNm(), status, addr);","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the legacy schema leaks nowhere into business code, and the table can be migrated without touching the domain.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"svc","label":"calls","rel":"association","to":"map"},{"from":"map","label":"builds","rel":"dependency","to":"cust"},{"from":"map","label":"reads/writes","rel":"dependency","to":"row"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ rename(id, name)"],"name":"Service","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"map","kind":"class","members":["+ find(id): Optional","+ save(customer)","- toDomain(row)","- toRow(customer)"],"name":"CustomerMapper","role":"Mapper","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"cust","kind":"class","members":["- name, email","+ changeEmail(e)"],"name":"Customer","role":"Domain object","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"row","kind":"record","members":["id, name, emailAddr"],"name":"Row","role":"Storage shape","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"cust","text":"knows nothing of Row or the mapper"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The domain model is rich and shaped differently from the schema.","Domain logic must be testable without a database.","Schema and model evolve at different speeds."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Classes and tables are nearly identical and logic is thin; the mapper is pure overhead.","A small team needs rapid CRUD (see Active Record)."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Hand-written mappers drifting out of sync with the schema; cover them with round-trip tests.","Lazy loading hidden in the mapper causing N+1 queries.","Mapping that bypasses constructors and builds invalid objects."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Hand-rolled mapper for a model an ORM could map in minutes.","A mapper per class that merely copies identically named fields."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and compact constructors keep hydration validated; MapStruct can generate repetitive conversions.","Records make good storage rows and DTO shapes, with the mapper converting to richer domain classes."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Active Record puts persistence in the entity, coupling it to the row. A data mapper keeps the entity ignorant, at the price of an extra layer.","ref":"active-record","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"The repository offers the domain-facing collection API; a mapper is typically what it calls to turn rows into objects.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The unit of work tracks which objects changed; mappers perform the writes for those objects at commit.","ref":"unit-of-work","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A DTO carries data across a boundary and has no behavior. A mapper converts between shapes and may build behavior-rich domain objects.","ref":"dto","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Entity mappings translate classes to tables; entities need no save method and no base class.","name":"EntityManager","where":"Hibernate / JPA"},{"detail":"Explicit SQL plus mapping rules turn result sets into plain objects.","name":"Mapper interfaces and result maps","where":"MyBatis"},{"detail":"Maps aggregate roots to tables without a persistence context or lazy loading.","name":"Aggregate mapping","where":"Spring Data JDBC"},{"detail":"Python's ORM maps plain classes to tables separately, in the data-mapper style.","name":"Classical mapping","where":"SQLAlchemy"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","enum Rank { A, B }","record Player(long id, String name, Rank rank) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class PlayerStore {","    private final Map<Long, Object[]> table = new HashMap<>();","","    Player load(long id) {","        Object[] row = table.get(id);","        return new Player(id, (String) row[0], Rank.valueOf((String) row[1]));","    }","","    void store(Player p) {","        table.put(p.id(), new Object[] {p.name(), p.rank().name()});","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"data-mapper","why":"A separate class converts between the domain `Player` and the storage row, in both directions."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"`Player` does not load or save itself; persistence lives in another class."},{"ref":"repository","why":"There is no domain-worded collection interface with queries; the class only translates shapes."},{"ref":"dto","why":"A DTO is a passive carrier; this class contains the conversion logic."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"data-mapper","why":"A mapper isolates both shapes so schema changes touch only the mapping."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"Entities would mirror the table's shape and carry persistence, so schema changes ripple into the model."},{"ref":"dto","why":"A DTO carries data over a boundary but does not translate to and from storage."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository defines the access API but still needs a translation mechanism inside."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A domain model uses value objects, enums and no setters. The legacy table has cryptic columns and denormalized fields, and the schema will be refactored separately next quarter. The model should not change when it does."},{"compile":{"template":["record Email(String v) {}","interface CustomerMapper { void save(Customer c); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The domain object now depends on its mapper, which is the coupling the pattern removes. Drop the method and let a service call `mapper.save(customer)`.","flawLines":[9],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Customer {","    private final long id;","    private Email email;","","    Customer(long id, Email email) { this.id = id; this.email = email; }","","    void changeEmail(Email next) { this.email = next; }","","    void save(CustomerMapper mapper) { mapper.save(this); }","","    long id() { return id; }","    Email email() { return email; }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Data Mapper pattern."},{"blocks":[["long id = request.customerId();"],["Row row = table.get(id);"],["Customer customer = new Customer(row.id(), new Email(row.emailAddr()));"],["customer.changeEmail(new Email(request.newEmail()));"],["table.put(id, new Row(customer.id(), customer.email().value()));"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Email(String value) {}","record Row(long id, String emailAddr) {}","record Request(long customerId, String newEmail) {}","final class Customer { Customer(long id, Email e) {} long id() { return 0; } Email email() { return null; } void changeEmail(Email e) {} }","class Snippet { void run(Request request, Map<Long, Row> table) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Resolve the key, read the row, hydrate the domain object, change it through behavior, then flatten it back. The mapper sits at both ends.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the update flow so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Mapper translates both directions","Domain objects are persistence-ignorant","Schema and model evolve independently","Active Record couples entity and table","Cost: extra layer and mapping code"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A data mapper is a layer that converts between domain objects and storage shapes in both directions. The domain object knows nothing about persistence and storage knows nothing about behavior. Active Record instead lets the entity load and save itself, which is simpler but couples the class to its table.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Data Mapper in your own words: who knows what, and how does it differ from Active Record?"}],"id":"data-mapper"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"An object wraps one database row and carries both its data and the methods to load and save itself.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An **active record** holds a row's fields, domain behavior and persistence: static finders like `find(id)` and instance methods like `save()` and `delete()`. There is no separate mapping layer, so simple CRUD is fast to build. The price is that business logic and storage are coupled in one class.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Shape:** class mirrors the table, one instance per row.","**Persistence:** `save`, `delete` on the instance, finders on the class.","**Fits:** CRUD-heavy apps with little domain logic."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Rails ActiveRecord, Django models and Laravel Eloquent popularized this style.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"created #1\nbalance 70\nrejected: insufficient funds","file":"ActiveRecord.java","regions":{"behavior":[25,29],"client":[45,58],"finders":[16,23],"persist":[31,37],"table":[5,9]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Optional;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\nfinal class Account {\n    private static final TreeMap<Long, Long> TABLE = new TreeMap<>();\n    private static long nextId = 1;\n\n    private Long id;\n    private long balance;\n\n    private Account(Long id, long balance) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.balance = balance;\n    }\n\n    static Account open(long initial) { return new Account(null, initial); }\n\n    static Optional<Account> find(long id) {\n        Long stored = TABLE.get(id);\n        return stored == null\n            ? Optional.empty()\n            : Optional.of(new Account(id, stored));\n    }\n\n    void withdraw(long amount) {\n        if (amount > balance)\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"insufficient funds\");\n        balance -= amount;\n    }\n\n    Account save() {\n        if (id == null) id = nextId++;\n        TABLE.put(id, balance);\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    void delete() { if (id != null) TABLE.remove(id); }\n\n    Long id() { return id; }\n    long balance() { return balance; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Account created = Account.open(100).save();\n        System.out.println(\"created #\" + created.id());\n\n        Account loaded = Account.find(created.id()).orElseThrow();\n        loaded.withdraw(30);\n        loaded.save();\n        long stored = Account.find(1).orElseThrow().balance();\n        System.out.println(\"balance \" + stored);\n\n        try {\n            loaded.withdraw(500);\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The class holds its own table. A static map stands in for the database. `id` is null until the first save, which is how a record knows it is new.","region":"table"},{"note":"Class-level finders build instances from stored rows. A factory creates unsaved records. The constructor stays private so every instance is valid.","region":"finders"},{"note":"Domain behavior sits on the same class as persistence. This is both the convenience and the coupling.","region":"behavior"},{"note":"`save()` assigns an id on first call and upserts the row. The instance is responsible for its own storage.","region":"persist"},{"note":"No mapper or repository: open, save, find, change, save again. Rule violations surface as exceptions from the record itself.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Feature-flag admin screen in a back-office tool.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Flags have a name, an on/off value and a rollout percentage. The screen lists them, toggles them and that is all. No rich rules and the schema is the model. An entity with public fields and built-in persistence is the fastest correct design, and Quarkus Panache supports this style directly.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["@Entity class FeatureFlag extends PanacheEntity {","    public String name;","    public boolean enabled;","    static FeatureFlag byName(String n) {","        return find(\"name\", n).firstResult();","    }","}","FeatureFlag.byName(\"dark-mode\").enabled = true;"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a working CRUD screen with almost no layers, which is right when the domain has nothing to protect.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"calls","rel":"association","to":"acct"},{"from":"acct","label":"reads/writes","rel":"dependency","to":"tbl"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["uses the record directly"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"acct","kind":"class","members":["- id, balance","+ find(id): Optional","+ open(initial)","+ withdraw(n)","+ save()","+ delete()"],"name":"Account","role":"ActiveRecord","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"tbl","kind":"class","members":["id, balance"],"name":"accounts table","role":"Row storage","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"acct","text":"domain logic and SQL live together"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Classes map almost one-to-one onto tables.","Logic is mostly validation and CRUD.","Speed of delivery beats long-term flexibility."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Domain rules are rich and must be tested without a database.","The object model diverges from the schema (inheritance, value objects)."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Business rules scattered across models and callbacks.","Tests that need a database for every rule.","Static finders and global connection state are hard to substitute.","Leaking records straight into API responses."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Fat models with hundreds of methods: introduce services or a [Data Mapper](kata:data-mapper).","Models that call other models, send emails and run jobs inside `save()` callbacks."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["In Java this is the exception, not the norm. Quarkus Panache offers it; Spring favors repositories.","Records suit immutable values; Active Record needs mutable, identity-bearing instances, so it fits Java less naturally."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A mapper keeps entities ignorant of storage and allows the model to differ from the schema. Active Record is simpler but couples the two.","ref":"data-mapper","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A repository separates access from the entity. With Active Record, the entity's static finders are the access API.","ref":"repository","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Active Record writes on every `save()`. A unit of work tracks changes and writes them together in one transaction.","ref":"unit-of-work","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A record mirrors a table and has behavior; a DTO is a passive shape for a boundary. Expose DTOs, not records, in APIs.","ref":"dto","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The framework that named the pattern: models with `find`, `save`, `where` and validations.","name":"ActiveRecord::Base","where":"Ruby on Rails"},{"detail":"Model instances call `save()` and `delete()`; managers provide class-level queries.","name":"django.db.models.Model","where":"Django"},{"detail":"Each model corresponds to a table and persists itself.","name":"Eloquent Model","where":"Laravel"},{"detail":"Hibernate with Panache active-record style: `persist()` on the entity and static `find`, `list`.","name":"PanacheEntity","where":"Quarkus"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["final class Db { static Post select(long id) { return null; } static Long upsert(Long id, String t) { return id; } static void remove(Long id) {} }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Post {","    private Long id;","    private String title;","","    static Post find(long id) { return Db.select(id); }","    Post save() { id = Db.upsert(id, title); return this; }","    void delete() { Db.remove(id); }","    void rename(String t) { title = t.strip(); save(); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"active-record","why":"The class holds its fields, behavior and its own find, save and delete."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"No separate class translates; persistence is inside `Post` itself."},{"ref":"repository","why":"The finder is a static method on the entity, not a separate collection-like object."},{"ref":"dto","why":"A DTO has no behavior and does not persist itself."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"active-record","why":"Entities mirror tables and persist themselves; it is the lightest design that works here."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"Schema and model are the same here, so the extra mapping layer buys nothing."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"No audit or time-travel need justifies the complexity of events and projections."},{"ref":"cqrs","why":"Read and write shapes are identical; splitting models would only add code."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"An internal admin tool has ten tables that map almost one-to-one onto classes. Features are CRUD with light validation, and a team of three must ship in two weeks. There is little domain logic to protect."},{"compile":{"template":["final class Table { static long nextId() { return 1; } static void insert(Note n) {} static void update(Note n) {} }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The record always inserts, even when it already has an id, so each edit creates a duplicate row. Branch on `id`: insert when it was null, otherwise `Table.update(this)`.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Note {","    private Long id;","    private String text;","","    Note save() {","        if (id == null) id = Table.nextId();","        Table.insert(this);","        return this;","    }","","    void edit(String t) { text = t; save(); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Active Record pattern."},{"blocks":[["long id = request.accountId();"],["Account account = Account.find(id).orElseThrow();"],["account.withdraw(30);"],["account.save();"],["receipt.print(account.balance());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Request(long accountId) {}","interface Receipt { void print(long b); }","final class Account { static Optional<Account> find(long id) { return Optional.empty(); } void withdraw(long a) {} Account save() { return this; } long balance() { return 0; } }","class Snippet { void run(Request request, Receipt receipt) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Get the key, load the record, apply behavior, persist it, then report the saved state. Each step uses the previous variable.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use case so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One instance per row, owning its persistence","Finders on the class, save and delete on the instance","Fast for CRUD where model equals schema","Couples logic to storage and hampers testing","Data Mapper is the decoupled alternative"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"An active record is an object that represents one row and owns its persistence: it has fields, domain behavior, finders, and save and delete. It is the fastest route for CRUD apps whose model matches the schema. It is the wrong choice when rich domain logic must be tested without a database or the model differs from the table.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Active Record in your own words: what does the object own, and when is it the wrong choice?"}],"id":"active-record"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Capture a business rule as a small object that answers yes or no for a candidate, and combine rules with and, or and not.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **specification** has one question: `isSatisfiedBy(candidate)`. Simple rules are named and unit-tested alone. **Combinators** build complex rules from them, so `eligible = adult.and(solvent).and(notDefaulted.not())` reads like the requirement. The same rule can validate, filter and select.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Leaf specs:** one condition each.","**Composite specs:** and, or, not.","**Uses:** validation, selection, construction criteria."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"In Java, a specification is a `Predicate` with domain names. Write a dedicated type when rules need names, reuse or translation to queries.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"ana: true\nbo: false\ncy: false\ndi: false","file":"Specification.java","regions":{"compose":[34,36],"rules":[20,30],"spec":[5,18],"use":[42,44]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\nrecord Applicant(String name, int age, int income, boolean defaulted) {}\n\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface Spec<T> {\n    boolean isSatisfiedBy(T candidate);\n\n    default Spec<T> and(Spec<T> other) {\n        return c -> isSatisfiedBy(c) && other.isSatisfiedBy(c);\n    }\n    default Spec<T> or(Spec<T> other) {\n        return c -> isSatisfiedBy(c) || other.isSatisfiedBy(c);\n    }\n    default Spec<T> not() {\n        return c -> !isSatisfiedBy(c);\n    }\n}\n\nrecord AtLeastAge(int years) implements Spec<Applicant> {\n    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Applicant a) { return a.age() >= years; }\n}\n\nrecord MinIncome(int amount) implements Spec<Applicant> {\n    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Applicant a) { return a.income() >= amount; }\n}\n\nrecord HasDefaulted() implements Spec<Applicant> {\n    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Applicant a) { return a.defaulted(); }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Spec<Applicant> eligible = new AtLeastAge(18)\n                .and(new MinIncome(30_000))\n                .and(new HasDefaulted().not());\n        var people = List.of(\n            new Applicant(\"ana\", 34, 52_000, false),\n            new Applicant(\"bo\", 17, 60_000, false),\n            new Applicant(\"cy\", 45, 20_000, false),\n            new Applicant(\"di\", 29, 80_000, true));\n        for (Applicant a : people) {\n            System.out.println(a.name() + \": \" + eligible.isSatisfiedBy(a));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The interface has one abstract method; `and`, `or` and `not` are default methods returning new specs. Composition is closed: combining specs yields a spec.","region":"spec"},{"note":"Each leaf rule is a tiny record with one condition and a name from the business vocabulary. Parameters live in the record.","region":"rules"},{"note":"Rules combine into the requirement exactly as a product owner would phrase it. None of the leaves changed.","region":"compose"},{"note":"The composed rule is applied to each applicant. The caller sees a single `isSatisfiedBy` and doesn't care how complex the rule is.","region":"use"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Promotion eligibility rules edited by the marketing team.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Campaigns say: customers who spent over 200 in the last year, are not in a blocked region, and either joined before 2024 or have a referral. Each condition is a specification with its own test. A campaign is assembled from configuration, and the same specification both filters the mailing list and checks eligibility at checkout.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Spec<Customer> vip = new SpentOver(200)","    .and(new InRegion(BLOCKED).not())","    .and(new JoinedBefore(2024).or(new HasReferral()));","mailing.filter(vip);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: marketing changes a campaign by recombining tested rules, and mailing and checkout can't disagree.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"age","rel":"realizes","to":"spec"},{"from":"inc","rel":"realizes","to":"spec"},{"from":"def","rel":"realizes","to":"spec"},{"from":"spec","label":"tests","rel":"dependency","to":"app"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"spec","kind":"interface","members":["+ isSatisfiedBy(c)","+ and(other)","+ or(other)","+ not()"],"name":"Spec<T>","role":"Specification","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"age","kind":"record","members":["+ years"],"name":"AtLeastAge","role":"Leaf spec","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"inc","kind":"record","members":["+ amount"],"name":"MinIncome","role":"Leaf spec","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"def","kind":"record","members":[],"name":"HasDefaulted","role":"Leaf spec","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"app","kind":"record","members":["+ age, income","+ defaulted"],"name":"Applicant","role":"Candidate","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"spec","text":"and/or/not return new specs"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Rules are numerous, change often, and are combined in many ways.","One rule must serve validation, filtering and query building.","Business experts need rules named in the domain's vocabulary."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["There are two fixed conditions: an `if` is clearer.","A plain `Predicate` with a lambda already says it well."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Rules that perform I/O inside `isSatisfiedBy`, making them slow and untestable.","In-memory filtering of huge sets when the rule should become a database query.","Deep combinator chains that hide a rule's meaning; name the combined spec."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Specs wrapping single trivial comparisons that appear once.","A generic spec framework before any rule is duplicated."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`java.util.function.Predicate` already has `and`, `or` and `negate`.","Sealed interfaces plus records make an inspectable rule tree that can be translated to SQL."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Both are small interchangeable objects with one method. A strategy computes a result to use; a specification returns a boolean and is designed to be combined.","ref":"strategy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Composite specs are a Composite: and, or and not treat single and combined rules uniformly.","ref":"composite","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Interpreter models a rule language with its own grammar and parser. A specification is a lighter, code-level rule combinator.","ref":"interpreter","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A chain lets the first handler that accepts act. A specification composes all conditions into one verdict.","ref":"chain-of-responsibility","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A repository can accept a specification as its query criteria, so the rule drives both memory and database filtering.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Composable `where`, `and`, `or`, `not` predicates executed via `JpaSpecificationExecutor`.","name":"org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification","where":"Spring Data JPA"},{"detail":"The Criteria API builds composable, type-safe conditions that become SQL.","name":"CriteriaBuilder and Predicate","where":"Jakarta Persistence"},{"detail":"Provides `and`, `or`, `negate`; the lightweight form of a specification.","name":"java.util.function.Predicate","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Static `and`, `or`, `not` combinators for predicates.","name":"com.google.common.base.Predicates","where":"Guava"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Instant;","record Coupon(Instant endsAt, int minSpend) {}","class Snippet { static void run(Instant now) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["interface Rule<T> {","    boolean test(T t);","    default Rule<T> and(Rule<T> o) { return t -> test(t) && o.test(t); }","    default Rule<T> not() { return t -> !test(t); }","}","record Expired(Instant now) implements Rule<Coupon> {","    public boolean test(Coupon c) { return c.endsAt().isBefore(now); }","}","record MinSpend(int amount) implements Rule<Coupon> {","    public boolean test(Coupon c) { return c.minSpend() <= amount; }","}","Rule<Coupon> usable = new Expired(now).not().and(new MinSpend(50));"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"specification","why":"Named boolean rules that combine with and/not into one verdict about a candidate."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"Strategies compute a value for the caller; these exist to be composed into a yes-or-no answer."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"Nothing passes the request along; all rules are combined into a single condition."},{"ref":"composite","why":"Combination is present, but the decisive trait is the boolean rule-over-candidate role."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"specification","why":"Named, testable rules combined with and/or/not and reused for validation and selection."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"Strategy picks one algorithm; it doesn't compose several conditions into one verdict."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"A chain stops at the first handler rather than combining all rules, and doesn't serve selection."},{"ref":"state","why":"Nothing changes behavior over a lifecycle; the problem is combining independent conditions."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Product owners keep changing loan-eligibility rules and combining them differently per market. The same rules must reject bad applications and select candidates for a retention mailing."},{"compile":{"template":["{{code}}"]},"explanation":"`and` uses `||`, so it behaves like `or` and accepts candidates that fail one rule. Combine with `&&`.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["@FunctionalInterface","interface Spec<T> {","    boolean isSatisfiedBy(T candidate);","","    default Spec<T> and(Spec<T> other) {","        return c -> isSatisfiedBy(c) || other.isSatisfiedBy(c);","    }","    default Spec<T> not() {","        return c -> !isSatisfiedBy(c);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Specification pattern."},{"blocks":[["Spec<Applicant> adult = new AtLeastAge(18);","Spec<Applicant> solvent = new MinIncome(30_000);"],["Spec<Applicant> eligible = adult.and(solvent);"],["Applicant ana = new Applicant(\"ana\", 34, 52_000, false);","boolean ok = eligible.isSatisfiedBy(ana);"],["System.out.println(ok);"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Spec<T> { boolean isSatisfiedBy(T t); default Spec<T> and(Spec<T> o) { return t -> isSatisfiedBy(t) && o.isSatisfiedBy(t); } }","record Applicant(String n, int a, int i, boolean d) {}","record AtLeastAge(int y) implements Spec<Applicant> { public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Applicant a) { return a.a() >= y; } }","record MinIncome(int m) implements Spec<Applicant> { public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Applicant a) { return a.i() >= m; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Leaf rules come first, then the combination that depends on them, then the evaluation that needs both the combined rule and a candidate.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One question: isSatisfiedBy(candidate)","Named leaf rules, each tested alone","and/or/not return new specs","Reused for validation, filtering and queries","Predicate suffices for simple, local rules"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A specification is an object that decides whether a candidate satisfies one business rule. Small named specs combine through and, or and not into larger rules, and can be reused for validation, filtering and query building. A plain Predicate is enough when rules are few, unshared and need no names or translation to queries.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Specification pattern: what a spec is, how rules combine, and when a plain Predicate is enough."}],"id":"specification"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Move data across a boundary in a simple object shaped for that boundary, not in your domain object.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **DTO** has fields and no behavior. It exists so that a remote call, a screen or a message gets exactly the data it needs in a stable shape. An **assembler** or mapper converts between DTOs and domain objects at the edge, keeping internals, secrets and lazy associations from leaking.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Shape:** one DTO per use case or contract.","**Content:** primitives, strings, nested DTOs, no entities.","**Direction:** request DTOs in, response DTOs out."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A Java record is a natural DTO: immutable, equality by value, and a compact constructor for boundary validation.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"UserSummary[id=7, displayName=Ada Lovelace, admin=false]\nrejected: email","file":"Dto.java","regions":{"dtos":[28,36],"entity":[3,26],"flow":[52,54],"mapper":[38,48]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.List;\n\nfinal class User {\n    private final long id;\n    private String firstName;\n    private String lastName;\n    private String email;\n    private String passwordHash;\n    private List<String> roles;\n\n    User(long id, String first, String last, String email,\n         String hash, List<String> roles) {\n        this.id = id;\n        this.firstName = first;\n        this.lastName = last;\n        this.email = email;\n        this.passwordHash = hash;\n        this.roles = roles;\n    }\n\n    long id() { return id; }\n    String firstName() { return firstName; }\n    String lastName() { return lastName; }\n    String email() { return email; }\n    boolean isAdmin() { return roles.contains(\"ADMIN\"); }\n}\n\nrecord UserSummary(long id, String displayName, boolean admin) {}\n\nrecord RegisterRequest(String firstName, String lastName, String email) {\n    RegisterRequest {\n        if (email == null || !email.contains(\"@\")) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"email\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class UserMapper {\n    static UserSummary toSummary(User u) {\n        return new UserSummary(\n            u.id(), u.firstName() + \" \" + u.lastName(), u.isAdmin());\n    }\n\n    static User toEntity(long id, RegisterRequest r) {\n        return new User(id, r.firstName(), r.lastName(), r.email(),\n                \"!\", List.of(\"USER\"));\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var request = new RegisterRequest(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\", \"ada@x.org\");\n        User user = UserMapper.toEntity(7, request);\n        System.out.println(UserMapper.toSummary(user));\n        try {\n            new RegisterRequest(\"Bo\", \"Ng\", \"nope\");\n        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The domain object has private state, including a password hash and roles. It must never cross the API boundary as is.","region":"entity"},{"note":"Two records: an outbound summary with only what the client needs, and an inbound request that validates at construction.","region":"dtos"},{"note":"The assembler is the only code that knows both shapes. It converts the entity to the summary and a request into a new entity.","region":"mapper"},{"note":"The boundary code deals only in DTOs; the entity is created from the request and summarized for output.","region":"flow"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A public REST API over a legacy orders schema.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The database has 40 columns per order, internal status codes and lazy-loaded relations. The public contract exposes `OrderResponse` with id, status text, line items and totals, versioned as `v1` and `v2`. A mapper isolates the legacy schema; renaming a column or adding an internal flag leaves `v1` clients untouched.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["record OrderResponseV1(","    String id, String status, Money total,","    List<LineDto> lines) {}","OrderResponseV1 body = OrderMapper.v1(order);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the API contract evolves on its own schedule, and internal fields or lazy proxies can't leak into JSON.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"map","label":"reads","rel":"dependency","to":"req"},{"from":"map","label":"creates","rel":"dependency","to":"sum"},{"from":"map","label":"reads, creates","rel":"dependency","to":"user"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"req","kind":"record","members":["+ firstName","+ lastName","+ email"],"name":"RegisterRequest","role":"DTO in","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"sum","kind":"record","members":["+ id","+ displayName","+ admin"],"name":"UserSummary","role":"DTO out","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"map","kind":"class","members":["+ toSummary(User)","+ toEntity(id, req)"],"name":"UserMapper","role":"Assembler","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"user","kind":"class","members":["- passwordHash","- roles"],"name":"User","role":"Domain entity","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"sum","text":"no behavior, no secrets"}],"title":"Class"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["You expose an API whose contract must outlive the domain model.","One call should return data from several aggregates in a single round trip.","Entities hold secrets, lazy associations or cycles."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Layers are in the same process and the entity is already the right shape.","A DTO would be a field-for-field copy of an entity with no boundary reason."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Behavior added to DTOs, which turns them into a second domain model.","Mapping code scattered across controllers instead of one assembler.","Reusing one DTO for requests and responses, coupling both directions."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Copies of the same fields at every layer: entity, model, dto, view, resource.","Mappers with `null` checks for every field because no one owns the contract."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records replace most hand-written DTO classes.","Sealed interfaces model a versioned payload that has several variants."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A data mapper moves data between entities and the database. A DTO assembler does the same between entities and the API. Different edges, same discipline.","ref":"data-mapper","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A facade exposes a coarse-grained service API and typically returns DTOs so callers never see domain internals.","ref":"facade","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Both snapshot state in a plain object. A memento is opaque and exists to restore its originator; a DTO is readable and exists to inform another party.","ref":"memento","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"DTOs are best immutable so they can be shared across threads and cached safely.","ref":"immutable-object","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A repository returns domain aggregates to the domain. DTOs are produced after it, at the outer boundary, not by it.","ref":"repository","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Final since Java 16: concise immutable carriers well suited to DTOs.","name":"Records (JEP 395)","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Generates entity-to-DTO mapping code at compile time.","name":"@Mapper","where":"MapStruct"},{"detail":"Repositories can return a projection type containing only the requested properties.","name":"Interface-based projections","where":"Spring Data"},{"detail":"Schema-defined, versionable transfer objects shared across services and languages.","name":"Generated message classes","where":"Protocol Buffers"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.List;","record Customer(String name) {}","record Money() { String format() { return \"\"; } }","record Line() { String label() { return \"\"; } }","record Order(long id, Customer customer, Money total, List<Line> lines) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["record OrderPayload(long id, String customerName, String total, List<String> lines) {","","    static OrderPayload from(Order o) {","        return new OrderPayload(","            o.id(),","            o.customer().name(),","            o.total().format(),","            o.lines().stream().map(Line::label).toList());","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dto","why":"An immutable, behavior-free carrier shaped for a consumer, with a conversion from the domain object."},{"ref":"memento","why":"A memento captures state to restore the originator later; this one is a readable projection for someone else."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"The mapper's job is database persistence; this converts a domain object to a transfer shape."},{"ref":"active-record","why":"There is no persistence logic: the object can't save or load itself."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"dto","why":"A versioned response shape insulates clients from entity changes and keeps secrets and proxies out of the payload."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"It maps objects to tables, solving persistence rather than the API contract."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade simplifies the call, but you'd still need a defined response shape."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository returns entities to the domain; it doesn't decide what the client sees."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A mobile client calls your service. The `Account` entity has a password hash, lazy-loaded transactions, and internal fields that change monthly. The mobile contract must stay stable across versions."},{"compile":{"template":["record User(long id, String name, String email) {}","interface UserRepository { User find(long id); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"A DTO that reaches back into a repository carries behavior and a dependency across the boundary. Keep it a pure data carrier; load entities in the service.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["record UserSummary(long id, String displayName, String email) {","    UserSummary {","        if (email == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"email\");","    }","","    User load(UserRepository repo) { return repo.find(id); }","","    static UserSummary from(User u) {","        return new UserSummary(u.id(), u.name(), u.email());","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the DTO pattern."},{"blocks":[["var request = new RegisterRequest(\"Ada\", \"Lovelace\", \"ada@x.org\");"],["User user = UserMapper.toEntity(7, request);"],["UserSummary out = UserMapper.toSummary(user);"],["System.out.println(out);"]],"compile":{"template":["record RegisterRequest(String a, String b, String c) {}","record User() {}","record UserSummary() {}","class UserMapper { static User toEntity(long id, RegisterRequest r) { return new User(); } static UserSummary toSummary(User u) { return new UserSummary(); } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"The inbound DTO comes first and is mapped into an entity, which is then mapped to the outbound DTO that is finally emitted.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the statements so that each one only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Plain data carrier with no behavior","Shaped for the boundary or use case","Mapper converts at the edge","Entities leak secrets and lazy state","Records are the idiomatic Java form"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A DTO is a plain, ideally immutable object that carries data across a boundary in a shape defined for that boundary. A mapper at the edge converts between DTOs and domain objects. Exposing entities leaks secrets, lazy proxies and internal structure, and couples the public contract to your model.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain DTOs: what they carry, where they are created, and why not expose entities directly."}],"id":"dto"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Record that something meaningful happened in the domain, as an immutable past-tense fact other parts can react to.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **domain event** such as `OrderPlaced` is raised by an aggregate when its state changes. It names a fact, not a request, and carries only the data about it. Handlers react in their own modules, so the aggregate never learns who is listening. That keeps the core small and the reactions decoupled.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Named in the past tense:** `OrderPlaced`, not `PlaceOrder`.","**Immutable:** a record of what happened.","**Raised by the aggregate,** dispatched after the state is saved."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Decide the delivery semantics: synchronous in the same transaction, or after commit. They fail differently.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"saved o-1\nmail: confirm o-1\nledger: book 4200\nmail: shipped o-1","file":"DomainEvent.java","regions":{"aggregate":[11,35],"bus":[37,51],"client":[62,65],"events":[7,9]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.function.Consumer;\n\nsealed interface DomainEvent permits OrderPlaced, OrderShipped {}\nrecord OrderPlaced(String orderId, int totalCents) implements DomainEvent {}\nrecord OrderShipped(String orderId) implements DomainEvent {}\n\nfinal class Order {\n    private final String id;\n    private final List<DomainEvent> events = new ArrayList<>();\n    private boolean shipped;\n\n    private Order(String id) { this.id = id; }\n\n    static Order place(String id, int totalCents) {\n        var order = new Order(id);\n        order.events.add(new OrderPlaced(id, totalCents));\n        return order;\n    }\n\n    void ship() {\n        if (shipped) throw new IllegalStateException(\"already shipped\");\n        shipped = true;\n        events.add(new OrderShipped(id));\n    }\n\n    List<DomainEvent> pullEvents() {\n        var out = List.copyOf(events);\n        events.clear();\n        return out;\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class EventBus {\n    private final Map<Class<?>, List<Consumer<DomainEvent>>> handlers =\n        new HashMap<>();\n\n    <E extends DomainEvent> void on(Class<E> type, Consumer<E> handler) {\n        handlers.computeIfAbsent(type, t -> new ArrayList<>())\n            .add(e -> handler.accept(type.cast(e)));\n    }\n\n    void publish(List<DomainEvent> events) {\n        for (var e : events)\n            handlers.getOrDefault(e.getClass(), List.of())\n                .forEach(h -> h.accept(e));\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static void say(String s) { System.out.println(s); }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var bus = new EventBus();\n        bus.on(OrderPlaced.class, e -> say(\"mail: confirm \" + e.orderId()));\n        bus.on(OrderPlaced.class, e -> say(\"ledger: book \" + e.totalCents()));\n        bus.on(OrderShipped.class, e -> say(\"mail: shipped \" + e.orderId()));\n\n        var order = Order.place(\"o-1\", 4200);\n        order.ship();\n        System.out.println(\"saved o-1\");\n        bus.publish(order.pullEvents());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Events are records in a sealed hierarchy: immutable, past tense, minimal data. Sealing lets handlers switch exhaustively.","region":"events"},{"note":"The aggregate records events as it changes state and exposes `pullEvents()`, which returns and clears them. It knows no handlers.","region":"aggregate"},{"note":"A tiny type-routed bus. `on` registers a typed handler; `publish` dispatches each event to handlers of its exact class, in registration order.","region":"bus"},{"note":"Change state, persist, then publish. Publishing after the save keeps handlers from reacting to a change that never committed.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Patient discharge in a hospital system.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Discharging a patient must trigger billing, bed cleaning and a pharmacy hand-off. The ward module should not import those modules. The `Patient` aggregate emits `PatientDischarged(patientId, bedId, at)`. Each department subscribes and reacts on its own. Adding a transport-booking step later means one more handler, not a change to ward code. Spring Modulith publishes such events between modules.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["record PatientDischarged(PatientId id, BedId bed, Instant at) {}","","@ApplicationModuleListener","void on(PatientDischarged e) { housekeeping.clean(e.bed()); }"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: modules stay independent; reactions are added or removed without editing the code that raised the event.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"agg","label":"raises","rel":"dependency","to":"evt"},{"from":"bus","label":"routes","rel":"dependency","to":"evt"},{"from":"bus","rel":"association","to":"h1"},{"from":"bus","rel":"association","to":"h2"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"agg","kind":"class","members":["- events: List","+ ship()","+ pullEvents()"],"name":"Order","role":"Aggregate","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"evt","kind":"interface","members":["OrderPlaced","OrderShipped"],"name":"DomainEvent","role":"Event","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"bus","kind":"class","members":["+ on(type, handler)","+ publish(events)"],"name":"EventBus","role":"Dispatcher","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"h1","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(event)"],"name":"MailHandler","role":"Handler","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"h2","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(event)"],"name":"LedgerHandler","role":"Handler","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"s","label":"ship()","style":"call","to":"o"},{"from":"o","label":"record OrderShipped","style":"call","to":"o"},{"from":"s","label":"save(order)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"pullEvents()","style":"call","to":"o"},{"from":"o","label":"[OrderShipped]","style":"return","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"publish(events)","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"handle(e)","style":"call","to":"h"}],"participants":[{"id":"o","name":"Order","role":"Aggregate"},{"id":"s","name":"Service"},{"id":"b","name":"EventBus","role":"Dispatcher"},{"id":"h","name":"Handler","role":"Handler"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Side effects belong to other modules or bounded contexts.","A business fact matters beyond the aggregate that produced it.","You want an audit trail or integration hook without coupling."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["One caller and one effect; a direct method call is clearer.","The reaction must succeed or fail with the original change and you cannot afford eventual consistency."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Publishing before the state change commits, so handlers act on rolled-back facts.","Events shaped like commands or like database rows.","Losing events when a handler fails after commit; pair with an outbox.","Hidden control flow: no single place shows what reacts."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An event for every setter. Events should mark facts that matter to the business.","Events that carry whole aggregates instead of the few facts handlers need."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and sealed interfaces fit events well; `switch` pattern matching handles them exhaustively.","Records and sealed interfaces give exhaustive, immutable event types."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Observer wires listeners to an object in-process. A domain event is a named business fact, often dispatched after commit and possibly across modules.","ref":"observer","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Domain events notify others. Event sourcing makes the event stream the source of truth, from which state is rebuilt.","ref":"event-sourcing","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"To avoid losing events between commit and publish, write them to an outbox in the same transaction.","ref":"transactional-outbox","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A command asks for something and can be rejected. An event reports what already happened and cannot be refused.","ref":"command","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A mediator coordinates colleagues it knows about. An event publisher does not know its subscribers.","ref":"mediator","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Publishes application events to `@EventListener` methods inside the context.","name":"ApplicationEventPublisher","where":"Spring Framework"},{"detail":"Aggregates register events; Spring Data publishes them when the repository saves.","name":"AbstractAggregateRoot and @DomainEvents","where":"Spring Data"},{"detail":"Delivers an event after commit (by default), so handlers see persisted state.","name":"@TransactionalEventListener","where":"Spring"},{"detail":"Event listener between application modules, with event publication tracking.","name":"@ApplicationModuleListener","where":"Spring Modulith"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record CartCheckedOut(long id) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Cart {","    private final long id = 7;","    private final List<Object> pending = new ArrayList<>();","    private boolean checkedOut;","","    void checkout() {","        if (checkedOut) throw new IllegalStateException();","        checkedOut = true;","        pending.add(new CartCheckedOut(id));","    }","","    List<Object> drain() { var out = List.copyOf(pending); pending.clear(); return out; }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"domain-event","why":"The aggregate records an immutable, past-tense fact and hands it out for others to react to."},{"ref":"observer","why":"No subscribers are registered with the cart; it only stores facts to be collected."},{"ref":"command","why":"`CartCheckedOut` is a fact that already happened, not a request to do something."},{"ref":"memento","why":"It captures what happened, not a snapshot of state for later restoration."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"domain-event","why":"Billing emits `PaymentCaptured`; other modules subscribe, so reactions are added without editing billing."},{"ref":"command","why":"Sending commands to named modules makes billing depend on them and encodes the reactions in billing."},{"ref":"mediator","why":"A mediator would have to know every colleague and be edited for each new reaction."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade offers a simplified API; it does not decouple producers from their reactions."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"When a payment is captured, receipts must be sent and the ledger updated. The billing module must not depend on those modules, new reactions will be added later, and the capture itself is a fact worth recording."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Bookings { Booking load(String id); void save(Booking b); }","interface EventBus { void publish(List<Object> events); }","class Booking { void confirm() {} List<Object> pullEvents() { return List.of(); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Handlers run before the change is persisted, so a failing save leaves them reacting to a fact that never became true. Save first, then publish (ideally via an outbox).","flawLines":[12],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class ConfirmBooking {","    private final Bookings bookings;","    private final EventBus bus;","","    ConfirmBooking(Bookings bookings, EventBus bus) {","        this.bookings = bookings; this.bus = bus;","    }","","    void handle(String id) {","        Booking b = bookings.load(id);","        b.confirm();","        bus.publish(b.pullEvents());","        bookings.save(b);","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Domain Event pattern."},{"blocks":[["String id = request.orderId();"],["Order order = orders.load(id);"],["order.ship();"],["orders.save(order);"],["bus.publish(order.pullEvents());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Request(String orderId) {}","final class Order { void ship() {} List<Object> pullEvents() { return List.of(); } }","interface Orders { Order load(String id); void save(Order o); }","interface EventBus { void publish(List<Object> events); }","class Snippet { void run(Request request, Orders orders, EventBus bus) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Load the aggregate, let it change and record the event, persist the new state, and only then publish what it recorded.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use case so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Immutable past-tense fact, not a request","Raised by the aggregate, which knows no handlers","Handlers react independently","Dispatch after commit to avoid phantom facts","Not the same as event sourcing"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A domain event is an immutable, past-tense record of a business fact, such as `OrderPlaced`. The aggregate raises it when its state changes, without knowing who listens. Other modules handle it independently. Dispatch after the state is saved, ideally through an outbox, so handlers never react to a change that rolled back.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Domain Event in your own words: what is it, who raises it, and when should it be dispatched?"}],"id":"domain-event"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Use one model to change state and a different one to read it, each shaped for its job.","type":"lead"},{"text":"**CQRS** splits the write side, which validates commands and protects invariants, from the read side, which serves queries from denormalized models built for the screens. Queries never change state, commands return little. The read model is usually updated from events and may lag behind the write model.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Commands:** intent to change state; may be rejected.","**Queries:** side-effect free, shaped for the consumer.","**Bridge:** events or change feeds update read models, often asynchronously."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Eventual consistency is the price: a user may read stale data right after writing it.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"before: 0\nafter: 2500\n[ada=2500, bob=700]\nrejected: duplicate o1","file":"Cqrs.java","regions":{"client":[51,57],"messages":[8,9],"read":[25,45],"write":[11,23]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\nrecord PlaceOrder(String orderId, String customer, int cents) {}\nrecord OrderPlaced(String orderId, String customer, int cents) {}\n\nfinal class OrderCommands {\n    private final Set<String> ids = new HashSet<>();\n    private final List<OrderPlaced> log = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    void handle(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n        if (cmd.cents() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount\");\n        if (!ids.add(cmd.orderId()))\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"duplicate \" + cmd.orderId());\n        log.add(new OrderPlaced(cmd.orderId(), cmd.customer(), cmd.cents()));\n    }\n\n    List<OrderPlaced> log() { return List.copyOf(log); }\n}\n\nfinal class RevenueView {\n    private final Map<String, Integer> byCustomer = new HashMap<>();\n    private int applied;\n\n    void catchUp(List<OrderPlaced> log) {\n        for (; applied < log.size(); applied++) {\n            var e = log.get(applied);\n            byCustomer.merge(e.customer(), e.cents(), Integer::sum);\n        }\n    }\n\n    int revenue(String customer) {\n        return byCustomer.getOrDefault(customer, 0);\n    }\n\n    List<String> top(int n) {\n        return byCustomer.entrySet().stream()\n            .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Integer>comparingByValue().reversed())\n            .limit(n).map(e -> e.getKey() + \"=\" + e.getValue()).toList();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var commands = new OrderCommands();\n        var view = new RevenueView();\n        commands.handle(new PlaceOrder(\"o1\", \"ada\", 1500));\n        commands.handle(new PlaceOrder(\"o2\", \"bob\", 700));\n        commands.handle(new PlaceOrder(\"o3\", \"ada\", 1000));\n        System.out.println(\"before: \" + view.revenue(\"ada\"));\n        view.catchUp(commands.log());\n        System.out.println(\"after: \" + view.revenue(\"ada\"));\n        System.out.println(view.top(2));\n        try {\n            commands.handle(new PlaceOrder(\"o1\", \"eve\", 100));\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Two message kinds: a command states intent and may fail; an event states a fact. They are separate types because they have different lifetimes.","region":"messages"},{"note":"The write side validates, enforces uniqueness and appends an event. It has no query methods apart from exposing its event log.","region":"write"},{"note":"The read side is a denormalized revenue-per-customer map. `catchUp` applies only events it has not seen, like a projector following a feed.","region":"read"},{"note":"Reading before `catchUp` returns 0: the read model lags by design. After it catches up, queries see all three orders.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Seat availability search for an airline.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Booking a seat needs strict locking, fare rules and payment checks on a normalized relational model. Search must answer 'which flights have two adjacent seats on Friday' in milliseconds for thousands of users. The booking service stays authoritative, and each confirmed booking emits an event that updates a denormalized search index. Search tolerates a second of lag, booking never accepts one.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["bookingService.handle(new HoldSeat(flight, seat, user));","// event: SeatHeld -> projector -> search index","List<FlightHit> hits = searchIndex.findAdjacent(date, 2);"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: each side scales and evolves independently; search latency no longer competes with booking correctness.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"ui","label":"commands","rel":"association","to":"cmd"},{"from":"ui","label":"queries","rel":"association","to":"qry"},{"from":"cmd","label":"emits","rel":"dependency","to":"evt"},{"from":"qry","label":"projects","rel":"dependency","to":"evt"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"ui","kind":"class","members":["sends commands","asks queries"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"cmd","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(PlaceOrder)","- ids: Set","- log: List"],"name":"OrderCommands","role":"Command side","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"qry","kind":"class","members":["+ revenue(customer)","+ top(n)","+ catchUp(log)"],"name":"RevenueView","role":"Query side","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"evt","kind":"record","members":["orderId, customer, cents"],"name":"OrderPlaced","role":"Event","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"handle(PlaceOrder)","style":"call","to":"w"},{"from":"w","label":"validate, append event","style":"call","to":"w"},{"from":"c","label":"revenue(ada)","style":"call","to":"r"},{"from":"r","label":"0 (stale)","style":"return","to":"c"},{"from":"w","label":"catchUp(log)","style":"async","to":"r"},{"from":"c","label":"revenue(ada)","style":"call","to":"r"},{"from":"r","label":"2500","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Client"},{"id":"w","name":"Commands","role":"Command side"},{"id":"r","name":"RevenueView","role":"Query side"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Read and write workloads differ sharply in shape or scale.","Queries need joins or aggregates the write model would struggle to serve.","Different teams own the command and query sides."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The domain is plain CRUD; one model is simpler and enough.","Users must read their own writes immediately everywhere."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Showing stale data after a write without telling the user.","Read models that cannot be rebuilt from a source of truth.","Separate stores adopted before a real need appears.","Queries that quietly mutate state."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Two services and a message bus for a screen that one SQL view would serve.","A command bus and projections for an app with five CRUD screens."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["You can apply CQRS in one process: separate command handlers and query classes over a single database first.","Records fit commands, events and read-model rows; virtual threads make simple synchronous projectors cheap."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"CQRS needs a feed to update read models; event sourcing supplies it. Each works alone: CQRS with plain state tables, event sourcing with one read model.","ref":"event-sourcing","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Domain events are the usual bridge that updates the read model after a command succeeds.","ref":"domain-event","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An outbox makes the write-to-read feed reliable without losing events on crash.","ref":"transactional-outbox","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A cache is a disposable copy filled on demand. A read model is a purpose-built store that is kept current by projections.","ref":"cache-aside","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A CQRS command is a message expressing intent. The GoF Command is an object encapsulating an operation to run or undo.","ref":"command","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Route commands to aggregates and queries to projections as separate messaging paths.","name":"CommandGateway and QueryGateway","where":"Axon Framework"},{"detail":"Separate command and query handlers in one process, a common starting point for CQRS.","name":"IRequest and INotification","where":"MediatR (.NET)"},{"detail":"A precomputed read model refreshed with `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW`.","name":"MATERIALIZED VIEW","where":"PostgreSQL"},{"detail":"Materializes a queryable read model from an event topic.","name":"KTable","where":"Kafka Streams"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Profile(String name) { Profile renamed(String n) { return new Profile(n); } }","record Renamed(UUID id, String name) {}","record ProfileCard(String name) {}","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class ProfileCommands {","    private final Map<UUID, Profile> writeStore = new HashMap<>();","    private final List<Renamed> feed = new ArrayList<>();","    void rename(UUID id, String name) {","        Profile p = writeStore.get(id).renamed(name);","        writeStore.put(id, p);","        feed.add(new Renamed(id, name));","    }","}","final class ProfileQueries {","    private final Map<UUID, ProfileCard> cards = new HashMap<>();","    ProfileCard card(UUID id) { return cards.get(id); }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"cqrs","why":"State changes go through a write model, reads come from a separate, denormalized model fed by the changes."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository offers one collection-like abstraction for both loading and storing; here they are split."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"State is stored directly in `writeStore`; the feed is a notification, not the source of truth."},{"ref":"dto","why":"`ProfileCard` is a read shape, but the pattern is the split of models, not the carrier."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"cqrs","why":"A separate, denormalized read model tuned for the dashboard removes read load from the write model."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository hides storage but does not change the model, so the six-table join remains."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"It changes how state is stored, not how reads are served; you would still need read models."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"Mapping is orthogonal; it does not provide a faster read shape."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A reporting dashboard is slow: it joins six tables on the same normalized model that serves transactional writes. Reads outnumber writes 100 to 1, and the business accepts a few seconds of staleness."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Received(String sku, int qty) {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"A query decrements stock, so reading changes state. Queries must be side-effect free; make reserving a command on the write side and let the view update from its event.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class StockView {","    private final Map<String, Integer> onHand = new HashMap<>();","","    int available(String sku) {","        onHand.merge(sku, -1, Integer::sum);","        return onHand.getOrDefault(sku, 0);","    }","","    void on(Received e) { onHand.merge(e.sku(), e.qty(), Integer::sum); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the CQRS pattern."},{"blocks":[["var commands = new OrderCommands();","var view = new RevenueView();"],["commands.handle(new PlaceOrder(\"o9\", \"ada\", 500));"],["view.catchUp(commands.log());"],["int total = view.revenue(\"ada\");"],["System.out.println(total);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record PlaceOrder(String id, String customer, int cents) {}","final class OrderCommands { void handle(PlaceOrder c) {} List<String> log() { return List.of(); } }","final class RevenueView { void catchUp(List<String> log) {} int revenue(String c) { return 0; } }","class Snippet { void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"A command appends an event first; the view can only project events that exist; the query reads the projected result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the flow so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Separate write model and read models","Commands change state; queries never do","Events or feeds update read models","Cost: eventual consistency and complexity","Useful only when read and write needs diverge"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"CQRS separates the model that handles commands and protects invariants from the models that serve queries. Read models are denormalized for consumers and updated from events or a change feed. The cost is eventual consistency and extra moving parts, so use it only when read and write needs genuinely diverge.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain CQRS in your own words: what is split, what keeps the two sides in sync, and what is the cost?"}],"id":"cqrs"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Store the sequence of events that happened, and derive current state by replaying them.","type":"lead"},{"text":"In **event sourcing** the append-only event log is the source of truth. An aggregate's state is a fold over its events. New state comes from commands that validate against the current state and append new events. You gain a full audit trail and the ability to rebuild state at any point in time. Current state is a projection, not the record.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Write:** append events; never update or delete.","**Read state:** replay events, or start from a snapshot.","**Concurrency:** append with an expected version."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Events are immutable facts. Mistakes are corrected by appending compensating events.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"70 at v2\n100 at v1\nconflict: expected v2, at v3","file":"EventSourcing.java","regions":{"client":[65,72],"commands":[45,56],"events":[6,8],"replay":[31,43],"store":[10,24]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nsealed interface Event permits Deposited, Withdrawn {}\nrecord Deposited(int cents) implements Event {}\nrecord Withdrawn(int cents) implements Event {}\n\nfinal class EventStore {\n    private final Map<String, List<Event>> streams = new HashMap<>();\n\n    List<Event> load(String id) {\n        return List.copyOf(streams.getOrDefault(id, List.of()));\n    }\n\n    void append(String id, int expected, List<Event> events) {\n        var s = streams.computeIfAbsent(id, k -> new ArrayList<>());\n        if (s.size() != expected)\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\n                \"conflict: expected v\" + expected + \", at v\" + s.size());\n        s.addAll(events);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Account {\n    private final List<Event> fresh = new ArrayList<>();\n    private int balance;\n    private int version;\n\n    static Account replay(List<Event> history) {\n        var account = new Account();\n        history.forEach(account::apply);\n        return account;\n    }\n\n    private void apply(Event e) {\n        switch (e) {\n            case Deposited d -> balance += d.cents();\n            case Withdrawn w -> balance -= w.cents();\n        }\n        version++;\n    }\n\n    void deposit(int cents) { record(new Deposited(cents)); }\n    void withdraw(int cents) {\n        if (cents > balance) throw new IllegalStateException(\"funds\");\n        record(new Withdrawn(cents));\n    }\n\n    private void record(Event e) { apply(e); fresh.add(e); }\n\n    void saveTo(EventStore store, String id) {\n        store.append(id, version - fresh.size(), fresh);\n        fresh.clear();\n    }\n\n    int balance() { return balance; }\n    int version() { return version; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var store = new EventStore();\n        var acc = Account.replay(store.load(\"acc-1\"));\n        acc.deposit(100);\n        acc.withdraw(30);\n        acc.saveTo(store, \"acc-1\");\n        var now = Account.replay(store.load(\"acc-1\"));\n        System.out.println(now.balance() + \" at v\" + now.version());\n        var then = Account.replay(store.load(\"acc-1\").subList(0, 1));\n        System.out.println(then.balance() + \" at v\" + then.version());\n        var a1 = Account.replay(store.load(\"acc-1\"));\n        var a2 = Account.replay(store.load(\"acc-1\"));\n        a1.deposit(10);\n        a2.deposit(5);\n        a1.saveTo(store, \"acc-1\");\n        try { a2.saveTo(store, \"acc-1\"); }\n        catch (IllegalStateException e) { System.out.println(e.getMessage()); }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Events are immutable records in a sealed hierarchy: what happened, in the past tense. No event says 'set balance'.","region":"events"},{"note":"An append-only stream per aggregate. `append` takes the version the writer saw and rejects the write if the stream moved: optimistic concurrency.","region":"store"},{"note":"State is never stored. `replay` folds the history through `apply`, and the exhaustive `switch` guarantees every event type is handled.","region":"replay"},{"note":"A command checks the invariant against current state, then records an event, which both updates state and queues it as new. `saveTo` appends only the new events.","region":"commands"},{"note":"Replaying all events gives the present; replaying a prefix gives the past. That time travel is a one-liner.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Warehouse stock ledger with a regulatory audit.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Auditors ask 'how many units of SKU 42 were on the shelf on 3 March, and why did the count change?' An update-in-place table can only answer the first part for today. As a stream of `Received`, `Picked` and `Adjusted` events, any date is a replay up to a timestamp, and every number carries its reason. EventStoreDB and Axon Server store such streams.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["List<StockEvent> upTo = store.load(sku42).stream()","    .filter(e -> e.at().isBefore(march4))","    .toList();","int onShelf = Stock.replay(upTo).quantity();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: any past state is reproducible and each change carries its cause, which satisfies auditors and enables debugging by replay.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"acct","label":"applies","rel":"dependency","to":"evt"},{"from":"store","label":"stream","rel":"aggregation","to":"evt"},{"from":"acct","label":"loads, appends","rel":"dependency","to":"store"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"acct","kind":"class","members":["- balance, version","- fresh: List","+ replay(history)","+ deposit(n)","+ saveTo(store, id)"],"name":"Account","role":"Aggregate","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"evt","kind":"interface","members":["Deposited","Withdrawn"],"name":"Event","role":"Event","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"store","kind":"class","members":["+ load(id): List","+ append(id, expected,","    events)"],"name":"EventStore","role":"Event store","row":1}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"load(id)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"history","style":"return","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"replay(history)","style":"create","to":"a"},{"from":"c","label":"withdraw(30)","style":"call","to":"a"},{"from":"a","label":"check, record event","style":"call","to":"a"},{"from":"c","label":"saveTo(store)","style":"call","to":"a"},{"from":"a","label":"append(id, version, events)","style":"call","to":"s"},{"from":"s","label":"ok or conflict","style":"return","to":"a"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Client"},{"id":"a","name":"Account","role":"Aggregate"},{"id":"s","name":"EventStore","role":"Event store"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["You need a complete audit trail or must answer 'what was true then'.","Behaviour depends on history, not just current state.","You want to rebuild or add read models from the past."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Plain CRUD with no audit need; the extra moving parts do not pay off.","Data must be erased on request (GDPR) and you cannot crypto-shred."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Changing event schemas: old events live forever, so version and upcast them.","Replaying long streams without snapshots.","Putting derived state in events or exposing the stream as a public API.","Reads that need immediate consistency from projections."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Event sourcing everywhere when only one aggregate needs history.","Events named after CRUD verbs like `Updated`, which carry no business meaning."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed event hierarchies and switch patterns make `apply` exhaustive; the compiler flags unhandled new events.","Sealed event hierarchies and switch patterns make `apply` exhaustive; the compiler flags unhandled new events."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Event sourcing gives CQRS a natural feed for read models. Each stands alone: CQRS with state tables, event sourcing with one query model.","ref":"cqrs","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A domain event notifies others and the state still lives elsewhere. In event sourcing the events are the state.","ref":"domain-event","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A memento snapshots state to restore it later. Event sourcing keeps every change, and a snapshot is only an optimization.","ref":"memento","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Events in the store can be published reliably to other systems via an outbox or a change-feed relay.","ref":"transactional-outbox","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Rebuilds event-sourced aggregates by applying stored events to handler methods.","name":"@EventSourcingHandler","where":"Axon Framework"},{"detail":"Append-only streams; appends can require an expected stream revision for optimistic concurrency.","name":"Streams with expected revision","where":"EventStoreDB"},{"detail":"Typed actors persist events and recover state by replaying them, with optional snapshots.","name":"EventSourcedBehavior","where":"Akka Persistence"},{"detail":"State of the tree is derived from the chain of commits; the history is the record.","name":"Commit history","where":"Git"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","sealed interface Event permits Credited, Debited {}","record Credited(int cents) implements Event {}","record Debited(int cents) implements Event {}","interface Log { List<Event> read(String id); }","class Snippet { void run(Log log, String streamId) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["int balance = 0;","for (Event e : log.read(streamId)) {","    balance = switch (e) {","        case Credited c -> balance + c.cents();","        case Debited d -> balance - d.cents();","    };","}","System.out.println(balance);"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"Current state is computed by folding the stored sequence of events; no balance is stored."},{"ref":"domain-event","why":"Events here are the stored record of truth, not transient notifications to listeners."},{"ref":"memento","why":"Nothing is a snapshot of state; the state is derived from all changes."},{"ref":"cqrs","why":"There is no split between command and query models in this snippet."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"An append-only log of changes is the single source of truth; any past state is a replay and every change has its cause."},{"ref":"domain-event","why":"Notifications leave state overwritten elsewhere; the history would still be separate and could drift."},{"ref":"cqrs","why":"Splitting read and write models does not by itself retain history."},{"ref":"data-mapper","why":"Mapping changes how rows become objects, not what history is kept."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A regulator requires proof of an account's balance on any past date and the reason behind every change. Today's design updates balances in place and keeps a separate audit table that has drifted from reality."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Event {}","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"Rewriting a past event destroys the audit trail and invalidates projections already built from it. Append a compensating event instead, such as `Reversed` followed by the correct event.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class Ledger {","    private final List<Event> log = new ArrayList<>();","","    void append(Event e) { log.add(e); }","","    void correct(int index, Event fixed) {","        log.set(index, fixed);","    }","","    List<Event> history() { return List.copyOf(log); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the Event Sourcing pattern."},{"blocks":[["var history = store.load(id);"],["var account = Account.replay(history);"],["account.withdraw(30);"],["account.saveTo(store, id);"],["System.out.println(account.balance());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Event {}","final class EventStore { List<Event> load(String id) { return List.of(); } }","final class Account { static Account replay(List<Event> h) { return new Account(); } void withdraw(int c) {} void saveTo(EventStore s, String id) {} int balance() { return 0; } }","class Snippet { void run(EventStore store, String id) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Load the stream, rebuild state from it, validate and record a new event against that state, append the new events, then read the result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the use case so each line only uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Append-only stream of immutable events","State is a fold over the events","Expected version gives optimistic concurrency","Corrections are new events, never edits","Snapshots only speed up replay"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Event sourcing stores an append-only stream of immutable events as the source of truth. Current state is obtained by replaying them, optionally from a snapshot. Writers append with the expected version; a mismatch means someone else wrote first, so the command reloads and retries. Mistakes are fixed with compensating events.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Event Sourcing in your own words: what is stored, how is state obtained, and how do you handle concurrent writers?"}],"id":"event-sourcing"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Bound how long a caller waits for an operation, so a slow dependency cannot hold its threads and its own callers hostage.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Every remote call needs an upper bound, because a hung call holds a thread, a connection and a user. Per-call timeouts are not enough: a request that makes five calls needs one **deadline** for the whole request, and each call gets what is left of it.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Connect timeout:** how long to establish the connection; usually short.","**Read (socket) timeout:** the longest gap without data, not the total time.","**Deadline:** an absolute point in time, propagated downstream."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A timeout abandons the wait, not the work. The remote side may still complete, so a timed-out write has an unknown outcome.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"auth:ok left=260\nstock:ok left=140\ntimeout: price > 140ms\ntimeout: deadline exceeded","file":"Timeout.java","regions":{"budget":[59,63],"client":[44,51],"deadline":[16,29],"dependency":[31,41]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.time.Clock;\nimport java.time.Duration;\nimport java.time.Instant;\nimport java.time.ZoneId;\nimport java.time.ZoneOffset;\nimport java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;\n\nfinal class FakeClock extends Clock {\n    private Instant now = Instant.EPOCH;\n    void advance(Duration d) { now = now.plus(d); }\n    public Instant instant() { return now; }\n    public ZoneId getZone() { return ZoneOffset.UTC; }\n    public Clock withZone(ZoneId zone) { return this; }\n}\n\nrecord Deadline(Clock clock, Instant at) {\n    static Deadline in(Clock clock, Duration budget) {\n        return new Deadline(clock, clock.instant().plus(budget));\n    }\n    Duration remaining() { return Duration.between(clock.instant(), at); }\n\n    Duration cap(Duration perCall) throws TimeoutException {\n        Duration left = remaining();\n        if (left.compareTo(Duration.ZERO) <= 0) {\n            throw new TimeoutException(\"deadline exceeded\");\n        }\n        return perCall.compareTo(left) < 0 ? perCall : left;\n    }\n}\n\nrecord Dependency(String name, FakeClock clock, Duration latency) {\n    String call(Duration timeout) throws TimeoutException {\n        if (latency.compareTo(timeout) > 0) {\n            clock.advance(timeout);\n            long ms = timeout.toMillis();\n            throw new TimeoutException(name + \" > \" + ms + \"ms\");\n        }\n        clock.advance(latency);\n        return name + \":ok\";\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static void fetch(Deadline deadline, Dependency dep, long perCallMs) {\n        try {\n            String r = dep.call(deadline.cap(Duration.ofMillis(perCallMs)));\n            System.out.println(r + \" left=\" + deadline.remaining().toMillis());\n        } catch (TimeoutException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"timeout: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n\n    static Dependency dep(String n, FakeClock c, long ms) {\n        return new Dependency(n, c, Duration.ofMillis(ms));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var clock = new FakeClock();\n        var deadline = Deadline.in(clock, Duration.ofMillis(300));\n        fetch(deadline, dep(\"auth\", clock, 40), 100);\n        fetch(deadline, dep(\"stock\", clock, 120), 200);\n        fetch(deadline, dep(\"price\", clock, 400), 250);\n        fetch(deadline, dep(\"audit\", clock, 5), 100);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A deadline is an absolute instant read from an injected `Clock`. `remaining()` is what the request may still spend.","region":"deadline"},{"note":"A scripted fake: if its latency exceeds the timeout it burns exactly the timeout on the fake clock and fails, otherwise it burns its latency.","region":"dependency"},{"note":"Each call receives `cap(perCall)`: the smaller of its own limit and the remaining budget. No call can outlive the request.","region":"client"},{"note":"A 300 ms budget: auth leaves 260, stock leaves 140, price is capped at 140 ms rather than its own 250 ms, and audit fails fast without calling.","region":"budget"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A checkout page that fans out to four services.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The gateway promises the browser a response within two seconds. It calls pricing, stock, loyalty and fraud in sequence. Without a shared deadline, four calls with a 1.5 s limit each can take six seconds, long after the browser gave up. The gateway stamps a deadline on the request and every outbound call carries the remaining budget, in gRPC as the deadline itself.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Deadline d = Deadline.in(clock, Duration.ofSeconds(2));","Price p = pricing.quote(cart, d.cap(Duration.ofMillis(800)));","Stock s = stock.check(cart, d.cap(Duration.ofMillis(800)));","// loyalty and fraud get whatever is left, or fail fast"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: worst-case latency is the budget, and work for a caller that has already left is never started.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"cap","rel":"dependency","to":"deadline"},{"from":"client","label":"call","rel":"dependency","to":"dep"},{"from":"deadline","label":"reads","rel":"association","to":"clock"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ fetch(deadline, dep)"],"name":"Client","role":"Caller","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"deadline","kind":"record","members":["+ remaining(): Duration","+ cap(perCall): Duration"],"name":"Deadline","role":"Budget","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"dep","kind":"interface","members":["+ call(timeout)"],"name":"Dependency","role":"Remote","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"clock","kind":"abstract","members":["+ instant(): Instant"],"name":"Clock","role":"Time source","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"dep","text":"throws on timeout"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"cap(250ms)","style":"call","to":"deadline"},{"from":"deadline","label":"140ms left","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"call(140ms)","style":"call","to":"dep"},{"from":"dep","label":"TimeoutException","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"deadline","name":"Deadline","role":"Budget"},{"id":"dep","name":"Dependency","role":"Remote"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Any call crosses a process or network boundary.","A request fans out and must answer within a promised latency.","A slow dependency is worse than a failed one."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The operation is a local, bounded computation.","Cancelling mid-way leaves a non-idempotent write in an unknown state."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["No timeout at all: many default clients wait forever.","Stacked timeouts where the inner exceeds the outer, so the inner never fires first.","Retrying a timed-out call that is not idempotent."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["One global 30 s timeout copied everywhere regardless of real latency.","Timeouts so tight that normal tail latency trips them constantly."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["`HttpRequest.timeout`, `HttpClient.connectTimeout` and `CompletableFuture.orTimeout` cover most cases.","`StructuredTaskScope.joinUntil(Instant)` applies one deadline to a whole group of subtasks (preview API)."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A timeout turns an unbounded wait into a failure; a retry decides what to do with that failure. Each attempt needs its own timeout inside the overall deadline.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Timeouts are the failures a breaker counts. The timeout bounds one call; the breaker stops making calls once timeouts pile up.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A bulkhead limits how many calls run at once; a timeout limits how long one call may run. Without timeouts, a bulkhead's permits are held forever.","ref":"bulkhead","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"A rate limiter bounds how often requests start. A timeout bounds how long each one may take.","ref":"rate-limiter","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Bounds the wait for a response; `HttpClient.Builder.connectTimeout` bounds connection setup.","name":"HttpRequest.Builder.timeout","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Completes the future exceptionally with `TimeoutException` after the given delay.","name":"CompletableFuture.orTimeout","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"Waits at most the given time and throws `TimeoutException`; the task itself keeps running unless cancelled.","name":"Future.get(long, TimeUnit)","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"`io.grpc.Deadline` is an absolute point in time that is propagated to servers and downstream calls.","name":"Deadline","where":"gRPC"},{"detail":"Wraps a `Future` or `CompletionStage` and fails it with `TimeoutException` after the configured duration.","name":"TimeLimiter","where":"Resilience4j"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.*;","import java.util.concurrent.*;","interface Remote { String fetch() throws Exception; }","class Snippet {","    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["Duration call(Remote remote, Instant giveUpAt, Clock clock) throws Exception {","    Duration left = Duration.between(clock.instant(), giveUpAt);","    if (left.isNegative() || left.isZero()) {","        throw new TimeoutException(\"out of time\");","    }","    Future<String> f = pool.submit(remote::fetch);","    try {","        f.get(left.toMillis(), TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);","    } catch (TimeoutException e) {","        f.cancel(true);","        throw e;","    }","    return left;","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"timeout","why":"The wait is bounded by an absolute give-up time, and the call is cancelled when the budget runs out."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"Nothing is attempted again after the failure; the exception is rethrown."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"There is no failure counter or open/closed state; each call is judged only by its own budget."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"No permits or pools limit concurrency; the code only limits how long one call may take."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"timeout","why":"A request deadline split across the shard calls bounds the wait, so threads are freed on time."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"Retrying a slow shard adds load and latency to a request that is already out of time."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Callers are not sending too many requests; they are waiting too long on one dependency."},{"ref":"cache-aside","why":"Caching might hide the slow shard for hot queries, but does nothing for cold ones."}],"prompt":"What is the first thing to add?","scenario":"A search endpoint promises answers within 500 ms and queries three shards one after another. When one shard is slow, the endpoint's own callers pile up and their threads are all blocked waiting."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;","interface Deadline { Duration remaining(); }","interface Shard { String query(Duration t) throws TimeoutException; }","class Snippet {","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"The remaining budget was read once, before the first call. After `a` consumes part of it, `b` still gets the full amount. Call `d.remaining()` again before each call.","flawLines":[4],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["String search(Deadline d, Shard a, Shard b) throws TimeoutException {","    Duration left = d.remaining();","    String x = a.query(left);","    String y = b.query(left);","    return x + y;","}","// Deadline.remaining() is evaluated once","// a.query(t) and b.query(t) may each wait up to t"],"prompt":"Tap the line that defeats the deadline."},{"blocks":[["Duration left = deadline.remaining();"],["if (left.isNegative()) throw new TimeoutException();"],["Duration limit = left.compareTo(perCall) < 0 ? left : perCall;"],["String body = client.fetch(url, limit);"],["return parse(body);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;","interface Deadline { Duration remaining(); }","interface Client { String fetch(String url, Duration t) throws TimeoutException; }","class Snippet {","  Object run(Deadline deadline, Duration perCall, Client client, String url) throws TimeoutException {","{{code}}","  }","  Object parse(String s) { return s; }","}"]},"explanation":"Read the remaining budget, fail fast when it is gone, derive this call's limit from it, make the call with that limit, then use the result.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the steps of one call guarded by a request deadline."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Every remote call needs an upper bound","One request deadline, with each call capped by the remaining budget","Connect, read and total time are different limits","A timeout abandons the wait, not the remote work","Timed-out writes have unknown outcome"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Sequential calls each within their own limit can add up to far more than the caller will wait, so the request needs one deadline that every call is capped by. A timeout only stops waiting; the remote work may still finish, so a timed-out write has an unknown outcome and needs idempotency before any retry.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain why per-call timeouts are not enough, and what a timeout does not do."}],"id":"timeout"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Retry transient failures after growing, randomized delays, so recovery is automatic without a thundering herd.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Many failures are momentary: a dropped connection, a 503, a leader election. A retry hides them, but only if it **backs off** so the dependency can recover, adds **jitter** so clients do not retry in lockstep, and has a hard limit on attempts.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Classify first:** retry transient errors, never permanent ones (400, validation).","**Backoff:** the delay ceiling doubles per attempt, up to a cap.","**Jitter:** pick a random delay below the ceiling to spread the load.","**Bound it:** a maximum number of attempts, inside the request deadline."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Retries multiply load: three layers each retrying three times turn one request into twenty-seven. Retry at one layer.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"flaky: ok waits=[50, 100, 200]\ndown: TransientException waits=[50, 100]\nbad: PermanentException waits=[]","file":"RetryBackoff.java","regions":{"errors":[7,14],"policy":[18,36],"wiring":[49,51]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.time.Duration;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.function.LongUnaryOperator;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\n@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")\nclass TransientException extends RuntimeException {\n    TransientException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")\nclass PermanentException extends RuntimeException {\n    PermanentException(String message) { super(message); }\n}\n\ninterface Sleeper { void sleep(Duration d); }\n\nrecord Retry(int maxAttempts, long baseMs, long capMs,\n             LongUnaryOperator jitter, Sleeper sleeper) {\n\n    long delayMs(int attempt) {\n        long ceiling = Math.min(capMs, baseMs << Math.min(attempt - 1, 20));\n        return jitter.applyAsLong(ceiling);\n    }\n\n    <T> T call(Supplier<T> op) {\n        for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {\n            try {\n                return op.get();\n            } catch (TransientException e) {\n                if (attempt == maxAttempts) throw e;\n                sleeper.sleep(Duration.ofMillis(delayMs(attempt)));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static Supplier<String> failing(int times, RuntimeException error) {\n        int[] calls = {0};\n        return () -> {\n            if (calls[0]++ < times) throw error;\n            return \"ok\";\n        };\n    }\n\n    static void run(String label, int attempts, Supplier<String> op) {\n        List<Long> waits = new ArrayList<>();\n        LongUnaryOperator half = ceiling -> ceiling / 2;  // test jitter\n        Retry retry = new Retry(attempts, 100, 1000, half,\n            d -> waits.add(d.toMillis()));\n        String result;\n        try {\n            result = retry.call(op);\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            result = e.getClass().getSimpleName();\n        }\n        System.out.println(label + \": \" + result + \" waits=\" + waits);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var busy = new TransientException(\"503\");\n        run(\"flaky\", 5, failing(3, busy));\n        run(\"down\", 3, failing(99, busy));\n        run(\"bad\", 5, failing(99, new PermanentException(\"400\")));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The classification is the type system: only `TransientException` is retried. A `PermanentException` propagates on the first attempt.","region":"errors"},{"note":"The ceiling is `min(cap, base * 2^(attempt-1))`. Jitter chooses a delay at or below it (full jitter). The shift is clamped to avoid overflow.","region":"policy"},{"note":"Jitter and sleeping are injected. Production passes a random function and a real sleep; the test passes a fixed function and a recorder, so nothing waits.","region":"wiring"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Uploading to object storage during a regional blip.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A batch job pushes thousands of files to a bucket. When the storage service returns throttling or 5xx errors, every worker would retry at the same instants and keep the service saturated. Exponential backoff with full jitter spreads the retries over time. Idempotent keys make a retried upload safe, and a cap of five attempts stops a bad day from becoming a stuck job.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var retry = new Retry(5, 200, 10_000, ThreadLocalRandom.current()::nextLong, Thread::sleep);","retry.call(() -> bucket.put(key, bytes));  // key makes put idempotent"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: transient errors vanish from the job's results, and the storage service sees a smoothed load instead of synchronized waves.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"retry","label":"invokes","rel":"dependency","to":"op"},{"from":"retry","label":"waits via","rel":"aggregation","to":"sleeper"},{"from":"op","label":"throws","rel":"dependency","to":"err"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"retry","kind":"record","members":["+ call(op): T","- delayMs(attempt)"],"name":"Retry","role":"Policy","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"op","kind":"interface","members":["+ get(): T"],"name":"Supplier<T>","role":"Operation","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"sleeper","kind":"interface","members":["+ sleep(Duration)"],"name":"Sleeper","role":"Waiter","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"err","kind":"class","members":[],"name":"TransientException","role":"Retryable","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"call(op)","style":"call","to":"retry"},{"from":"retry","label":"get()","style":"call","to":"op"},{"from":"op","label":"TransientException","style":"return","to":"retry"},{"from":"retry","label":"sleep(jittered)","style":"call","to":"sleeper"},{"from":"retry","label":"get()","style":"call","to":"op"},{"from":"op","label":"result","style":"return","to":"retry"},{"from":"retry","label":"result","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"retry","name":"Retry","role":"Policy"},{"id":"op","name":"Operation"},{"id":"sleeper","name":"Sleeper"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Failures are transient and the operation is idempotent or keyed.","Many clients may fail together, so spreading retries matters.","A short outage should not surface as a user-visible error."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The error is permanent: invalid input, authorization, not found.","The caller is interactive and has no time budget left for another attempt."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Retry storms: retries at several layers multiply load on a struggling service.","Retrying non-idempotent writes duplicates their effect.","Backoff with no jitter keeps clients synchronized.","Ignoring a `Retry-After` hint the server already sent."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads make blocking sleeps cheap, so a plain loop like this is fine at scale.","Pair it with a [Circuit Breaker](kata:circuit-breaker) so retries stop when a dependency is clearly down."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A retry asks again after a failure; a breaker stops asking once failures are frequent. Retry sits inside the breaker so every attempt counts toward tripping it.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Each attempt needs its own timeout, and all attempts together need an overall deadline. Without both, retries only prolong the wait.","ref":"timeout","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Retries produce duplicates by design. The receiving side must make a second delivery harmless.","ref":"idempotent-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A rate limiter rejects requests that arrive too fast; a retry re-sends after rejection. Backoff is the polite response to a 429.","ref":"rate-limiter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"The backoff schedule is a strategy: fixed, exponential, decorrelated jitter. The policy swaps it without touching the calling code.","ref":"strategy","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Configures `maxAttempts` and a wait via `IntervalFunction`, including `ofExponentialRandomBackoff`.","name":"Retry / RetryConfig","where":"Resilience4j"},{"detail":"Annotation-driven retry with `delay`, `multiplier` and `random` for jitter.","name":"@Retryable and @Backoff","where":"Spring Retry"},{"detail":"`LEGACY`, `STANDARD` and `ADAPTIVE` modes retry throttling and transient errors with exponential backoff and jitter.","name":"RetryMode","where":"AWS SDK for Java v2"},{"detail":"Route-level retries with `retry_on`, `num_retries` and a configurable exponential back-off base interval.","name":"retry_policy","where":"Envoy"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;","import java.util.function.Supplier;","class Snippet {","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["String fetch(Supplier<String> op) throws InterruptedException {","    long ceiling = 100;","    for (int i = 1; ; i++) {","        try {","            return op.get();","        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {","            if (i == 5) throw e;","            Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(ceiling));","            ceiling = Math.min(ceiling * 2, 5_000);","        }","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"A bounded loop re-invokes the operation after randomized, growing waits."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"There is no shared state across calls and no fail-fast mode; each call retries independently."},{"ref":"timeout","why":"Nothing bounds how long a single attempt may take; only the number of attempts is limited."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"The loop does not admit or reject requests by rate; it re-sends after its own failures."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"Exponential growth with random jitter desynchronizes the instances and spreads the load."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Isolation limits blast radius inside one instance, but does not desynchronize 2,000 instances."},{"ref":"cache-aside","why":"Caching would help reads, but connection recovery itself is the problem."},{"ref":"timeout","why":"A timeout bounds each wait; it does not change when the clients come back."}],"prompt":"What change addresses the actual problem?","scenario":"After a brief database failover, 2,000 service instances all see connection errors at the same moment. Each retries after exactly 1 second, and the database falls over again on every retry wave."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.Supplier;","class Snippet {","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"It catches every RuntimeException, so permanent errors such as a 400 are retried five times for nothing. Catch only the transient exception type and let everything else propagate.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["<T> T call(Supplier<T> op) throws InterruptedException {","    for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {","        try {","            return op.get();","        } catch (RuntimeException e) {","            if (attempt == 5) throw e;","            Thread.sleep(100L << attempt);","        }","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that makes this retry loop dangerous."},{"blocks":[["try {","    return op.get();"],["} catch (TransientException e) {"],["    if (attempt == maxAttempts) throw e;"],["    long ceiling = Math.min(capMs, baseMs << (attempt - 1));"],["    sleeper.sleep(Duration.ofMillis(jitter.applyAsLong(ceiling)));","}"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.function.*;","class TransientException extends RuntimeException { private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L; }","interface Sleeper { void sleep(Duration d); }","class Snippet {","  <T> T call(Supplier<T> op, int maxAttempts, long baseMs, long capMs, LongUnaryOperator jitter, Sleeper sleeper) {","    for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {","{{code}}","    }","  }","}"]},"explanation":"Attempt first. Only on a transient failure check the attempt budget, then compute the ceiling, then wait a jittered time before the loop tries again.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the body of one retry loop iteration."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Only transient errors are retried","Exponential delay up to a cap","Jitter prevents synchronized retry waves","Bounded attempts inside an overall deadline","The operation must be idempotent"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Backoff gives the failing dependency time to recover instead of hammering it. Jitter randomizes the delays so many clients do not retry at the same instants. Retries also cap attempts, retry only transient errors, and require an idempotent operation, because the first attempt may have succeeded.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain why a retry needs backoff and jitter, and what must be true of the operation being retried."}],"id":"retry-backoff"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Stop calling a failing dependency for a while, failing fast or falling back, then probe carefully to see whether it recovered.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A **breaker** wraps calls and counts failures. In **closed** state calls pass through. After enough consecutive failures it **opens**: calls are rejected instantly, which spares the dependency and frees your threads. After a cool-down it goes **half-open** and lets one probe through to decide.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Closed:** normal; failures are counted.","**Open:** reject immediately or use a fallback.","**Half-open:** one trial call; success closes, failure reopens."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Breakers react to measured failure, unlike a timeout that bounds one call. Production breakers usually trip on a failure rate over a sliding window.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"0 cached CLOSED 1\n0 cached CLOSED 2\n0 cached OPEN 3\n100 cached OPEN 3\n1000 cached OPEN 4\n1500 cached OPEN 4\n2000 live CLOSED 5\n2100 live CLOSED 6","file":"CircuitBreaker.java","regions":{"call":[20,30],"permit":[32,38],"record":[40,48],"state":[5,5]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\nimport java.util.function.LongSupplier;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\nenum State { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }\n\nfinal class CircuitBreaker {\n    private final int threshold;\n    private final long openMs;\n    private final LongSupplier clock;\n    private State state = State.CLOSED;\n    private int failures;\n    private long openedAt;\n\n    CircuitBreaker(int threshold, long openMs, LongSupplier clock) {\n        this.threshold = threshold; this.openMs = openMs; this.clock = clock;\n    }\n    synchronized State state() { return state; }\n\n    <T> T call(Supplier<T> op, Supplier<T> fallback) {\n        if (!permit()) return fallback.get();\n        try {\n            T result = op.get();\n            record(true);\n            return result;\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            record(false);\n            return fallback.get();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private synchronized boolean permit() {\n        if (state == State.OPEN && clock.getAsLong() - openedAt >= openMs) {\n            state = State.HALF_OPEN;  // the caller that flips it is the probe\n            return true;\n        }\n        return state == State.CLOSED;\n    }\n\n    private synchronized void record(boolean ok) {\n        if (ok) {\n            state = State.CLOSED;\n            failures = 0;\n        } else if (state == State.HALF_OPEN || ++failures >= threshold) {\n            state = State.OPEN;\n            openedAt = clock.getAsLong();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var now = new AtomicLong();\n        var breaker = new CircuitBreaker(3, 1000, now::get);\n        int[] calls = {0};\n        Supplier<String> op = () -> {\n            calls[0]++;\n            if (now.get() < 2000) throw new IllegalStateException(\"down\");\n            return \"live\";\n        };\n        for (long t : new long[] {0, 0, 0, 100, 1000, 1500, 2000, 2100}) {\n            now.set(t);\n            var r = breaker.call(op, () -> \"cached\");\n            System.out.println(t + \" \" + r + \" \" + breaker.state()\n                + \" \" + calls[0]);\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Three states are the whole model. Closed passes calls, open rejects them, half-open admits a single probe.","region":"state"},{"note":"The operation runs outside any lock. Success or failure is reported back; a rejected call gets the fallback without touching the dependency.","region":"call"},{"note":"The admission gate. An open breaker whose cool-down elapsed flips to half-open, and only that caller is the probe. Concurrent callers see half-open and are rejected.","region":"permit"},{"note":"Success closes and clears the count. A failed probe, or the threshold-th consecutive failure, opens and stamps the time from the injected clock.","region":"record"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A product page that shows recommendations.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The page calls a recommendation service that sometimes degrades and answers in ten seconds. Without a breaker every page view burns a thread waiting. With one, after a few failures the page stops asking and shows a static bestseller list for thirty seconds. Then a single request probes the service, and the personalized list returns when it is healthy again.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var reco = new CircuitBreaker(5, 30_000, System::currentTimeMillis);","List<Item> items = reco.call(","    () -> recommender.forUser(userId),","    () -> bestsellers.top(10));"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a degraded dependency costs the page some personalization instead of its latency and its thread pool.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"cb","label":"current","rel":"composition","to":"state"},{"from":"cb","label":"guards","rel":"dependency","to":"op"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"cb","kind":"class","members":["- state: State","- failures: int","+ call(op, fallback): T"],"name":"CircuitBreaker","role":"Breaker","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"state","kind":"enum","members":["CLOSED","OPEN","HALF_OPEN"],"name":"State","role":"State","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"op","kind":"interface","members":["+ get(): T"],"name":"Supplier<T>","role":"Protected call","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"cb","text":"clock injected for tests"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"call() while closed","style":"call","to":"cb"},{"from":"cb","label":"get()","style":"call","to":"dep"},{"from":"dep","label":"failure x N","style":"return","to":"cb"},{"from":"cb","label":"trip to OPEN","style":"call","to":"cb"},{"from":"client","label":"call() while open","style":"call","to":"cb"},{"from":"cb","label":"fallback, no call","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"call() after cool-down","style":"call","to":"cb"},{"from":"cb","label":"probe (half-open)","style":"call","to":"dep"},{"from":"dep","label":"ok: close","style":"return","to":"cb"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"cb","name":"Breaker","role":"Breaker"},{"id":"dep","name":"Dependency"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A dependency can fail slowly and drag callers down with it.","A reasonable fallback exists: cache, default, degraded feature.","Many callers share one dependency and should react together."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The call is local or cannot meaningfully fail independently.","There is no acceptable fallback and failing fast is no better than waiting."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Tripping on application errors like 404 instead of infrastructure failures.","One breaker for many endpoints, so a bad one blocks the healthy ones.","A fallback that itself calls the failing system.","Multi-instance breakers disagree; each instance learns on its own."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Breakers around in-process calls.","Thresholds never tuned, so the breaker flaps or never opens."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Use a library for sliding windows, slow-call thresholds and metrics; hand-rolled counters rarely get these right.","Combine it with [Timeout](kata:timeout): without one, a hung call never registers as a failure."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Retry hides brief failures; the breaker stops the retrying when failures persist. Place the breaker so each retry attempt is counted.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Timeouts convert hangs into failures the breaker can count. Without them a slow dependency never looks failed.","ref":"timeout","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A bulkhead caps concurrent calls to protect your resources; a breaker uses outcomes to stop calling. Use both.","ref":"bulkhead","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The breaker is a small state machine, but its transitions are driven by call outcomes and a clock, and its states only gate access.","ref":"state","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A breaker is a protection proxy around a remote call that decides whether the call may happen.","ref":"proxy","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Sliding window by count or time, `failureRateThreshold`, `waitDurationInOpenState`, and `HALF_OPEN` trial calls.","name":"CircuitBreaker","where":"Resilience4j"},{"detail":"A common abstraction (`CircuitBreakerFactory`) over Resilience4j and other implementations.","name":"Spring Cloud CircuitBreaker","where":"Spring Cloud"},{"detail":"Cluster-level limits plus ejection of hosts that return consecutive 5xx errors.","name":"circuit_breakers and outlier_detection","where":"Envoy"},{"detail":"`consecutive5xxErrors` and `baseEjectionTime` eject unhealthy endpoints from the load-balancing pool.","name":"DestinationRule outlierDetection","where":"Istio"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.*;","class Snippet {","    LongSupplier clock = System::nanoTime;","    long blockedUntil;","    int misses;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["<T> T guarded(Supplier<T> op, Supplier<T> other) {","    if (blockedUntil > clock.getAsLong()) return other.get();","    try {","        T r = op.get();","        misses = 0;","        return r;","    } catch (RuntimeException e) {","        if (++misses >= 5) blockedUntil = clock.getAsLong() + 30_000;","        return other.get();","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Consecutive failures trip a blocked window during which the call is skipped and a substitute is used."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"The operation is never re-invoked after a failure; a substitute result is returned instead."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Blocking depends on failure counts, not on how many requests arrived per unit of time."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Nothing limits concurrency; the gate is driven by past failures."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"It detects the sustained failure, fails fast to free threads, and probes automatically for recovery."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"Retrying a dependency that is slow for everyone adds load and ties up even more threads."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"The problem is not too much inbound traffic; it is a dependency that stopped answering."},{"ref":"cache-aside","why":"Payment authorization cannot be served from a cache."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A payments gateway starts answering every request after 20 seconds. Your checkout service keeps sending traffic, its worker threads are all blocked, and unrelated pages are timing out too. Once the gateway recovers, you want traffic to resume without operator action."},{"compile":{"template":["class Snippet {","    enum State { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }","    State state = State.OPEN;","    long openMs = 1000;","    long elapsed() { return 2000; }","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"`state != OPEN` also admits every caller while half-open, so a flood hits the still-unhealthy dependency. Return true only for the caller that flipped the state, and false for others until the probe finishes.","flawLines":[5],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["synchronized boolean permit() {","    if (state == State.OPEN && elapsed() >= openMs) {","        state = State.HALF_OPEN;","    }","    return state != State.OPEN;","}","// HALF_OPEN is meant to admit exactly one probe"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the half-open state."},{"blocks":[["if (!permit()) return fallback.get();"],["try {","    T result = op.get();"],["    record(true);","    return result;"],["} catch (RuntimeException e) {","    record(false);"],["    return fallback.get();","}"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.Supplier;","class Snippet {","    boolean permit() { return true; }","    void record(boolean ok) {}","    <T> T call(Supplier<T> op, Supplier<T> fallback) {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Ask permission first, then run the operation. Success is recorded before returning; failure is recorded in the catch before the fallback is used.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the breaker's call path for a closed breaker."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Closed, open and half-open","Trips on accumulated failures, not one call","Open means fail fast or fallback","Half-open admits a single probe","Complements timeouts and retries, not replaces them"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Closed passes calls and counts failures; open rejects calls immediately or uses a fallback; half-open admits one probe after a cool-down. A timeout bounds one call and a retry repeats it. The breaker uses accumulated failures to stop calling at all, protecting the dependency and your threads.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Describe the three breaker states and what a breaker adds beyond a timeout and a retry."}],"id":"circuit-breaker"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give each dependency or consumer its own bounded share of resources, so one failure cannot consume everything.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The name comes from ship compartments: a breach floods one section, not the hull. Software bulkheads cap the **concurrency** each downstream or tenant may use, through separate thread pools, semaphores or connection pools. When one dependency stalls, only its compartment fills; the others keep working.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Semaphore bulkhead:** counts concurrent calls on the caller's thread; cheap.","**Thread-pool bulkhead:** a dedicated pool and queue per dependency; isolates blocking calls.","**Reject fast** when full instead of queueing without bound."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The size of a compartment is a capacity decision: concurrency is roughly throughput times latency (Little's law).","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"inventory rejected: inventory is full\npayments paid\ninventory ok","file":"Bulkhead.java","regions":{"bulkhead":[10,27],"reject":[5,8],"saturate":[48,50],"wiring":[39,40]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\n@SuppressWarnings(\"serial\")\nclass BulkheadFullException extends RuntimeException {\n    BulkheadFullException(String name) { super(name + \" is full\"); }\n}\n\nfinal class Bulkhead {\n    private final String name;\n    private final Semaphore permits;\n\n    Bulkhead(String name, int maxConcurrent) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.permits = new Semaphore(maxConcurrent);\n    }\n\n    <T> T call(Supplier<T> op) {\n        if (!permits.tryAcquire()) throw new BulkheadFullException(name);\n        try {\n            return op.get();\n        } finally {\n            permits.release();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static String attempt(Bulkhead b, Supplier<String> op) {\n        try {\n            return b.call(op);\n        } catch (BulkheadFullException e) {\n            return \"rejected: \" + e.getMessage();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        var inventory = new Bulkhead(\"inventory\", 2);\n        var payments = new Bulkhead(\"payments\", 2);\n        var release = new CountDownLatch(1);\n        var entered = new CountDownLatch(2);\n        Supplier<String> stuck = () -> {\n            entered.countDown();\n            try { release.await(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\n            return \"done\";\n        };\n        var t1 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> inventory.call(stuck));\n        var t2 = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> inventory.call(stuck));\n        entered.await();\n        System.out.println(\"inventory \" + attempt(inventory, () -> \"ok\"));\n        System.out.println(\"payments \" + attempt(payments, () -> \"paid\"));\n        release.countDown();\n        t1.join();\n        t2.join();\n        System.out.println(\"inventory \" + attempt(inventory, () -> \"ok\"));\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A dedicated, unchecked rejection tells callers the compartment is full, so they can degrade instead of waiting.","region":"reject"},{"note":"A `Semaphore` is the compartment. `tryAcquire` never blocks, and `finally` returns the permit even when the operation throws.","region":"bulkhead"},{"note":"One bulkhead per dependency. Inventory slowness can consume at most two permits and never touches payments.","region":"wiring"},{"note":"Two virtual threads hold both inventory permits. `entered.await()` guarantees both are inside, so the next outcome is deterministic.","region":"saturate"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A reporting service that shares a pool with checkout.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A web app uses one thread pool for everything. A new reporting endpoint runs slow queries, and during month-end all threads end up inside it. Checkout requests queue behind them and time out, though checkout itself is healthy. Giving reporting its own pool of ten and checkout its own of fifty means a reporting storm degrades reporting only.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var reporting = new Bulkhead(\"reporting\", 10);","var checkout  = new Bulkhead(\"checkout\", 50);","","reporting.call(() -> reports.monthEnd(tenant));  // may be rejected"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: the blast radius of a slow dependency is its compartment, and the core business path stays available.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"caller","rel":"association","to":"b1"},{"from":"caller","rel":"association","to":"b2"},{"from":"b1","label":"guards","rel":"dependency","to":"d1"},{"from":"b2","label":"guards","rel":"dependency","to":"d2"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"caller","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(request)"],"name":"Service","role":"Caller","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"b1","kind":"class","members":["- permits: Semaphore","+ call(op): T"],"name":"Bulkhead","role":"Compartment A","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"b2","kind":"class","members":["- permits: Semaphore","+ call(op): T"],"name":"Bulkhead","role":"Compartment B","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"d1","kind":"interface","members":[],"name":"Inventory","role":"Dependency","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"d2","kind":"interface","members":[],"name":"Payments","role":"Dependency","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"caller","label":"call(op)","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"tryAcquire: ok","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"op.get()","style":"call","to":"dep"},{"from":"dep","label":"result","style":"return","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"result, permit released","style":"return","to":"caller"},{"from":"caller","label":"call(op) when full","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"BulkheadFullException","style":"return","to":"caller"}],"participants":[{"id":"caller","name":"Caller"},{"id":"b","name":"Bulkhead","role":"Compartment"},{"id":"dep","name":"Dependency"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["One slow dependency could exhaust threads or connections shared with healthy ones.","Tenants or priorities must not starve each other.","Failure should degrade a feature, not the whole service."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Load is uniform and a single well-sized pool already serves it.","Splitting would leave each compartment too small to absorb normal bursts."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Compartments sized by guess; measure latency and concurrency first.","Without timeouts, permits are held forever and the compartment stays full.","Unbounded queues in a thread-pool bulkhead hide the saturation.","Idle compartments waste capacity that others could use."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads make threads cheap, so a semaphore per dependency is the idiomatic bulkhead.","Thread-pool bulkheads still make sense to isolate CPU-bound or blocking native calls."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The bulkhead limits how many calls are in flight; the breaker stops calls based on past failures. The bulkhead protects you immediately, the breaker after evidence.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A rate limiter bounds arrivals per unit of time. A bulkhead bounds concurrent work in progress, regardless of how fast it arrived.","ref":"rate-limiter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Timeouts release permits held by slow calls. Without them a bulkhead only slows the moment it fills.","ref":"timeout","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A thread-pool bulkhead is a dedicated pool per dependency; the pattern is the partitioning, not the pool.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"specializes"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`SemaphoreBulkhead` limits concurrent calls with `maxConcurrentCalls`; `ThreadPoolBulkhead` uses a dedicated pool and queue.","name":"Bulkhead and ThreadPoolBulkhead","where":"Resilience4j"},{"detail":"Per-cluster `max_connections`, `max_pending_requests` and `max_requests` cap concurrency to each upstream.","name":"circuit_breakers thresholds","where":"Envoy"},{"detail":"Per-container CPU and memory limits isolate a misbehaving pod from its neighbors.","name":"resources requests and limits","where":"Kubernetes"},{"detail":"A separate connection pool per data source caps database concurrency for each consumer.","name":"HikariConfig.setMaximumPoolSize","where":"HikariCP"},{"detail":"A bounded worker pool per connector limits how many requests are processed at once.","name":"Executor maxThreads","where":"Tomcat"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;","import java.util.function.Supplier;","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Gate {","    private final Semaphore slots;","","    Gate(int n) { slots = new Semaphore(n); }","","    <T> T run(Supplier<T> op) {","        if (!slots.tryAcquire()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"busy\");","        try { return op.get(); } finally { slots.release(); }","    }","}","// one Gate per downstream: new Gate(10), new Gate(50)"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Each downstream gets its own bounded share of concurrency, and excess work is rejected rather than queued."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Permits are returned when work finishes, so this bounds concurrency, not the number of arrivals per second."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Rejection depends on how many calls are running now, not on a history of failures."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"No threads are created or managed; the code only counts and gates concurrent callers."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Per-tenant concurrency limits keep A's backlog from consuming the capacity B and C depend on."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"B and C would retry into the same saturated pool and make it worse."},{"ref":"cache-aside","why":"A's queries are slow and probably unique; caching does not stop them from occupying workers."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Nothing downstream is failing; the shared pool is being monopolized."}],"prompt":"Which pattern addresses the root cause?","scenario":"An API serves three tenants from one worker pool. Tenant A launched a batch that sends a thousand slow queries; tenants B and C now see timeouts although their own traffic is small and normal."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;","import java.util.function.Supplier;","class Snippet {","    final Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(10);","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"`acquire()` blocks when all permits are taken, so callers pile up behind a saturated dependency and the isolation is lost. Use `tryAcquire()` (or a short timeout) and reject.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["<T> T call(Supplier<T> op) throws InterruptedException {","    permits.acquire();","    try {","        return op.get();","    } finally {","        permits.release();","    }","}","// permits = new Semaphore(10)"],"prompt":"Tap the line that undermines this bulkhead."},{"blocks":[["if (!permits.tryAcquire()) {","    throw new BulkheadFullException(name);","}"],["try {"],["    return op.get();"],["} finally {","    permits.release();","}"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;","import java.util.function.Supplier;","class BulkheadFullException extends RuntimeException { private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L; BulkheadFullException(String n) { super(n); } }","class Snippet {","    final Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(2);","    final String name = \"x\";","    <T> T call(Supplier<T> op) {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Reserve a permit first and reject if none; the try block starts only after the permit is held, and finally guarantees its release.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the guarded call."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Separate bounded compartments per dependency or tenant","Caps concurrency (work in flight), not arrival rate","Reject fast when full rather than queue unboundedly","Semaphore or dedicated pool","Needs timeouts to release permits"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A bulkhead gives each dependency or tenant its own cap on concurrent work, so one slow dependency can fill only its compartment. A rate limiter bounds arrivals per time; a bulkhead bounds work in flight. Permits are held while a call runs, so timeouts are required or a hung call keeps its permit forever.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain what a bulkhead isolates, how it differs from a rate limiter, and why it needs timeouts."}],"id":"bulkhead"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Cap how many requests are admitted per unit of time, rejecting or delaying the excess.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A rate limiter protects a service from callers, whether buggy, abusive or just popular. The **token bucket** is the common algorithm: tokens refill at a steady rate up to a capacity, and each request spends one. The capacity allows short **bursts**; the refill rate sets the long-run average.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Token bucket:** bursts up to capacity, steady average rate.","**Fixed or sliding window:** count requests per interval; simpler, with boundary spikes in the fixed form.","**Leaky bucket:** smooths output to a constant rate."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"A rejection should say when to come back. HTTP 429 with `Retry-After` turns a limit into a protocol the client can follow.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"0ms a 200\n0ms a 200\n0ms a 200\n0ms a 429 retry-after=500ms\n0ms b 200\n500ms a 200\n500ms a 429 retry-after=500ms\n10000ms a 200\n10000ms a 200\n10000ms a 200\n10000ms a 429 retry-after=500ms","file":"RateLimiter.java","regions":{"bucket":[6,32],"demo":[51,58],"perClient":[34,45]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\nimport java.util.function.LongSupplier;\n\nfinal class TokenBucket {\n    private final double capacity;\n    private final double perSecond;\n    private final LongSupplier nanos;\n    private double tokens;\n    private long last;\n\n    TokenBucket(int capacity, double perSecond, LongSupplier nanos) {\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n        this.perSecond = perSecond;\n        this.nanos = nanos;\n        this.tokens = capacity;\n        this.last = nanos.getAsLong();\n    }\n\n    /** Returns 0 if admitted, else milliseconds until a token exists. */\n    synchronized long acquire() {\n        long now = nanos.getAsLong();\n        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - last) * perSecond / 1e9);\n        last = now;\n        if (tokens >= 1) {\n            tokens -= 1;\n            return 0;\n        }\n        return (long) Math.ceil((1 - tokens) / perSecond * 1000);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Limiter {\n    private final Map<String, TokenBucket> buckets = new HashMap<>();\n    private final LongSupplier nanos;\n\n    Limiter(LongSupplier nanos) { this.nanos = nanos; }\n\n    synchronized long acquire(String client) {\n        return buckets\n            .computeIfAbsent(client, c -> new TokenBucket(3, 2, nanos))\n            .acquire();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var clock = new AtomicLong();\n        var limiter = new Limiter(clock::get);\n        String[] who = {\"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"a\", \"a\"};\n        long[] atMs = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 500, 500, 10_000, 10_000, 10_000, 10_000};\n        for (int i = 0; i < who.length; i++) {\n            clock.set(atMs[i] * 1_000_000);\n            long wait = limiter.acquire(who[i]);\n            System.out.println(atMs[i] + \"ms \" + who[i] + \" \"\n                + (wait == 0 ? \"200\" : \"429 retry-after=\" + wait + \"ms\"));\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"Tokens refill lazily from elapsed time on each call, so no timer thread exists. Time comes from an injected supplier, which makes the behavior testable.","region":"bucket"},{"note":"One bucket per client key, created on first use, so one noisy client cannot spend another's allowance. A real map needs eviction.","region":"perClient"},{"note":"Capacity 3, refill 2 per second. Client `a` bursts three, is refused with a wait, gets one after 500 ms, and is full again after ten seconds, capped at three.","region":"demo"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A public API with per-key quotas.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A SaaS API gives each key 100 requests per second with bursts of 200. A customer's retry loop goes wrong and floods the API. The limiter sits at the gateway, keyed by API key, and answers 429 with a `Retry-After` header. Well-behaved clients back off, the runaway customer only hurts itself, and other tenants are unaffected. Limits across several gateway nodes need shared state, usually in Redis.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["long wait = limiter.acquire(apiKey);","if (wait > 0) {","    return Response.status(429)","        .header(\"Retry-After\", (wait + 999) / 1000).build();","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: abuse and accidents are contained per client, and compliant clients get a clear signal for when to retry.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"lim","label":"per client","rel":"composition","to":"tb"},{"from":"tb","label":"reads","rel":"association","to":"clock"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"lim","kind":"class","members":["+ acquire(client): long"],"name":"Limiter","role":"Registry","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"tb","kind":"class","members":["- tokens: double","- last: long","+ acquire(): long"],"name":"TokenBucket","role":"Algorithm","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"clock","kind":"interface","members":["+ getAsLong()"],"name":"LongSupplier","role":"Time source","row":2}],"notes":[],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"client","label":"acquire(key)","style":"call","to":"lim"},{"from":"lim","label":"acquire()","style":"call","to":"tb"},{"from":"tb","label":"refill from elapsed","style":"call","to":"tb"},{"from":"tb","label":"0 (token spent)","style":"return","to":"lim"},{"from":"lim","label":"admitted","style":"return","to":"client"},{"from":"client","label":"acquire(key) when empty","style":"call","to":"lim"},{"from":"lim","label":"429, retry-after","style":"return","to":"client"}],"participants":[{"id":"client","name":"Client"},{"id":"lim","name":"Limiter"},{"id":"tb","name":"TokenBucket"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["You expose an API and must contain abusive or buggy clients.","A downstream has a known throughput ceiling or a paid quota.","Fairness among tenants matters more than peak throughput."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The real limit is concurrency, not arrival rate: use a [Bulkhead](kata:bulkhead).","Rejecting requests is worse than queueing them for a short time."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Fixed windows allow double the rate across a window boundary.","Per-node limiters multiply the limit by the node count.","Unbounded per-key maps are a memory leak under key churn.","Skewed clocks: use a monotonic source such as `System.nanoTime`."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["The JDK has no rate limiter; use Bucket4j, Resilience4j or Guava's `RateLimiter` rather than hand-rolling for production.","Per-key state is a `ConcurrentHashMap` problem; evict idle keys or use a cache with expiry."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A rate limiter caps arrivals per second; a bulkhead caps concurrent work in progress. A slow dependency can overload you at a low rate, which only a bulkhead catches.","ref":"bulkhead","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"The limiter says no; backoff with jitter is how a good client responds, ideally honoring `Retry-After`.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A limiter protects the callee by shaping incoming load. A breaker protects the caller by reacting to the callee's failures.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Gateways are the usual place to enforce per-client limits before requests reach services.","ref":"api-gateway","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Timeouts bound how long a request may take once started; rate limits bound how many may start.","ref":"timeout","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`RateLimiter.create(permitsPerSecond)` with `tryAcquire()` and `acquire()`; a smooth, bursty token-bucket style limiter.","name":"RateLimiter","where":"Guava"},{"detail":"Configured with `limitForPeriod`, `limitRefreshPeriod` and `timeoutDuration` for how long a caller may wait.","name":"RateLimiter","where":"Resilience4j"},{"detail":"A token-bucket library for Java with local and distributed (Redis, JCache) backends.","name":"Bucket","where":"Bucket4j"},{"detail":"Redis-backed token bucket via `RedisRateLimiter`, with `replenishRate` and `burstCapacity`.","name":"RequestRateLimiter filter","where":"Spring Cloud Gateway"},{"detail":"Applies a token bucket (`max_tokens`, `tokens_per_fill`, `fill_interval`) per listener or route.","name":"local_ratelimit HTTP filter","where":"Envoy"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["class Snippet {","    double perSecond = 2, burst = 3, budget = 3;","    long last = System.nanoTime();","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["synchronized boolean allow() {","    long now = System.nanoTime();","    double earned = (now - last) * perSecond / 1e9;","    budget = Math.min(burst, budget + earned);","    last = now;","    if (budget < 1) return false;","    budget -= 1;","    return true;","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"A budget refills with elapsed time up to a burst cap, and each admitted request spends one unit."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Nothing is released when work finishes; the budget depends on time, not on work in progress."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Admission does not depend on failures of a downstream, only on the earned budget."},{"ref":"timeout","why":"No call is being bounded in duration; the method only decides whether a request may start."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"A per-key token bucket contains the heavy client and can return 429 with a retry hint."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"It caps concurrency, but a fast stream of cheap requests can still overload you at low concurrency."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"No downstream is failing; the load is the problem, and it comes from one caller."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"That is the client's side of the story; it does nothing to protect the server."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Your public API has a customer whose script sometimes sends 5,000 requests in a second, degrading latency for everyone. Normal clients send a handful per second. You want to contain the heavy client and tell it when to retry."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.LongSupplier;","class Snippet {","    LongSupplier clock = System::nanoTime;","    double tokens = 3, perSecond = 2, capacity = 3;","    long last = 0;","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"Tokens accumulate without bound while a client is idle, so after an hour it can burst thousands of requests. Clamp the refill: `tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + earned)`.","flawLines":[3],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["synchronized boolean allow() {","    long now = clock.getAsLong();","    tokens += (now - last) * perSecond / 1e9;","    last = now;","    if (tokens < 1) return false;","    tokens -= 1;","    return true;","}","// capacity is the burst size"],"prompt":"Tap the line that lets idle clients take unlimited bursts."},{"blocks":[["long now = nanos.getAsLong();"],["tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - last) * perSecond / 1e9);"],["last = now;"],["if (tokens < 1) return false;"],["tokens -= 1;","return true;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.function.LongSupplier;","class Snippet {","    LongSupplier nanos = System::nanoTime;","    double tokens = 3, perSecond = 2, capacity = 3;","    long last = 0;","    boolean acquire() {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Read the clock, refill from the elapsed time, record the new timestamp, reject if less than one token remains, and only then spend a token.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the token bucket's acquire step."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Tokens refill at a steady rate, capped at capacity","Each request spends a token; none left means reject","Capacity controls burst size, refill rate the average","Lazy refill from elapsed time and an injected clock","Reject with a retry hint; keep one bucket per client"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A bucket holds up to a capacity of tokens and refills at a fixed rate, computed lazily from elapsed time. Each request spends a token; with none left it is rejected, ideally with a retry hint. Capacity sets how large a burst is allowed; the refill rate sets the sustained average.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain how a token bucket works and what burst capacity and refill rate each control."}],"id":"rate-limiter"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Make handling a message twice have the same effect as handling it once, by recognizing messages already processed.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Brokers give **at-least-once** delivery: acks get lost, consumers crash after working, producers publish twice. The consumer therefore records each message's unique id and skips ids it has seen. The record of the id and the business effect must commit **atomically**, or a crash between them brings back duplicates or loses work.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Key:** a stable id from the producer, not one generated on receipt.","**Store:** processed ids, in the same database as the effect when possible.","**Retention:** ids can expire once redelivery is impossible."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Exactly-once delivery does not exist across a network. What you build is at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"m1 applied\nm2 applied\nm1 duplicate\nm3 applied\nm2 duplicate\n{alice=750, bob=300}","file":"IdempotentConsumer.java","regions":{"deliveries":[29,34],"handle":[13,21],"message":[7,7]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nrecord Deposit(String messageId, String account, long cents) { }\n\nfinal class AccountConsumer {\n    private final Set<String> processed = new HashSet<>();\n    private final Map<String, Long> balances = new TreeMap<>();\n\n    /** With a database: check, effect and mark in one transaction. */\n    synchronized boolean handle(Deposit m) {\n        if (processed.contains(m.messageId())) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        balances.merge(m.account(), m.cents(), Long::sum);\n        processed.add(m.messageId());\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    Map<String, Long> balances() { return balances; }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var consumer = new AccountConsumer();\n        List<Deposit> broker = List.of(\n            new Deposit(\"m1\", \"alice\", 500),\n            new Deposit(\"m2\", \"bob\", 300),\n            new Deposit(\"m1\", \"alice\", 500),   // redelivery after a lost ack\n            new Deposit(\"m3\", \"alice\", 250),\n            new Deposit(\"m2\", \"bob\", 300));    // duplicate publish\n        for (Deposit d : broker) {\n            boolean applied = consumer.handle(d);\n            String verdict = applied ? \" applied\" : \" duplicate\";\n            System.out.println(d.messageId() + verdict);\n        }\n        System.out.println(consumer.balances());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The producer assigns `messageId`. Deduplicating on content or on a receive-time id would treat legitimate repeats as duplicates, or duplicates as new.","region":"message"},{"note":"Check, apply, mark, as one unit. In a database this is one transaction, ideally with a unique constraint on the id as the real guard.","region":"handle"},{"note":"A scripted broker delivers `m1` again after a lost ack and `m2` twice. Both repeats are acknowledged and skipped, so balances count each deposit once.","region":"deliveries"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Crediting loyalty points from order events.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An orders service publishes `OrderPaid` events to Kafka. The loyalty service consumes them and adds points. A rebalance replays the last few hundred events. Without protection, customers receive double points. The consumer inserts the event id into a `processed_event` table with a unique constraint in the same transaction that updates the points. A replayed event hits the constraint, the transaction rolls back, and the message is acknowledged.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["@Transactional","void on(OrderPaid e) {","    if (!processed.insertIfAbsent(e.eventId())) return;","    points.add(e.customerId(), e.amount() / 100);","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: replays, retries and producer duplicates become harmless, so the system can safely favor at-least-once delivery.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"cons","label":"checks, marks","rel":"association","to":"seen"},{"from":"cons","label":"applies","rel":"association","to":"fx"},{"from":"cons","label":"reads id","rel":"dependency","to":"msg"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"cons","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(msg): boolean"],"name":"Consumer","role":"Receiver","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"seen","kind":"interface","members":["+ contains(id)","+ add(id)"],"name":"ProcessedStore","role":"Dedup record","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"fx","kind":"class","members":["+ apply(msg)"],"name":"Business effect","role":"Side effect","row":1},{"col":0.5,"id":"msg","kind":"record","members":["+ messageId: String"],"name":"Message","role":"Message","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"cons","text":"check, effect, mark: one transaction"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"broker","label":"deliver(m1)","style":"call","to":"cons"},{"from":"cons","label":"seen(m1)?","style":"call","to":"store"},{"from":"store","label":"no","style":"return","to":"cons"},{"from":"cons","label":"apply + mark(m1), commit","style":"call","to":"store"},{"from":"cons","label":"ack lost","style":"async","to":"broker"},{"from":"broker","label":"redeliver(m1)","style":"call","to":"cons"},{"from":"cons","label":"seen(m1)?","style":"call","to":"store"},{"from":"store","label":"yes: skip","style":"return","to":"cons"},{"from":"cons","label":"ack","style":"return","to":"broker"}],"participants":[{"id":"broker","name":"Broker"},{"id":"cons","name":"Consumer"},{"id":"store","name":"Store"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Delivery is at-least-once, as with most brokers and webhooks.","The effect is not naturally idempotent, like incrementing a balance.","Producers or retries can publish the same event twice."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The operation is already idempotent, such as setting a field to a value.","Ids are unavailable and cannot be added upstream."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Marking the id outside the effect's transaction, so a crash between them loses or repeats work.","Using an id generated on receipt, which differs for each delivery.","Expiring ids sooner than the broker's redelivery window.","Ordering assumptions: dedup does not fix out-of-order delivery."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A dedup table in front of handlers that are idempotent by construction.","Separate Redis dedup cache with no transactional link to the database it guards."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Prefer a database unique constraint as the final guard; an in-memory set protects only one process.","`ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet().add(id)` checks and records atomically, but only within one JVM."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The outbox gives reliable at-least-once publishing on the producer side. The idempotent consumer absorbs the duplicates it produces.","ref":"transactional-outbox","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Retries cause duplicate deliveries by design. Idempotence on the receiving end is what makes retrying safe.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"With several consumers on one queue, the same message can reach two of them; the shared dedup store decides which one acts.","ref":"competing-consumers","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Saga steps and compensations are retried, so each participant must handle repeated commands idempotently.","ref":"saga","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A dedup store looks like a cache of seen ids, but it is authoritative: losing an entry causes a double effect, not just a slower read.","ref":"cache-aside","relation":"oftenConfused"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The producer setting dedups broker-side retries per partition; consumers still need their own deduplication for end-to-end effects.","name":"enable.idempotence","where":"Kafka"},{"detail":"Repeating a POST with the same key returns the original result instead of charging twice.","name":"Idempotency-Key header","where":"Stripe API"},{"detail":"Duplicates with the same id within a five-minute window are not delivered again.","name":"FIFO MessageDeduplicationId","where":"Amazon SQS"},{"detail":"Marks duplicates by a key from a `MetadataStore` and routes or discards them.","name":"IdempotentReceiverInterceptor","where":"Spring Integration"},{"detail":"`idempotentConsumer(expression, repository)` skips messages whose key the repository has seen.","name":"Idempotent Consumer EIP","where":"Apache Camel"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Set;","import java.util.HashSet;","record PaymentCaptured(String eventId, String account, long cents) { }","@interface Transactional { }","@interface Tx { }","class Ledger { void credit(String a, long c) { } }","class Snippet {","    Set<String> seenEvents = new HashSet<>();","    Ledger ledger = new Ledger();","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["@Transactional","public void on(PaymentCaptured e) {","    boolean fresh = seenEvents.add(e.eventId());","    if (!fresh) {","        return;","    }","    ledger.credit(e.account(), e.cents());","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"idempotent-consumer","why":"The handler records the message id and skips ids it already saw, inside the same transaction as the effect."},{"ref":"transactional-outbox","why":"Nothing is written for later publishing; this code receives and applies an event."},{"ref":"cache-aside","why":"The id set is authoritative state for correctness, not a disposable copy used to speed up reads."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Skipping is decided by message identity, not by how many arrived in a time window."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"idempotent-consumer","why":"Recording the event id with the credit makes every repeated delivery a no-op."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"It would reject repeats arriving fast, but legitimate distinct events would be rejected too."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"You are the receiver; the provider already retries, and more retrying does not prevent duplicates."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Nothing downstream is failing; the duplicates come from the sender's retries."}],"prompt":"Which pattern prevents the double credit?","scenario":"A payment webhook provider retries any callback that does not return 200 within five seconds. Your handler credits a customer's balance and sometimes answers in six seconds, so the same webhook, with the same event id, arrives two or three times."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Deposit(String id, String account, long cents) { }","class Snippet {","    Set<String> processed = new HashSet<>();","    Map<String, Long> balances = new HashMap<>();","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"The id is marked before the effect and in a separate step. A crash between them loses the deposit, because the redelivery is skipped. Mark and effect must commit atomically, in one transaction.","flawLines":[3],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["void on(Deposit m) {","    if (processed.contains(m.id())) return;","    processed.add(m.id());","    balances.merge(m.account(), m.cents(), Long::sum);","}","","// processed and balances are separate stores","// process may crash at any point"],"prompt":"Tap the line that makes this consumer unsafe."},{"blocks":[["begin();"],["if (processed.contains(m.id())) { rollback(); return; }"],["effect.apply(m);"],["processed.add(m.id());"],["commit();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Msg(String id) { }","interface Effect { void apply(Msg m); }","class Snippet {","    Set<String> processed = new HashSet<>();","    Effect effect;","    void begin() { }","    void commit() { }","    void rollback() { }","    void handle(Msg m) {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Open the transaction, check for a duplicate, apply the effect, then record the id, so both are committed together or neither is.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the steps of handling one message."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["At-least-once delivery means duplicates","A producer-assigned stable message id as the key","Record id and apply effect atomically","A unique constraint is the real guard","Retention must cover the redelivery window"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Networks and brokers deliver at least once: lost acks, retries and redeliveries produce duplicates. The consumer stores the producer-assigned message id and skips seen ids. It is correct only if recording the id and applying the effect commit atomically, ideally with a unique constraint.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Why do consumers need to be idempotent, and what makes the deduplication itself correct?"}],"id":"idempotent-consumer"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Write the outgoing message to a table in the same transaction as the data, then publish it from there reliably.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Updating a database and publishing to a broker cannot share one atomic transaction (the dual-write problem). With an **outbox**, the message becomes a row in the same database commit as the business change. A separate **relay** reads unsent rows, publishes them, and marks them sent.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Atomic:** the state change and its event commit or roll back together.","**Relay:** a poller or change-data-capture tails the outbox.","**Delivery:** at-least-once, so consumers must deduplicate."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The outbox fixes lost and phantom events, not duplicates. A relay crash between publish and mark re-sends the row.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"true\nfalse\n[o-1] outbox=1\npoll1 sent=0\npoll2 sent=1\npoll3 sent=0\n[OrderPlaced:o-1]","file":"TransactionalOutbox.java","regions":{"database":[11,31],"publish":[58,67],"relay":[33,46],"write":[52,57]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.io.IOException;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.function.Consumer;\n\nrecord OutboxRow(long id, String type, String payload) {}\ninterface Broker { void publish(OutboxRow row) throws IOException; }\n\nfinal class Db {\n    record Tx(List<String> orders, List<OutboxRow> rows) {}\n    final List<String> orders = new ArrayList<>();\n    final List<OutboxRow> outbox = new ArrayList<>();\n    private final Set<Long> published = new HashSet<>();\n    private long seq;\n    void placeOrder(Tx tx, String id) {\n        tx.orders().add(id);\n        tx.rows().add(new OutboxRow(++seq, \"OrderPlaced\", id));\n    }\n    boolean inTransaction(Consumer<Tx> work) {\n        var tx = new Tx(new ArrayList<>(), new ArrayList<>());\n        try {\n            work.accept(tx);\n        } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n            return false; // rollback: both buffers are dropped\n        }\n        orders.addAll(tx.orders()); // commit: data and message together\n        outbox.addAll(tx.rows());\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    int relay(Broker broker) {\n        int sent = 0;\n        for (OutboxRow row : outbox) {\n            if (published.contains(row.id())) continue;\n            try {\n                broker.publish(row);\n            } catch (IOException e) {\n                return sent; // keep the row; retry on the next poll\n            }\n            published.add(row.id()); // mark only after the broker acked\n            sent++;\n        }\n        return sent;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var db = new Db();\n        System.out.println(db.inTransaction(tx -> db.placeOrder(tx, \"o-1\")));\n        System.out.println(db.inTransaction(tx -> {\n            db.placeOrder(tx, \"o-2\");\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"constraint violated\");\n        }));\n        System.out.println(db.orders + \" outbox=\" + db.outbox.size());\n        int[] calls = {0};\n        var seen = new ArrayList<String>();\n        Broker flaky = row -> {\n            if (calls[0]++ == 0) throw new IOException(\"broker down\");\n            seen.add(row.type() + \":\" + row.payload());\n        };\n        for (int poll = 1; poll <= 3; poll++) {\n            System.out.println(\"poll\" + poll + \" sent=\" + db.relay(flaky));\n        }\n        System.out.println(seen);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A transaction buffers both the order and its outbox row. Commit writes them together; any exception drops both, so a rolled-back order never leaves a message behind.","region":"database"},{"note":"The relay scans unpublished rows. It marks a row published only after the broker acknowledges, so a failure keeps the row for the next poll.","region":"relay"},{"note":"The first transaction commits an order and its row. The second throws before commit: no order, no outbox row, and the sequence gap is harmless.","region":"write"},{"note":"A scripted broker fails on its first call. Poll 1 sends nothing, poll 2 delivers the row, poll 3 finds nothing left. Delivery happened exactly once here, but could repeat after a crash.","region":"publish"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Announcing a new booking to downstream systems.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A booking service inserts a reservation and must tell billing, email and analytics. Publishing to Kafka after the commit loses the event if the process dies in between; publishing before the commit announces bookings that roll back. The service inserts a `booking_created` row into an `outbox` table inside the transaction, and Debezium streams that table's inserts to Kafka.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["@Transactional","public void book(Booking b) {","    bookings.save(b);","    outbox.save(new OutboxEvent(","        b.id(), \"BookingCreated\", json(b)));","}   // commit writes both rows; a relay publishes later"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: no booking exists without its event, and no event exists without its booking, even across crashes and broker outages.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"svc","label":"one tx","rel":"association","to":"db"},{"from":"relay","label":"reads","rel":"association","to":"db"},{"from":"relay","label":"publishes","rel":"dependency","to":"broker"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ placeOrder(id)"],"name":"OrderService","role":"Producer","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"db","kind":"class","members":["orders table","outbox table"],"name":"Db","role":"Database","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"relay","kind":"class","members":["+ relay(broker)"],"name":"Relay","role":"MessageRelay","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"broker","kind":"interface","members":["+ publish(row)"],"name":"Broker","role":"Broker","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"db","text":"data + message commit together"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"s","label":"BEGIN","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"s","label":"INSERT order + outbox","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"s","label":"COMMIT","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"r","label":"SELECT unsent","style":"call","to":"d"},{"from":"d","label":"rows","style":"return","to":"r"},{"from":"r","label":"publish(row)","style":"call","to":"b"},{"from":"b","label":"ack","style":"return","to":"r"},{"from":"r","label":"mark sent","style":"call","to":"d"}],"participants":[{"id":"s","name":"Service","role":"Producer"},{"id":"d","name":"Database","role":"Database"},{"id":"r","name":"Relay","role":"MessageRelay"},{"id":"b","name":"Broker","role":"Broker"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A state change must be followed by an event, without losing or inventing either.","Distributed transactions across database and broker are unavailable or too costly.","Other services depend on your events for their own consistency."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The event is purely best-effort telemetry where loss is acceptable.","The database is also your broker, such as event sourcing, where the log is already the outbox."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Duplicates after relay crashes: consumers need ids and deduplication.","Outbox growth: purge or archive sent rows, or the table degrades.","Ordering: polling by id can reorder across concurrent transactions; key per aggregate."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Outbox rows that carry full database snapshots instead of intent-level events.","Polling every 50 ms where change-data-capture would remove the load."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records model outbox rows well; serialize payloads with Jackson into a JSON column.","Use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` to let several relays poll without duplicates."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The outbox guarantees at-least-once publication. The idempotent consumer on the other side makes the redelivered duplicates harmless.","ref":"idempotent-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A saga needs each local step and its message to be atomic. Each participant uses an outbox to publish its commands and events safely.","ref":"saga","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Domain events describe what happened inside a model. The outbox is the delivery mechanism that gets them out of the service reliably.","ref":"domain-event","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A unit of work batches changes into one commit. The outbox adds a message row to that same commit and a relay that publishes it.","ref":"unit-of-work","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"With event sourcing the event log is the source of truth, so publishing from it replaces a separate outbox table.","ref":"event-sourcing","relation":"alternative"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"An SMT (`io.debezium.transforms.outbox.EventRouter`) turns inserts into the outbox table into Kafka messages routed by aggregate type.","name":"Outbox Event Router","where":"Debezium"},{"detail":"Persists event publications in the same transaction and republishes incomplete ones, giving an outbox for application events.","name":"Event publication registry","where":"Spring Modulith"},{"detail":"Writes messages to a `message` table inside the business transaction and relays them to Kafka via CDC or polling.","name":"Message table","where":"Eventuate Tram"},{"detail":"Entity Framework integration stores messages in the same transaction before delivery to the transport.","name":"Transactional outbox (.NET)","where":"MassTransit"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.UUID;","@interface Transactional {}","record OrderId(String value) {}","class Order { void markCancelled() {} OrderId id() { return new OrderId(\"1\"); } }","interface Orders { Order require(OrderId id); void save(Order o); }","record EventRow(UUID id, String type, String payload) {}","interface Events { void insert(EventRow row); }","class Holder { Orders orders; Events events;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["@Transactional","void cancel(OrderId id) {","    Order order = orders.require(id);","    order.markCancelled();","    orders.save(order);","    events.insert(new EventRow(","        UUID.randomUUID(), \"OrderCancelled\", order.id().value()));","}","// a separate job reads unsent EventRow records and sends them to the broker"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"transactional-outbox","why":"The event is a row saved in the same database transaction as the change; a separate relay publishes it."},{"ref":"domain-event","why":"Domain events model what happened, but this snippet is about getting it out atomically via a table."},{"ref":"unit-of-work","why":"Committing several changes together is part of it, but the point is a message row and a later relay."},{"ref":"saga","why":"A saga coordinates many steps across services with compensation; this is one local transaction."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"transactional-outbox","why":"Store the event in the same commit as the payment and let a relay publish it, so a crash cannot drop it."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"Retries run in the process; if the process dies between commit and publish there is nothing left to retry."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"Breakers protect against a failing dependency but cannot recover an event lost with a dead process."},{"ref":"idempotent-consumer","why":"It handles duplicates downstream; it cannot invent an event that was never published."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fixes the lost event?","scenario":"A service saves a payment row and then publishes PaymentCaptured to Kafka. Occasionally the process dies between the commit and the publish, and billing never learns about the payment. Distributed transactions across the database and Kafka are not available."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.util.List;","record OutboxRow(long id) {}","interface Broker { void publish(OutboxRow row) throws IOException; }","class Holder { List<OutboxRow> unsent() { return List.of(); } void markPublished(long id) {}","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"Marking the row before the broker acknowledges means a failed or crashed publish leaves a row flagged as sent that never was. Publish first, mark after the ack, and accept possible duplicates.","flawLines":[4],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["int relay(Broker broker) {","    int sent = 0;","    for (OutboxRow row : unsent()) {","        markPublished(row.id());","        try {","            broker.publish(row);","        } catch (IOException e) {","            return sent;","        }","        sent++;","    }","    return sent;","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the outbox guarantee."},{"blocks":[["tx.begin();"],["orders.save(order);"],["outbox.save(new OutboxRow(order.id(), \"OrderPlaced\", json(order)));"],["tx.commit();"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Tx { void begin(); void commit(); }","record Order(String id) {}","record OutboxRow(String id, String type, String payload) {}","interface Orders { void save(Order o); }","interface Outbox { void save(OutboxRow r); }","class Snippet { Tx tx; Orders orders; Outbox outbox; Order order = new Order(\"1\");","  String json(Order o) { return \"{}\"; }","  void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Open the transaction, write the state and the message row inside it, and commit once. Nothing is published here; the relay sends the row afterwards.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the write path so that data and message commit together."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Dual-write problem: database and broker can't commit atomically","Message saved as a row in the business transaction","Relay publishes then marks the row, by polling or CDC","At-least-once delivery, so duplicates are possible","Consumers need idempotency"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Writing to a database and publishing to a broker can't be atomic, so a crash can lose or invent events. The outbox stores the message in the same transaction as the data; a relay later publishes unsent rows and marks them sent. It gives at-least-once delivery, so consumers must deduplicate.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Transactional Outbox: the problem it solves, how it works, and what guarantee it gives."}],"id":"transactional-outbox"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Replace a distributed transaction with a sequence of local transactions, each paired with a compensation that undoes it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"No single ACID transaction spans services, so a **saga** runs one local transaction per service. If step N fails, the saga executes the **compensating actions** for steps N-1 down to 1. Intermediate states are visible to others, so a saga gives atomicity of outcome but not isolation.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Orchestration:** a coordinator tells each participant what to do.","**Choreography:** participants react to each other's events.","**Compensation:** a semantic undo (refund), not a rollback."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Compensations can fail too, and some steps can't be undone (an email sent). Order steps so irreversible ones come last.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"true [do reserve, do hold-points, do charge, do ship] stock=4 held=10\nfalse [do reserve, do hold-points, fail charge, undo hold-points, undo reserve] stock=5 held=0","file":"Saga.java","regions":{"definition":[40,46],"orchestrator":[8,33],"run":[54,56],"step":[6,6]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nrecord Step(String name, Runnable action, Runnable compensation) {}\n\nfinal class Saga {\n    private final List<Step> steps;\n    final List<String> log = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    Saga(List<Step> steps) { this.steps = steps; }\n\n    boolean run() {\n        Deque<Step> done = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        for (Step step : steps) {\n            try {\n                step.action().run();\n                log.add(\"do \" + step.name());\n                done.push(step);\n            } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n                log.add(\"fail \" + step.name());\n                while (!done.isEmpty()) {\n                    Step undo = done.pop();\n                    undo.compensation().run();\n                    log.add(\"undo \" + undo.name());\n                }\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    static int stock = 5;\n    static int held = 0;\n\n    static Saga checkout(boolean cardOk) {\n        return new Saga(List.of(\n            new Step(\"reserve\", () -> stock--, () -> stock++),\n            new Step(\"hold-points\", () -> held += 10, () -> held -= 10),\n            new Step(\"charge\", () -> {\n                if (!cardOk) throw new IllegalStateException(\"declined\");\n            }, () -> {}),\n            new Step(\"ship\", () -> {}, () -> {})));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (boolean cardOk : new boolean[] {true, false}) {\n            stock = 5;\n            held = 0;\n            var saga = checkout(cardOk);\n            boolean ok = saga.run();\n            System.out.println(ok + \" \" + saga.log\n                + \" stock=\" + stock + \" held=\" + held);\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A step pairs a forward action with its compensation. Defining both together makes it harder to add an action nobody knows how to undo.","region":"step"},{"note":"The orchestrator runs steps in order and pushes each success onto a stack. On failure it pops the stack, so compensations run in reverse order of completion.","region":"orchestrator"},{"note":"The workflow is data: reserve stock, hold points, charge the card, ship. The irreversible shipment is last. A declined card throws inside `charge`.","region":"definition"},{"note":"Run one: everything succeeds. Run two: the charge fails, the points hold and the reservation are undone in reverse order, and state returns to the start.","region":"run"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Booking a trip across flight, hotel and payment services.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Each service owns its database, so there is no shared transaction. The booking orchestrator reserves a seat, then a room, then charges the card. If the card is declined, it cancels the room and releases the seat. Each compensation is idempotent and retried until it succeeds, and the saga's state is persisted after each step so a restarted orchestrator resumes where it stopped.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["new Saga(List.of(","    new Step(\"seat\",  () -> flights.hold(trip), () -> flights.release(trip)),","    new Step(\"room\",  () -> hotels.hold(trip),  () -> hotels.release(trip)),","    new Step(\"charge\", () -> payments.charge(trip), () -> payments.refund(trip))",")).run();"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a failure at any point leaves every service consistent again, without locks held across the network.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"saga","label":"steps","rel":"composition","to":"step"},{"from":"step","label":"calls","rel":"dependency","to":"inv"},{"from":"step","label":"calls","rel":"dependency","to":"pay"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"saga","kind":"class","members":["- steps: List<Step>","+ run(): boolean"],"name":"Saga","role":"Orchestrator","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"step","kind":"record","members":["+ action: Runnable","+ compensation: Runnable"],"name":"Step","role":"LocalTransaction","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"inv","kind":"class","members":["+ reserve()","+ release()"],"name":"InventoryService","role":"Participant","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"pay","kind":"class","members":["+ charge()","+ refund()"],"name":"PaymentService","role":"Participant","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"step","text":"every action has an undo"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"o","label":"reserve","style":"call","to":"i"},{"from":"i","label":"ok","style":"return","to":"o"},{"from":"o","label":"charge","style":"call","to":"p"},{"from":"p","label":"declined","style":"return","to":"o"},{"from":"o","label":"release (compensate)","style":"call","to":"i"},{"from":"i","label":"ok","style":"return","to":"o"}],"participants":[{"id":"o","name":"Orchestrator","role":"Orchestrator"},{"id":"i","name":"Inventory","role":"Participant"},{"id":"p","name":"Payment","role":"Participant"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A business process spans services that own their data.","Locks or two-phase commit across services are impractical.","Every step has a meaningful business-level undo."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["One database can run the work in a local ACID transaction.","Steps need strict isolation, such as exact balances visible to all."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Dirty reads: other requests see intermediate state until compensation runs.","Every step and compensation must be idempotent and retryable.","Orchestrator state must be durable, or a crash strands the saga half done."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A saga for two steps in the same service and database.","Choreographed sagas across many services nobody can trace end to end."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Records and sealed events model saga steps and state cleanly.","Durable-execution engines such as Temporal persist progress for you."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Each step is a command with an undo. A saga adds the failure policy: on error it runs the undos in reverse order.","ref":"command","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A unit of work gives ACID atomicity in one database. A saga offers eventual atomicity across services, using compensations instead of rollback.","ref":"unit-of-work","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Each saga step must update its data and send its next message atomically, which is what the outbox provides.","ref":"transactional-outbox","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Messages and compensations can be redelivered, so each participant must apply them at most once in effect.","ref":"idempotent-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A saga's progress is a state machine, but State models one object's behavior. A saga coordinates several services and their undo.","ref":"state","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Saga classes with `@StartSaga` and `@EndSaga` react to events and dispatch commands, with persisted association values.","name":"@Saga and @SagaEventHandler","where":"Axon Framework"},{"detail":"Java SDK helper that registers compensations as the workflow proceeds and runs them in reverse on failure.","name":"io.temporal.workflow.Saga","where":"Temporal"},{"detail":"Orchestration-based saga framework for Java microservices, built on a transactional messaging layer.","name":"Sagas","where":"Eventuate Tram"},{"detail":"A state machine routes failures to compensating states, the standard way to implement orchestrated sagas on AWS.","name":"Catch and Retry states","where":"AWS Step Functions"},{"detail":"Compensation boundary events and handlers undo completed activities when a process is cancelled or fails.","name":"BPMN compensation events","where":"Camunda"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.ArrayDeque;","import java.util.Deque;","record Trip(String id) {}","interface Svc { void hold(Trip t); void release(Trip t); void charge(Trip t); }","class Holder { Svc flights; Svc hotels; Svc payments;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["boolean book(Trip trip) {","    Deque<Runnable> undo = new ArrayDeque<>();","    try {","        flights.hold(trip);  undo.push(() -> flights.release(trip));","        hotels.hold(trip);   undo.push(() -> hotels.release(trip));","        payments.charge(trip);","        return true;","    } catch (RuntimeException e) {","        undo.forEach(Runnable::run);","        return false;","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"saga","why":"Local steps across services each register a compensation, and a failure runs them in reverse order."},{"ref":"command","why":"Commands with undo are used here, but the idea is the cross-service failure workflow, not request objects."},{"ref":"unit-of-work","why":"A unit of work commits or rolls back changes in one data store; these calls span independent services."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"No handler decides whether to pass along a request; every step always runs in a fixed order."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"saga","why":"Local transactions with compensations, such as releasing the reservation, restore consistency without a distributed transaction."},{"ref":"unit-of-work","why":"It needs one transactional resource; here three independent databases are involved."},{"ref":"circuit-breaker","why":"It stops calling a failing service but does not undo what earlier services already did."},{"ref":"retry-backoff","why":"A declined payment will not succeed on retry; the earlier steps need compensating."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"Placing an order must reserve stock in the inventory service, charge the payment service and create a shipment, each with its own database. If payment fails, the stock reservation must not stay held. Two-phase commit is not offered by the services."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Step(String name, Runnable action, Runnable compensation) {}","class Holder { List<Step> steps;","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"`addLast` puts the newest step at the tail while `pop` takes from the head, so compensations would run in forward order. Use `push` so the most recent step is undone first.","flawLines":[6],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["boolean run() {","    Deque<Step> done = new ArrayDeque<>();","    for (Step step : steps) {","        try {","            step.action().run();","            done.addLast(step);","        } catch (RuntimeException e) {","            while (!done.isEmpty()) done.pop().compensation().run();","            return false;","        }","    }","    return true;","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the compensation logic."},{"blocks":[["Deque<Step> done = new ArrayDeque<>();"],["step.action().run();","done.push(step);"],["// payment declined: the action throws"],["Step undo = done.pop();","undo.compensation().run();"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","record Step(String name, Runnable action, Runnable compensation) {}","class Snippet { void run(Step step) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Track completed steps first, record each success after its action, hit the failure, and only then pop completed steps newest-first to compensate.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the failure handling of one saga run."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Sequence of local transactions across services","Each step has a compensation, a semantic undo","On failure run compensations in reverse order","No isolation: intermediate states are visible","Steps and compensations must be idempotent and durable"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A saga splits a cross-service operation into local transactions, each with a compensating action. On failure, completed steps are compensated in reverse order, restoring consistency without distributed locks. It can't provide isolation: other requests may see intermediate states, and compensations must be idempotent.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain a Saga: why it exists, how failure is handled, and what it cannot give you."}],"id":"saga"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let the application load data into a cache on demand and keep the database as the source of truth.","type":"lead"},{"text":"On a read the application checks the cache first. On a **miss** it reads the database, stores the result with a **TTL**, and returns it. On a write it updates the database and **invalidates** the cached entry. The cache never talks to the database itself; the application owns both sides.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Read:** hit returns; miss loads, stores, returns.","**Write:** update the source, then delete the cached copy.","**Expiry:** a TTL bounds how stale a missed invalidation can be."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The cache can briefly disagree with the database. Cache-aside gives eventual consistency, not a transaction across both.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"Ada reads=1\nAda reads=1\nGrace reads=2\nGrace reads=3\nfalse reads=4","file":"CacheAside.java","regions":{"read":[36,45],"source":[13,22],"write":[47,50]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Optional;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\nimport java.util.function.LongSupplier;\n\ninterface UserStore {\n    Optional<String> find(String id);\n    void save(String id, String name);\n}\n\nfinal class CountingStore implements UserStore {\n    private final Map<String, String> rows = new HashMap<>();\n    int reads;\n\n    public Optional<String> find(String id) {\n        reads++;\n        return Optional.ofNullable(rows.get(id));\n    }\n    public void save(String id, String name) { rows.put(id, name); }\n}\n\nfinal class UserService {\n    private record Entry(String name, long expiresAt) { }\n\n    private final UserStore store;\n    private final LongSupplier clock;\n    private final long ttlMs;\n    private final Map<String, Entry> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    UserService(UserStore store, LongSupplier clock, long ttlMs) {\n        this.store = store; this.clock = clock; this.ttlMs = ttlMs;\n    }\n\n    Optional<String> get(String id) {\n        Entry hit = cache.get(id);\n        if (hit != null && hit.expiresAt() > clock.getAsLong()) {\n            return Optional.of(hit.name());\n        }\n        Optional<String> row = store.find(id);\n        long expiresAt = clock.getAsLong() + ttlMs;\n        row.ifPresent(n -> cache.put(id, new Entry(n, expiresAt)));\n        return row;\n    }\n\n    void rename(String id, String name) {\n        store.save(id, name);\n        cache.remove(id);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var now = new AtomicLong();\n        var db = new CountingStore();\n        db.save(\"u1\", \"Ada\");\n        var users = new UserService(db, now::get, 60_000);\n        System.out.println(users.get(\"u1\").get() + \" reads=\" + db.reads);\n        System.out.println(users.get(\"u1\").get() + \" reads=\" + db.reads);\n        users.rename(\"u1\", \"Grace\");\n        System.out.println(users.get(\"u1\").get() + \" reads=\" + db.reads);\n        now.set(61_000);\n        System.out.println(users.get(\"u1\").get() + \" reads=\" + db.reads);\n        System.out.println(users.get(\"zz\").isPresent() + \" reads=\" + db.reads);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A counting fake of the database makes the pattern's effect visible: every call to `find` is a cache miss somewhere above.","region":"source"},{"note":"Check the entry and its expiry against the injected clock. Only a present row is cached. A miss falls through to the store and fills the cache.","region":"read"},{"note":"Write the database first, then evict. Evicting rather than updating the cache avoids racing writers leaving an older value cached.","region":"write"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A product catalog read by every page view.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A catalog service reads product details from PostgreSQL, thousands of times a second for a small hot set. Product data changes a few times a day. Putting Redis in front with a ten-minute TTL cuts database load by orders of magnitude. The price-update job writes the database and then deletes the key, so the next read reloads the fresh value. A restart of Redis only causes a temporary rise in database reads.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["Product p = cache.get(key);","if (p == null) {","    p = db.findProduct(id);","    cache.set(key, p, Duration.ofMinutes(10));","}"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: hot reads come from memory, and the cache can be lost at any time without losing data.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"svc","label":"read, evict","rel":"association","to":"cache"},{"from":"svc","label":"load, write","rel":"association","to":"store"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"svc","kind":"class","members":["+ get(id): Optional","+ rename(id, name)"],"name":"UserService","role":"Application","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"cache","kind":"class","members":["- entries: Map","- ttlMs: long"],"name":"Cache","role":"Cache","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"store","kind":"interface","members":["+ find(id)","+ save(id, name)"],"name":"UserStore","role":"Source of truth","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"cache","text":"unaware of the store"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"app","label":"get(key)","style":"call","to":"cache"},{"from":"cache","label":"miss","style":"return","to":"app"},{"from":"app","label":"find(id)","style":"call","to":"db"},{"from":"db","label":"row","style":"return","to":"app"},{"from":"app","label":"put(key, row, ttl)","style":"call","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","label":"get(key) again","style":"call","to":"cache"},{"from":"cache","label":"hit","style":"return","to":"app"}],"participants":[{"id":"app","name":"App"},{"id":"cache","name":"Cache"},{"id":"db","name":"Database"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Reads far outnumber writes and a small hot set dominates.","The data tolerates seconds or minutes of staleness.","The cache may be cold or lost without data loss."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Reads must always see the latest write, such as balances.","Access is uniformly spread, so hit rates stay low."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Stampede: many readers miss on a popular key and hit the database together.","Race: a reader loads old data, a writer evicts, and the reader then caches the stale row.","Absent rows never cached, so repeated lookups for missing keys hit the database.","No TTL: a missed eviction stays wrong forever."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Caching to hide a missing index or an N+1 query.","Caching entities that are written as often as read."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Caffeine's `LoadingCache` gives per-key single-flight loading, so only one thread loads a missing key.","`ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent` is fine for in-process caches, but keep slow loads out of it."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Cache-aside often lives inside a repository, so callers see one data-access seam and never the cache.","ref":"repository","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"A caching proxy is transparent to the caller and the cache sits in the call path. In cache-aside, application code explicitly manages the cache.","ref":"proxy","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"A decorating repository can add cache-aside behavior without changing callers. Read-through and write-through caches move the loading into the cache itself.","ref":"decorator","relation":"alternative"},{"difference":"Event sourcing stores events as the truth and derives state; a cache is a disposable copy of state the database already holds.","ref":"event-sourcing","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Cache-aside reduces calls; retry and breaker cover the calls that still reach the database.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"The cache abstraction gives declarative read-and-fill and eviction around a method, backed by Caffeine, Redis and others.","name":"@Cacheable and @CacheEvict","where":"Spring Framework"},{"detail":"Computes a missing value once per key, with expiry and size-based eviction.","name":"LoadingCache and Cache.get(key, mappingFunction)","where":"Caffeine"},{"detail":"`CacheBuilder.newBuilder().expireAfterWrite(...)` builds a loading cache with a `CacheLoader`.","name":"CacheBuilder and LoadingCache","where":"Guava"},{"detail":"Entity caches filled on a load and invalidated on update, through JCache or Infinispan providers.","name":"Second-level cache","where":"Hibernate"},{"detail":"Native TTLs make time-bounded entries cheap, the usual store behind cache-aside.","name":"EXPIRE and SET with EX","where":"Redis"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","interface Profile { String id(); }","interface Mem { Profile get(String k); void put(String k, Profile p, Duration t); void remove(String k); }","interface Db { Profile findProfile(String id); void update(Profile p); }","class Snippet {","    Mem memory; Db db;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["Profile load(String id) {","    Profile p = memory.get(id);","    if (p != null) return p;","    p = db.findProfile(id);","    memory.put(id, p, Duration.ofMinutes(5));","    return p;","}","void save(Profile p) {","    db.update(p);","    memory.remove(p.id());","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"cache-aside","why":"The application itself checks, fills on a miss, and evicts after writing the database."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"The caller does not reach the data through a stand-in object; this code manages the cache explicitly."},{"ref":"repository","why":"A repository is the data-access seam; the distinguishing feature here is the explicit cache fill and eviction."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"State is not derived from a log of events; the database holds the truth."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"cache-aside","why":"Read-heavy, rarely changing, staleness tolerated, and the database stays the source of truth."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Callers are not abusive; the same data is simply read repeatedly."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"It would protect other features from this load, but not reduce the load itself."},{"ref":"event-sourcing","why":"It changes how truth is stored, which is not the problem here."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A reference-data service answers 5,000 reads per second for 2,000 country and currency records that change maybe weekly. The database is struggling, but losing the cache must never lose data, and a few minutes of stale data is acceptable."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Db { Optional<String> find(String id); void save(String id, String name); }","class Snippet {","    Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();","    Db db;","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"A reader can miss, load the old row, and cache it between the eviction and the database write, leaving the stale name cached. Write the database first, then evict.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["void rename(String id, String name) {","    cache.remove(id);","    db.save(id, name);","}","","Optional<String> get(String id) {","    var hit = cache.get(id);","    if (hit != null) return Optional.of(hit);","    var row = db.find(id);","    row.ifPresent(n -> cache.put(id, n));","    return row;","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that can leave stale data cached."},{"blocks":[["String v = cache.get(key);"],["if (v != null) return v;"],["v = db.load(key);"],["cache.put(key, v);"],["return v;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Db { String load(String k); }","class Snippet {","    Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();","    Db db;","    String read(String key) {","{{code}}","    }","}"]},"explanation":"Look in the cache, return on a hit, otherwise load from the source, fill the cache with the loaded value, then return it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the read path."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["The application manages the cache; the database is the truth","Read: check, load on miss, fill with TTL","Write: update the database, then evict","Eviction avoids races that updating can cause","Risks: staleness and stampedes"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Reads check the cache, load from the database on a miss and store with a TTL. Writes update the database and then delete the cached entry, because updating the cache can leave an older value after racing writers. The risks are brief staleness and stampedes on a hot key.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the read and write paths of cache-aside, why writes evict instead of update, and the main risk."}],"id":"cache-aside"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Let several identical consumers pull from one queue, so throughput scales by adding consumers and each message goes to one of them.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Producers write to a single **queue**. A pool of interchangeable **consumers** each take the next message, process it, and acknowledge it. The broker hands every message to one consumer, so adding or removing consumers changes capacity without touching producers.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Scales:** consumer count, independently of producers.","**Requires:** stateless, interchangeable workers.","**Gives up:** ordering across consumers, unless you partition by key."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Delivery is almost always at-least-once: a consumer that crashes before acking causes redelivery. Consumers must tolerate duplicates.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"jobs=20 exactlyOnce=true total=210","file":"CompetingConsumers.java","regions":{"consumer":[11,35],"message":[7,9],"produce":[47,47],"queue":[39,42],"start":[44,46],"stop":[48,48]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\nimport java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\n\nrecord Job(int id, int cost) {\n    static final Job STOP = new Job(-1, 0);\n}\n\nfinal class Worker implements Runnable {\n    private final BlockingQueue<Job> queue;\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Integer> handled;\n    private final AtomicLong total;\n\n    Worker(BlockingQueue<Job> queue,\n           ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Integer> handled, AtomicLong total) {\n        this.queue = queue;\n        this.handled = handled;\n        this.total = total;\n    }\n\n    public void run() {\n        try {\n            while (true) {\n                Job job = queue.take();\n                if (job == Job.STOP) return;\n                handled.merge(job.id(), 1, Integer::sum);\n                total.addAndGet(job.cost());\n            }\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        var queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<Job>();\n        var handled = new ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Integer>();\n        var total = new AtomicLong();\n        int workers = 4;\n        try (var pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            for (int w = 0; w < workers; w++) {\n                pool.submit(new Worker(queue, handled, total));\n            }\n            for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) queue.put(new Job(i, i));\n            for (int w = 0; w < workers; w++) queue.put(Job.STOP);\n        } // close() waits for every worker to finish\n        boolean once = handled.values().stream().allMatch(n -> n == 1);\n        System.out.println(\"jobs=\" + handled.size()\n            + \" exactlyOnce=\" + once + \" total=\" + total.get());\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"A job is an immutable record. `STOP` is a sentinel (poison pill) compared by identity, so it can never collide with real work.","region":"message"},{"note":"Each worker loops on `take()`. The queue is the only shared coordination point: whoever calls `take()` first owns that job. Workers share no other state about which job is whose.","region":"consumer"},{"note":"One shared `LinkedBlockingQueue`, plus thread-safe bookkeeping used only to verify that no job was handled twice.","region":"queue"},{"note":"Four identical consumers start on virtual threads. Scaling out is changing `workers`; nothing else knows the number.","region":"start"},{"note":"The producer only enqueues. It does not know how many consumers exist or which one will take a job.","region":"produce"},{"note":"One `STOP` per consumer lets each exit its loop. Leaving the `try` block waits for all workers, so the printout is deterministic.","region":"stop"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Thumbnail generation after photo uploads.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A photo app publishes one `PhotoUploaded` message per upload to a queue. Traffic is spiky: ten times the load on Sunday evenings. A single worker falls hours behind, so the team runs the same worker image as N replicas on the same queue and scales N from queue depth. A message that fails three times moves to a dead-letter queue.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["// every replica runs the same loop against queue \"thumbnails\"","var msg = queue.receive(\"thumbnails\", Duration.ofSeconds(60));","thumbnailer.render(msg.photoId());   // idempotent: same output","queue.delete(msg.receiptHandle());   // ack after the work"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: Sunday peaks are absorbed by adding replicas, and a crashed worker just means its message reappears for another one.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"producer","label":"send","rel":"dependency","to":"queue"},{"from":"c1","label":"receive","rel":"association","to":"queue"},{"from":"c2","label":"receive","rel":"association","to":"queue"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0,"id":"producer","kind":"class","members":["+ send(message)"],"name":"Producer","role":"Producer","row":0},{"col":1,"id":"queue","kind":"interface","members":["+ receive(): Message","+ ack(message)"],"name":"MessageQueue","role":"Channel","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"c1","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"ConsumerA","role":"Consumer","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"c2","kind":"class","members":["+ run()"],"name":"ConsumerB","role":"Consumer","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"queue","text":"each message goes to one consumer"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"p","label":"send(m1)","style":"async","to":"q"},{"from":"p","label":"send(m2)","style":"async","to":"q"},{"from":"a","label":"receive()","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"q","label":"m1","style":"return","to":"a"},{"from":"b","label":"receive()","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"q","label":"m2","style":"return","to":"b"},{"from":"a","label":"ack(m1)","style":"call","to":"q"},{"from":"b","label":"ack(m2)","style":"call","to":"q"}],"participants":[{"id":"p","name":"Producer"},{"id":"q","name":"Queue","role":"Channel"},{"id":"a","name":"ConsumerA","role":"Consumer"},{"id":"b","name":"ConsumerB","role":"Consumer"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Jobs are independent and one worker cannot keep up.","Load is spiky and you want to scale workers on queue depth.","A slow or crashed worker must not stall the others."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["Messages must be processed in strict global order.","Every message must reach every subscriber: that is publish/subscribe."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Acking before processing loses work on a crash; acking after causes duplicates.","A poison message retried forever blocks capacity: set a retry limit and a dead-letter queue.","Ordering needs partitioning by key, which caps parallelism at the partition count."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Adding consumers when the bottleneck is the database they all write to.","Competing consumers on a queue that carries only a handful of messages per hour."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads make one thread per in-flight message cheap, so a blocking consumer loop is fine.","`ExecutorService` with `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` gives structured start and shutdown, as in the sample."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"Producer-Consumer is the in-process hand-off through a bounded buffer. Competing Consumers applies it across processes with a broker, acknowledgements, redelivery and scale-out.","ref":"producer-consumer","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"A thread pool shares a work queue among threads of one JVM and loses the queue on exit. Competing consumers share a durable queue among separate processes.","ref":"thread-pool","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"Observer or pub/sub delivers each event to every subscriber. Competing consumers deliver each message to exactly one member of the group.","ref":"observer","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"At-least-once delivery means a message can reach a second consumer after a timeout. Each consumer must be idempotent to make that harmless.","ref":"idempotent-consumer","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Separate queues and consumer pools per workload keep one slow job type from consuming all capacity.","ref":"bulkhead","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Consumers sharing a `group.id` split a topic's partitions between them; each partition is read by one member at a time.","name":"Consumer groups","where":"Apache Kafka"},{"detail":"Several consumers on one queue receive messages round-robin; `basic.ack` confirms and unacked messages are redelivered.","name":"Work queues","where":"RabbitMQ"},{"detail":"A received message is hidden from other consumers until it is deleted or the timeout expires, then reappears.","name":"Visibility timeout","where":"Amazon SQS"},{"detail":"Worker threads compete for tasks from one `BlockingQueue`: the same idea inside a single JVM.","name":"ThreadPoolExecutor","where":"JDK"},{"detail":"A work-queue Job runs several identical pods that each pull items from an external queue until it is empty.","name":"Job with parallelism","where":"Kubernetes"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.function.Consumer;","record Message(String body, String receiptHandle) {}","interface MessageQueue { Message receive(String name, Duration hide); void delete(String handle); }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class JobWorker {","    private final MessageQueue queue;","    private final Consumer<String> handler;","    JobWorker(MessageQueue q, Consumer<String> h) { queue = q; handler = h; }","    void poll() {","        Message m = queue.receive(\"jobs\", Duration.ofSeconds(30));","        if (m == null) return;","        handler.accept(m.body());","        queue.delete(m.receiptHandle());","    }","}","// deployed as N identical replicas on the same queue name"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"competing-consumers","why":"Identical workers pull from one queue; a received message is hidden from the others and deleted only after the work succeeds."},{"ref":"observer","why":"Observer would give every replica every message. Here a message is taken by one consumer only."},{"ref":"idempotent-consumer","why":"Nothing records processed message ids or skips duplicates; the code only shows pull, process and ack."},{"ref":"thread-pool","why":"A thread pool lives inside one JVM and owns its threads; these are separate replicas sharing an external queue."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"competing-consumers","why":"Independent jobs on one queue can be drained in parallel by adding identical workers, with no producer change."},{"ref":"rate-limiter","why":"Limiting intake lowers the arrival rate but does not drain the backlog; capacity must grow."},{"ref":"bulkhead","why":"Isolating pools protects other workloads but adds no throughput for this one."},{"ref":"api-gateway","why":"A gateway sits in front of synchronous client calls and does nothing for background job throughput."}],"prompt":"Which pattern most directly fixes the backlog?","scenario":"An invoicing service renders PDFs from a queue. Each job takes about two seconds and is independent. On the first of the month the backlog reaches hours, and one worker pegs a single CPU."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.function.Consumer;","record Message(String body, String receiptHandle) {}","interface MessageQueue { Message receive(String name, Duration hide); void delete(String handle); }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The message is acknowledged before the work is done. A crash in the handler loses the job for good. Delete after `handler.accept` succeeds, and make the handler idempotent for redelivery.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class JobWorker {","    private final MessageQueue queue;","    private final Consumer<String> handler;","    JobWorker(MessageQueue q, Consumer<String> h) { queue = q; handler = h; }","    void poll() {","        Message m = queue.receive(\"jobs\", Duration.ofSeconds(30));","        if (m == null) return;","        queue.delete(m.receiptHandle());","        handler.accept(m.body());","    }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks reliable consumption."},{"blocks":[["Message m = queue.receive(\"jobs\", Duration.ofSeconds(30));"],["if (m == null) return;"],["handler.accept(m.body());"],["queue.delete(m.receiptHandle());"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.time.Duration;","import java.util.function.Consumer;","record Message(String body, String receiptHandle) {}","interface MessageQueue { Message receive(String name, Duration hide); void delete(String handle); }","class Snippet { void run(MessageQueue queue, Consumer<String> handler) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Receive first, bail out on an empty poll, do the work, and acknowledge last so a failure leaves the message to reappear for another consumer.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order one consumer iteration so that every line uses what came before."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Many identical consumers on one queue; each message goes to one","Scale out by adding consumers, producers unaffected","At-least-once delivery means redelivery and duplicates","Handlers should be idempotent; ordering is not guaranteed","Pub/sub fans out to all subscribers instead"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"Several identical workers read one queue and each message goes to one of them, so you scale by adding workers. Delivery is at-least-once: unacknowledged messages are redelivered, so handlers must be idempotent and ordering is lost. Pub/sub instead copies each message to every subscriber.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain Competing Consumers: how it scales, what delivery guarantee you get, and how it differs from publish/subscribe."}],"id":"competing-consumers"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Give clients a single entry point that authenticates, routes and aggregates calls to the services behind it.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Instead of every client knowing every service address, protocol and auth scheme, a **gateway** terminates the request at the edge. It applies cross-cutting policy once, routes by path or header, and can compose several backend calls into one response tailored to a client type.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Routing:** path, host or header to a service.","**Policy at the edge:** authentication, rate limits, TLS, logging.","**Composition:** one client call fans out to several services.","**Translation:** public REST to internal gRPC, or mobile to desktop payloads."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The gateway is on every request path. It must stay thin and highly available, or it becomes a bottleneck and a distributed monolith's front door.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"/users/7 -> 200 user:7\n/home -> 200 user:7 | orders:2\n/orders/7 -> 401 unauthorized\n/billing -> 404 no route","file":"ApiGateway.java","regions":{"aggregate":[34,42],"client":[50,58],"gateway":[11,32],"wire":[47,49]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\n\nrecord Request(String path, String token) {}\nrecord Response(int status, String body) {}\n\ninterface Service { Response handle(Request request); }\n\nfinal class Gateway {\n    private final Map<String, Service> routes = new TreeMap<>();\n    private final Set<String> tokens;\n\n    Gateway(Set<String> tokens) { this.tokens = tokens; }\n\n    Gateway route(String prefix, Service service) {\n        routes.put(prefix, service);\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    Response handle(Request req) {\n        if (!tokens.contains(req.token())) {\n            return new Response(401, \"unauthorized\");\n        }\n        if (req.path().equals(\"/home\")) return home(req);\n        return routes.entrySet().stream()\n            .filter(e -> req.path().startsWith(e.getKey()))\n            .findFirst()\n            .map(e -> e.getValue().handle(req))\n            .orElse(new Response(404, \"no route\"));\n    }\n\n    private Response home(Request req) {\n        var user = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(\n            () -> routes.get(\"/users\").handle(req));\n        var orders = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(\n            () -> routes.get(\"/orders\").handle(req))\n            .exceptionally(e -> new Response(206, \"orders unavailable\"));\n        return new Response(200,\n            user.join().body() + \" | \" + orders.join().body());\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var gateway = new Gateway(Set.of(\"t-1\"))\n            .route(\"/users\", r -> new Response(200, \"user:7\"))\n            .route(\"/orders\", r -> new Response(200, \"orders:2\"));\n        for (var req : new Request[] {\n                new Request(\"/users/7\", \"t-1\"),\n                new Request(\"/home\", \"t-1\"),\n                new Request(\"/orders/7\", \"bad\"),\n                new Request(\"/billing\", \"t-1\")}) {\n            var res = gateway.handle(req);\n            System.out.println(req.path() + \" -> \"\n                + res.status() + \" \" + res.body());\n        }\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The gateway owns a sorted route table and the auth check. Unauthenticated requests are rejected before any service sees them; unknown paths get a 404.","region":"gateway"},{"note":"`/home` is composed: both calls run concurrently. A failing optional dependency degrades to a 206 fragment instead of failing the whole page.","region":"aggregate"},{"note":"Services are registered behind prefixes. They are lambdas here; in production they are remote clients with their own timeouts.","region":"wire"},{"note":"The client sees one address. A valid token reaches services and composes; a bad token or unknown path never leaves the gateway.","region":"client"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A mobile banking app on twelve backend services.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The home screen needs balance, recent transactions and offers. Calling three services over a cellular link costs three round trips and exposes internal topology. A gateway offers `GET /mobile/home`: it validates the JWT once, calls the services in parallel with a 300 ms budget, drops the optional offers on timeout and returns one compact payload. The web client gets its own, different composition.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["// route: /mobile/home  ->  composition","var balance = accounts.balance(user);      // required","var recent  = ledger.recent(user, 5);      // required","var picks   = offers.forUser(user)         // optional","                    .orTimeout(300, MILLISECONDS)","                    .exceptionally(e -> List.of());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: one round trip for the app, one place for auth and rate limits, and services stay free to be split or renamed behind the facade.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"client","label":"calls","rel":"dependency","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","label":"routes","rel":"association","to":"users"},{"from":"gw","label":"routes","rel":"association","to":"orders"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"client","kind":"class","members":["+ request(path)"],"name":"Client","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"gw","kind":"class","members":["- routes: Map","+ handle(request)"],"name":"Gateway","role":"ApiGateway","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"users","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(request)"],"name":"UserService","role":"Service","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"orders","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(request)"],"name":"OrderService","role":"Service","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"gw","text":"auth, limits, routing, composition"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"GET /home + token","style":"call","to":"g"},{"from":"g","label":"get user","style":"async","to":"u"},{"from":"g","label":"get orders","style":"async","to":"o"},{"from":"u","label":"user","style":"return","to":"g"},{"from":"o","label":"orders","style":"return","to":"g"},{"from":"g","label":"composed page","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Client"},{"id":"g","name":"Gateway","role":"ApiGateway"},{"id":"u","name":"Users","role":"Service"},{"id":"o","name":"Orders","role":"Service"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Many clients need one stable, secured address for many services.","Cross-cutting policy (auth, quotas, TLS) belongs at the edge.","Different client types need differently shaped responses."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["There is one service and one client: a plain reverse proxy is enough.","Services call each other: east-west traffic belongs to a mesh, not the edge gateway."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Business logic creeping into the gateway until every team must change it.","A single shared gateway owned by one team becomes a release bottleneck.","Aggregation without timeouts and fallbacks makes the slowest service your latency."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Gateway routes that rewrite payloads field by field for each service.","A gateway in front of a monolith that only forwards requests."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Virtual threads and `CompletableFuture` make fan-out composition short; set a deadline on each call.","Java 21 records make good immutable request and response envelopes at the gateway boundary."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"A facade simplifies an in-process subsystem. A gateway is a networked facade that also enforces security, quotas and protocol translation for remote clients.","ref":"facade","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"A proxy stands in for one object with the same interface. A gateway exposes a different, client-oriented API over many services.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A gateway is a shared north-south entry point at the edge. A sidecar is per-instance and handles that service's own traffic, including service-to-service calls.","ref":"sidecar","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"The gateway is the routing facade a strangler migration needs: paths move from legacy to new services without clients noticing.","ref":"strangler-fig","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"Quotas and throttling are typically enforced at the gateway, per client or API key, before traffic reaches services.","ref":"rate-limiter","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Routes match on predicates and pass through filters for auth, rate limiting, retries and path rewriting.","name":"RouteLocator and GatewayFilter","where":"Spring Cloud Gateway"},{"detail":"The `HTTPRoute` resource binds hosts and paths to Services behind a `Gateway`.","name":"Gateway API: Gateway and HTTPRoute","where":"Kubernetes"},{"detail":"Managed gateway with routes, authorizers, usage plans and integrations to Lambda or HTTP backends.","name":"Amazon API Gateway","where":"AWS"},{"detail":"HTTP connection manager with route configuration, used as the data plane of Emissary, Istio ingress and others.","name":"Envoy edge proxy","where":"Envoy"},{"detail":"Plugin-based gateway: authentication, rate limiting and transformations attach to routes and services.","name":"Kong Gateway","where":"Kong"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Auth { boolean valid(String token); }","interface Quota { boolean tryAcquire(String client); }","interface Backend { Response call(Request r); }","record Request(String path, String token, String client) {}","record Response(int status) { static Response status(int s) { return new Response(s); } }","class Holder {","  Auth auth; Quota quota; Backend users; Backend orders;","  static String firstSegment(String p) { return p.split(\"/\")[1]; }","  {{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class Edge {","    Response handle(Request req) {","        if (!auth.valid(req.token())) return Response.status(401);","        if (!quota.tryAcquire(req.client())) return Response.status(429);","        return switch (firstSegment(req.path())) {","            case \"users\"  -> users.call(req);","            case \"orders\" -> orders.call(req);","            default      -> Response.status(404);","        };","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"api-gateway","why":"One entry point authenticates, throttles and routes requests to several backend services."},{"ref":"facade","why":"A facade simplifies a local subsystem; it does not enforce auth and quotas for remote clients."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"The checks here are fixed sequential code, not pluggable handlers deciding whether to pass the request on."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The switch picks a destination by path; there is no interchangeable algorithm behind one interface."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"api-gateway","why":"One edge entry point validates tokens once, composes the five calls into one, and hides the changing topology."},{"ref":"sidecar","why":"A sidecar handles each service's own traffic; it does not give the client one composed endpoint."},{"ref":"ambassador","why":"An ambassador helps a client reach one remote resource; it does not compose or secure many services for external callers."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Adapting one interface to another does not aggregate calls or centralize authentication."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A mobile app renders its home screen from five microservices, so it makes five calls on a slow network. Security wants a single place to validate tokens, and the service topology is changing every quarter."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;","interface Backend { Response call(Request r); }","record Request(String path) {}","record Response(String body) {}","class Holder {","  Backend users; Backend offers;","  {{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"Joining the optional `extras` call without a timeout or fallback lets the slowest, least important service dictate the page's latency and failures. Add `orTimeout` and `exceptionally` to the optional call.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["Response home(Request req) {","    var user = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(","        () -> users.call(req));","    var extras = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(","        () -> offers.call(req));","    return new Response(","        user.join().body() + extras.join().body());","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that makes the gateway fragile."},{"blocks":[["if (!auth.valid(req.token())) return Response.status(401);"],["Backend target = routes.get(prefix(req.path()));"],["if (target == null) return Response.status(404);"],["Response res = target.call(req);"],["return res;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.Map;","interface Auth { boolean valid(String t); }","interface Backend { Response call(Request r); }","record Request(String path, String token) {}","record Response(int status) { static Response status(int s) { return new Response(s); } }","class Snippet { Auth auth; Map<String, Backend> routes;","  String prefix(String p) { return p; }","  Response handle(Request req) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Authenticate before spending any backend work, resolve the route, handle the missing route, call the service, then return its response.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the gateway's request handling so each step depends only on earlier ones."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["One entry point for routing, auth, quotas and TLS","Can compose several backend calls into one response","Keep it thin: no business logic, with timeouts and fallbacks","It is a shared north-south hop, a bottleneck risk","A sidecar is per instance; the gateway is shared at the edge"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A gateway is the single edge entry point that authenticates, rate-limits, routes and composes calls to backend services, so clients stay simple. It should stay thin: routing and policy, not business logic. A sidecar sits beside each service instance and handles its own traffic, including service-to-service calls.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain an API Gateway: what it centralizes, what it should not do, and how it differs from a sidecar."}],"id":"api-gateway"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Run a helper process beside each service instance to provide cross-cutting capabilities without changing the service.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The **sidecar** shares the service's host or pod, lifecycle and network namespace, but it is a separate process, often in another language. It intercepts or complements the service's traffic to add TLS, retries, metrics, log shipping or config reload, so every service gets them uniformly.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Co-located:** deployed and scaled with the service instance.","**Isolated:** its own process, resources and release cadence.","**Language-neutral:** the same capability for Java, Go and Python services."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The in-process sample shows the seam only: the sidecar wraps the app behind the same interface. In production the hop is a real localhost or pod-network connection.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"200 order:42\n401 denied\n200 order:7\nlog: [/orders/42 200, /orders/9 401, /orders/7 200]\nmetrics: {200=2, 401=1}","file":"Sidecar.java","regions":{"app":[12,17],"deploy":[43,43],"port":[10,10],"sidecar":[19,39],"traffic":[44,52]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\nrecord Request(String path, Map<String, String> headers) {}\nrecord Response(int status, String body) {}\n\ninterface Handler { Response handle(Request request); }\n\nfinal class OrderApp implements Handler {\n    public Response handle(Request request) {\n        String id = request.path().substring(\"/orders/\".length());\n        return new Response(200, \"order:\" + id);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Sidecar implements Handler {\n    private final Handler app;\n    private final Set<String> tokens;\n    final List<String> accessLog = new ArrayList<>();\n    final Map<Integer, Integer> metrics = new TreeMap<>();\n\n    Sidecar(Handler app, Set<String> tokens) {\n        this.app = app;\n        this.tokens = tokens;\n    }\n\n    public Response handle(Request request) {\n        String token = request.headers().getOrDefault(\"token\", \"\");\n        Response res = tokens.contains(token)\n            ? app.handle(request)\n            : new Response(401, \"denied\");\n        accessLog.add(request.path() + \" \" + res.status());\n        metrics.merge(res.status(), 1, Integer::sum);\n        return res;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var sidecar = new Sidecar(new OrderApp(), Set.of(\"s3cret\"));\n        for (var call : List.of(\n                Map.entry(\"/orders/42\", \"s3cret\"),\n                Map.entry(\"/orders/9\", \"wrong\"),\n                Map.entry(\"/orders/7\", \"s3cret\"))) {\n            var req = new Request(call.getKey(),\n                Map.of(\"token\", call.getValue()));\n            var res = sidecar.handle(req);\n            System.out.println(res.status() + \" \" + res.body());\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"log: \" + sidecar.accessLog);\n        System.out.println(\"metrics: \" + sidecar.metrics);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The seam is one interface. The app is reachable only through it, which is what lets a sidecar interpose without the app knowing.","region":"port"},{"note":"Business logic only. No authentication, no access log, no metrics. It trusts that whatever reaches it was already vetted.","region":"app"},{"note":"The sidecar implements the same interface and wraps the app: it checks the token, forwards allowed calls, then records the access log and status counters for every call.","region":"sidecar"},{"note":"Deployment pairs them: callers talk to the sidecar, which owns the only reference to the app. In Kubernetes this is two containers in one pod.","region":"deploy"},{"note":"Denied calls never reach the app, yet they are still logged and counted. Capabilities arrive without a line changed in `OrderApp`.","region":"traffic"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Uniform mTLS and metrics across a polyglot fleet.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A platform has 200 services in Java, Go and Python, and security demands mutual TLS with rotated certificates plus consistent request metrics. Implementing that in three client libraries means three upgrade campaigns. Instead each pod gets an Envoy sidecar. The service speaks plain HTTP to localhost, and the proxy handles certificates, retries, and emits uniform metrics.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["// the service knows nothing about TLS or retries","HttpRequest req = HttpRequest.newBuilder(","    URI.create(\"http://localhost:15001/payments/charge\"))","    .POST(body).build();","client.send(req, BodyHandlers.ofString());"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: a certificate or retry policy change is one proxy rollout, not a rebuild of 200 services in three languages.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"sidecar","rel":"realizes","to":"handler"},{"from":"app","rel":"realizes","to":"handler"},{"from":"sidecar","label":"forwards to","rel":"association","to":"app"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"handler","kind":"interface","members":["+ handle(request)"],"name":"Handler","role":"Port","row":0},{"col":0,"id":"sidecar","kind":"class","members":["- app: Handler","+ handle(request)"],"name":"Sidecar","role":"Sidecar","row":1},{"col":1,"id":"app","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(request)"],"name":"OrderApp","role":"Service","row":1}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"sidecar","text":"auth, logs, metrics, retries"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"caller","label":"request + token","style":"call","to":"sc"},{"from":"sc","label":"handle(request)","style":"call","to":"app"},{"from":"app","label":"response","style":"return","to":"sc"},{"from":"sc","label":"response + metrics","style":"return","to":"caller"}],"participants":[{"id":"caller","name":"Caller"},{"id":"sc","name":"Sidecar","role":"Sidecar"},{"id":"app","name":"OrderApp","role":"Service"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["Many services in several languages need the same networking or operational capability.","The capability should upgrade independently of the service.","A failing helper must not crash the service's process."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["A handful of services in one language: a shared library is simpler and faster.","Latency budgets are microseconds: each hop adds a localhost round trip."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Every pod pays the sidecar's memory and CPU; at scale that is real cost.","Startup and shutdown ordering: the app can start before its proxy is ready.","A sidecar upgrade means restarting pods, unless it supports hot restart."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A sidecar that holds business logic or shares the app's state.","One sidecar per concern, so a pod runs five helper containers."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Plain `java.net.http.HttpClient` toward localhost is enough; leave TLS, retries and discovery to the proxy.","Expose `/health` and `/metrics` endpoints and let sidecars probe and scrape them, instead of embedding agents."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"An ambassador is a sidecar focused on outbound calls to one remote resource, such as a sharded cache. A general sidecar also covers inbound traffic and operational concerns.","ref":"ambassador","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Decorator wraps an object inside one process and language. A sidecar wraps a service across a process boundary, with its own lifecycle and failure modes.","ref":"decorator","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A sidecar is usually implemented as a proxy, but it is defined by deployment: co-located, per instance, and handling cross-cutting concerns for its host.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"A gateway is one shared edge component for external clients. Sidecars are per instance and carry service-to-service traffic.","ref":"api-gateway","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Resilience policies such as retries, timeouts and breakers are commonly delivered by the sidecar instead of in-app libraries.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Namespaces labeled for injection get an `istio-proxy` container added to each pod, which handles mTLS, routing and telemetry.","name":"Envoy sidecar injection","where":"Istio"},{"detail":"A Rust micro-proxy injected into each pod as the service-mesh data plane.","name":"linkerd2-proxy","where":"Linkerd"},{"detail":"An `initContainers` entry with `restartPolicy: Always` starts before the app and runs for the pod's life.","name":"Native sidecar containers","where":"Kubernetes"},{"detail":"A sidecar that exposes HTTP and gRPC APIs for state, pub/sub and service invocation to the app on localhost.","name":"daprd","where":"Dapr"},{"detail":"A common deployment tails the app's log volume and forwards it, so the app only writes files.","name":"Log-shipping sidecar","where":"Fluent Bit"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["spec:","  containers:","  - name: orders","    image: shop/orders:1.8","    ports: [{containerPort: 8080}]","  - name: proxy","    image: envoyproxy/envoy:v1.30","    ports: [{containerPort: 8443}]","    volumeMounts: [{name: certs, mountPath: /etc/certs}]","# traffic enters on 8443, is TLS-terminated, then forwarded to localhost:8080"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"sidecar","why":"A helper container shares the pod with the service and handles its TLS and traffic; the app is unchanged."},{"ref":"api-gateway","why":"A gateway is one shared edge component for many services; this proxy exists once per service instance."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"The proxy does not convert one interface to another; it adds a capability around the same interface."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"Decorator wraps within one process. This is a separate container with its own image and lifecycle."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this deployment?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"sidecar","why":"A per-instance proxy delivers mTLS and metrics uniformly across languages and upgrades independently of the services."},{"ref":"api-gateway","why":"A gateway covers north-south traffic at the edge; it does not secure service-to-service calls."},{"ref":"decorator","why":"An in-process decorator would need a version per language and a rebuild of every service."},{"ref":"strangler-fig","why":"That pattern replaces legacy systems incrementally; it does not add cross-cutting capabilities."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A company runs 150 services in Java, Go and Python. Security requires mutual TLS with rotated certificates and consistent request metrics, and the platform team cannot coordinate rebuilds of every service for each change."},{"compile":{"template":["interface Handler { Response handle(Request r); }","record Request(String token, String user) {}","record Response(int status) { static Response denied() { return new Response(401); } }","class OrderApp implements Handler { void markAuthenticated(String user) {} public Response handle(Request r) { return new Response(200); } }","{{code}}"]},"explanation":"The sidecar reaches into the app's internal state, so the two are coupled and the sidecar can't be swapped or deployed alone. Pass identity through the request, such as a header, and keep the app contract unchanged.","flawLines":[8],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["final class AuthSidecar implements Handler {","    private final OrderApp app;","","    AuthSidecar(OrderApp app) { this.app = app; }","","    public Response handle(Request r) {","        if (!valid(r.token())) return Response.denied();","        app.markAuthenticated(r.user());","        return app.handle(r);","    }","","    private boolean valid(String t) { return !t.isEmpty(); }","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the sidecar idea."},{"blocks":[["String token = request.headers().getOrDefault(\"token\", \"\");"],["if (!tokens.contains(token)) return new Response(401, \"denied\");"],["Response res = app.handle(request);"],["metrics.merge(res.status(), 1, Integer::sum);"],["return res;"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.util.*;","interface Handler { Response handle(Request r); }","record Request(Map<String, String> headers) {}","record Response(int status, String body) {}","class Snippet { Handler app; Set<String> tokens; Map<Integer, Integer> metrics = new TreeMap<>();","  Response handle(Request request) {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Read the credential, reject early so the app is never called, forward allowed requests, record the outcome from the response, then return it.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the sidecar's handling of one inbound call."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Helper process co-located with each service instance","Cross-cutting capability without changing the service","Language-neutral and independently upgradable","Costs: per-pod resources, extra hop, startup ordering","A shared library is simpler for one language"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A sidecar is a helper process deployed with each service instance, sharing its lifecycle and network. It supplies TLS, retries, metrics or log shipping uniformly across languages and upgrades independently. The costs are per-instance resource use, an extra network hop and startup ordering concerns.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Sidecar pattern: what it is, why it is a separate process, and the cost."}],"id":"sidecar"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Put a helper next to a client to handle its outbound calls to a remote resource, so the client stays simple.","type":"lead"},{"text":"The client talks to a local endpoint as if the resource were next door. The **ambassador** owns the awkward parts of reaching the real one: service discovery, sharding, connection pooling, TLS, retries and failover. It is a sidecar specialized for the outbound path.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Client sees:** one stable local address and a simple protocol.","**Ambassador owns:** routing, retries, timeouts, credentials.","**Useful for:** legacy clients you cannot modify."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"Think of it as the client's diplomat: it speaks the remote system's language and policies so the client doesn't have to.","tone":"info","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"a2:user:1\nb1:user:2\na2:user:3\ntrace: [a1, a2, b1, a1, a2]","file":"Ambassador.java","regions":{"ambassador":[18,35],"app":[37,45],"node":[9,16],"port":[5,7],"wire":[50,53]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.io.IOException;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface KeyValue {\n    String get(String key) throws IOException;\n}\n\nrecord Node(String name, boolean up, List<String> trace)\n        implements KeyValue {\n    public String get(String key) throws IOException {\n        trace.add(name);\n        if (!up) throw new IOException(name + \" is down\");\n        return name + \":\" + key;\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class Ambassador implements KeyValue {\n    private final List<List<KeyValue>> shards;\n\n    Ambassador(List<List<KeyValue>> shards) { this.shards = shards; }\n\n    public String get(String key) throws IOException {\n        int shard = Math.floorMod(key.hashCode(), shards.size());\n        IOException last = null;\n        for (KeyValue replica : shards.get(shard)) {\n            try {\n                return replica.get(key);\n            } catch (IOException e) {\n                last = e;\n            }\n        }\n        throw new IOException(\"shard \" + shard + \" unavailable\", last);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class ProfileService {\n    private final KeyValue store;\n\n    ProfileService(KeyValue store) { this.store = store; }\n\n    String load(String user) throws IOException {\n        return store.get(\"user:\" + user);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        var trace = new ArrayList<String>();\n        var ambassador = new Ambassador(List.of(\n            List.of(new Node(\"a1\", false, trace), new Node(\"a2\", true, trace)),\n            List.of(new Node(\"b1\", true, trace), new Node(\"b2\", true, trace))));\n        var service = new ProfileService(ambassador);\n        for (String user : List.of(\"1\", \"2\", \"3\")) {\n            System.out.println(service.load(user));\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"trace: \" + trace);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The client-side contract is a plain `KeyValue`. Whether it is a map, one server or a cluster is invisible to callers.","region":"port"},{"note":"A scripted fake node: it records every attempt in a shared trace and fails deterministically when `up` is false, so failover is testable without real time.","region":"node"},{"note":"The ambassador picks the shard from a stable hash of the key, then tries that shard's replicas in order. Only if all fail does the client see an error.","region":"ambassador"},{"note":"The application code is a single call. It has no shard map, no retry loop and no knowledge of replicas.","region":"app"},{"note":"Two shards of two replicas. Replica `a1` is down. Calls to shard A fall through to `a2` while shard B answers on its first replica.","region":"wire"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"A legacy app talking to a sharded Redis cluster.","type":"lead"},{"text":"An old billing service only speaks to one Redis address and has no cluster awareness. Rewriting its client library is out of scope. Next to each instance runs a proxy that exposes `localhost:6379`, hashes keys to shards, follows failover to replicas and reuses pooled connections. The app is unchanged while the cache tier grows from two shards to eight.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["// legacy code: unchanged, believes in one local Redis","try (Jedis redis = new Jedis(\"localhost\", 6379)) {","    redis.set(\"invoice:\" + id, json);","}","// the ambassador on :6379 picks shard and replica"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: topology, failover and connection policy move out of the app, so scaling the cache tier needs no application release.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"app","label":"uses","rel":"association","to":"kv"},{"from":"amb","rel":"realizes","to":"kv"},{"from":"node","rel":"realizes","to":"kv"},{"from":"amb","label":"replicas","rel":"aggregation","to":"node"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"app","kind":"class","members":["- store: KeyValue","+ load(user)"],"name":"ProfileService","role":"Client","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"kv","kind":"interface","members":["+ get(key): String"],"name":"KeyValue","role":"Port","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"amb","kind":"class","members":["- shards: List","+ get(key): String"],"name":"Ambassador","role":"Ambassador","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"node","kind":"record","members":["+ get(key): String"],"name":"Node","role":"RemoteResource","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"amb","text":"shard, retry, failover"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"app","label":"get(key)","style":"call","to":"amb"},{"from":"amb","label":"get(key)","style":"call","to":"a1"},{"from":"a1","label":"IOException","style":"return","to":"amb"},{"from":"amb","label":"get(key)","style":"call","to":"a2"},{"from":"a2","label":"value","style":"return","to":"amb"},{"from":"amb","label":"value","style":"return","to":"app"}],"participants":[{"id":"app","name":"App","role":"Client"},{"id":"amb","name":"Ambassador","role":"Ambassador"},{"id":"a1","name":"Replica a1","role":"RemoteResource"},{"id":"a2","name":"Replica a2","role":"RemoteResource"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["A client cannot be changed but must reach a complex remote resource.","Connection policy (retry, TLS, discovery) should be shared across clients and languages.","You want to test the client against a local stub."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["One client library already does the job and you control the code.","Added localhost latency or another moving part outweighs the benefit."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Blind retries through the ambassador on non-idempotent calls cause duplicates.","Hidden failover can mask an outage; export metrics from the ambassador.","Client timeouts must exceed the ambassador's retry budget, or they cancel mid-failover."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["An ambassador that transforms business data instead of managing connectivity.","One ambassador per call site instead of per client instance."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Sealed result types or checked exceptions model 'all replicas down' explicitly for callers.","For new code, a client library with the same policy may beat a separate process."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"An ambassador is a sidecar dedicated to one concern: the client's outbound access to a remote resource. A sidecar is the general co-located helper.","ref":"sidecar","relation":"specializes"},{"difference":"Same stand-in shape, but an ambassador is deployed beside the client and adds connection policy; a remote proxy simply represents one remote object.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"},{"difference":"An adapter converts one interface into another in-process. An ambassador keeps the interface and manages how the remote call is made.","ref":"adapter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"Breakers, retries and timeouts live naturally in the ambassador so clients inherit them.","ref":"circuit-breaker","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"The ambassador is a good place for retry with backoff and failover, applied uniformly to every client.","ref":"retry-backoff","relation":"combines"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"Proxy that shards keys across memcached or Redis servers so clients talk to a single address.","name":"twemproxy (nutcracker)","where":"Twitter / Twemproxy"},{"detail":"Runs next to the app, listens locally and handles TLS and IAM-based authentication to Cloud SQL.","name":"Cloud SQL Auth Proxy","where":"Google Cloud"},{"detail":"The injected Envoy captures the app's outbound traffic and applies discovery, retries, timeouts and mTLS on the way out.","name":"Outbound listener","where":"Istio / Envoy"},{"detail":"The proxy runs as a second container in the Pod; the app reaches it on `localhost` through the shared network namespace.","name":"Multi-container Pod","where":"Kubernetes"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.util.List;","interface KeyValue { String get(String key) throws IOException; }","{{code}}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["final class LocalCacheEndpoint implements KeyValue {","    private final List<List<KeyValue>> shards;","","    LocalCacheEndpoint(List<List<KeyValue>> shards) { this.shards = shards; }","","    public String get(String key) throws IOException {","        int i = Math.floorMod(key.hashCode(), shards.size());","        IOException last = new IOException(\"empty shard\");","        for (KeyValue replica : shards.get(i)) {","            try { return replica.get(key); } catch (IOException e) { last = e; }","        }","        throw last;","    }","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"ambassador","why":"A client-side stand-in hides sharding and replica failover for a remote resource behind a simple local interface."},{"ref":"proxy","why":"A plain proxy guards or represents one object. This one adds routing, retries and failover policy for a cluster."},{"ref":"api-gateway","why":"A gateway faces many external clients at the edge; this serves one client's outbound calls."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"The replicas are not interchangeable algorithms selected by the caller; the helper chooses and retries on its own."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"ambassador","why":"A local helper presents the old single address while handling sharding, TLS and tokens on the client's behalf."},{"ref":"api-gateway","why":"A gateway is an edge entry for external clients; here an internal client needs help reaching a resource."},{"ref":"strangler-fig","why":"No system is being replaced piece by piece; the client's destination simply changed shape."},{"ref":"sidecar","why":"Close, but sidecar is the general helper. The need here is specifically outbound access, which is an ambassador."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A legacy billing service opens one connection to a single database address and cannot be rebuilt. The database has been moved to a sharded cluster with TLS and token authentication, and it will be resharded again next year."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.util.List;","interface KeyValue { String get(String key) throws IOException; }","class Holder implements KeyValue { List<List<KeyValue>> shards;","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"On a replica failure the helper must fail over to the next replica, and only report an error when all are exhausted. Exiting the process takes the client down; remove it and continue the loop.","flawLines":[7],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["public String get(String key) throws IOException {","    int shard = Math.floorMod(key.hashCode(), shards.size());","    for (KeyValue replica : shards.get(shard)) {","        try {","            return replica.get(key);","        } catch (IOException e) {","            System.exit(1);","        }","    }","    throw new IOException(\"shard \" + shard + \" unavailable\");","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that breaks the ambassador's contract."},{"blocks":[["int shard = Math.floorMod(key.hashCode(), shards.size());"],["for (KeyValue replica : shards.get(shard)) {","    try { return replica.get(key); }","    catch (IOException e) { last = e; }","}"],["throw new IOException(\"shard \" + shard + \" unavailable\", last);"]],"compile":{"template":["import java.io.IOException;","import java.util.List;","interface KeyValue { String get(String key) throws IOException; }","class Snippet { List<List<KeyValue>> shards;","  String get(String key) throws IOException { IOException last = null;","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Pick the shard first, then try its replicas in order and return on success; the failure is raised only after the loop has exhausted every replica.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order the ambassador's work for one read."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Helper deployed with the client for its outbound calls","Client sees a simple local address","Owns sharding, retries, failover, TLS and credentials","A sidecar specialized for the outbound path","Lets unchanged legacy clients reach modern resources"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"An ambassador is a helper beside the client that makes outbound calls to a remote resource: it hides discovery, sharding, TLS, retries and failover behind a simple local address. It is a sidecar specialized for outbound access, and useful when the client cannot be changed.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Ambassador pattern and how it differs from a general sidecar."}],"id":"ambassador"},{"cards":[{"blocks":[{"text":"Replace a legacy system incrementally by routing one slice of traffic at a time to new code behind a facade.","type":"lead"},{"text":"Put a routing **facade** in front of the legacy system. Build one capability in the new system, switch its route, watch it, and repeat. The legacy system shrinks until nothing routes to it and it can be switched off. Like the fig that grows around a host tree, the new system never needs a big-bang cutover.","type":"p"},{"items":["**Slice:** a path, a feature or a customer segment.","**Switch:** by route rule, percentage or flag, reversible instantly.","**Finish:** delete the legacy code and the routing rule."],"type":"bullets"},{"text":"The migration only pays off if it ends. An unretired legacy system plus a new one doubles the maintenance surface.","tone":"warning","type":"callout"}],"role":"definition","type":"text"},{"expectedOutput":"alice: modern /invoices/1\nbob: modern /invoices/1\ncarol: modern /invoices/1\ndave: legacy /invoices/1\nmodern /customers/9\nlegacy /reports/q3","file":"StranglerFig.java","regions":{"backends":[38,39],"facade":[8,34],"rules":[40,42],"traffic":[43,48]},"role":"implementation","source":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedHashMap;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\ninterface Backend { String handle(String userId, String path); }\n\nfinal class StranglerFacade implements Backend {\n    private final Backend legacy;\n    private final Backend modern;\n    private final Map<String, Integer> migrated = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n\n    StranglerFacade(Backend legacy, Backend modern) {\n        this.legacy = legacy;\n        this.modern = modern;\n    }\n\n    StranglerFacade migrate(String prefix, int percent) {\n        migrated.put(prefix, percent);\n        return this;\n    }\n\n    public String handle(String userId, String path) {\n        for (var rule : migrated.entrySet()) {\n            if (path.startsWith(rule.getKey())) {\n                int bucket = Math.floorMod(userId.hashCode(), 100);\n                return bucket < rule.getValue()\n                    ? modern.handle(userId, path)\n                    : legacy.handle(userId, path);\n            }\n        }\n        return legacy.handle(userId, path);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Backend legacy = (u, p) -> \"legacy \" + p;\n        Backend modern = (u, p) -> \"modern \" + p;\n        var facade = new StranglerFacade(legacy, modern)\n            .migrate(\"/customers\", 100)\n            .migrate(\"/invoices\", 50);\n        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String user : List.of(\"alice\", \"bob\", \"carol\", \"dave\")) {\n            out.add(user + \": \" + facade.handle(user, \"/invoices/1\"));\n        }\n        out.add(facade.handle(\"u1\", \"/customers/9\"));\n        out.add(facade.handle(\"u1\", \"/reports/q3\"));\n        out.forEach(System.out::println);\n    }\n}","type":"code","walkthrough":[{"note":"The facade is itself a `Backend`, so clients are unaffected. Each migrated prefix has a rollout percentage; anything unmatched falls through to legacy.","region":"facade"},{"note":"Both systems sit behind one interface. Here they are lambdas; in reality they are two deployments reached over the network.","region":"backends"},{"note":"The routing table is the migration plan: customers fully moved, invoices at 50 percent. Raising a number moves more traffic, lowering it is an instant rollback.","region":"rules"},{"note":"The bucket is a stable hash of the user id, so each user always lands on the same side, avoiding flip-flopping between systems.","region":"traffic"}]},{"blocks":[{"text":"Retiring a ten-year-old monolithic order system.","type":"lead"},{"text":"A gateway rule table sits in front of the monolith. The team extracts customer profiles first and routes `/customers/**` to the new service. Invoices move next: 5 percent of users, then 25, 50 and 100 while error rate and p99 latency are compared against the monolith. A shadow mode replays requests to both and diffs the responses. When a path hits 100 percent for a release cycle, its monolith code is deleted.","type":"p"},{"language":"java","lines":["var facade = new StranglerFacade(monolith, orders)","    .migrate(\"/customers\", 100)   // done","    .migrate(\"/invoices\", 25)     // canary","    ;                            // /reports stays legacy"],"type":"code"},{"text":"Payoff: each step is small, observable and reversible, so the migration ships continuously instead of as one risky launch.","tone":"positive","type":"callout"}],"role":"example","type":"text"},{"diagrams":[{"edges":[{"from":"facade","rel":"realizes","to":"backend"},{"from":"legacy","rel":"realizes","to":"backend"},{"from":"modern","rel":"realizes","to":"backend"},{"from":"facade","label":"default","rel":"association","to":"legacy"},{"from":"facade","label":"migrated","rel":"association","to":"modern"}],"kind":"class","nodes":[{"col":0.5,"id":"backend","kind":"interface","members":["+ handle(user, path)"],"name":"Backend","role":"Facade contract","row":0},{"col":0.5,"id":"facade","kind":"class","members":["- migrated: Map","+ handle(user, path)"],"name":"StranglerFacade","role":"Facade","row":1},{"col":0,"id":"legacy","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(user, path)"],"name":"Legacy","role":"LegacySystem","row":2},{"col":1,"id":"modern","kind":"class","members":["+ handle(user, path)"],"name":"Modern","role":"NewSystem","row":2}],"notes":[{"attachTo":"facade","text":"routing table = migration plan"}],"title":"Class"},{"fragments":[],"kind":"sequence","messages":[{"from":"c","label":"GET /reports","style":"call","to":"f"},{"from":"f","label":"not migrated","style":"call","to":"l"},{"from":"l","label":"response","style":"return","to":"f"},{"from":"f","label":"response","style":"return","to":"c"},{"from":"c","label":"GET /customers","style":"call","to":"f"},{"from":"f","label":"migrated","style":"call","to":"m"},{"from":"m","label":"response","style":"return","to":"f"},{"from":"f","label":"response","style":"return","to":"c"}],"participants":[{"id":"c","name":"Client"},{"id":"f","name":"Facade","role":"Facade"},{"id":"l","name":"Legacy","role":"LegacySystem"},{"id":"m","name":"Modern","role":"NewSystem"}],"title":"Sequence"}],"role":"structure","type":"diagram"},{"lists":[{"items":["The legacy system is too big or risky to rewrite in one go.","You can intercept traffic at a seam: HTTP, queue or UI route.","Business must keep running throughout."],"title":"Use when","tone":"positive"},{"items":["The system is small enough to rewrite and cut over in a sprint.","No clean seam exists, so every slice drags in shared database writes."],"title":"Avoid when","tone":"negative"},{"items":["Two systems writing the same data need synchronization, or one must own it per slice.","Never decommissioning legacy: the end state becomes two systems.","Inconsistent per-user routing causes flickering behavior; hash on a stable key."],"title":"Pitfalls","tone":"warning"},{"items":["A facade that translates every payload, becoming a third system.","Strangling a system that would be cheaper to leave alone."],"title":"Overuse smells","tone":"warning"},{"items":["Route rules fit well as records in a sealed hierarchy, loaded from configuration.","Spring Cloud Gateway predicates express the route table without custom code."],"title":"Modern Java","tone":"info"}],"role":"tradeoffs","type":"lists"},{"role":"compare","rows":[{"difference":"The strangler's router is a facade over two backends, with the difference that its routing decisions change over time and the facade is meant to be removed or simplified.","ref":"facade","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An existing gateway is the natural place to hold migration routes. The gateway is permanent; the strangler routing rules are temporary.","ref":"api-gateway","relation":"combines"},{"difference":"An adapter permanently translates between interfaces. A strangler routes between implementations of the same interface, then goes away.","ref":"adapter","relation":"oftenConfused"},{"difference":"The facade is a proxy in front of two systems, choosing between them per request rather than guarding one.","ref":"proxy","relation":"lookAlike"}],"type":"comparison"},{"entries":[{"detail":"`Path` and `Weight` predicates route matching traffic to the new service while everything else proxies to the legacy one.","name":"Route predicates","where":"Spring Cloud Gateway"},{"detail":"`route.weight` splits traffic between legacy and new versions of a destination, adjustable without redeploying clients.","name":"VirtualService weighted routes","where":"Istio"},{"detail":"A listener rule forwards a percentage of requests to a target group fronting the new implementation.","name":"ALB weighted target groups","where":"AWS"},{"detail":"Per-path `location` blocks send migrated routes to the new upstream and the default to the old one.","name":"location with proxy_pass","where":"NGINX"}],"role":"sightings","type":"references"}],"exercises":[{"compile":{"template":["interface Platform { String handle(String user, String path); }","class Holder { Platform newPlatform; Platform oldPlatform; int invoiceShare = 25;","{{code}}","}"]},"id":"spot-1","kind":"identify","lines":["String handle(String user, String path) {","    boolean moved = path.startsWith(\"/customers\")","        || path.startsWith(\"/invoices\")","            && Math.floorMod(user.hashCode(), 100) < invoiceShare;","    return moved","        ? newPlatform.handle(user, path)","        : oldPlatform.handle(user, path);","}"],"options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"strangler-fig","why":"A routing layer sends migrated paths and a growing user share to the new system and everything else to the old one."},{"ref":"strategy","why":"A strategy is selected by the client to vary an algorithm; here a facade moves live traffic between two whole systems."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Nothing converts an interface; both targets have the same one."},{"ref":"chain-of-responsibility","why":"There is no chain of handlers passing a request along; one condition picks a target."}],"prompt":"Which pattern is this?"},{"id":"pick-1","kind":"choose","options":[{"correct":true,"ref":"strangler-fig","why":"Route feature by feature through the existing HTTP seam, keeping each step small and reversible until legacy has no traffic."},{"ref":"adapter","why":"Adapting the old API forever keeps legacy alive instead of retiring it."},{"ref":"sidecar","why":"A sidecar adds capabilities to a service; it does not migrate functionality between systems."},{"ref":"saga","why":"That coordinates a distributed transaction; it plays no role in swapping implementations."}],"prompt":"Which pattern fits best?","scenario":"A ten-year-old order system has no tests and runs the business. The company wants to move to a new platform without a risky cutover. Traffic enters through one HTTP layer, and different features can move at different times."},{"compile":{"template":["import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;","interface Platform { String handle(String user, String path); }","class Holder { Platform newPlatform; Platform oldPlatform; int share = 25;","{{code}}","}"]},"explanation":"A random bucket sends the same user to different systems on consecutive requests, producing inconsistent data and flicker. Derive the bucket from a stable key, such as `floorMod(user.hashCode(), 100)`.","flawLines":[2],"id":"flaw-1","kind":"tapLine","lines":["String handle(String user, String path) {","    int bucket = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(100);","    if (path.startsWith(\"/invoices\") && bucket < share) {","        return newPlatform.handle(user, path);","    }","    return oldPlatform.handle(user, path);","}"],"prompt":"Tap the line that makes the rollout unreliable."},{"blocks":[["Platform replacement = new InvoiceService(db);"],["facade.migrate(\"/invoices\", 5);"],["facade.migrate(\"/invoices\", 50);"],["facade.migrate(\"/invoices\", 100);"],["legacy.removeInvoices();"]],"compile":{"template":["interface Platform { String handle(String user, String path); }","class InvoiceService implements Platform { InvoiceService(Object db) {} public String handle(String u, String p) { return p; } }","class Facade { Facade migrate(String prefix, int percent) { return this; } }","class Legacy { void removeInvoices() {} }","class Snippet { Object db; Facade facade; Legacy legacy;","  void run() {","{{code}}","} }"]},"explanation":"Build the replacement, send a small canary share, widen it as metrics hold, reach full traffic, and only then delete the legacy code.","id":"order-1","kind":"reorder","prompt":"Order one strangler step, from first to last."},{"id":"recall-1","keyPoints":["Routing facade in front of legacy and new systems","Migrate one slice at a time by path, percentage or flag","Each step is observable and reversible","Stable routing keys avoid per-request flip-flopping","It must end: decommission legacy and remove the routes"],"kind":"recall","modelAnswer":[{"text":"A routing facade sits in front of the legacy system. You rebuild one capability at a time and shift its traffic by path or percentage, watching metrics, with instant rollback by lowering the share. When nothing routes to legacy, you delete it and the temporary routes.","type":"p"}],"prompt":"Explain the Strangler Fig pattern: how traffic moves, what makes each step safe, and how the migration ends."}],"id":"strangler-fig"}],"topic":{"categories":[{"id":"foundations","summary":"Principles every pattern builds on.","template":"principle","title":"Foundations"},{"id":"creational","summary":"How objects get made.","template":"pattern","title":"Creational"},{"id":"structural","summary":"How objects are composed into larger structures.","template":"pattern","title":"Structural"},{"id":"behavioral","summary":"How objects share responsibility and communicate.","template":"pattern","title":"Behavioral"},{"id":"concurrency","summary":"Coordinating work across threads.","template":"pattern","title":"Concurrency"},{"id":"enterprise","summary":"Structuring applications and their data access.","template":"pattern","title":"Enterprise & Architecture"},{"id":"resilience","summary":"Keeping distributed systems working when parts fail.","template":"pattern","title":"Resilience & Cloud"}],"codeLanguage":"java","id":"design-patterns","items":[{"aliases":["SRP","Single Responsibility Principle"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":1,"id":"srp","intent":"Give each class one reason to change.","title":"Single Responsibility"},{"aliases":["OCP","Open/Closed Principle"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":1,"id":"ocp","intent":"Extend behavior by adding code, not by editing working code.","title":"Open/Closed"},{"aliases":["LSP","Liskov Substitution Principle"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"lsp","intent":"A subtype must be usable anywhere its supertype is, without surprises.","title":"Liskov Substitution"},{"aliases":["ISP","Interface Segregation Principle"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"isp","intent":"Prefer several small, focused interfaces to one fat one.","title":"Interface Segregation"},{"aliases":["DIP","Dependency Inversion Principle"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"dip","intent":"Depend on abstractions; let low-level details implement what high-level code needs.","title":"Dependency Inversion"},{"aliases":["Favor Composition","Has-a over Is-a"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"composition-over-inheritance","intent":"Reuse behavior by holding collaborators, not by extending classes.","title":"Composition over Inheritance"},{"aliases":["Code to an Interface","Program to a Supertype"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"program-to-interface","intent":"Depend on what a type can do, not on the concrete class that does it.","title":"Program to an Interface"},{"aliases":["Law of Demeter","Principle of Least Knowledge"],"category":"foundations","difficulty":2,"id":"tell-dont-ask","intent":"Tell objects what to do instead of pulling out their state to decide for them.","title":"Tell, Don't Ask"},{"aliases":["Virtual Constructor"],"category":"creational","difficulty":1,"id":"factory-method","intent":"Let a method, overridable or injectable, decide which concrete class to create.","title":"Factory Method"},{"aliases":["Kit"],"category":"creational","difficulty":3,"id":"abstract-factory","intent":"Create whole families of related objects without naming their concrete classes.","title":"Abstract Factory"},{"aliases":["Fluent Builder"],"category":"creational","difficulty":1,"id":"builder","intent":"Assemble a complex object step by step and validate it when built.","title":"Builder"},{"aliases":["Clone"],"category":"creational","difficulty":2,"id":"prototype","intent":"Create new objects by copying an existing one.","title":"Prototype"},{"aliases":["Single Instance"],"category":"creational","difficulty":2,"id":"singleton","intent":"Guarantee one instance per scope; today usually an enum or a DI-managed singleton.","title":"Singleton"},{"aliases":["Wrapper"],"category":"structural","difficulty":1,"id":"adapter","intent":"Make an incompatible interface look like the one your code expects.","title":"Adapter"},{"aliases":["Handle/Body"],"category":"structural","difficulty":2,"id":"bridge","intent":"Split an abstraction from its implementation so each can vary on its own.","title":"Bridge"},{"aliases":["Object Tree"],"category":"structural","difficulty":2,"id":"composite","intent":"Treat single objects and groups of objects through one interface.","title":"Composite"},{"aliases":["Wrapper"],"category":"structural","difficulty":2,"id":"decorator","intent":"Add behavior to an object by wrapping it in another object with the same interface.","title":"Decorator"},{"aliases":["Simplified Interface"],"category":"structural","difficulty":2,"id":"facade","intent":"Offer one simple entry point to a complex subsystem.","title":"Facade"},{"aliases":["Cache"],"category":"structural","difficulty":3,"id":"flyweight","intent":"Share immutable state between many objects to save memory.","title":"Flyweight"},{"aliases":["Surrogate"],"category":"structural","difficulty":2,"id":"proxy","intent":"Stand in for another object to control access, defer work, or add checks.","title":"Proxy"},{"aliases":["Chain of Command","Handler Chain"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"chain-of-responsibility","intent":"Pass a request along a chain of handlers until one deals with it.","title":"Chain of Responsibility"},{"aliases":["Action","Transaction"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"command","intent":"Wrap a request in an object so it can be queued, logged, retried or undone.","title":"Command"},{"aliases":[],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":3,"id":"interpreter","intent":"Represent a small language as a grammar of classes and evaluate sentences in it.","title":"Interpreter"},{"aliases":["Cursor"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":1,"id":"iterator","intent":"Walk a collection without exposing how it is stored.","title":"Iterator"},{"aliases":["Controller"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"mediator","intent":"Centralize how a group of objects talk to each other.","title":"Mediator"},{"aliases":["Snapshot","Token"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"memento","intent":"Capture an object's state so it can be restored later without breaking encapsulation.","title":"Memento"},{"aliases":["Publish-Subscribe","Listener","Dependents"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"observer","intent":"Notify any number of dependents when an object changes.","title":"Observer"},{"aliases":["Objects for States"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"state","intent":"Let an object change its behavior when its internal state changes.","title":"State"},{"aliases":["Policy"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":1,"id":"strategy","intent":"Swap one algorithm for another behind a single interface.","title":"Strategy"},{"aliases":[],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":2,"id":"template-method","intent":"Fix the skeleton of an algorithm in a base class and let subclasses fill in the steps.","title":"Template Method"},{"aliases":["Double Dispatch"],"category":"behavioral","difficulty":3,"id":"visitor","intent":"Add new operations to a class hierarchy without changing the classes.","title":"Visitor"},{"aliases":["Value Object"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":1,"id":"immutable-object","intent":"Share state safely between threads by never changing it.","title":"Immutable Object"},{"aliases":["Monitor Object","Guarded Wait"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":2,"id":"guarded-suspension","intent":"Make a thread wait until a condition holds before it proceeds.","title":"Guarded Suspension"},{"aliases":["Bounded Buffer"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":2,"id":"producer-consumer","intent":"Decouple threads that make work from threads that do it with a queue.","title":"Producer-Consumer"},{"aliases":["Worker Pool","Executor"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":1,"id":"thread-pool","intent":"Reuse a fixed set of worker threads for many short tasks.","title":"Thread Pool"},{"aliases":["CompletableFuture","Promise"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":2,"id":"future-promise","intent":"Represent a result that is not ready yet and compose what happens when it is.","title":"Future / Promise"},{"aliases":["Shared-Exclusive Lock"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":2,"id":"read-write-lock","intent":"Allow many concurrent readers but only one writer.","title":"Read-Write Lock"},{"aliases":["DCL"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":3,"id":"double-checked-locking","intent":"Avoid locking on every access to a lazily created object; mostly obsolete today.","title":"Double-Checked Locking"},{"aliases":["Actor"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":3,"id":"active-object","intent":"Give an object its own thread and queue so callers invoke it asynchronously.","title":"Active Object"},{"aliases":["Event Loop","Dispatcher"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":3,"id":"reactor","intent":"Handle many I/O sources on few threads by dispatching ready events to handlers.","title":"Reactor"},{"aliases":["Divide and Conquer"],"category":"concurrency","difficulty":3,"id":"fork-join","intent":"Split a task into subtasks, run them in parallel, and combine the results.","title":"Fork-Join"},{"aliases":["N-Tier","Layers"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":1,"id":"layered-architecture","intent":"Organize code into layers where each only talks to the layer below.","title":"Layered Architecture"},{"aliases":["Ports and Adapters","Clean Architecture"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"hexagonal","intent":"Keep the domain in the center and plug the outside world in through ports and adapters.","title":"Hexagonal Architecture"},{"aliases":["MVC"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"mvc","intent":"Separate data, presentation and input handling into three roles.","title":"Model-View-Controller"},{"aliases":["MVVM"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"mvvm","intent":"Bind a view to a view model that exposes state and commands, free of UI classes.","title":"Model-View-ViewModel"},{"aliases":["DI","Inversion of Control"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":1,"id":"dependency-injection","intent":"Hand an object its collaborators instead of letting it create or look them up.","title":"Dependency Injection"},{"aliases":["Registry"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"service-locator","intent":"Look up services from a global registry; the pattern DI exists to replace.","title":"Service Locator"},{"aliases":["Collection-like Persistence"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"repository","intent":"Hide storage behind a collection-like interface for domain objects.","title":"Repository"},{"aliases":[],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"unit-of-work","intent":"Track changes during a business transaction and commit them together.","title":"Unit of Work"},{"aliases":["ORM Mapper"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"data-mapper","intent":"Move data between objects and a database while keeping them ignorant of each other.","title":"Data Mapper"},{"aliases":[],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"active-record","intent":"An object wraps a database row and knows how to save and load itself.","title":"Active Record"},{"aliases":["Predicate Object"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"specification","intent":"Express a business rule as a small object that can be combined and reused.","title":"Specification"},{"aliases":["DTO","Transfer Object"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":2,"id":"dto","intent":"Carry data across a boundary in a plain object shaped for that boundary.","title":"Data Transfer Object"},{"aliases":[],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"domain-event","intent":"Record that something meaningful happened in the domain so other parts can react.","title":"Domain Event"},{"aliases":["Command Query Responsibility Segregation"],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"cqrs","intent":"Use separate models for changing state and for reading it.","title":"CQRS"},{"aliases":[],"category":"enterprise","difficulty":3,"id":"event-sourcing","intent":"Store the sequence of events that happened and derive current state from them.","title":"Event Sourcing"},{"aliases":["Deadline"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":1,"id":"timeout","intent":"Bound how long you wait for a call so a slow dependency cannot hold you hostage.","title":"Timeout"},{"aliases":["Exponential Backoff","Retry","Jitter"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":1,"id":"retry-backoff","intent":"Retry transient failures with growing, randomized delays.","title":"Retry with Backoff"},{"aliases":["Fallback"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"circuit-breaker","intent":"Stop calling a failing dependency for a while and fail fast or fall back.","title":"Circuit Breaker"},{"aliases":["Isolation"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"bulkhead","intent":"Isolate resources per consumer so one failure cannot sink everything.","title":"Bulkhead"},{"aliases":["Throttling","Token Bucket"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"rate-limiter","intent":"Cap how many requests are accepted per unit of time.","title":"Rate Limiter"},{"aliases":["Idempotent Receiver","Deduplication"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":3,"id":"idempotent-consumer","intent":"Make processing a message twice have the same effect as once.","title":"Idempotent Consumer"},{"aliases":["Outbox"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":3,"id":"transactional-outbox","intent":"Write the message in the same transaction as the data, then publish it reliably.","title":"Transactional Outbox"},{"aliases":["Process Manager","Long-Running Transaction"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":3,"id":"saga","intent":"Coordinate a multi-service workflow with local transactions and compensations.","title":"Saga"},{"aliases":["Lazy Loading Cache"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"cache-aside","intent":"Load data into a cache on demand and keep the database as the source of truth.","title":"Cache-Aside"},{"aliases":["Work Queue"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":3,"id":"competing-consumers","intent":"Let several consumers pull from one queue to scale throughput.","title":"Competing Consumers"},{"aliases":["Edge Service"],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"api-gateway","intent":"Offer clients one entry point that routes, aggregates and secures calls to services.","title":"API Gateway"},{"aliases":[],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"sidecar","intent":"Run a helper process next to a service to provide cross-cutting capabilities.","title":"Sidecar"},{"aliases":[],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"ambassador","intent":"Put a helper next to a client to handle its outbound calls.","title":"Ambassador"},{"aliases":[],"category":"resilience","difficulty":2,"id":"strangler-fig","intent":"Replace a legacy system piece by piece behind a routing facade.","title":"Strangler Fig"}],"javaRelease":21,"path":[{"items":["srp","ocp","lsp","isp","dip","composition-over-inheritance","program-to-interface","tell-dont-ask"],"title":"Foundations"},{"items":["strategy","factory-method","builder","adapter","decorator","observer","template-method","facade"],"title":"First patterns"},{"items":["iterator","command","state","composite","proxy","singleton","chain-of-responsibility","abstract-factory"],"title":"Core GoF"},{"items":["bridge","mediator","memento","prototype","flyweight","visitor","interpreter"],"title":"Advanced GoF"},{"items":["layered-architecture","dependency-injection","service-locator","dto","mvc","mvvm","hexagonal","repository","data-mapper","active-record","unit-of-work","specification","domain-event"],"title":"Application architecture"},{"items":["immutable-object","thread-pool","future-promise","producer-consumer","guarded-suspension","read-write-lock","double-checked-locking","fork-join","active-object","reactor"],"title":"Concurrency"},{"items":["timeout","retry-backoff","circuit-breaker","bulkhead","rate-limiter","cache-aside","idempotent-consumer","competing-consumers"],"title":"Resilience"},{"items":["api-gateway","sidecar","ambassador","strangler-fig","transactional-outbox","saga","cqrs","event-sourcing"],"title":"Distributed systems"}],"requiredExercises":[["identify"],["choose"],["tapLine","reorder"],["recall"]],"roles":{"compare":{"symbol":"arrow.left.arrow.right","title":"Compare & relate","type":"comparison"},"definition":{"symbol":"text.book.closed","title":"Definition","type":"text"},"example":{"symbol":"building.2","title":"Example of use","type":"text"},"implementation":{"symbol":"chevron.left.forwardslash.chevron.right","title":"Implementation","type":"code"},"sightings":{"symbol":"binoculars","title":"In the wild","type":"references"},"structure":{"symbol":"point.3.connected.trianglepath.dotted","title":"Structure","type":"diagram"},"tradeoffs":{"symbol":"scale.3d","title":"When to use","type":"lists"}},"schemaVersion":1,"sortOrder":0,"subtitle":"GoF, concurrency, enterprise and resilience patterns in modern Java","symbol":"square.stack.3d.up","templates":{"pattern":{"roles":["definition","implementation","example","structure","tradeoffs","compare","sightings"]},"principle":{"optionalRoles":["structure"],"roles":["definition","implementation","example","tradeoffs","compare","sightings"]}},"title":"Design Patterns"}}
